1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Salvatore Giovanni Riggitano''' was born on 24 June 1876 at Via Ottaviano in Milazzo, a large town in Messina province on the northern coast of Sicily, in what was then the Kingdom of Italy. He was the youngest child of Santi R
2 CONC iggitano (1824-1898), a judicial officer or government functionary (usciere) and Maria Alioto (1834-after 1908). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the United States, he first used the name '''Jean Santi Prevost''' or '''Jean Lanti Prevost''', and then finally '''John R. Prevost'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took the surname "Prevost" as it was the birth surname of Jeanne Eugenie Prevost, the mother of his second wife, Suzanne Fontaine.  They were the grandparents of https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Leo-XIV/6000000218365400850 (bor
2 CONC n as Robert Francis Prevost).
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/discussions/298726 See here] for a Geni discussion thread about his origins, name, and parentage.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==First marriage==
2 CONT
2 CONT Salvatore Giovanni Riggitano married Daisy Hughes (1875-1939) on 14 April 1914 at the Immanuel Baptist Church in Chicago.  They were in the same household in the 1920 census with no children listed.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Second marriage==
2 CONT
2 CONT No record has been found for John Prevost’s marriage to Suzanne Louise Marie Fabre (1894-1979). Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Centi Prevost, Jr., born 22 July 1917 in Lackawanna, New York
2 CONT # Louis Lanti Marius Prevost, born 28 July 1920 in Chicago, Illinois
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Timeline==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Salvatore Riggitano, as he was then known, arrived in the United States at New York in May 1903 on the SS ''Perugia''.
2 CONT * In September 1918, Salvatore Riggitano and wife Daisy were living at 1716-18 Greenleaf Avenue in Chicago. He was working as a "teacher of language" at the Gordon-Detwiler Institute of Language at 116 South Michigan in the city, 
2 CONC and was listed as a non-declared alien on his World War I draft registration.
2 CONT * In 1922, he was running his own Riggitano Language School in Chicago, where he taught Italian, French, and Spanish. It was located at 1530 [https://chicagology.com/skyscrapers/skyscrapers180/ Kimball Hall], a 16-storey skyscrape
2 CONC r built in 1917 that catered to the music and arts community; it is now part of DePaul University's campus.
2 CONT * Per Giovanni Ermenegildo Schiavo's 1928 book ''The Italians in Chicago: A Study in Americanization'': "At present there ae in Chicago several schools of languages in the loop which are directed by Italians. ... Professor Riggita
2 CONC no's studio in the Kimball Building is also well known."
2 CONT * In 1929, "Signor S. G. Riggitano" was a member of the faculty of the Chicago Piano College, teaching Italian, French, and Spanish. The college's main office was in Suite 927 of the Kimball Building.
2 CONT * Teacher of Romance languages at a private language school in Chicago, per the 1950 U.S. Federal Census.
2 CONT * At the time of his death in May 1960, he and Suzanne were living at 320 West 107th Street, Chicago. He was described in his death notice as "devoted husband of Suzanne; loving father of John C. and Louis M.; grandfather of three
2 CONC ."
2 CONT * He was buried Wednesday, 24 May 1960, going from the mortuary at 12 East 112th Place in Chicago to interment at Holy Sepulchre. His funeral was at Holy Rosary Slovak Church at 9 AM with a Mass at 9:40 AM.
2 CONT
