2 CONC ay 2025. [https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Leo-XIV/6000000218365400850 
2 CONT **Pope Leo XIV]’s paternal grandparents were Jean Lanti Prevost (born Salvatore Giovanni Riggitano; 1876–1960), an Italian man from Milazzo, Sicily; and Suzanne Fontaine, a Frenchwoman from Le Havre. **Riggitano was born on June 2
2 CONC 4, 1876, and later immigrated to the United States during the Italian diaspora and settled in Illinois, where he worked as a teacher of music and language. Suzanne Fontaine was born in 1894 to Ernest and Eugénie (née Prevost) Font
2 CONC aine. Ernest was born on September 17, 1857, in Saint-Pierre-sur-Dives. Riggitano and Fontaine began a relationship by 1917, while Riggitano was still married to another woman, although he was separated. 
2 CONT **Fontaine gave birth to their first son, John Centi—the uncle of Leo XIV—on July 23, 1917. Leo XIV's father, Louis, followed in 1920. The two stayed together until Riggitano's death in 1960 and jointly adopted the surname "Prevos
2 CONC t", which had been Fontaine's mother's maiden name. Riggitano also changed his name to "Jean Lanti Prevost". 
2 CONT **Fontaine died in a Detroit hospital on October 10, 1979, at the age of 83.
2 CONT *Bosman, Julie. (16 May 2025) ''The New York Times''.  “A Century-Old Romance That Gave the Pope His Family Name.” < [https://www.nytimes.com/2025/05/16/us/pope-leo-grandfather.html?unlocked_article_code=1.H08.AECp.ukjvEyk0woON&sm
2 CONC id=url-share link] > Accessed 17 May 2025. “Online genealogists found that Pope Leo’s paternal grandparents in Chicago were accused of having an “illicit affair” in the 1910s, adding another layer to the pope’s complex family hist
2 CONC ory.”
2 CONT * Vargas, Ramon Antonio.  (17 May 2025).  ''The Guardian''.  “Pope Leo’s grandfather was immigrant from Sicily, genealogists reveal.” < [https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/may/17/pope-leo-grandfather-sicily-immigrant link] > A
2 CONC ccessed 18 May 2025.  “ …. The information about Leo’s Sicilian roots surfaced in part on a form in 1940 that Prevost – living in Chicago at the time – was required to fill out because he was a foreign national and had not become 
2 CONC a naturalized US citizen. …”
2 CONT * Kirst, Sean. “A pope with direct family ties to kindness of Father Baker.” (17 May 2025). ''Central Current.''  < [https://centralcurrent.org/sean-kirst-a-pope-with-direct-family-ties-to-kindness-of-father-baker/ link] >.  Acces
2 CONC sed 18 May 2025.  “Pope Leo XIV’s grandmother gave birth to the Pope’s uncle in Lackawanna and lived in what was then called “The Infant Home,’ often for single mothers with nowhere else to go.” An official in the Lackawanna clerk
2 CONC ’s office — who said he’s received at least a dozen inquiries about that record in recent days, with several coming from Europe — said that reference is almost certainly to the Infant Home founded by Baker …”
2 CONT ** [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nelson_Baker Nelson Henry Baker] (February 16, 1842 – July 29, 1936) was an American Catholic monsignor in Lackawanna, New York, whom the Vatican has declared as venerable.
