1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/people/Salvatore-Giovanni-Gaetano-Riggitano-John-R-Prevost/6000000218366187822#/tab/discussion  John R. Prevost]  (1876-1960) married (no record found) '''Suzanne Louise Marie Fontaine'''.  She was born 2 Feb
2 CONC ruary 1894 in Le Havre, France, the daughter of  Ernest and Eugénie (née Prevost), and died 10 October 1979 at Harper Memorial Hospital in Detroit.  Suzanne was buried next to her husband in the Holy Sepulchre Catholic Cemetery in
2 CONC  Alsip, Cook County, Illinois (suburban Chicago).
2 CONT
2 CONT John Prevost (born as Salvatore Giovanni Gaetano Riggitano in Milazzo, Sicily) and Suzanne were the grandparents of https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Leo-XIV/6000000218365400850 (born as Robert Francis Prevost).
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Centi Prevost, Jr., born July 22, 1917 in Lackawanna, New York. Married Genevieve Christiane Kunz.
2 CONT # Louis Marius Prevost, born July 28, 1920 in Chicago, Illinois.  Married Mildred Agnes Martinez.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.geni.com/discussions/298671?msg=1768039 See here] for a Geni discussion thread about her origins, name, and parentage.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note''': John Prevost was born as Salvatore Giovanni Gaetano Riggitano.  He also used the name Jean Santi Prevost.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' Her birth surname was Fontaine. But she gave her own birth surname on records in the U.S., such as her sons' birth records, as ''Fabre''. Her sons also gave that as her name on their Social Security records until one S
2 CONC SA record for John in the 1980s, after Susanne's death, in which she is listed as Fontaine; on his SSA records prior to that, she was listed as Fabre. It is unclear if that is a change John himself made or if it is an indexing err
2 CONC or. Subsequent SSA records, for brother Louis, use Fabre.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' There are conflicting records in the U.S. regarding her birth year; most say 1894 or 1896, and one outlier says 1892. Her birth record is for 2 February 1894  at Havre, Le Havre, Seine-Maritime, Normandie, France, daug
2 CONC hter of Ernest Auguste Fontaine and Jeanne Eugénie PRÉVOST (Archives de Seine-Maritime via Filae) < [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-14001-46748542-/suzanne-louise-marie-fontaine-in-france-church-baptisms-civil-births?i
2 CONC ndId=externalindividual-3271229bcac69aaa653a79f4143f003e&auth=cb0e4d2366ddba6b66a053ec61295c61&callback_token=AKSxK2WV4cNOMLC6aIN2uVXQaqvCpH8pilUqrKWr&mrid=c2b18952e4563f7377025cc28f8c00db link] >.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note''': According to her immigration declaration, she went to the "Jeanne d'Arc" Home (253 West 24th Street, New York, New York.  [https://sideways.nyc/discover/IR5C2jkfpAaIRjbh9L2ug/jeanne-darc-home The Jeanne D’Arc Home], “o
2 CONC riginally founded in 1896 as a refuge for French girls separated from their families, has remained to this day [sic: now closed] a safe and welcoming place for women in Manhattan. Run by the Congregation of Divine Providence, the 
2 CONC home accepts women from all religions and cultures - often women from other countries who have come to New York temporarily to study or to work. On any given day, the home houses 140 women, and the staff work to create a safe and 
2 CONC warm community for all of its members. While it is a home, and visitors must respect that, it is also a wonderful historic site, representative of the hospitable spirit of New York.”
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' https://www.geni.com/people/Vincent-Cento/6000000218565580891, fruit dealer in Quincy, Illinois, was the husband of https://www.geni.com/people/Victoria-Cento/6000000218563202821?through=6000000218565580891, sister of 
2 CONC https://www.geni.com/people/Salvatore-Giovanni-Gaetano-Riggitano-John-R-Prevost/6000000218366187822?through=6000000218563202821.  He was interviewed by reporters for the ''Quincy Daily Herald'', published in two parts on  19 March
2 CONC  1917:  ([https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000218628585878&graph_node_id=profile-34843006110&mode=tagged “Riggitano in Triangle.”]). Cento claimed that he and Riggitano knew the Fontaine family when they came to Ital
2 CONC y during the Pan-European War around 1896-97. He said that when [https://www.geni.com/people/Suzanne-Prevost/6000000218366242821 Suzanne] immigrated to the US she sought out Riggitano in Chicago. Riggitano brought Suzanne to Quinc
2 CONC y for a week for a visit, but was cut short when a detective showed up with an arrest warrant for Suzanne and Riggitano.  It is not currently (May 2025) known which, if any, of her details Cento described were true.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' On her Alien Registration Form ([https://media.geni.com/p14/b8/8d/b6/c3/53444868d149f86d/9dc68990-c344-4b77-b95b-06668432c393_original.jpg?hash=440101b3a57d68295939ca1c7786c5fed0c6e795e0ef6ac53d715a47be028b58.174763799
2 CONC 9 document attached]), processed on December 3, 1940, she named herself Suzanne Marie Prevost, maiden name Suzanne Fontaine, residing at 5465 Ellis Avenue, Chicago.  Married, 2 children.  She arrived to New York City from Le Havre
2 CONC , France on March 10-20, 1916 [sic: arrived 22 March 1915].  Her usual occupation is teaching and her current occupation is housewife.  This record verifies her original name.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' SSDI says her last mailing address was in Birmingham, Michigan, while her Michigan death index clarifies that her last physical residence was in Detroit proper. On her Archdiocese of Chicago cemetery record index, it u
2 CONC ses her last recorded address in the area, 212 East 141st Place in Dolton, Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note''': Per her obituary, she was a member of the Third Order of Carmelites, who are commonly known as the "Lay Carmelites" since most (but not all) members of the order live as lay people.
2 CONT
