2 CONT ===Miscellany 
2 CONT * From < [https://en.geneanet.org/genealogyblog/post/2025/05/pope-leos-european-grandparents-the-mystery-solved geneanet.org/genealogyblog] > (15 May 2025) “[https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000218366
2 CONC 242821&photo_id=6000000218593684831&position=1 The Jeanne d’Arc Home for Friendless French Women]” where Suzanne Fontaine lived in 1915 and 1916. ''The Congregation of Divine Providence'' archivist has confirmed to us that Suzanne
2 CONC  was indeed registered at the Home the day of her arrival in NYC [March 22, 1915].”
2 CONT * “When Manhattan had a French neighborhood” (January 17, 2011) ''Ephemeral New York'' < [https://ephemeralnewyork.wordpress.com/tag/jeanne-darc-home/ link] > Accessed 18 May 2025. 
2 CONT * “A home for “friendless French girls”” (August 4, 2008) ''Ephemeral New York''  < [https://ephemeralnewyork.wordpress.com/2008/08/04/the-home-for-friendless-french-girls/ link] > Accessed 18 May 2025.  “ About 125 women currentl
2 CONC y live in the Jeanne D’Arc Home, a residence hotel on Eighth Avenue and 24th Street run by an order of nuns. It’s probably the only residency in the city, single-sex or otherwise, that has its own chapel, and one of the few that p
2 CONC rohibits men past the front desk.  The home has an interesting history. An original brick building was put up in 1896 by the Fathers of Mercy of the Church of St. Vincent de Paul, a Catholic church on 23rd Street that still holds 
2 CONC mass in French. The home was to be a place where “friendless French girls who come to this country” could establish themselves and find work as “femmes de chambre, bonnes d’enfant, and gouvernantes,” as an 1896 ''New York Times'' 
2 CONC article explains. 
2 CONT * Home > Products > Jeanne d'Arc Home, No. 253 W. 24th Street , New York, NY, 1912, Messrs. Daus & Otto < [https://www.stcroixarchitecture.com/products/jeanne-d-arc-home-no-253-w-24th-street-new-york-ny-1912-messrs-daus-otto digit
2 CONC al images] > “Large numbers of women in the city created a lot of unease.” The general public worried about the safety and propriety of these independent pioneers, so many of the first buildings had a “heavy moral overlay.” Then, 
2 CONC the homes served as a stopover on the road to marriage. “They were about raising women to be good wives,” adds Harkrader.
2 CONT ** “Inside New York’s last remaining all-women apartment buildings” (13 March 2019) ''New York Post''.  < [https://nypost.com/2019/03/13/inside-new-yorks-last-remaining-all-women-apartment-buildings/ link] > 
2 CONT * Lay Carmelites (Third Order)  < [https://www.ocarm.org/en/carmelites-around-the-world/lay-carmelites-third-order link] >  The Lay Carmelite Order of the Blessed Virgin Mary (historically known as the Third Order of Carmel) is an
2 CONC  association mainly of lay persons. Its members, responding to a special call of God, freely and deliberately commit themselves “to live in the following of Jesus Christ” according to the charism, traditions, and spirit of Carmel 
2 CONC in union with the Prior General of the Carmelite Order. The members, though not in Religious Life, choose to live out their baptismal commitment according to the spirit of the Carmelite Order. Members are brothers and sisters of t
2 CONC he Carmelite Family and share in the same call to holiness and in the same mission of the Carmelite Order.
