1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Note:''' Per his New York State birth index entry (#57195), he was born "Jean"; he later became "John".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' His New York State birth index entry says that he was born 22 July 1917, while his World War II draft registration card, Michigan death index, and Social Security records say 23 July 1917.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''John C. Prevost''' was a scholar of Romance languages and culture, particularly French, at multiple universities in the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prevost was raised in Chicago and [https://www.newspapers.com/image/355141811/ graduated] from a four-year course at Hyde Park High School, in the Woodlawn neighborhood of the city's South Side, in June 1934.
2 CONT
2 CONT After [https://books.google.com/books?id=xTObKiHPvqkC&q=john+centi+prevost receiving] his B.A. in French in 1937 and his M.A. in 1939, he began his career working as a librarian at his alma mater, the University of Chicago, by Oct
2 CONC ober 1940. (In July 1940, he was living at 5465 Ellis Avenue in Chicago and was a witness for the naturalization of [[6000000218423998826]].) He was still working as a librarian in September 1941 when he was enlisted as a private 
2 CONC ("Branch Immaterial - Warrant Officers") in the United States Army. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1945, the University of Chicago's alumni magazine [https://books.google.com/books?id=gIKCIUFPZtcC&q=%22john+c.+prevost%22 said] he was "out of service and has joined the faculty of Wayne University in Detroit, Michigan, as an i
2 CONC nstructor in French". On the 1950 U.S. Federal Census, he was back in Chicago and his employer was listed as the University of Chicago.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1952, Provost published ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=jgQ5PAAACAAJ Le Dandysme en France (1817-1839)]'', his 436-page Ph.D. dissertation through the University of Chicago Department of Romance Languages; his conce
2 CONC ntration was French. In 1957, an edited version was published in Paris (by Minard) and Geneva (by Droz). ''Le Dandysme'' received a positive review in the December 1957 issue of ''The French Review'', with Richard Switzer (Northwe
2 CONC stern University) writing that "Mr. Prevost has made a solid contribution to the study of one fact of nineteenth-century French manners and their literary reflections", and that "[t]he work is very well documented from contemporar
2 CONC y sources." The book was [https://books.google.com/books?id=s4GSckZQDmQC reprinted in 1982] and remains commonly cited by other authors writing on the topic.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1955, he was [https://books.google.com/books?id=GKHOAAAAMAAJ&q=%22john+c.+prevost%22 teaching] at the University of Detroit. In 1958, he was an expert guest on a WJBK radio panel about U.S.-Africa foreign policy, representing t
2 CONC he UD French department.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 13 August 1959 in Cook County, Illinois, John married Genevieve Christiane Kunz, a Swiss immigrant. She would later predecease him, in 1990.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1960 to 1975, Dr. Prevost was a professor of foreign languages at Wayne State University. While there, he deepened his involvement with the American Association of Teachers of French, including [https://books.google.com/books
2 CONC ?id=6sw4AAAAIAAJ&q=%22john+c.+prevost%22 serving] on their executive council until 1971. He also published in their journal, ''The French Review'', including a 1965 pedagogical [https://www.jstor.org/stable/384817 article] titled 
2 CONC ''Teaching French Civilization: An Approach''.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1975, he returned to the University of Detroit, staying there until his retirement in 1985.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 24 April 1963, Dr. Prevost was awarded the Ordre des Palmes académiques (described in the ''Detroit Free Press'' as "Les palms academiques") by the government of France for "contributing service to France and the French languag
2 CONC e". His award was presented by French consul [https://biographie.whoswho.fr/decede/biographie-alain-chaillous_9229 Alain Chaillous].
2 CONT
2 CONT In his non-academic life, Dr. Prevost was a devout Catholic and "a member and daily communicant of the Shrine of the Little Flower in Royal Oak", per a ''Detroit Free Press'' news item about his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
