1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Louis Marius Prevost''', the younger son of John (Riggitano) Prevost and Suzanne Fontaine, was born 28 July 1920 in Chicago, Illinois.  He died on 08 November 1997 at his home in suburban [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homewood
2 CONC ,_Illinois Homewood], Cook County, Illinois of colon cancer, and was buried the Assumption Cemetery in Glenwood.  He was an educator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Prevost was the father of Robert Francis Prevost, elected the Catholic Pope on 8 May 2025, and who took the name [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Leo_XIV Pope Leo XIV].
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis’ father, John R. Prevost (1876-1860), was born as Salvatore Giovanni Gaetano Riggitano in Milazzo, Sicily, and emigrated to the United States in 1903, where he became a teacher of Romance languages with his own business.  Hi
2 CONC s mother, Suzanne Fontaine (1894-1979), emigrated from Le Havre, France in 1915.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis grew up at 5465 S. Ellis Ave. in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyde_Park,_Chicago Hyde Park], an integrated neighborhood on the city's South Side. He attended Hyde Park High School and then graduated from Woodrow Wilson Jun
2 CONC ior College in June 1940 and Central YMCA College's Hyde Park campus in 1943.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis served in the United States Navy Reserves during World War II. He enlisted in the Inactive Voluntary Navy Reserves April 18, 1942 as a Apprentice Seaman under the [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Naval_Reserve_Mi
2 CONC dshipmen%27s_School V-7 officer training program] which allowed him to finish up collage before fully enlisting in the military. On August 2, 1943, he was transferred to active service and shipped off to [https://en.wikipedia.org/
2 CONC wiki/United_States_Naval_Reserve_Midshipmen%27s_School Midshipmen's School] at Columbia University, New York, earning his officer commission to Ensign in the Active Navy Reserves upon graduated November 24, 1943. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after graduation, he was assigned to the USS LST-286, a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landing_Ship,_Tank#LST_Mk.2 LST-1 Class Tank Landing Ship] out of New Orleans. Aboard the LST he took part in the [https://en.wikipedia.or
2 CONC g/wiki/Normandy_landings Normandy landings], codename Operation Neptune. LST-286 which Louis was assigned, was part of Assault Group O3 which landed troops and vehicles at [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omaha_Beach Omaha Beach] on
2 CONC  D-Day. He later participated in [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Dragoon Operation Dragoon], first picking up troops and equipment in Bagnoli, Italy August 8, 1944 and then landing them on the beaches of Sothern France. F
2 CONC ollowing the conclusion of amphibious operations in the Europe-Africa-Middle East Theater USS LST-286 was assigned to the Asiatic-Pacific Theater. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 27, 1944, Louis was transferred to the USS LCI(L)-188 a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landing_Craft_Infantry landing craft infantry (large)] ship. During this time he performed duties as - deck division ship maintenanc
2 CONC e, procuring and distributing supplies, ships armor and fire control  maintenance, and executive officer. in charge of personnel records, morale, welfare of the crew. On March 1, 1945 he was promoted to Lieutenant, Junior Grade an
2 CONC d assumed the role as Commanding Officer in charge of the ship. He maintained this role thought the end of WWII returning the ship safely back to the US to participate in Navy Day in Memphis TN on 27-28 November 1945. He remained 
2 CONC as Commanding Officer of the ship until April 8, 1946 when he was transferred to the USS LC(FF)-995, a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landing_Craft_Infantry#LC(FF)_%E2%80%93_flotilla_flagship landing craft flotilla flagship] as Co
2 CONC mmanding Officer until he was transferred back to Inactive Navy Reserve status on July 28, 1946. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After his active duty in WWII, Louis attended [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DePaul_University DePaul University] in Chicago, receiving a bachelor of arts degree in education 1947, From June 14, 1948 to August 7, 1948 as part of t
2 CONC he an annual reserve cruise, Louis was assigned to the USS [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Palau Palau (CVE-122)], a [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commencement_Bay-class_escort_carrier Commencement Bay-class escort carrier] wh
2 CONC ich main mission was to deliver aircraft to  Yeşilköy, Turkey. Louis' role was to demonstrated 16mm motion pics to school children as training and publicity. The ship also assisted in the evacuation of United Nations delegation an
2 CONC d officials from Haifa on July 8th during the second phase of the Arab-Israeli War. The ship transited to the Isle of Rhodes and remained there until July 24th. The USS Palau returned to Haifa after a truce in the war was negotiat
2 CONC ed, and then returned to Norfolk, Virginia, on August 7th. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 1, 1949, Louis joined the Navy Reserves and recieved a promotion to Lieutenant. He again attended DePaul University in Chicago, receiving a master of arts degree in education with a minor in political science in 1949. L
2 CONC ouis remained in the Navy Reserves with the intent of military retirement, but because of his inactivity due to his civilian education career, he was forced to take a honorable discharge on December 31, 1956. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis Prevost married on 25 January 1949 in Cook County to [https://www.geni.com/people/Mildred-Prevost/6000000218365739839?through=6000000218365400850 Mildred Martinez] (1911-1990).  Also a Chicago native, she was the daughter of
2 CONC  the New Orleans-born Joseph Martinez and Louise Baquié.  They were in the same graduate education program at DePaul University.  She worked as a librarian before their marriage, and volunteered in school libraries while working a
2 CONC s a housewife and mother.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children together:
2 CONT # Louis Martin Prevost
2 CONT # John Joseph Prevost
2 CONT # [https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Leo-XIV/6000000218365400850?through=6000000218365739839 Robert Francis Prevost], born 14 September 1955 at Mercy Hospital in Chicago, an Augustinian priest who became Pope Leo XIV on 8 May 2025;
2 CONC  the first Pope born in the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis and Mildred Prevost raised their children in their own [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Leo_XIV#/media/File:Pope_Leo_XIV_childhood_home.jpg home] in Dolton, Illinois, a suburb bordering the far South Side of Chicago, and 
2 CONC  were members of the parish of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Mary_of_the_Assumption_Church_(Chicago)#/media/File:Saint_Mary_of_the_Assumption_Church_and_School_Riverdale,_Illinois_Circa_1920_01.jpg St. Mary of the Assumptio
2 CONC n] in nearby Riverdale, where their sons attended school.
2 CONT
2 CONT He later became superintendent of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brookwood_School_District_167 Brookwood School District 167] in Glenwood, Illinois. He was also a catechist.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' Some sources say his middle name was "Manius". Primary sources from his adulthood in which he gave his own name, as well as profiles of his son Robert in which Robert was interviewed, say "Marius".
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Note:''' In 1950, newly married https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-Prevost/6000000218365690853 and  https://www.geni.com/people/Mildred-Prevost/6000000218365739839 were living at 212 East 141st Place, Dolton, Illinois. Their cen
2 CONC sus record shows a foster son, Raymond Fuller, aged 15.  He was probably this https://www.geni.com/people/Raymond-Fuller-Sr/6000000218648273840, but confirmation and further details are sought.  See [https://www.geni.com/discussio
2 CONC ns/298917?msg=1769392 discussion].
2 CONT
