2 CONT
2 CONT A 1900 U.S. census record revealed that the family was listed as “black.” At the time, Joseph Maritinez was working as a cigar maker.  They owned the property and lived at 1933 North Prieur Street, a site now taken by the Claiborn
2 CONC e overpass. The house was located in New Orlean’s 7th Ward, which housed a large Creole community.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000218517378857&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT >Image: 1900 US census record / MyHeritage
2 CONT
2 CONT >< [https://www.google.com/maps/place/1933+N+Prieur+St,+New+Orleans,+LA+70116/@29.9763358,-90.0627196,16z/data=!3m1!4b1!4m5!3m4!1s0x8620a8a7a823382f:0xf1be0138b02cbd15!8m2!3d29.9763358!4d-90.0627196?hl=en-us&entry=ttu&g_ep=EgoyMDI
2 CONC 1MDUwNi4wIKXMDSoASAFQAw%3D%3D google map of current day 1933 N Prieur Street] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family moved to Chicago, Illinois, where Pope Leo XIV’s mother, Mildred, was born on December 30, 1911. Later census records identified the family’s race as “white” as seen in the 1920 U.S. census record with the family still 
2 CONC living in Chicago. The record shows his mother’s siblings (Irma, Marguerite, Lydia, Louise, and Hilda) were born in Louisiana and she in Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000218517484821&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT > Image: 1920 U.S. census record / MyHeritage
