2 CONC d to a historic enclave of Afro-Caribbean culture, links Leo XIV to the rich and sometimes overlooked Black Catholic experience in America. …” “Both Joseph Norval Martinez and Louise Baquié were people of color, no doubt about it,
2 CONC ” Mr. Honora said.”
2 CONT *“Family of Pope Leo XIV” ''Wikipedia'' [https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Family_of_Pope_Leo_XIV&oldid=1290731780 revision] of 16 May 2025;  < [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Family_of_Pope_Leo_XIV link] > Accessed 16 May
2 CONC  2025. [https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Leo-XIV/6000000218365400850 Pope Leo XIV]’s maternal grandparents were the Hispaniola-born mixed-race Joseph Martínez, and the New Orleans-born Louise Baquié (also rendered Baquiex), a mixe
2 CONC d-race Black Creole.[18][19][20] Joseph was born to Jacques and Marie Martinez in Santo Domingo, Dominican Republic (at the time occupied by Haiti). Jacques and Marie were registered on the 1910 United States census as Maltese and
2 CONC  Spanish respectively, however they may have done so to pass as white.[21] Louise was born to Ferdinand Baquié and Eugenie Grambois in New Orleans.[22] Joseph and Louise married on September 17, 1887, at Our Lady of the Sacred Hea
2 CONC rt in New Orleans.[19][22] In census documents, Joseph and Louise are variably described as Black or mulatto (mixed-race, of African and European heritage).[23] In the 1900 United States census, when they lived in New Orleans, the
2 CONC y are listed as Black. However, in the 1920 United States census after they had moved to Chicago, they are listed as white.[24] Genealogist Chris Smothers speculated that such a change may have been a "survival strategy" if they p
2 CONC assed as white.[23]
