1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT Not the son of https://www.geni.com/people/Nicol%C3%B2-Martino/6000000218411705833?through=6000000218369181822 & https://www.geni.com/people/Catherina-Mirasco/6000000218411917821?through=6000000218369181822 (disproved)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Likely the son  of https://www.geni.com/people/Francoise-Panquinette/6000000218794430855 & https://www.geni.com/people/Jacques-Martin/6000000218794355871?through=6000000218794430855
2 CONT
2 CONT Maternal-paternal great-grandparents of https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Leo-XIV/6000000218365400850: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Martínez, who was born in New Orleans on February 15, 1823, and died there on February 23, 1891, was a tailor. His death certificate indicates that his parents were originally from Cuba. He married Mrs. María Rosa Ramos on
2 CONC  July 14, 1848, who was born in New Orleans on September 24, 1826, and died in that city on May 13, 1904. They were classified as mulatos in the 1850 and 1870 Census.
2 CONT
2 CONT Great-grandparents: '''Jacques Martínez''', who was born around 1790, married? [no license found] to Madame Marguerite Cadnet, who was born in New Orleans around 1810 and passed away in New Orleans on April 20, 1879.  Her death wa
2 CONC s reported by their son, https://www.geni.com/people/Jacques-Martinez/6000000218368560821?through=6000000218369181822 (1822-1891).
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT “Pope Leo’s Maternal Roots”
2 CONT Posted by Sean Daly on May 22, 2025
2 CONT https://en.geneanet.org/genealogyblog/post/2025/05/pope-leos-maternal-roots
2 CONT
2 CONT Contrary to what his name might suggest, it is not certain that the MARTINEZ family is of Spanish origin. Indeed, a mystery surrounds Jacques MARTINEZ (1823-1891), husband of Maria Rosa RAMOS (1826-1904). His death certificate men
2 CONC tions parents from Cuba, but it is likely this refers to his wife’s family because his mother, Marguerite CADNET or CADENETTE, came from a family from New Orleans. His father, also called Jacques MARTINEZ, was at one point thought
2 CONC  to be an Italian named Jacques MARTINO (~1808-1852), but Antonio A. Herrera-Vaillant, honorary president of the Venezuelan Academy of Genealogy, has shown that the latter married and had other children [PDF]. It is more likely th
2 CONC at Jacques MARTINEZ senior was himself born in New Orleans, to Françoise Rosalie PANQUINETTE and a father whose original name was… Jacob MARTINE. Having become Santiago MARTIN and then MARTINEZ, his name was Hispanicized, like tho
2 CONC se of many people during the period of Spanish domination of Louisiana (1763-1803).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the New Orleans archives, Jacob MARTINE was born in Prague (the Bohemian region in today’s Czech Republic). The history of the PANQUINET / PANQUINETTE family is well documented: it is a family of Roma descent, some me
2 CONC mbers of which were among the 13 Roma families deported from Dunkirk (France) in 1720 on the ship Le Tilleul. Discriminated against in France, those who were called “Bohemians” in certain registers were gradually assimilated into 
2 CONC other categories used at the time in the European colonies of the New World: white/black/mixed-race, free/enslaved.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Some researchers confuse this person with a sailor named Jacques Martino, who was born in San Remo, Genoa, around 1808, arrived in Louisiana as a minor in 1821, became an American citizen on October 23, 1834; and died in New Orl
2 CONC eans on July 9, 1852, married to Josephine Paturzo, who was born in Louisiana around July 1823 and died in New Orleans on May 9, 1908. They had three children: Nicole, Joseph, and Jacques Martino, who were minors in 1852, with pro
2 CONC perties in Plaquemines Parish according to his succession file. Chronologically, this affiliation is not possible as the dates do not match reality.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From “APPENDICES: Acadians Who Found Refuge in Louisiana, February 1764-early 1800s.”  < [http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-MARTIN.htm#MARTIN link] >
2 CONT
2 CONT >Jacques, son of '''François Martin and Catherine Vincent of Prague''', then in Germany, married Françoise-Rosalie, called Rosalie, daughter of Pierre-Dionysius Panquinet, "beadle/verger of this parish," at New Orleans in Septembe
2 CONC r 1781.  Their '''son Jacques III was born at New Orleans in June 1789''', Edmond-Célestin in October 1793, Raphaël-Élie in December 1795, Joachim in February 1798, and Louis-Joseph in September 1800.  Their daughter married into 
2 CONC the Abadie family at Baton Rouge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Older notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT We can trace the surname Martínez only back to a previous generation. It is lost to the father of the tailor James Jacques, the Pope's great-great-grandfather, therefore also named Jacques Martínez or Jacques Martino , according t
2 CONC o some genealogical records. This note is not insignificant, since these other sources raise the possibility that Jacques Martino was Italian, emigrated to Cuba, and once there, Spanish-ized his surname, changing it to Martínez.
2 CONT
2 CONT That Jacques Martínez/Martino spent at least part of his life in Cuba - he married Marguerite Cadeneth - is supported by the death certificate of his son, the tailor, which states that his parents were born in Cuba.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacques Sr. lived between 1806 and 1852, and since Spain didn't lose Cuba until 1898, he could have been Spanish even if he was born in Italy, specifically in San Remo. His place of death is listed as New Orleans , which, if his t
2 CONC ransalpine origin is to be believed, would mean that he emigrated from Italy to Cuba and from there to the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
