1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ---- 
2 CONT Seen as the mother of https://www.geni.com/people/Jacques-Martinez/6000000218369181822
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT When Francoise Rosalie Panquinette was born on 25 October 1763, in New Orleans, Louisiana, New France, her father, Pierre Denis dit Panquinette, was 45 and her mother, Marie Louise Castel, was 25. She married Jacques Martin on 5 S
2 CONC eptember 1785, in New Orleans, Louisiana, New France. They were the parents of at least 4 sons and 2 daughters. She died after 8 December 1812.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jacques Martinez''', son of François Martin and Catherine Vincent of Prague,  then in Germany, married Françoise-Rosalie, called Rosalie, daughter of Pierre-Dionysius Panquinet, "beadle/verger of this parish," at New Orleans on
2 CONC  5 September 1781.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Jacques Martinez and Rosalie Panquinet, born in New Orleans, included:
2 CONT
2 CONT # daughter, buried as a “Panquinet” in February 1786
2 CONT #Jacques was born at New Orleans in June 1789
2 CONT #Edmond-Célestin in October 1793
2 CONT # Raphaël-Élie in December 1795
2 CONT # Joachim in February 1798
2 CONT #Louis-Joseph in September 1800
2 CONT #daughter married into the Abadie family at Baton Rouge.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT “Pope Leo’s Maternal Roots”
2 CONT Posted by Sean Daly on May 22, 2025
2 CONT https://en.geneanet.org/genealogyblog/post/2025/05/pope-leos-maternal-roots
2 CONT
2 CONT Contrary to what his name might suggest, it is not certain that the MARTINEZ family is of Spanish origin. Indeed, a mystery surrounds Jacques MARTINEZ (1823-1891), husband of Maria Rosa RAMOS (1826-1904). His death certificate men
2 CONC tions parents from Cuba, but it is likely this refers to his wife’s family because his mother, Marguerite CADNET or CADENETTE, came from a family from New Orleans. His father, also called Jacques MARTINEZ, was at one point thought
2 CONC  to be an Italian named Jacques MARTINO (~1808-1852), but Antonio A. Herrera-Vaillant, honorary president of the Venezuelan Academy of Genealogy, has shown that the latter married and had other children [PDF]. It is more likely th
2 CONC at Jacques MARTINEZ senior was himself born in New Orleans, to Françoise Rosalie PANQUINETTE and a father whose original name was… Jacob MARTINE. Having become Santiago MARTIN and then MARTINEZ, his name was Hispanicized, like tho
2 CONC se of many people during the period of Spanish domination of Louisiana (1763-1803).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the New Orleans archives, Jacob MARTINE was born in Prague (the Bohemian region in today’s Czech Republic). The history of the PANQUINET / PANQUINETTE family is well documented: it is a family of Roma descent, some me
2 CONC mbers of which were among the 13 Roma families deported from Dunkirk (France) in 1720 on the ship Le Tilleul. Discriminated against in France, those who were called “Bohemians” in certain registers were gradually assimilated into 
2 CONC other categories used at the time in the European colonies of the New World: white/black/mixed-race, free/enslaved.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Showns, Elizabeth Mills. “Assimilation? Or Marginalization and Discrimination? Romani Settlers of the Colonial Gulf (Christophe Clan).” (Updated 6 June 2015) Posted at E. S. Mills, ''Historic Pathways'' (www.historicpathways.
2 CONC com)  < [https://www.historicpathways.com/download/romanisettlers.pdf PDF] > page 49-50
2 CONT
2 CONT The ten known children of Louise Castel and Pierre Denis dit Panquinette were as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 29. V.  FRANÇOISE ROSALIE PANQUINETTE, born 25 October 1763 and baptized 29 October 1763, at which time her godparents were the ship captain Francisco Xavier de Acosta and Marie Joseph Gauvain, about whom nothing more is known.191
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT At twelve (on 13 December 1775), she served as godmother to a child of the Rom Marguerite Casberg and her husband Michel De Ville, who had already joined the  Rapides clan.192 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 5 September 1785, Rosalie married SANTIAGO MARTINEZ (originally, JACOB MARTINE), said then to be a native of “Perne in Germany.” Their official witness was Jean Baptiste Saussi  of the family discussed at notes 41–42. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosalie’s first child, a daughter, was buried as a “Panquinet” in February 1786, five months after the marriage.19
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1791 census places the family next door to Rosalie’s parents on Bourbon Street and identifies “Santiago Martinez” as a tailor. The 1795 chimney tax list of New Orleans again places “Mr. Martine” next door to his mother-in-law 
2 CONC “Mme. Widow Panquinet” at nos. 75 and 76 of Block 6 in the Third Quarter.194 
2 CONT
2 CONT Martine also served with the Louisiana Infantry for 9 years and 4 months and with the New Orleans militia for 15 years and 8 months. He was elevated to corporal first-class on 1 May 1787, militia sergeant second-class on 1 May 179
2 CONC 1, and militia sergeant first-class on 1 June 1796. His service sheet credits him with participation in Gàlvez’s American Revolutionary campaigns of Baton Rouge (1779), Mobile (1780), and Pensacola (1781). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Personally, he was said to have “accredited valor; average application & capacity [and] good conduct.”195
2 CONT
2 CONT Notarial records also reveal that, as “Santiago Martinez,” he bought a newly imported female slave off the ship Amable Victoria in 1792, but sold her two months later, making only 5 piasters off his 245 piaster investment. As “Jac
2 CONC ques Martinez” he made another turn.
2 CONT
2 CONT around purchase in May and June 1809, acquiring an enslaved male for his kinsman Nicolas De Ville of Rapides; and on 18 January,1812 he sold together a black mother and her daughter, whose, purchase by him has not been discovered.
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT At his burial on 8 December 1812, he was called a native of Prague in Bohemia and a resident of New Orleans, aged sixty. Rosalie survived him.196 
2 CONT
2 CONT Neither bohème nor gitano has been found attached to either of their names.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT #Showns, Elizabeth Mills. “Assimilation? Or Marginalization and Discrimination? Romani Settlers of the Colonial Gulf (Christophe Clan).” (Updated 6 June 2015) Posted at E. S. Mills, Historic Pathways (www.historicpathways.com)  < 
2 CONC [https://www.historicpathways.com/download/romanisettlers.pdf PDF] > page 49-50
2 CONT ##191. Ibid. For De Acosta’s occupation, see ibid., 2:47 (Cazellar).
2 CONT ## 192. Ibid., 3:99 (De Vil). The Casberg–De Ville couple appear on the 1773 census next to Widow Varangue; they had, however, shed their “Gypsy” label. Unlike the widow and the La  Prairies, the De Villes were not called bohèmes 
2 CONC in known records of Rapides. This couple are the forebears of the well-known Louisiana genealogist Winston De Ville, FASG.
2 CONT ## 193. Archdiocese of New Orleans, 3:202–03 (Martin, Marten, Martinez). See also the two prior discussions of the family’s Saussier connections. For the burial of the first Martinez  Panquinette daughter, see Ibid., 4:236 (Panqui
2 CONC net). Regarding Jacob Martine’s varied fore names, Spanish scribes of this society routinely translated French, German, and English names into their Spanish equivalents.
2 CONT ##194. 1791 Census of New Orleans, p. 18. Winston De Ville, The 1795 Chimney-Tax of New  Orleans: A Guide to the Census of Proprietors and Residents of the Vieux Carré (Ville  Platte, La.: Smith Publications, 1994), 20.
2 CONT ##195. For translated abstracts of Spanish colonial service records, see Jack D. L. Holmes, Honor and Fidelity: The Louisiana Infantry Regiment and the Louisiana Militia Companies, 1766–1821 (Birmingham: Louisiana Collection Serie
2 CONC s, 1975), 206.
