1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Showns, Elizabeth Mills. “Assimilation? Or Marginalization and Discrimination? Romani Settlers of the Colonial Gulf (Christophe Clan).” (Updated 6 June 2015) Posted at E. S. Mills, ''Historic Pathways'' (www.historicpathways.
2 CONC com)  < [https://www.historicpathways.com/download/romanisettlers.pdf PDF] > page 43
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise3 “Louison” Castel (Marguerite Cécile Christophe2, Marie Agnes Simon dite de L’espine1 and Jean Christophe1) was born about 1739, probably in the Choctaw nation where her parents traded pelts. About 1755, likely at New Orlea
2 CONC ns where her mother was then living, she married '''Pierre Denis [La Gandelle?] dit Panquinette''' (var. '''Panguinet, Pantinet''', etc.), who is said to have been born about 1719 at Biloxi. He was buried at New Orleans, as sexton
2 CONC  of the parish, on 4 May 1793, after dying “the preceding night, aged 74.” 171 Louise died in New Orleans at five a.m. on the morning of 9 July 1828, at the stated age of ninety-eight, and was buried the following day.172
2 CONT
2 CONT Louise’s husband shared her Romani roots. Like others, he is often identified with no surname in his earliest records. Thereafter, even when the name “Panquinette” or its phonetic equivalents appear, it is frequently labeled a dit
2 CONC . No known record states an identity for his parents. How- ever, the surname is highly uncommon; only one person carries that name in colonial records from the preceding generation: the “Widow Pantinet, boëmienne” lived with one c
2 CONC hild on Royal Street in New Or- leans at the time of the 1732 census. A second (undated) list of that era, titled “Those persons who own land in the city of New Orleans,” identi- fies her as a homeowner. As “La Paline” [La Patine—
2 CONC that is, the woman called Patine], that document assigns her the same neighbors as “Widow Pantinet” of the population return.173 …
2 CONT
2 CONT The ten known children of Louise Castel and Pierre Denis dit Panquinette were as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * 25 i. MARIE LOUISE4 PANQUINETTE, born 24 September 1756 and baptized 28 September 1756 
2 CONT * 26 ii MARIE ANNE PANQUINETTE, born and baptized 25 April 1758 at New Orleans
2 CONT * 27 iii. PIERRE LOUIS PANQUINETTE, born 9 May 1760, privately baptized shortly after birth, when death seemed imminent
2 CONT * 28 iv. JEAN PIERRE PANQUINETTE, born 5 November 1762 and baptized two days late
2 CONT * 29 v. FRANÇOISE ROSALIE PANQUINETTE, born 25 October 1763 and bap- tized 29 October 1763
2 CONT * 30 vi. JEAN PIERRE PANQUINETTE, born 29 August 1766 and baptized 31 Au gust
2 CONT * 31 vii ANASTASIE PANQUINETTE, born 9 January 1769 and baptized 21 Janu ary 1769
2 CONT * 32 vi. JEAN CHARLES PANQUINETTE, born 15 June 1771 and baptized 17 June 1771
2 CONT * 33 viii.MARIE CLAIRE PANQUINETTE, born 5 November 1773 and baptized 7 November, with her godparents being Marie Thérèse Dupree and Pierre De Flandres 
2 CONT * 34 ix.  CHARLOTTE SOPHIE PANQUINETTE, born 5 April 1776 and baptized 27 May 1776
2 CONT * 35 x. HENRI PANQUINETTE, whose baptismal record has not been found. He had two wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT … This adjacent lot perhaps had been purchased for service as a shoemaker’s shop, considering that the 1791 census of Bourbon Street attributes that occupation to both Pierre and his eldest son. However, the house was occupied in 
2 CONC that year by Pantinette’s daughter and son-in- law, the tailor Jacques Martinez.182
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Spanish regime, the family’s social stature rose somewhat, as Pierre became a fixture in St. Louis Parish. Church records call him the parish beadle (1773–76) and sacristan or sexton (at his death in 1793). In the last deca
2 CONC de of his life he witnessed a number of marriages—appar- ently in a functionary position, considering that in some cases he acted for those in the noble class. Although Pierre left Louise a home, she was not an idle widow. The 179
2 CONC 5 census of Block 6 of the Third Quarter enu- merates the dwelling of “Widow Panquinet, dressmaker,” with nine white inhabitants, next door to the tailor “Mr. Martine” (her son-in-law) and his family of five whites and four slaves
2 CONC . Pierre’s post as sacristan was assumed by his bachelor son, Charles. It does not appear, however, that other offspring shared their closeness to the church. When, at the close of the century, bishops arrived in the colony to con
2 CONC duct confirmations, only two of the many Panquinette progeny sought that sacrament for them- selves or their children.183
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
