2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT From Showns, Elizabeth Mills. “Assimilation? Or Marginalization and Discrimination? Romani Settlers of the Colonial Gulf (Christophe Clan).” (Updated 6 June 2015) Posted at E. S. Mills, ''Historic Pathways'' (www.historicpathways.
2 CONC com)  < [https://www.historicpathways.com/download/romanisettlers.pdf PDF] > page 45
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT >When Pierre Denis Panquinette presented his own first child for baptism in 1756, the godfather was “Nicolas ''Jean Louis'', the infant’s paternal uncle.”177 <BR>
2 CONT
2 CONT >That identification of a paternal uncle seems to provide the link needed to place “Widow Pantinet, ''boëmienne''” with one child in 1732 on the ''Le Tilleul'' roll. Clustered with Marie Agnes Simon, among the twelve married depor
2 CONC ted in May 1720 (see Fig. 1) was “''Marie Jeanne'' Lestat, wife of Jean Louis, native of Beble in Cologne, of the age of 24 years.” She was at that time the mother of one child, a two-year-old called “''Pierre'' La Gandelle.”178 I
2 CONC dentifying the Pierre of 1720, son of Marie Jeanne (Mme. Jean Louis), as Pierre Denis, whose child had a paternal uncle named Nicolas ''Jean Louis'' could also provide a resolution to another identity issue—that of the otherwise u
2 CONC nplaced “Marie Jeanne” who served with Pierre Denis as a godmother to a La Fontaine child in 1745.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT #From Showns, Elizabeth Mills. “Assimilation? Or Marginalization and Discrimination? Romani Settlers of the Colonial Gulf (Christophe Clan).” (Updated 6 June 2015) Posted at E. S. Mills, ''Historic Pathways'' (www.historicpathways
2 CONC .com)  < [https://www.historicpathways.com/download/romanisettlers.pdf PDF] > page 43 - 45
2 CONT ## 176. The identity of this Panquinette godfather is also muddled by conflicting published records and the diocesan policy of not allowing a reexamination of the original register. Archdiocese of New Orleans, 2:121 (Fayard-Fissea
2 CONC u), renders the name as Louis Panquinette, fils (i.e., the son). However, Brother Jerome Lepré, a descendant of the La Garenne–Fisseau– Fayard family who has the reputation of being a careful researcher, has published his own acco
2 CONC unt of the baptism in which he renders the godfather’s name as Louis Pierre Panquinette. See Jerome Lepré, “Fayard Family,” New Orleans Genesis 22 (July 1983): 337.
2 CONT ## 177. Archdiocese of New Orleans, 2:220 (Pantinet).
2 CONT ## 178. Some interpretations of the evidence might challenge this identification. If Pierre, son of Marie Jeanne, was also the son of Marie Jeanne’s husband Jean Louis, then one would expect the child’s surname to be Louis or Jean
2 CONC  Louis. It is possible that Pierre “La Gandelle” was born to Marie Jeanne prior to her marriage to Jean Louis—or equally possible that “La Gandelle” was a dit of the Jean Louis family. As a dit for young Pierre, the name could rep
2 CONC resent “the little one from the Gand.” At least one other Rom on Le Tilleul was from the Gand, a commune in old Franche-Comte.
2 CONT # Ostendorf, Ann. "Louisiana Bohemians: Community, Race, and Empire." Early American Studies: An Interdisciplinary Journal, vol. 19 no. 4, 2021, p. 659-698. Project MUSE, https://doi.org/10.1353/eam.2021.0021. “On May 21st, 1720, 
2 CONC a clerk at the port of Dunkerque recorded thirteen “Bohémienne,” along with their husbands and children, on the rolls of ''Le Tilleul'', a vessel leaving for the small but growing French colony of Louisiana. These families, most o
2 CONC f whom hailed from diverse places throughout what is today northeastern France and Belgium, had been gathered together as part of a national purge that exiled unwanted French subjects to its various Atlantic outposts. Their demarc
2 CONC ation on the passenger list under the heading gens de force, or exiled convict laborers, reveals the intention that these women and their families would supplement the meager labor force of the colony.  …
2 CONT # Descendants  of  https://www.geni.com/people/Marie-Jeanne-de-Lestat/6000000218809823840 include writers https://www.geni.com/people/Anatole-Broyard/6000000013062363391?through=6000000009777455995  (5th great grandson) and his da
2 CONC ughter https://www.geni.com/people/Bliss-Broyard/6000000009777455995 (6th great granddaughter). ([https://www.geni.com/path/Bliss-Broyard+is+related+to+Marie-Jeanne-de-Lestat?from=6000000009777455995&path_type=blood&to=60000002188
2 CONC 09823840 relationship path]). 
2 CONT # https://www.thirteen.org/wnet/aalives/profiles/broyard.html [https://www.blissbroyard.com/ Bliss Broyard] is the author of ''My Father, Dancing'' (1999), a New York Times notable book of the year, and ''One Drop: My Father's Hid
2 CONC den Life -- A Story of Race & Family Secrets'' (2007). 
