1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT The following info is from "The Ladner Odyssey" by Nap Cassibry II: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was the daughter of Pierre LaFontaine and Juana Maria Marcassin. She married Pierre Louis Panquinette (Panguiet) and they were married for nine years when he died in 1795. They had one daughter, Marguerite. She married Claude
2 CONC  Christian Ladner on May 6 1801. Her daughter, Marguerite, would eventually marry Claude's nephew, Joseph Moran, Jr. Anne appears to have died sometime between 1833 and 1843.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since her daughter and only child was the widow of Joseph Moran, Jr. and the mother of Joseph Moran III on whose land the Moran Cemetery is situated I am placing her there. Another reason is because Claude made a deposition in 184
2 CONC 8 stating that he was living at Pierre Harvey's house which is about 1/4 mile from the Moran Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne's date of death is a wild estimate. 
2 CONT (findagrave.com)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT #Ostendorf, Ann. "Louisiana Bohemians: Community, Race, and Empire." Early American Studies: An Interdisciplinary Journal, vol. 19 no. 4, 2021, p. 659-698. Project MUSE, https://doi.org/10.1353/eam.2021.0021. Page 22 of 41 (PDF), 
2 CONC book page number 679. “…  A decade before, Marie’s older sister, Louise Castel, had wed the Bohemian deportee Pierre Lorpandelle Pantinet, who became a dance instructor and parish verger. One of their sons, Pierre Louis Pantinet, 
2 CONC married Jean Frederic La Fontaine’s granddaughter Ana Maria in 1786. By the early 1770s, a La Fontaine daughter, Marie Angelique, had married Jean Louis Casberg, the grandson of the Bohemian Jean Louis Evrard. Another of Jean Fred
2 CONC eric La Fontaine’s sons, Valentine, married the granddaughter of the Le Tilleul Bohemian Marie La Garenne in 1778.27 …” 
