2 DATE 23 AUG 1901
2 PLAC St. Louis Cemetery #1, New Orleans, Orleans, Louisiana, United States
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Obituary
2 CONT
2 CONT LIFE OF MRS. JACOB ABE OTTO - ROYAL BLOOD AND THE HIGHER ATTRIBUTES OF SPLENDID PERSONALITY
2 CONT
2 CONT "In New Orleans Her Home Was the Gathering Place for Aristocrats in Days Agone -Final Interment To Be Made in This City.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are two kinds of high society. One is founded on wealth and develops character according to the influences, good or evil, that wealth and its opportunities bring to bear upon the individual, the social evolution gradually el
2 CONC evating those who are fittest high above those who are not. One rests upon the broad and enduring foundation of proved character and merit, and uses wealth. In one wealth is an essential component part. To the other wealth is but 
2 CONC an adjunct, although so useful as to be practically essential. In the atmosphere of one the mental vision is affected by the flash and glitter of unnecessary display. The atmosphere of the other is purer, softer, evener In one wea
2 CONC lth is like the servant who enters into familiar conversation at the table. In the other wealth is a well-trained servant, whose presence and service are evident not because of the menial strutting or obtrusion, but by the seeming
2 CONC  absence of necessity for further attendance or additional service. There are two kinds of high society. One is only "society." The other is aristocracy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Jacob Otto, born '''Isabelle Baquié''', belonged to the latter. Her place in it was hers by right of birth. She held it by the supplemental higher right of her personal character. She filled her place as the family traditions
2 CONC  required, the aristocracy of the monarchy which had fled, remaining aristocracy in the democracy it adopted. True aristocracy is higher than and superior to political environments, adapts itself with equal facility to either mona
2 CONC rchy or republic, and is the best product of both.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter or a daughter of the Viscountess Régner du Timat, and a native of the West Indies because her parents' families fled France at the time of the Revolution, Mrs. Otto was reared from childhood to womanhood in this city. Her
2 CONC e she married Jacob Otto, one of a prominent family of Pennsylvania Quakers. Shortly after the civil war Mr. Otto removed to New York, where he died in 1876.
2 CONT
2 CONT In New Orleans, while Mr. Otto was one of the leading merchants here, and later in New York, where he was equally prominent in commercial circles, the home that his wife graced was a centre of the higher social life. Mrs. Otto did
2 CONC  not entertain ostentatiously, however. Her friend and associates were representatives of the best social elements, whether natives or strangers. She entertained informally. In those days, the days of the old South, the hospitable
2 CONC  tables of the South's gentle folk were the scenes of the most delightful social intercourse. There social prominence gave one not a few friends and a vast array of "bowing acquaintances," but large circles of friends, whose conve
2 CONC rsation, begun under favorable social conditions, led to enduring ties.
2 CONT Mr. Otto's personal character and qualifications, together with his wife's refinement, graciousness and charm of manner, made their home one of the most delightful among the circles of the elect, and after their removal to New Yor
2 CONC k the house in Grammercy Square was for Southerners in New York what their home in New Orleans had been.
2 CONT
2 CONT The charm that the Ottos' home held for their friends did not lie in any particular talent of Mrs. Otto that overshadowed defects, but in the evenly high standard of womanhood that was hers. She combined the polish and education o
2 CONC f the French aristocracy with what was best in the higher social life of the old South. Hospitable, gentle, considerate of all, charitable in her views, generous in her assistance of those who both needed and deserved assistance, 
2 CONC the contour of her character was graceful and beautiful. She was an accomplished French scholar, and equally facile in conversation in her adopted tongue. Animated, light hearted, keenly intelligent, her conversation was ever ente
2 CONC rtaining and profitable. The chief value of worldly goods in her eyes lay in the power that they afforded her, first to help those who needed help, second to make life pleasant without a surfeit of pleasure.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her circle of friends included many eminent men and women who have long preceded her to the grave, both in New Orleans and New York, and her life after her husband's death, twenty-five years ago, was very quiet and retired. In her
2 CONC  earlier days, she entertained many of the most distinguished foreigners who visited this country. The Marquis de Chambrun and his wife, a granddaughter of the Marquis de la Fayette, were during their residence in New York on the 
2 CONC most intimate terms with the Ottos, and the Duke de Broglie, grandson of Mme. de Stael, was one of their most intimate friends. Titles, however, were measured by Mrs. Otto by the individual merit of those who wore them, and among 
2 CONC those whom she honored most and loved best were men and women who bore no titles, whose superiority of character was to her an all sufficient patent of nobility.
2 CONT The secret side of Mrs. Otto's life was, however, the most beautiful. Her dinners, all her social entertainments, were without ostentation. Her charity she strove to keep secret. Half the pleasure of doing good was taken from her 
2 CONC if any thing like public approval resulted. She did not do good for vanity's sake, and wanted no reward save the secret knowledge that she had done good and that her beneficiaries justified her by their future.
2 CONT Among Mrs. Otto's relatives in New York during her fatal illness was Miss Jeanne Faure, who ministered tenderly to the old lady. Beside her constantly was her devoted maid, Suise O'Gorman, whose twenty-five years of service closed
2 CONC  yesterday, when the body of Mrs. Otto was taken charge of by relatives here, and when the faithful maid went to the home of Baroness von Meysenbug, Mrs. Otto's favorite grandniece.
2 CONT This morning at 10 o'clock the casket bearing Mrs. Otto's body will be buried from the St. Louis Cathedral in the vault where lie the bodies of Mrs. Otto's grandmother, Viscountess du Timat, of her mother, Mrs. J. M. R. Baquié, an
2 CONC d of her sister, Mrs. J. A. Baquié. The vault will then be finally sealed.
2 CONT Mrs. Otto's nearest surviving relatives are her immediate nephews and nieces and the children: George Baquié, J. P. N. Baquié, Mrs. Virginia Schoenfeld, Mrs. Quentell's children, comprising Baronness von Meysenbug, Mrs. Otto's fav
2 CONC orite grandniece, who was deeply attached to her, Mrs. Atwood Violett, Carl and William Quentell, Dr. C. C. Baquié's children, Mrs. Erickson, Mrs. Paul Faure and Mrs. Geno[?] Reichard, now living abroad."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Times-Democrat of New Orleans; 23 Aug 1901, Fri; Page 3; Column 7. Credits to Newspapers.com.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
