1 NOTE {geni:about_me} GANOSSE, Jean-Pierre (1689-1766)
2 CONT https://philidor3.cmbv.fr/ark:/13681/1hdkx5xyrvgnzebqi6j6/not-622610 Learn more about:
2 CONT Civil Status
2 CONT LAST NAME: GANOSSE First Name(s): Jean-Pierre Gender: M
2 CONT Other Form(s) of Name: DE GANOSSE
2 CONT Date(s): 1689-6-29 / 1766-2-10
2 CONT Biographical Notes
2 CONT Son of an organist active in the large Béarnese town of Monein from the end of the 17th century, and father-in-law of a second organist working in the same town in the middle of the following century, Jean-Pierre GANOSSE probably 
2 CONC served as a bridge between these two men, but his musical activity remains to be documented.
2 CONT
2 CONT • June 29, 1689, Monein [Pyrénées-Atlantiques]: Jean-Pierre Ganosse is born. He is the eldest son of Jacques Ganosse and Marie Ducos. His father's profession is not specified in his baptismal certificate; however, when his younger
2 CONC  brother was born, he was described as an organist.
2 CONT
2 CONT • June 10, 1721, Pardies [Pyrénées-Atlantiques]: Jean-Pierre Ganosse marries Jeanne Maubourguet, originally from this village near Monein.
2 CONT
2 CONT • 1722–1727, Pardies: They become the parents of four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT • 1729–1743, Monein: They welcome eight more children, who this time are baptized in the Saint-Girons church in Monein. Baptismal records present Jean-Pierre Ganosse as "noble," "bourgeois," or even "lord of Artix" but do not indi
2 CONC cate whether he held a musical position.
2 CONT
2 CONT • February 3, 1750, Monein: Jean-Pierre Ganosse gives his daughter in marriage to Jean-Baptiste Baquié.
2 CONT
2 CONT • April 7, 1751, Monein: He carries his newborn grandson to the baptismal font. He is referred to as a "jurat" and his son-in-law as an organist.
2 CONT
2 CONT • June 14, 1754, Monein: He becomes a widower.
2 CONT
2 CONT • February 10, 1766, Monein: Jean-Pierre Ganosse, "bourgeois and former first jurat," dies.
