2 CONT **Drouin Institute (Archived marriage to Marie Anne Aucoin record - see attached in Media tab)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-40001/familysearch-family-tree?itemId=476011917&action=showRecord FamilySearch Family Tree] via father [http://www.geni.com/profile-34677877635 Pierre Thibodeau] by [ht
2 CONC tp://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 11 2015, 0:43:19 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Milron P Rieder & Norma Gaudet Rieder, Acadian Church Records Port Royal 1702-1721 v III, Metairie LA, 1977, p 59 Tibeaudeau Pierre & Aucoin Marie Anne . Pierre was born between 1676-1678, according to the Port Royal cens
2 CONC us records.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Anne-Marie Aucoin on 25 November 1706, in Port-Royal, Acadie. They are the parents of at least the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Marguerite (1707-1772) md Maurice Comeau
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Paul (1708-) md Marie-Marguerite Trahan
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Marie-Rose (1710-) md Jean Blanchard
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Olivier (1712-1757) md Elisabeth Melanson
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) Elisabeth (1717-abt 1789) md Cosme dit Brasseux
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Catherine-Josephte (1721-1790) md (a) Jean-Baptiste Morvan and (b) Joseph Pitre
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) Anne-Marie (abt 1722-1788) md (1) Timothee Guenard, (2) Joseph Loiseau, and (3) Francoise-Marie Rivard
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) Ursule (1723-) md Jean Savoie
2 CONT
2 CONT 9) Pierre (1724-1790) md Francoise Saulnier
2 CONT
2 CONT He is also known as Pierre Thibodeau Lejeune (meaning the younger), as he has an older brother named Pierre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre was born, raised, and married in the Port Royal area. In 1698, his father and four of this Pierre's brothers left Port Royal to start a new settlement, along with a friend named Pierre Gaudet. They traveled by boat and even
2 CONC tually decided to settle along the Chipody River. They cleared the land for the settlement and farming, started a gristmill and sawmill, hunted furs, etc. They became friends with the local Indians and had a very good relationship
2 CONC  with them.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1700, Pierre (senior) returned to Chipdy with his wife and nephew. He spent the winters in Port Royal and the spring through fall in Chipody. Chipody is now known as Shepody.
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point, this Pierre and his wife, Anne-Marie Aucoin, joined his brothers in Chipody where according to acadiansingray.com, he died prior to 14 October 1734. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Please note that they would most likely have taken his body back across the river for burial in Port Royal, as that was the only cemetery at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.albertcountymuseum.com/pierre-thibodeau/
