1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Dictionary of Louisiana Biography < [https://www.lahistory.org/resources/dictionary-louisiana-biography/dictionary-louisiana-biography-c/ link] >
2 CONT
2 CONT >In 1760, she [Marguerite Cecile Christophe]married at Mobile a caulker named Nicolas Joseph Sarde dit Barranco (variously Barangue and Varangue), with whom she lived at New Orleans during the 1763 census. The following summer, th
2 CONC e Barranco-Castel clan and two associates (Vincent Porier and Andrés Lacasagne) moved to the unsettled lands near the Red River rapids. After the deaths of Lacasagne and Barranco (the latter between June 1765 and May 1766), Christ
2 CONC ophe and some of her offspring remained at the rapids—forming a close association with the Apalache and Pascagoula Indians who migrated there from the Mobile district. 
2 CONT
2 CONT E.S.M. (Elizabeth Shown Mills, Tuscaloosa, Alabama) 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Love's Legacy: The Mobile Marriages Recorded in French, Transcribed, with Annotated Abstracts in English, 1724-1786'' by Jacqueline Olivier Vidrine. University of Southwestern Louisiana: Lafayette, LA, 1985, p.313. Text:
2 CONT Mob. mb I:44a-2
2 CONT December 15, 1760
2 CONT fo.44
2 CONT One bann published; dispensed with two.
2 CONT Joseph Sarde dit Barrangue (Barranco),1 native of (Calini? Cadins? Cadise?), son of Pierre Barrangue and of Marie Anteque or Auteque.
2 CONT Cécile Christophe, native of New Orleans, widow of Francois Castel, daughter of Jean Christophe and of Marie.2
2 CONT Witnesses signing: Two unidentified crosses, one surely the bride's; (Jean Claude) Mortalle, husband of Marie Louise Cajot; Jolӱ, same signature as August 18, 1760 (Mob. mb I:43); Paul, not identified; (Nicolas) Ceringe (dit L'Esp
2 CONC erance).
2 CONT 1. Although the priest carefully inserted "Sarde dit" above and between "Joseph" and "Barangue", the groom signed "Joph Barranco". In Natchitoches by June 1765, he died before 1769. Natchitoches Colonial Archives, no. 449, no. 458
2 CONC 6, research by Elizabeth Shown Mills.
2 CONT 2. The bride may have been the daughter of Marie Langlaise. See Mob. mb I:30. Cécile, born about 1722, was living at Rapides (Louisiana) in 1773. AGI, PPC, legajo 189B:1066.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References 
2 CONT # Mills, Elizabeth Shown. Assimilation? Or Marginalization and Discrimination? Romani Settlers of the Colonial Gulf (Christophe Clan). PDF download at Elizabeth Shown Mills, Historic Pathways (https://www.historicpathways.com : [6
2 CONC  Dec 2018]). [p.25-34.] (Historic Pathways.com, 2015)Hyperlink to article < [https://www.historicpathways.com/download/romanisettlers.pdf PDF] >
