2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/De_L%27espine-5
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Agnes de L’espine was booth about 1694 and died 10 October 1743 in Mobile, Louisiana Territory, New France
2 CONT
2 CONT Her burial record indicates she came from Brest (France); one reading of a daughter's marriage record suggests she came from "Bruges in Flander." (Mills, p 13)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie may have gone by or been wrongly named "Langloise".[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT At her 1720 deportation she was identified as “Marie Agnes de L’espine, wife of Jean Christophe;” she appeared with her daughters in a cluster labeled “forced laborers: bohèmes.” (Mills, p 14)
2 CONT
2 CONT She married or otherwise mated with the following individuals: (Mills, p 13)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Jean Baptiste Pierret (about 1710), about whom nothing further is known
2 CONT * Jean Christophe (btw 1718-1720), field surgeon; with whom she was deported from France in 1720; he was born "in the fields" about 1687.
2 CONT * Jean Gaspart (by 1725), drummer
2 CONT * __?__ Simon? (Her surname at her 1743 death, which also indicated she was then wife of Joseph, Boheme, invalid.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children include 
2 CONT # Marguerite Cecile Christophe was born about 1723 to Jean Christophe and Marie Agnes Simon dite de L'espine, who were married prior to being deported to Biloxi together aboard Le Tilleul in 1720 .[1] Marguerite Cecile's father wa
2 CONC s an "untrained ‘barber surgeon’ who cut limbs as well as hair while her mother was listed among 'forced laborers: bohèmes," a term equated with Roma or gypsies.[1]
2 CONT
