1 NAME Jacqueline /Du Suau De La Croix/
2 GIVN Jacqueline
2 _MARNM Du Suau De La Croix
1 NAME Jacqueline /Du Suau De La Croix, Lemelle/
2 GIVN Jacqueline
2 SURN Du Suau De La Croix, Lemelle
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1730
2 PLAC New Orleans, Basse Louisiane, Nouvelle France (LA US)
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1790
2 PLAC New Orleans, Provincia de la Louisiana, New Spain (LA US)
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes for Lemelle Jacqueline: - Jacqueline had at least two other children: Marie Jeanne Davion (bc1750) who had children of François Lemelle, Jacques Lemelle's brother, and Louis Dusuau de la Croix (bc1755), the s
2 CONC on of Jacqueline by Joseph Dusuau de la Croix. - Excerpt from "In Business Themselves: Women of Color and Their Commercial Transactions in Eighteenth Century English Cape Town and New Orleans" (Mikal G. Ison): New Orleans, Louisia
2 CONC na, is another example of not only women of color free, but slave women and their activities that were passed from mothers to their daughters in the cities of the United States.In the article "urban-urban freedom slavery: the eman
2 CONC cipation of Jacqueline Lemelle, historian , L. Virginia Gould, gives a glimpse into the life of a woman of color who began her life as a slave, but got her freedom through a relationship with her white owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1762, at the age of thirty-two, Jacqueline was bought by Santiago Lemelle, a ship captain and merchant.  After living with ten Lemelle years as his slave, Jacqueline and her three daughters were released. After obtaining her fr
2 CONC eedom and that of her children, Jacqueline remained in the household of Lemelle, which suggests that their relationship was reciprocal. It is extremely important to note that when Lemelle died in 1784, Jacqueline and her children 
2 CONC inherited most of his furniture, his property on Royal Street, and his two slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://gw.geneanet.org/jelumac?lang=en&iz=0&p=jacqueline&n=n Geneanet Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 15 2018, 15:52:53 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline Lemelle, mulâtresse esclave, was born circa 1730, probably in New Orleans. She bore children for (1) Joseph Louis du Suau de la Croix, native of Gap or Tallard, Dauphiné, France, son of Monsieur du Séau de la Croix and 
2 CONC Françoise Borel; (2) Jacques Étienne “Santiago” Lemelle, son of François Lemelle dit Bellegarde of Saint-Sulpice Parish, Paris, France and Marie-Louise Mariette of Trève, Germany and (3) unknown. In 1767, at New Orleans, Jacques L
2 CONC emelle sold a 5-month-old quarteronne daughter of his slave Jacqueline, mulâtresse, to Gaspard Gardelle, for the purpose of manumitting her after the age of 18 months. Lemelle stipulated that the child would remain with her mother
2 CONC  for 18 months. Infant valued at 1,400 pesos: Hall, Afro-Louisiana, line 143. In 1769, at New Orleans, Jacques Lemelle manumitted a quarteron child of his mulâtresse, Jacqueline: Hall, Afro-Louisiana, line 156. On 10 November 1772
2 CONC , at New Orleans, Jacques Lemelle manumitted Jacqueline, mulâtresse, age 42 and the following quarterons, her children: Agathe, age 13, Tonton, age 3, Adélaïde, age 1: Orleans Parish, Notarial Acts, Almonáster y Roxas, no. 271.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.mylhcv.com/1792-opelousas-census-free-people-color/
