2 CONT None of the prominent Acadian historians and genealogical researchers name his parents, though many trees name Mathurin Rhibauda & Marie Dolbeau. No source has been found so far.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT The Acadian Thibodeau family stems from one man, Pierre Thibodeau. He was a miller who was born in France around 1631. He was also a settler. ≤ref name=CORMIER>Clément Cormier, “TIBAUDEAU, PIERRE,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biogra
2 CONC phy, vol. 2, University of Toronto/Universit Laval, 2003–, accessed September 17, 2019, http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/tibaudeau_pierre_2E.html. [http://www.biographi.ca/fr/bio.php?id_nbr=1126 En Français].≤/ref>  His origins and 
2 CONC date of arrival in Acadia are unknown.≤ref name=DGFA>White, Stephen A., Patrice Gallant, and Hector-J Hébert. Dictionnaire Généalogique Des Familles Acadiennes. Moncton, N.-B.: Centre D’études Acadiennes, Université De Moncton, 19
2 CONC 99, Print. p. 1508.≤/ref>≤ref name=SW94>White, Stephen A. La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des “”Retrouvailles94””, Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne, vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994). THIBODEAU,  [https://ww
2 CONC w.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/node/55  37 Families] ≤/ref>
2 CONT Around 1660 Pierre married Jeanne Thériot, daughter of Jean Thériot and Perrine Reau,≤ref name=DGFA/> in Port-Royal Acadia≤ref name=mass/>(today Annapolis Royal, Nova Scotia, Canada).
2 CONT Between about 1661 and 1689, the couple had 16 children:≤ref name=DGFA/>  
2 CONT
2 CONT #Marie (1661 - 1711)
2 CONT #Marie  (1663 - )
2 CONT #Marie Jeanne (1664 - 1703)
2 CONT #Anne-Marie  (1666 - ) 
2 CONT #Anne Catherine (1667 - )
2 CONT #Pierre  (1670 - ) 
2 CONT #Jeanne  (1672 - 1741) 
2 CONT #Jean (1674 - 1746)
2 CONT #Antoine  (1676 - ) 
2 CONT #Pierre  (1678 - )
2 CONT #Michel  (1680 - )
2 CONT #Cécile  (1680 - )
2 CONT #Marie Anne (1682 - 1720) 
2 CONT #Claude 1685 - 1756)
2 CONT #Catherine Josèphe (1686 - )
2 CONT #Charles  (1689 - 1756)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre was a founder of [[Space:Chipoudy, Acadie|Chipoudy]]::"In 1698 Pierre Thibaudeau, a resident of Port Royal, began his settlement at Shepody... A miller of Pré Ronde... he and his sons exploredthe Shepody area in the first y
2 CONC ear... The first wintering by three ofthe Thibaudeau's sons was in 1699/1700, when they did very well trading furs with the Indians. De Villieu heard of the activity and immediately protested that they were on his father-in-law's 
2 CONC seigneurie without permission, although the precise boundaries of La Vallière's grant are almost impossible to determine from the wording of the concession.Thibaudeau had dreamed of a seigneurie of his own, like that of his old fr
2 CONC iend Mathieu Martin at Cobequid... but showed a willingness to compromise which de Villieu declined. When in 1702 de Villieu himself was willing to compromise, the Thibaudeau group, beleiving that a petition of their own to Paris 
2 CONC would succeed, in turn refused. Indeed they were confident enough of success to invest a great deal of effort in dyking, building sluices (aboiteaux), and even erecting a grist-and-saw mill with machinery from New England. In 1703
2 CONC  they had their lands confirmed but "without prejudice" to the rights of La Vallière. The old man died thinking himself a true seigneur but, in an arrêt of June 2,1705, the Thibaudeau's right to the land was expressly granted as a
2 CONC  concession from La Vallière's seigneurie. Officially, the Thibaudeaus could not extend their lands, nor could others settle without assumingthe legal position and charges consequent to being censitaires of theseigneur of Beaubass
2 CONC in. In 1702 there appear to have been seven households at Shepody (roughly thirty-three people)."≤ref name=Clark> Clark, Andrew Hill, Acadia; the geography of early nova Scotia to 1760. Madison:  University of Wisconsin Press, 196
2 CONC 8. p145 (Thibodeau founder ofChipoudy-Shepody)≤/ref>
2 CONT Thibodeau’s land holdings and possessions varied greatly through theyears as shown in the Port-Royal censuses of 1678,≤ref>Tim Hebert; 1678 Port Royal Acadian Census noting that the correlations for this census were done by Rev. C
