2 CONT GIVN Pierre
2 CONT SURN Thibodeaux
2 CONT TITL Seigneur de Chipoudy [Guidry.GED]
2 CONT Pierre THIBODEAU, of the Province of Poitou, France, was about 23 years old when he came to Acadia in 1654. He was indentured to Emmanuel LEBORGNE, a merchant from LaRochelle, France. Thibodeaux sought his fortune in the new land.
2 CONC  After his indenture was completed, he married Jeanne TERRIOT, whose family had come to Acadia much earlier. The census of 1671 lists Thibodeau, a "ploughsman," age 40, as having removed from Port Royal to Pre Ronde. He and his wi
2 CONC fe had five daughters and one son. In time he added a gristmill to his marshland farm and a sawmill to his brookside holdings. His family grew to 16 children -- seven sons and nine daughters. He had 159 grandchildren, more than ha
2 CONC lf of whom were descendents of the seven Thibodeau brothers. Four of the boys -- Pierre l'Aisne, Pierre, Michel and Charles -- were part of the "Grand Derangement" or deportation of the Acadians in 1755. They eventually settled in
2 CONC  the Bayou LaFourche area and the Opelousas
2 CONT
