1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LHXQ-QBY
2 CONT
2 CONT '''François Savoie''' is the Ancestral Patriarch of the Acadian Savoie Family
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''François Savoie''' was born 1621 in France based on census reports [1],  but his birth record has not been found. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding François' location of birth, genealogist <[http://stephenwhite.acadian-home.org/frames.html Stephen A. White]> remains silent.[2] However, linguist <[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geneviève_Massignon Geneviève Massignon]
2 CONC > [3] speculated that he may have come from <[https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martaizé Martaizé]>, near Loudun, France, because the name Savoie is among the many Acadian names that are found in the nearby Seigneury d'Aulnay (compris
2 CONC ing of the villages of Angliers, Aulnay, Martaizé and La Chausée).[4].  A 2021 search of the indexed parish registers in the immediate vicinity of these villages show 43 baptism, marriages, or burials of a "Savoie" between the yea
2 CONC rs 1600 and 1650, demonstrating that the surname was not uncommon in that region.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has been said that he was the illegitimate son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-of-Savoy-prince-of-Carignano/6000000004154417190 Tommaso Francesco di Savoia], but that claim is perhaps the wishful thinking of researchers 
2 CONC who want to find a royal connection. [10].
2 CONT
2 CONT François probably came to Acadia around 1643.  The time and location of his death are not known; he was living in 1671 and not in 1678.
2 CONT
2 CONT Y Haplogroup from SNP : R1b-M269→Z367 (R-M269→Z367)
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family  
2 CONT
2 CONT From ''Généalogie des Français d'Amérique du Nord'' (Genealogy of French in North America) <[http://www.francogene.com/quebec--genealogy/085/085608.php link]> 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of François SAVOIE and Catherine LEJEUNE [85608] SAVOIE, François (..), ploughman (laboureur), born about 1621 (rec. 1671), died between census 1671 and census 1678 Port-Royal (Acadie) Him : Origin : would be from Marta
2 CONC izé (860149) (nom existe), but records are missing and there is no trace of that person (Origine : serait de Martaizé (860149) (nom existe), mais registres lacunaires et aucune trace de lui)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Married about 1651, from .. (Acadie) LEJEUNE, Catherine, born about 1633 (rec. 1671)
2 CONT *       1) Andrée, married about 1683 Jean PRÉGENT dit LEBRETON
2 CONT *       2) Catherine, married about 1676 François LEVRON
2 CONT *       3) Françoise, born about 1653 (rec. 1671, rec. 1686) or 1652 (rec. 1693) Port-Royal (Acadie), died 1711-12-27, buried Port-Royal (Acadie), married about 1670 Jean CORPORON
2 CONT *       4) Germain, married about 1678 Marie BRAULT dit VINCELOTTE
2 CONT *       5) Jeanne, born about 1658 (1671), 1657 (rec. 1686), 1658 (rec. 1693), 1665 (rec. 1698), 1657 (rec. 1699) or 1656 (rec. 1701) Port-Royal (Acadie), died 1735-11-03, buried 1735-11-04 Port-Royal (Acadie), married about 1675 
2 CONC Étienne PELLERIN
2 CONT *       6) Marie1, born about 1656 (rec. 1699), married about 1675 Jacques TRIEL dit LAPERRIÈRE
2 CONT *       7) Marie2, married about 1688 Gabriel ou Pierre CHIASSON
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 1671 Port Royal Census, Francois 50 was a farmer, Catherine 38, one married daughter - Francoise 18, Germain 16, Marie 14, Jeanne 13, Catherine 9, Francois 8, Barnabe 6, Andree 4, Marie 1-1/2. They had 4 cattle an
2 CONC d 6 arpents of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “Oral tradition and some Acadian/Cajun genealogists have attributed François Savoie (1621-1678), the patriarch of the Cajun Savoie family of Louisiana and Canada, as an illegitimate son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Thomas-of-Sa
2 CONC voy-prince-of-Carignano/6000000004154417190 Thomas Francis], Prince of Carignan; however, this has been a rather disputed claim.”
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://ancestorbios.blogspot.com/2018/01/french-peasant-or-italian-prince.html?m=1 “French Peasant or Italian Prince – François Joseph Savoie”] (2018)
2 CONT
2 CONT François Joseph Savoie has perhaps gained more notoriety centuries after his death than he had during his life. He was born in about 1621 and made his way to the colony of Acadia during the 1640s. He settled in Port-Royal, and in 
2 CONC about 1651, he married Catherine Lejeune, who is believed to have been born in France in about 1633. They had nine children born between about 1653 and 1669. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The entire family was listed in the 1671 Acadia census as living in Port-Royal, with François being a farmer. Despite the large number of people in his household, he only owned 4 cattle and no sheep. Oldest daughter Françoise was 
2 CONC listed a second time in the household next door as the 18-year-old wife of Jean Corporan and mother of a newborn daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT It's unknown when François and his wife Catherine died. Like most early settlers of Acadia, within a couple of generations, their descendants scattered to a number of places in America, many of them ending up in Louisiana. Amongst
2 CONC  some descendants, stories were passed down that claimed François was not a Frenchman — that he was actually the illegitimate son of an Italian nobleman named <[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Francis,_Prince_of_Carignano Tom
2 CONC aso Francesco]>, making him a “prince.” While this story hasn’t been disproven, there’s no documentation to back it up. Other research has shown there was a family named Savoie in the village of Martaizé, France, and other Acadian
2 CONC  families appear to be from the same area. This seems a more likely place of origin for François.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Timeline''' 
2 CONT *Occupation: labourer
2 CONT *Census: 1671 Port Royal; François Sçavois, 50, laborer. 4 head of cattle, 10 arpents workable land. The only known documentation on him. (Census PR 1671 50a - «laboureur, 4 bêtes à cornes, 6 arpents en labour»).
2 CONT * Immigration: ABT 1643 Port Royal
2 CONT *Event: Historical Note probably came to Canada from La Chausee, Martaise, Grand-Poitou, Vienne, France but there are no record of him there or any other Savoies in that period.
2 CONT *Event: Name Variations "Sçavois" as per 1671 census
2 CONT * Event: NB surname appeared regularly in Brittany, Paris and Val d'Aosta in that period. Spelled SAVOYE in parts of Italy directly bordering on France.
2 CONT *  Event: Census Note 1671 the census was taken by Pere Laurent Molin, Cordelier
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth seen as 04/07/1621 without reference
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT >arrivé en Acadie vers 1643. Souche des Savoie au Canada. Nous ne connaissons pas exactement le lieu de naissance de François en France. Nous ne savons pas si François était marié ou célibataire lorsqu'il quitta la France. Toutefo
2 CONC is, selon Fidèle Thériault, François serait né de la paroisse de Martaizé dans le Poitou, en France. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From a resource: Acadians Who Found Refuge in Louisiana, February 1764-early 1800s
2 CONT
2 CONT SAVOIE/SAVOY [sah-VWAH, sah-VOY] ACADIA
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois Savoie, a farmer from Martaize, near Loudun, south of the Loire valley in France, born in c1621, came to Acadia probably in the 1640s and married Catherine, sister of Edmée Lejeune, wife of early Acadian settler Francois 
2 CONC Gautrot.  Francois Savoie and Catherine Lejeune had nine children, only two of them sons:  Germain, born at Port-Royal in c1654, and Francois, fils, in c1663.  Their six daughters married into the Corporon, Triel dit Laperrière, P
2 CONC ellerin, Levron dit Nantois, Préjean dit Le Breton, and Chiasson families.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Germain was the only one of Francois's son to create a family of his own.  In c1678, he married Marie, daughter of Vincent Breau dit Vincelotte, at Port-Royal.  Germain and Marie remained in the Port-Royal area and had a dozen chi
2 CONC ldren, including five sons, all born at Port-Royal, who created families of their own.  Three of their daughters married into the Blanchard, Babineau dit Deslauriers, and Poirier families.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Oldest son Germain, fils, born in c1682, married Geneviève, daughter of Nicolas Babineau, at Port-Royal in January 1709.  They had 10 children, including fives sons who married into the Landry, Blanchard, Martin, and Bourg familie
2 CONC s.  Three of their daughters married into the Dupuis, Pellerin, and Lanoue families.  Germain, fils and his family also remained in the Port-Royal area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois, born in c1684, married Marie-Josèphe, daughter of Alexandre Richard, at Port-Royal in November 1707.  They had 13 children, including seven sons who married into the Thibodeau, Haché, Comeau, Arcand, Richard, Arseneau, P
2 CONC réjean, Lord, and Melanson families.  Five of their daughters married into the Arseneau, Comeau, Hébert dit Manuel, and Thibodeau families.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean, born in c1692, married first to Marie, daughter of Jean Dupuis, at Port-Royal in November 1718, and then to Ursule, daughter of Pierre Thibodeau le jeune, probably at Port-Royal in c1745.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul, born in c1696, married Judith, daughter of Jacques Michel, at Port-Royal in November 1722.  Unlike his father and his brothers, who remained at Port-Royal, Paul and his family moved to Chepoudy probably in the 1720s.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Youngest son Charles, born in May 1703, married Francoise, daughter of Étienne Martin, at Port-Royal in January 1730. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT “The Savoie surname is common, not only in New Brunswick, but also throughout North America. It is found in several regions of Canada, in New England and in Louisiana. However, the origin of the name is still obscure. Some allege 
2 CONC that the surname was borne by persons who came originally from the French Department of Savoie.  The esurname was already well established in France during the 17th century. There are several ancient French spellings for the name 
2 CONC such as Savoy, Savoye, Savoix, Savois, Scavoie, and Scavois. Savoie is the first spelling which has prevailed over the others.  The Acadian Savoie family is descended from only one pioneer, François Savoie who was born in France i
2 CONC n 1621. He came to Acadia around 1642 during the administration of governor Charles de Menou d'Aulnay. Unfortunately, the precise birth place is not known.  Genevieve Massignon was one of the first historians to systematically stu
2 CONC dy the French archives for the origins of the Acadian families. During the course of her research she discovered in ancient parish registers the name of several families known in Acadia. The Savoies are among them.  René Perron, o
2 CONC f the Amitiés Acadiennes Association recently found in one of the parish registers of LaFerté-Gaucher, west of, Paris, the baptismal certificate of a daughter of one "François Savoye" baptized in November 1617.  Upon his arrival i
2 CONC n Acadia, François Savoie settled at Port-Royal. By 1671 he was married and the father of nine children, including three sons. His wife, Catherine Lejeune, was the sister of Edmée Lejeune, wife of another Acadian pioneer, François
2 CONC  Gautreau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [Telegraph-Journal, Wednesday, August 10, 1994; p. A8; contributed by Fidele Theriault of Fredericton, New Brunswick]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Origins Discussions
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/discussions/68785 “Is Francois Savoie really the son of Tomaso?”]
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/discussions/186267 “Francois Joseph Savoie SOLVED”]
2 CONT *[https://www.geni.com/discussions/251287?msg=1570076 “Francois Savoie Oral Tradition discusion”] 
2 CONT *Augustines Alley blog on how Francios Joseph Savoie's descendants passed down knowledge of their origins [https://augustinesalley.wordpress.com/2015/01/27/the-duchy-of-savoy-and-the-inheritance-of-our-faith/ THE DUCHY OF SAVOY AN
2 CONC D THE INHERITANCE OF OUR FAITH]
2 CONT *https://beaugrand.wordpress.com/2010/07/04/history-of-the-reginment-carignan-saliere/
2 CONT *http://www.acadian-home.org/carignan-regiment.html
2 CONT * speculative parentage issues. [http://www.geni.com/people/Tommaso-Francesco-di-Savoia-principe-di-Carignano/6000000004154417190 Tommaso Francesco di Savoia, principe di Carignano] & [http://www.geni.com/people/Marie-de-Bourbon-S
2 CONC oissons/6000000000293367254 Marie de Bourbon-Soissons]
2 CONT * See “Discussion of Tomasso as parent of Francois” [https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000185709118853 document attached]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://www.geni.com/discussions/68785?msg=770652&page=1
2 CONT
2 CONT A discussion of the lineage of Mr. Savoie concluded with a ''spokesperson for the House of Savoy'' who stated in rebuttal to any suggestion that Francois Joseph Savoie was related to Prince Tomasso Francesco di Savoia:
2 CONT
2 CONT "While I can appreciate the conviction of your genealogical pursuit, I must say that I am truly appalled by the letter written to the Real Casa di Savoia.
2 CONT
2 CONT What outright arrogance! And to not have the decency or cultural sense to address the family properly! The spoken style for both HRH the Duke of Aosta and HRH the Duke of Apulia are just that! (HRH) - His Royal Highness.
2 CONT
2 CONT I nearly fainted upon seeing a comment that suggested taking an illegal swab of the Family! Some jokes are simply not acceptable. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I think we must all remember -  "La nobiltà non è cosa che si acquista nascendo, ma virtuosamente vivendo"  ;) Might I say that keeping this in mind, the genealogy has already been decided. Hopes and dreams.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enjoy the history that you have. It starts in 1621 and Not earlier until you've produced some archival evidence. And please, carissimi, do keep in mind that even if some link is made (and as you were informed - it is nothing but f
2 CONC anciful to even consider) you certainly have absolutely no claims of any sort any place whatsoever!  The forumist suggesting otherwise ought to be examined for insanity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants 400 years removed, connected BY AN ILLEGITIMATE offspring, never recognized, would continue to hold precisely NO connection to the Real Casa ...  and never will. Do you know how many royal houses have 150 year illegiti
2 CONC mately connected offspring that nobody cares about and have no claim to a thing? Plenty running around Europe right now.  So understand dismissal of the Real Casa di Savoia and know that the Burden of Proof lies in your hands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until then.
2 CONT
2 CONT Distinti Saluti,  VdCB"
2 CONT
2 CONT So remember that the connection is spurious and considered fanciful but the discussion over how generations of Francois Savoy related families believed the connection to be true is a history in and of itself. 
2 CONT
