2 TITL Mariage François Savoie et Catherine Lejeune.pdf
1 NOTE BIOGRAPHY: It is believed by some researchers that Francois was the first in line as the oldest son to rule Savoy-Carignano, Royal House of Savoy--but this is highly disputed.
2 CONT
2 CONT François may have had a title of Prince. It is suspected he did because two of his brothers were Princes. Perhaps he fought against the King of France and was banished. We must remember that the whole Empire of Savoy was devastat
2 CONC ed and in ruins after the 30-Years War. The 30-Years War lasted from 1618 to 1648, however, in Savoy it lasted until 1659. From Turin, Italy, François somehow made his way to the "New World" in Port Royal, where he found a much d
2 CONC ifferent kind of life than he had known. Between the years 1600 and 1632, several Frenchmen tried to settle in Acadia but failed. England had also tried and failed. The Frenchmen and the Scotsmen who had tried to settle the area h
2 CONC ad all returned to France and England, respectively. In 1632, France again gained control of Acadia and a Frenchman named Isaac de Razilly went to Acadia with 300 hand-picked men to settle the country. He died in 1635. Under the d
2 CONC irection of d'Dulnay, who made strong efforts to recruit French men, women and families, ships began arriving bringing groups of workers who were fishermen, farmers, carpenters, housewives, children and others, looking for a bette
2 CONC r way of life. Their goal was to form their own nation. François was about 22 years old when he arrived at Port Royal. Some authors give his arrival as 1642, others as 1643.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paraphrased from Terrebonne Life Lines, vol. 17, p. 16 & 17. "From the House of Savoy to the Farmer" by Alton Henry Detiveaux
