2 CONT Vincent arrived in Acadia in 1652; as a single man approx. age 21 years; he was a farmer in Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Researchers found a second Vincent living with hes mother, Mary Reneaume, in the area of St. Jean de Sauves after 1671 (19 years after Vincent left for Acadie), but he is referred to as Vincent le Jeune (the Younger). They say it 
2 CONC was fairly common practice among the French at the time to have an older brother or sister as the god-parent of a newborn sibling and to give the infant the same name as the god-parent. In this case the older Vincent, who went to 
2 CONC Acadie, would have been godparen* to the younger brother, also named Vincent, who remained in France. --"A Breau Genealogy" by Clarence T. Breaux & Robert Brault
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Vincent Breau/Brault & Marie Bourg had 11 children Note: Vincent comes from La Chaussee France in the province of Vienne. He lived in Port Royal, Acadia. He was one of the founding families of Acadia.(Port Royal is now known as An
2 CONC napolis, Nova Scotia)
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Né en
2 CONT 1611
2 CONT Naissance, vers 1611
2 CONT 34 ans
2 CONT 1645
2 CONT Mariage avec Marie-René BRAULT, en 1645, à Grande-Chaussée, Loudun, Poitou, France -, -, POITO, FRA
2 CONT 74 ans
2 CONT 1685
2 CONT Décès, vers 1685
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Vincent was a native of LaChaussee, near Loudun, present Vienne department, France. He arrived in Port Royal in the mid 1600's where circa 1661 he married Marie Bourg (Arsenautl 1978). After the Expulsion of 1755 several Breaux fa
2 CONC milies eventually found their way to Louisiana. The first to arrive were Vincent's grandson, Athanase and his wife Marie LeBlanc along with his distant cousin Jean-Baptiste Breaux both settling along the Mississippi River in the A
2 CONC cadian Coast). In 1768 some nine Breaux families arrived in Louisiana with other Acadians from Maryland (along with Andreas Meisch), where they had been exiled after the Explusion. Spanish authorities ordered the entire congregati
2 CONC on to settle in Natchez but three of the Breauxs (Alexis and his brother Honore and his cousin Joseph) managed to join their relatives in St. James. Dissatisfied with conditons at Natchez other Breauxs eventually settled in the Ac
2 CONC adian Coast. One of the first Breauxs to move from the Mississippi River to the Teche country was Firmin and his wife Marguerite Breaux, near present day Breaux Bridge. (Notes obtained from Robert  C. West (an Atlas of Louisiana S
2 CONC urnames)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1671 cenus of Acadie - Vicent Brot as shown as age 40, his wife, Marie Bour was age 26. They had 4 children: Marie 9, Anthoine 5, Margueritte 3, Pierre 1. they had 9 cattle, 7 sheep and 4 arpents of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1686 census Vincent was deceased. His widow Marie was shown as 41. She had 10 children living with her: Antoine 20, Marguerite 18, Pierre 16, Anne 17, Francoise 11, Jeanne 9, Marie 7, Jeanne 5, Rene 3, Isabelle 5 months. They had 
2 CONC 4 arpents of land, 19 cattle, 5 sheep and 5 hogs.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1693 census (Marie had not remarried), Marie was shown as 48 years old. She had 8 children still living with her: Marguerite 24, Pierre 22, Anne 20, Franois 18, Jean 15, Jeanne 9, Rene 8, Elisabet 7. She owned 12 arpents, 14 cattl
2 CONC e, 20 sheep, 8 hogs, and 1 gun.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1698 census marie was shown as 53. She had 8 children still at home: Margueritte 28, Franoics 24, Jean 22, Anne 20, Marie 18, Jeanne 17, Rene 15 and Elisabeth 13. She had 18 arpents, 12 cattle, 15 sheep, 12 hogs and 30 fruit trees
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT 1700 census above info unchanged with two exceptions. Pierre age 27 was back home (his wife perhaps deceased as he married in 1692 and 1704 and Marguerrite was missing. No marriage has been located so assume she died between 1698 
2 CONC and 1700 census.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Bourg Breaux did not appear in a census report following 1700.  She died in 1730 so assume she lived with one of her children.  In the 1714 census there was a widow Breau with one male and 2 females which could have been her
2 CONC .
