2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://membres.lycos.fr/ancetre/comeau.htm> (copy filed L-13-30).
2 CONT This is a short bio of Pierre Comeau, Father-in-law of Jeanne Bourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT PIERRE COMEAU
2 CONT
2 CONT The Comeau surname comes from the old French word "come" meaning "hair":  this word applies just as easily to the men, at the trees with the horses.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this last case, one makes the word "comé" qualify and one says "a horse comé" to mean that it has a "beautiful mane".  He is to be believed that the ancestor of Comeau is characterized by his beautiful hair.
2 CONT
2 CONT The French origins of Pierre are difficult to check.  It is more than probable that it is of Burgundy.  It is in this area that the majority of Comeau's lived in the Middle Ages and still today.  According to some genealogists, Pi
2 CONC erre, who is lord, the other, son of a lord who leaves the hearth for the army and then Acadia, but all these stories are often due to imagination or embellissement.  One never has put foot in America, the other was probably never
2 CONC  born.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is in Acadia only one source of Comeau.  Pierre was born between 1597 and 1606, son of Jean de Comeau and Marguerite Ocquidem.  Pierre arrived in Acadia with the expedition of Isaac de Razilly, in 1632, or before 1650, date 
2 CONC of birth of his first son, Étienne.  He acquired a farm at  Port-Royal, where he raised his family.  He is a wet cooper of trade, an essential occupation in this time since all meats and fish are packed in salt barrels for conserv
2 CONC ation.  The resources of fishing are exported in this way to France.
2 CONT
2 CONT He marries Rose Bayols about 1649, at Port-Royal.  Two of the six children of the couple concern us, to have let run during centuries their acadian blood in our veins.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first, Pierre "elder", known as the esturgeon, born in 1653, married about 1677, Jeanne Bourg, born also in 1653, daughter of Antoine Bourg and Antoinette Landry.  They live part of their life in Port-Royal at the top of the r
2 CONC iver.  Pierre died at Port-Royal April 8, 1730.  Nineteen children are born, eleven girls and eight sons, whose Abraham Comeau and Jeanne Comeau weave our bonds to Comeau, in this genealogy.  Abraham marries in 1702, Marguerite Pi
2 CONC tre, born in 1684, daughter of the ancestors Jean Pitre, aka Bénèque and Marie Pesselet.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second, Jeanne Comeau, born about 1662, married between 1674 and 1678, Étienne Hébert, born in 1654 at Grang-Pre, son of Étienne Hébert and Marie Gaudet.  Their farm is located at Riviere-aux-Canards.  On the acadian census of
2 CONC  1686, they make party of the ten families established in Baye des Mines.  Etienne Hebert at age 32 and Jeanne Commeaux, at age 24.   Étienne Hébert is buried in Saint-Charles des Mines, November 2, 1713.
2 CONT
2 CONT On voit le nom de lancêtre Pierre Comeau pour la dernière fois, au dernier grand recensement du XVIIe siècle en Acadie, fait en 1686, par lintendant de Meulles. Le patriarche Pierre " Comeaux " vit toujours (il est âgé de 88 ans).
2 CONC  Il trépasse vers 1691, à plus de 90 ans
2 CONT
2 CONT One sees the name of the ancestor Pierre Comeau for the last time, with the last great census of the XVIIth century in Acadia, made in 1686, by the intendant of Meulles.  The patriarch Pierre "Comeaux" lives today (he is 88 years 
2 CONC old).  He died about 1691, at more than 90 years.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
