2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category: Port-Royal, Acadie]]
2 CONT {{Acadian}} [[Category: Great Upheaval]] [[Category: Louisiana First Families]] [[Category: Acadian Immigrants to Louisiana]] [[Category: The Wall of Names at the Acadian Memorial]] [[Category: Louisiana Acadian Coast Settlers]]
2 CONT {{Louisiana Family}}
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==Joseph, sixth son of [[Savoie-65|François Savoie]] le jeune and [[Richard-174|Marie-Josèphe Richard]], born at Minas in June 1727, married, according to Stephen A. White, [[Prejean-35|Anne]], daughter of [[Prejean-3
2 CONC 6|Joseph Préjean]] and [[Comeau-57|Marie-Louise Comeau]], in c1758, no place given, but it probably was during exile on the Gulf of St. Lawrence shore.  Anne gave Joseph a daughter, Marguerite, in c1760.  By then, they may have ta
2 CONC ken refuge in the French stronghold at Restigouche at the head of the Baie des Chaleurs.  The British attacked Restigouche in late June 1760, and another British force accepted the garrison's, and the Acadians', surrender there th
2 CONC e following October.  A Joseph Savoye with a family of four appears on a 24 October 1760 list of 1,003 Acadians surrendered with the garrison; this may have been Joseph à François.  The British held them in a prison compound in No
2 CONC va Scotia for the rest of the war.  In August 1763, British officials counted Joseph, Anne, and two children on Georges Island, Halifax harbor, near his older brother Charles and his family.  Joseph, Anne, and daughter Marguerite,
2 CONC  now age 5, followed Charles and his family from Halifax to Louisiana in 1764-65.  One wonders what happened to their other child and if it was a son or a daughter.  Anne was pregnant on thevoyage and gave birth to a son--[[Savoie
2 CONC -989|Joseph-André]], called André--either aboard ship or at New Orleans soon after their arrival.After baptizing their newborn son at New Orleans in September, they followed brother Charles and his family to Cabahanncoer, where An
2 CONC ne gave Joseph another daughter, Josèphe-Barbe, in November 1766; they baptized her at New Orleans the following March--four children, at least two daughters and a son, between 1760 and 1766, in greater Acadia and Louisiana.  Jose
2 CONC ph died by December 1767, in his late 30s, when Anne remarried to an Hébert at Cabahannocer.  Only her and Joseph's son married.  He settled on upper Bayou Lafourche, where he created a vigorous line. <ref>Joseph (1727-c1767) à Ge
2 CONC rmain à François Savoie http://www.acadiansingray.com/Acadians%20of%20LA-Intro-10c.html
2 CONT </ref> :Joseph Savoie is on the Wall of Names at the Acadian Memorial in St. Martinville, Louisiana Plaque 6 Left. Listed with Joseph is Anne Prejean, his wife and two children Marguerite and Joseph Andre.<ref>Title: The Wall of N
2 CONC ames at the Acadian Memorial; Author: Wall of Names Committee; Jane G. Bulliard, Chair; second edition, 2015; Bodemuller The Printer, USA; Personal Copy. p. 24.</ref> :Joseph Savoie, son of Francois Savoie and Marie Josephe Richar
2 CONC d, was born in Acadie 8 Jun 1727. <ref>Source: [[#S15]]</ref>He was christened at Grand Pré 15 Jul 1727. <ref>Source: [[#DGFA]] p. 1461 :Text: Sponsors: Joseph BREAU & Marie-Josephe SAVOIE.</ref> <ref>Library and Archives Canada, 
2 CONC Fonds de la paroisse catholique Saint-Charles-des-Mines (Grand-Pré, N.-É.) - 1869;  Canadiana, Heritage, Parish registers: Nova Scotia : C-1869 (Image 243): http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1870/243?r=0&s=4</ref>
2 CONC <ref>Title: Catholic Church Records, vol. 1a: Acadian Records, 1707-1748; Author: Diocese of Baton Rouge; Publication: Diocese, 1999. The Registersof St. Charles aux Mines in Acadia, from Grand Pré, carried into exile to St. Gabri
2 CONC el Church of Iberville, LA. Personal copy in possessionof  [[Girouard-4019|Girouard-4019]]; pp. 177-178:Text: Sponsors: Joseph BREAUX & Marie Joseph SAVOIE (SGA-2, 77).</ref> Joseph died before 22 Dec 1767, the date his widow Anne
2 CONC  remarried toJoseph Hebert in Cabanocey.<ref>Title: Some Late Eighteenth-Century Louisianians; Census Records of the Colony 1758-1796; Author: Jacqueline K. Voorhies; Publication: Lafayette, LA: University of Southwestern Louisian
2 CONC a, 1973; p. 424:Text: "LIST OF THE ACADIANS WHO HAVE BEEN MARRIED SINCE THE ESTABLISHMENT OF KABAHANOSSÉ," citing: Papeles procedentes de Cuba, legajo 187-I-A, appended to a letter dated 14 Feb 1768, written by Louis JUDICE to the
2 CONC  governor. ::Joseph HEBERT & Anne PRÉJEAN, widow SAVOY.</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Residence ===
2 CONT :Census: 12 Aug 1763 Halifax, Acadia <ref>Source: [[#DGFA]] p. 1461:Text: Joseph SAVOIE, son of François SAVOIE & Marie(-Josèphe) RICHARD, was at Halifax in the census of 1763.</ref> :Census: 9 Apr 1766 Cabahannocer (Lower), St. J
2 CONC ames, LA  <ref>Source: [[#DGFA]] p. 1461 :Text: age 37. </ref> <ref> Title: Cabanocey, Lillian C. Bourgeois; New Orleans: Pelican Publishing, 1957; reprinted Pelican Publ., 1998. copy in possession of  [[Girouard-4019|Girouard-401
2 CONC 9]]; p. 167 :Text: Joseph SAVOYE, 37, with 6 arpents of land, 1 hog, and 1 gun; :Anne PREJEN, wife, 38; :[space] :Marguerite, daughter 7.</ref> :Joseph and Anne Prejean, daughter of  Joseph Prejean and Marie LouiseComeau, were mar
2 CONC ried in Acadie about 1758. <ref>Source: [[#DGFA]] p. 1461</ref> :[http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=katheriot&id=I12678 Children]
2 CONT # Marguerite SAVOIE b: Abt 1759 in Acadia
2 CONT # SAVOIE b: Bef 12 Aug 1763 in Acadia
2 CONT # Andre SAVOIE c: Abt 22 Sep 1765 in New Orleans, Orleans, LA# Joseph Francois SAVOIE b: 4 Nov 1766 in LA c: 17 Mar 1767 in New Orleans, Orleans, LA
