1 NOTE Additional references: 45-4; L-2-28.
2 CONT Married 9 Jul 1652 to Pierre Boucher (bap. 1 Aug 1622, buried 21 Apr 1717 at Boucherville, son of Gaspard & Nicole Lemaine, first married to Marie Madeleine Christienne with 1 child, future Governor of Trois-Rivieres and founder o
2 CONC f Boucherville) at Notre Dame de Immaculee Conception de Quebec, Quebec, Canada; have copy of this record; sources:  family (15 Children) listed in Jette pg 136; Tanguay Vol I pg 71 shows family of 16 children; Tanguay Vol II pg 3
2 CONC 73 shows family of 2 children; (ct 05 Audouart);  Drouin pg 154; "Our French Canadian Forefathers" (copy filed 60-2).
2 CONT Durocher/Ferguson Genealogy from the internet (copy filed 21-6).
2 CONT "The Grand Families" from the internet (copy filed 21-6).
2 CONT Genealogy pages relative to the Crevier family acquired frim the internet from "CometSystems" (copy filed "CREVIER").
2 CONT "Our French Canadian Forefathers" (copy filed 60-2).
2 CONT "Our French Canadian Ancestors"-Laforest Vol VII Chapter 4.
2 CONT Jette pg 292.
2 CONT <http://www.myerchin.org/ChristopheJeanneCREVIER.html> (copy filed 21-6).
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following, inre to her husband, was acquired from <http://www.electracat.com/Linda2/i180.htm>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:"One Hundred French Canadian Family Histories" by Philip J. Moore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher worked as a wood joiner for the Jesuits at Notre-Dame-Des-Angers. His relationship with the Jesuits was to shape the history of his family and Canada. His son Pierre ( 1622-1717) received his education from the Jes
2 CONC uits. That education was the ideal preparation for his future career.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Boucher served the Jesuits in Huronia from 1637 to 1641. During this time he lived amongst the Indians, learning their culture, customs and languages. When he returned to Quebec, Governor Huault de Montmagny engaged him as 
2 CONC a soldier, interpreter and representative to the Indian tribes. His close involvement with the Governor resulted in his rapid rise in the military. He was present at the founding of Montreal in 1642. In 1644 he became interpreter 
2 CONC and clerk for the fort at Trois-Rivieres.
2 CONT
2 CONT The colony was in a precarious state during the 1640's. The Iroquois were about to push the French back into the sea. When Louis d'Ailleboust replaced Montmagny as governor he appointed Pierre Boucher captain of the town of Trois-
2 CONC Rivieres. Pierre forced the reluctant habitants to move into the stockade and mount a continuous guard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre's immediate superior in Trois-Rivieres was its acting governor, Duplessis-Kerbodot. To stop the ravages of the Iroquois in the vicinity of the fort the governor led a group of settlers and soldiers out of the fort to do bat
2 CONC tle. Pierre opposed the idea and stayed back with a few men in the fort. The governor led his men into an ambush. The Iroquois slaughtered 22 of them, including the governor himself and our Jean Poisson, on August 19, 1652.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following August, 600 Iroquois surrounded the fort held by Pierre and his force of forty adolescents and old men. They repulsed their attacks for nine days. The Iroquois asked for negotiations. To conceal the weakness of his f
2 CONC orces, Pierre went along to parley.
2 CONT
2 CONT From his position of weakness he obtained an incredible agreement "The peace was concluded on the conditions they would hand over to me all the prisoners they had in their army, whether they were French or Indian, that they would 
2 CONC go and get whom they were holding in their villages and bring them back within forty days, and that the most important among the Iroquois nations would come with presents to Quebec to seek peace from our governor M. de Lauzon, and
2 CONC  to conclude it. This was carried out in every particular. And when they went away, they left me six of their children as hostages."
2 CONT
2 CONT His rewards for these incredible feats was to be made commander of the fort he saved and later to be appointed governor of Trois-Rivieres and the surrounding region. Letters of nobility also followed from Paris. His stature in the
2 CONC  colony made him the logical choice for emissary to the court of Louis XIV. Due to the urgency of the mission he risked a hazardous fall passage, leaving in October 20, 1660.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I had the honor of speaking to the king, who questioned me about the state of the country, of which I gave him an accurate account, and His Majesty promised me that he would help the country and take it under his protection; whic
2 CONC h he has done." Boucher talked with other important personages at the court, Prince de Conde and Colbert.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Boucher left France July 15, 1662, with a hundred working men he recruited with money he borrowed. The voyage back was wild, 60 men, "both soldiers and workmen" died at sea. The King sent two ships and 100 soldiers with pro
2 CONC visions and munitions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most significant result of his mission to the court was the Carignan regiment that arrived in 1665 to assure the security of the colony against the Iroquois.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Boucher was an idealist with utopian ideas he tried to implement in his seigneury, planted in Indian country near Montreal. His first wife was Indian. His second was Jeanne Crevier, daughter of Christopher Crevier. Pierre d
2 CONC ied at his manor house, in Boucherville, at the age of 95.
2 CONT
2 CONT His testament,"I do not leave you with great wealth, but the little I do leave you has been very properly acquired. I have done what I could to leave you more, I have neglected nothing to that end, having indulged in no foolish ex
2 CONC pense, as you all know, but it did not please God, who is the master, to give me more. I leave you for friends many people of rank and distinction and many honest people. I leave you no enemies for my part, as far as I know. I hav
2 CONC e done what I could to live without reproach; try to do the same."
2 CONT
2 CONT Occasionally one encounters greatness and occasionally goodness, but rarely in the same person. Pierre Boucher was such a person. School children in Perche learn about him as one of the great men of their province.
2 CONT * . 1685 Migrated to Canada 1635 with his father
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from:  <http://myweb.cableone.net/moocows1/familytree/kevin/f11.htm>.
