1 NAME Frederick Henry /Cook/ Sr
2 GIVN Frederick Henry
2 SURN Cook
2 NSFX Sr
2 _MARNM Cook
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Saint Louis Cemetery Number 3
2 ADDR
3 CITY New Orleans
3 STAE Louisiana
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 9 FEB 1829
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE England
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 DEAT
2 DATE 26 FEB 1899
2 ADDR
3 CITY New Orleans
3 STAE Louisiana
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Frederick Henry Cook, Sr. was born the youngest child of Harry Cook and Ann Bradwin WRIGHT Cook while they were living in St. Giles-in-the-Fields and St. George Bloomsbury, London, England. His father Harry migrate
2 CONC d to New York in 1833 with three of his brothers, and the following year young Frederick (age 5) with his many siblings and mother sailed aboard the Barque, "William Glen Anderson" arriving on 31 May 1834.
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick grew up here in New York City in the Boroughs of Manhattan and Brooklyn where he and his brothers were all educated. Frederick and brothers Francis and Ferdinand Cook all earned top-quality work experience here in the ci
2 CONC ty at the famous engineering and industrial manufacturing foundry "Novelty Iron Works". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Frederick later became a naturalized citizen of the USA on 13 Dec 1850 before a Superior Court in the State of New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------
2 CONT Published: Times-Picayune,  New Orleans, LA, Feb. 27, 1899, Page 12
2 CONT
2 CONT "FREDERICK COOK
2 CONT
2 CONT Whose Inventions Have Made His Name Famous, and Whose Life Was a Model in Many Ways, Called to His Maker
2 CONT
2 CONT In the 70th year of his age Mr. Frederick Cook gave up his spirit to its Maker, and in that there came a desolation in the household that had never before been experienced. A husband and father, who had been everything a loving wi
2 CONC fe, devoted children and stanch friends could appreciate, admire and love in man, has been called to a reward earned by a sincere life, an observance of the golden rule, and by doing all things required of man by the Creator of ma
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT Every business or society man in the community has said that of Mr. Cook. He has been a man admired of men, because of his manliness, his artistic and inventive genius, his honesty and sincerity. He was an Englishman, the son of a
2 CONC  retired banker, and was born in London seventy years ago. He came to this country with his brothers and engaged in the machinery business, which he kept up all his life. In the early 40s he was one of the few shippers of machiner
2 CONC y that had to be transported by vessel around Cape Horn. He was the builder and owner of the Belleville Iron Works, which was situated in Algiers, LA. Out of that shop came a Bagasse-burner, of which he was the inventor. It is sti
2 CONC ll in service on Ames Plantation, opposite Carrollton.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the War Mr. Cook left this country and went to Canada, and there engaged in the oil business with Colonel Havemeyer, father of the present oil magnate. He sold out there eventually and returned to Louisiana and again took u
2 CONC p the machinery business. He also ventured in sugar planting in St. Landry Parish. About this time he invented the arrow cotton tie, which yielded him a veritable mine. At the time of his death he had large interests in sugar indu
2 CONC stries, and was still deriving revenue from his tie and Bagasse-burner. He was the inventor of some sixty mechanical contrivances for which he obtained patents. He was honored by a number of scientific societies. He made the first
2 CONC  commercial success of petroleum, and was invited by the government of Austria to visit the country and demonstrate the fact in the navy. Although tempted by a big consideration, his wife refused to consent to his going, so he rem
2 CONC ained here.
2 CONT
2 CONT His spare hours were devoted to scientific study and occasionally to art. As he excelled in engineering and mechanical science, so did he in art, in that he did some work with the brush that permitted his paintings to go on exhibi
2 CONC tion with professionals and receive credit. A magnificent piece of work in this respect is a head of the crucified Christ. The coloring, the features, the anguish depicted, indicate to the beholder at once that the brain that conc
2 CONC eived it and the hand that executed it were in delightful harmony. There are other paintings about he house done by his hand to disclose that his skill in this respect had latitude.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Cook was married in this city to Miss Tamelie Cavalier, a daughter of one of the ante-bellum merchants of this city. She survives, as well as all of the children born to the union, who are Misses Alice and Louise and Mrs. Lloy
2 CONC d Posey, Walter H., Sidney G. and Fred C. Cook.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although at an advanced age, it was believed if Mr. Cook could have been induced to relax on study, business and energy, he might have lived for years. But he could not be induced to live the life of an idle man. He felt the vital
2 CONC ity so alive in him that he would only yield when it had given out completely.
2 CONT
2 CONT The home of the dead was swarming with friends yesterday and last night. Death came shortly after noon. The funeral will be from the family residence, No. 2915 Chestnut Street, this evening at 3 o'clock, and the interment will be 
2 CONC in the new St. Louis Cemetery on Esplanade Street."
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT Published March 4, 1899 The Louisiana Planter and Sugar Manufacturer Volume 22
2 CONT
2 CONT "FREDERICK COOK
2 CONT
2 CONT We are called upon to chronicle the death of this distinguished gentleman who has been a prominent figure in the sugar industry of Louisiana for nearly half a century. Mr. Cook was born in England and came to the United States in 
2 CONC his youth and was employed at the Novelty Iron Works in New York, the establishment that did much of the best engineering work for the sugar planters of Louisiana in ante-bellum days. Before the Civil War he established the immens
2 CONC e Belleville Iron Works in Algiers and engaged largely in the manufacture of sugar machinery. After the Civil War he engaged in sugar planting in Louisiana, but a lucky investion of his, the Arrow cotton tie, became so successful 
2 CONC as to monopolize his attention for a number of years.
2 CONT
2 CONT During recent years he has been engaged in erecting bagasse furnaces of his own invention and also in the erection of Babcock and Wilcox boilers, for which company he has been the agent for many years. Mr. Cook was an engineer of 
2 CONC high order, and thoroughly comprehended the most obscure problems of modern mechanical engineering. He was one of the advanced thinkers and workers in our industry and will be greatly missed. Mr. Cook was 70 years of age at the ti
2 CONC me of his death and died of pneumonia on Sunday morning, February 26."
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT Frederick and Tamelie's children:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Frederick Charles Cook, Jr. b.1860 2. Alice Roselie H. COOK Richardson b.1867 - married Thomas Rice Richardson 3. Mathilde Marie COOK Posey - married Edward Lloyd Posey, Sr. 4. Walter Henry Cook - married Marie Leonie Aldige - 
2 CONC married Louise Marie Joubert de Villemarest 5. Sidney George Cook - married Daisy Isabelle Beebe - married Florence Falkenstein  - #30488332, Putnam Co, IN, 1906 - married Margaret H. O'Connor 6. Louise Amelia COOK Finley - marrie
2 CONC d Ridgely Finley
