1 NOTE {geni:about_me} SMITH-- On Saturday evening, December 4, 1954, at 10:45 o'clock, ALEXANDER L. SMITH, in his 82nd year, of 2304 Prentiss avenue; husband of the late Louise Gouvion; father of Mrs. Estelle de Monsabert, Mrs. John Bus
2 CONC h. W. Wallace. Thomas P., Lawrence P. and Richard C. Smith: stepfather of Louise, Charles E. and Raymond J. Lods; also survived by nine grandchildren and nine great-grandchildren: a native of Grand Coteau, La.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relatives and friends are invited to attend the funeral from the P. J. McMahon and Sons funeral home. 4800 Canal street, near cemeteries, at 2 o' clock Monday afternoon.  December 6, 1954, followed by services at St. Raphael's chu
2 CONC rch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Interment in Metairie cemetery.
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT THE TIMES-PICAYUNE MONDAY, DECEMBER 6, 1954 PAGE 2.  --------------------------------------------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT  SMITH FUNERAL SERVICES TODAY
2 CONT
2 CONT Religious Rites to Be at St. Raphael's Church 
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services for Alexander L. Smith 80, retired contractor and home builder, will be held at 2 p. m. Monday from the P. J. McMahon and Sons Funeral Home, Inc., 4800 Canal, followed by religious rites at St. Raphael's church an
2 CONC d interment in Metairie cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Smith a native of Grand Coteau, La., died at his home, 2304 Prentiss ave., at 10:45 p. m. Saturday after a lengthy illness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Smith came to New Orleans 60 years ago and spent his entire career in the building business.  He was vice-president of the Prentiss Park Corp.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to members of his family, he is credited with being the first home builder to introduce the Spanish bungalow-type of home and multiple apartment buildings in New Orleans.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was one of the first home builders to originate and design what are termed  "homes for the future" and designed and constructed many of the homes in the Metairie and Gentilly areas.
2 CONT
2 CONT He received a number of national awards for his original designs in homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his building activities, he was for many years chief appraiser and inspector for a number of New Orleans homesteads.  He retired about two years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by four sons, Lawrence P., W. Wallace, Thomas P., and Richard C. Smith two stepsons, Charles E., and Raymond J. Lods; two daughters, Mrs. Estelle de Monsabert and Mrs. John Busch; a stepdaughter, Miss Louise Lods, a
2 CONC ll of New Orleans; nine grandchildren and nine great-grandchildren.
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT THE TIMES-PICAYUNE MONDAY, DECEMBER 6, 1954 PAGE 3.
