2 CONT  F. C. 'Cook' Smith, 75, Dies While Working in Yard 
2 CONT
2 CONT F. C. "Cook" Smith, 75, died suddenly at 9 a.m. today at his home on East Grolee St.
2 CONT
2 CONT Smith had suffered with a heart ailment for several years.  He died while working in his yard.
2 CONT
2 CONT The body is resting at Lafond and Son Funeral Home.  Services will be held at the Grand Coteau Catholic church.  Time of the funeral had not been set.
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of Grand Coteau, Smith was a resident of Opelousas for 11 years.  He was a retired carpenter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are his wife, the former Isabelle Roy of Opelousas; four sons, Sidney of Grosbeck, Tex., Llewellyn of Lake Charles, Leonard of New Iberia, Wilton of Dallas, Tex., three daughters, Mrs. Lucille Louviere of Franklin, Mrs. 
2 CONC W. W. Smith of New Orleans, and Mrs. Beulah Joseph of Opelousas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Also surviving are two brothers, John Smith of Grand Coteau and Alex Smith of New Orleans; and five sisters, Mrs. Henry Richard, Mrs. James Fox and Miss Marie Smith of Grand Coteau, Sister Estelle of Alton, Ill., and Mrs. Willie D
2 CONC evalcourt of Sunset.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 32 DAILY WORLD Opelousas, Louisiana, Tuesday, June 8, 1954. Note: Photo included with obituary. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  GRAND COTEAU NOTES 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Wilton Smith of Baton Rouge and Miss Coralie Mixon of New Orleans visited Mr. Smith's parents, Mr. and Mrs. F. C. Smith Sunday.
2 CONT
2 CONT PAGE TWO CLARION-NEWS, THURSDAY, APRIL 25, 1935
2 CONT
2 CONT  Grand Coteau. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Just about 9 o'clock Tuesday night the town was quite lively, the young folks gathered together all the tin pans, horns, bells, etc. for a "charivarie," and as Mr. Cook Smith and his bride drove in from Grand Point, where the wedd
2 CONC ing took place at 4 p.m., they did justice to their "instruments."  All were invited in by the "Happy Pair," refreshments were served and good wishes given the couple for their future life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge J. A. Smith and daughter, Marie, drove to Grand Point Tuesday to attend the Smith and Roy wedding.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Zitor Smith and little nephew, Sydney, spent Friday in Arnaudville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Gordon Oge spent Tuesday in Opelousas on business.
2 CONT
2 CONT  THE ST. LANDRY CLARION  OPELOUSAS, LA., SATURDAY JANUARY 21, 1911 Page 4, Column 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Grand Coteau Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT Messrs. Cook Smith, and Eaton Guilbeau spent Sunday in Grandpoint.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Cook Smith left for Grandpoint, Tuesday where he is to be married to Miss Isabel Roy, of that place.  The couple will make Grand Coteau their home.
2 CONT
