2 DATE 12 JUL 1963
1 TITL Sr
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Name was Marie Louise Smith.  In the 1940 census, she is listed as Sister Estelle Smith working as a nurse in a hospital/nursing home in Alton, IL.
2 CONT
2 CONT St. Joseph Hospital School of Nursing in charge of Srs of Charity of St Vincent de Paul Alton, Illinois July 23, 1963
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Mrs. Lavergne,
2 CONT
2 CONT You did lose a wonderful aunt and we a wonderful Sister, in death of our loved Sister Estelle.  Her death was a shock to all of us, but we believe she went just as she would have liked to go, and surely she was ready to meet her m
2 CONC aker.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order that a number of her loved ones may know the details of her death, we wrote a short account and are enclosing a copy.  Sister Estelle has been the recipient of numerous Mass offerings and other spiritual gifts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus far we have received the following; two gifts of Gregorian masses (30 successive Masses) 12 High Masses 250 low Masses, 2 enrolments in the Jesuit Sem. Fund Association. 1 enrolment in the Mass, Association of Our Blessed Mot
2 CONC her of Sorrows, 1 enrolment in the Cenacle Crusade of Prayers.  A contribution to the Heart Assoc. for heart research, and money for her poor.  Besides these we know she has had many other Masses and prayers from her numerous frie
2 CONC nds. With prayerful wishes to you from all our Sisters, Sincerely Sr. Pauline. P. S. The enclosed card was in Sr. Estelle's possessions
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Estelle.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Others, Lord, yes Others"  This could well be said to have been the motto of our Dear Sr. Estelle during the forty two years she spent in the service of others at St. Joseph's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thursday night, July 11, was the same as any other night for Sister Estelle.  She began her rounds of visiting each patient throughout the night, until it was time for Mass on Friday morning.  After breakfast she spoke to some of 
2 CONC the Sisters about the patients, and then before retiring quickly left the Hospital to go to two funerals homes to pray for patients who had died at the Hospital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morning after morning, in spite of her fatigue, Sister would never retire before going to pray for the repose of the soul of some patient, or friend, or benefactor who had gone to his eternal reward.
2 CONT
2 CONT Each evening at five o'clock Sister could be found in the Chapel for the evening meditation with the other Sisters.  However, if she were late in retiring or was particularly tired, it was understood that she would rise a little l
2 CONC ater.  Hence, when she did not appear for meditation on Friday evening, no one was alarmed.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sister was not up at about eight 0'clock, one of the Sisters, who had a message to give to Sr. Estelle, knocked softly at Sister's door, and then called her, but received no reply.  Sister then quietly entered the room, and f
2 CONC ound Sr. Estelle turned on her right side apparently in a sound sleep.  Not wishing to disturb Sister, for she felt she needed the rest, The Sister left the room, but realizing that Sr. Estelle would want to be awakened, since it 
2 CONC was nearly time for her to report for night duty, she re-entered the room.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was then that she discovered that Sister was not breathing.  It was Friday evening- the Sacred Heart, to whom Sister had such great devotion, had come to take her Home just as she would have wished.  Sister immediately called t
2 CONC he priest, the doctor and the other Sisters.  While waiting for the priest to come to anoint Sister Estelle, the Sisters shocked and grief stricken, knelt around her bed, and recited the Rosary and other prayers.  The doctor said 
2 CONC that Sister evidently died in her sleep without pain or struggle.  Sister Estelle had known for some time that she had a heart condition, an had often been reminded "to take it easy."  She tried very hard to limit her activities, 
2 CONC but more often than not, she would forget, in her eagerness to do for others.
2 CONT
2 CONT At about four 0'clock Saturday afternoon Sister's remains were placed in the hospital lounge.  She looked so peaceful.  Throngs of people from all walks of life- Catholic and non Catholic, rich and poor- Colored and white- all cam
2 CONC e to pay their respects and pray for one who had been a true friend, and inspiration, a comfort to them in their hour of suffering and need-one who had truly been to them an "Angel of Mercy."
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout the day and night of Saturday and Sunday some of the Sisters were constantly with Sister; there to console those who came and wept as they related Sister's kindness to them.  over and over again was heard the same eulog
2 CONC y given on Sister from those who knew and loved her.  Each had his individual story of how Sister had helped him.  We will cite but a few.- An elderly gentleman, after having said a prayer for Sister said " My wife died in this ho
2 CONC spital, and although we were perfect strangers to Sr. Estelle she came to the funeral.  I was very touched at such kindness."  He could say no more for he began to weep.-He looked so lonely as he walked away. As one middle -aged w
2 CONC oman entered the lounge, she looked around and then nervously blurted out to one of the Sisters, "I'm not of your faith, but I just had to come.  Sister Estelle was so kind and good to me when I was a patient here."  Another excla
2 CONC imed, "Sister Estelle is a Saint!  She was so good to my mother when she was ill.  She could think of so many little things to do to make her comfortable, and ease her suffering."  Many of the nurses, particularly those who worked
2 CONC  on the night shift with Sister, after having knelt and prayed for her, turned and said " My how Sister will be missed.  She was always there when you needed her.  I don't know what we will do at night without her."  others came i
2 CONC n, gazed at Sister, knelt and prayed for the repose of her soul, and then unable to control their grief left quickly without saying a word.  Pages, however, could be written on all that had been said concerning her goodness and ki
2 CONC ndness towards others.  Sister also received numerous Mass offerings and prayers.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Monday morning Sister's remains were taken over to St. Patrick's Church, where a Solemn Requiem Mass was celebrated for the repose of her soul.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Sanctuary were three Monsignors, and two Priests, friends of Sister Estelle.  The body of the Church was filled with Sisters of Charity, Sisters of other Communities, Doctors, nurses and others who knew and loved our dear S
2 CONC ister Estelle.  The Sisters sang the Mass, Sister was then laid to rest in St. Patrick's Cemetery near many of her old friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Sister Estelle appeared before Our Lord, and He presented her with her well earned crown as He greeted her with the words, "Come thou blessed of my Heart, for I was hungry, and you gave me to eat, sick and you visited me, etc
2 CONC .  We can picture her joy and happiness as she quickly replied with a smile, "Lord, now that my work on earth is done,  And my new work in Heaven's begun May I forget the Crown I've won While thinking still of Others." 
2 CONT
2 CONT  Newspaper Clippings 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Estelle, Hospital Staff Nun, Dies in Sleep   Sister Estelle, night supervisor and member of the staff of St. Joseph's Hospital since 1921. died in her sleep Friday afternoon at the Hospital.  She was 70.
2 CONT
2 CONT Earlier in the day she had visited two funeral homes where she had offered her sympathy to families of persons who had died.  In her 42 years at St. Joseph's Sister Estelle was known for her unfailing devotion to patients, and aft
2 CONC er she became night supervisor, she maintained her personal touch with people in the hospital.
2 CONT
2 CONT Every night she visited every room in the hospital to comfort patients.  She often gave them religious medals and holy pictures.  In addition to her other pressing duties, Sister Estelle always was in or near the room of a patient
2 CONC  in danger of death, offering prayers and comforting the families.  
2 CONT
2 CONT  Personal Touch. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When she was in charge of the operating room and X-ray dept. at the hospital, she managed her time so that she could visit the patients and maintained a personal touch. Sister Estelle had suffered from a cardiac condition for some
2 CONC  time, and her superior at the hospital had wanted to lighten her work, but it was Sister Estelle's wish to continue with her full time duties. Sister Estelle was born in Grand Coteau La. Apr. 15, 1893.  Her parents were the late 
2 CONC Mr. + Mrs. John Smith.  She attended Sacred Heart Academy in Louisiana, and received her nursing and X-Ray training at St. Joseph's Hospital where she had been since she made her profession, and entered the Daughters of Charity of
2 CONC  St. Vincent de Paul at Marrilac. She was the oldest nun at St. Joseph's in length of service.  Two sisters, Mrs. Electa Richard, + Mrs. Zita Fox, both of Grand Coteau, Louisiana survive her. Funeral services will be Mon. at 9:30 
2 CONC in St. Patrick's Church, followed by burial in St. Patrick's Cemetery. The body will be in the reception lounge, on the first floor of the hospital where visiting hours will be after 7: P.M. The Rosary will be recited at 7:30 P.M.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Hundreds at Bier of Nun Sister Estelle 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hundreds of Altonians visited the bier of Sr. Estelle Sun. at St. Joseph's Hospital, and St. Patrick's Church was filled to capacity this morning for the Solemn Requiem High Mass. sister Estelle who had been on the staff of the Ho
2 CONC spital for 42 years, was known to hundreds of former patients at the Hospital.  Some of the visitors to the Hospital arrived as early as 5 am Sun. and the crowd continued until 10:P.M. The Rev. Fr. Frank Westhoff, hospital Chaplai
2 CONC n was celebrant of the Mass: the Rev. Fr. John Spreen, deacon and the Rev. Fr. Louis Sondgeroth was sub-deacon. In the Sanctuary were the Rev. Fathers John J. Crosson, Msgr. James Suddes, the Rev. Fr. William Croke, the Rev. Fr. J
2 CONC . Haggerty, and the Rev. Fr. William Brennan, St. Louis. Pallbearers were Dr. Donald Bottom, Dr. John Corbin, Dr. Ricard Worcester, Dr. Hubert Mc. Cuistion, John Goolsby, and Dave Saville. Student nurses from the hospital, in unif
2 CONC orm, formed an honor guard at the Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sweet Dreams, Sister 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Estelle, was a living symbol of Christian love applied through her work for an institution, but directed also intimately to the human beings her hospital cared for as individuals.  Not too many make this mark in life.  We c
2 CONC oncentrate on benefitting people through institutions and lose sight of the people, or we get so tied up with individuals that we flub our responsibility to the institutions that could broaden our influence. Sister Estelle managed
2 CONC  to accomplish the positive side of both.  Night supervisor of St. Joseph's Hospital, with heavy and intricate organizational responsibilities that could try the patience as well as consume the time, she yet kept herself open for 
2 CONC direct contact with every patient on a daily basis. Her sympathy with human suffers drove her to visit nightly every patient in the hospital to lend the comfort of her presence.  And she remained constantly nearby the rooms of tho
2 CONC se dangerously ill and near death, on the chance that her quick response might prolong life or grant opportunity for final comfort. Mercifully Sister Estelle was spared the long suffering some go through as death approaches. At. 7
2 CONC 0, and warned to take it easier, because of a heart condition, she continued at her customary pace.  And Friday afternoon, during her sleep after night duty, she slipped quietly away into the eternal dream that comes to us all.  F
2 CONC riday July 12, 1963 
2 CONT
2 CONT  She Tied an Urchin's Shoe.  Thank you for a fine editorial tribute to a great lady.  We who knew and worked with Sister Estelle at St. Joseph's have lost a dear friend. She was truly one of those rare human beings who could see G
2 CONC od's goodness in everyman. The day before she died, Sister Estelle was literally racing down the main lobby hall.  It was long past the time she should have been in bed, and she was, I suppose, trying to hide from some of the othe
2 CONC r Sisters who would have been quite stern about the late afternoon hours she was keeping after a long night work.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Estelle often kept these "late" hours and it was a secret many of us shared with her. But there she was:-her Rosary jingling, her habit making a little wisping sound as she moved along.  And all of a sudden she was down, ty
2 CONC ing the shoelace of some little urchin waiting for his grown-ups in the hall. It was a menial task, but done with the same love Sister did all the menial and great tasks she was asked to do. That was the last time I saw her, and t
2 CONC hat is how I will remember her, serving others, the great and small-with love. Around our department we have many things to remember. Sister Estelle by:-Medals she was forever distributing.  Sister Estelle's Traveling Pharmacy Box
2 CONC , her holy cards, a bottle of medicine she never picked up, a time sand-glass she always turned over and "said a prayer for the sick' for every grain of sand that trickles through, a red paper heart with the inscription "Love of t
2 CONC he Blessed Mother" a particular practice she was to perform one day during the winter. Of all the things she left behind, nothing will be of greater tribute or will remind us of her more than all the people she served.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Boland Godfrey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alton, Illinois Sept. 9, 1963
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Mrs. Lavergne Our late and respected Sister Estelle has gone to her eternal reward, but her influence on earth will go on.  She will long be remembered by those of us who had the privilege of knowing her.  We, her religious f
2 CONC amily, wish to write an account of her beautiful yet simple life, for the edification of others who did not share this privilege. To do this, we need the assistance of her many friends and relatives; therefore, we are asking you t
2 CONC o write us regarding your contacts with her or anything you may have heard of her from others.  Incidents portraying her charity, kindness, goodness, fidelity, devotion to duty, faith etc., would be excellent.  If there were incid
2 CONC ents of human frailty, these too would be helpful in portraying her life and the efforts she made to become the beautiful soul that she was. We shall greatly appreciate any information that you can give us. Sincerely Sister Paulin
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Remarks on Sister Estelle Smith, who died July 12, 1963 St. Joseph's Hospital, Alton Ill. 70 years of Age. 43 years of Vocation. In one of his conferences to his daughters, St. Vincent recalled the words of Pope Clement VII. "Brin
2 CONC g me a member of a religious order of women who has persevered in obedience to her rules, let me have sufficient evidence of the fact, and I will ask fro no other evidence of her sanctity." If Sister Estelle was frequently proclai
2 CONC med a saint by those who came in contact of her, her fidelity to the spirit of our Holy Rules merited for her this supreme eulogy. Devotion to the Sacred Heart, which characterized her life, found its fulfillment in 'the principal
2 CONC  end for which God has called and established the Daughters of Charity-to honor our Lord Jesus Christ as the Source and Model of all Charity...'
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years. Her devotion to the Sacred Heart took root and increased in the fervent atmosphere of a truly Christian home.  Marie Louise Smith, as she was called, was born Apr. 15, 1893, the youngest of ten children: six girls and
2 CONC  four boys, of Alexander Smith and Estelle Cavelier. When she was only two years old, the family joined in a Novena to the Sacred Heart for her recovery from a serious illness, and the favor was obtained in her complete restoratio
2 CONC n to health. She received her early education from the Madames of the Sacred Heart, at Sacred Heart School, in Grand Coteau, La.  It was in this parish that she received her First Holy Communion and the Sacrament of Confirmation i
2 CONC n 1903. Memories of Sister' years preceding the entrance into the Community are recalled by her two sisters, Zita and Electa, several nieces and a cousin.  Her devoted cousin relates the following:- Marie Louise's mother was from 
2 CONC a very fine family from New Orleans, La. She was a sweet, soft spoken, but positive person.  Her father, 'Judge Smith,' as he was lovingly called by many, was my mother's oldest brother. An Aunt, once said to me in speaking of him
2 CONC , to see that man riding a horse, or following a plow, you would immediately know that he was every inch a gentleman.'  Her home, like so many of ours, was a humble one, but it was rich in other ways, for in it love and harmony pr
2 CONC evailed.  Marie Louise had many admirers and she shared and enjoyed our simple pleasures, such as dancing and partying.  I had the privilege of being not only her cousin but her friend and confidante. She told me of her intention 
2 CONC to enter the Convent long before she could summon the courage to tell her family. she also told me the reason why; this I very much doubt she told to anyone else.  I do think she had the Vocation at that time, but I am sure that G
2 CONC od in his goodness, Who was is never outdone in generosity, gave her the Vocation, and the strength to follow it, as there were many obstacles and heartaches to surmount. The positive characteristics of her mother remarked above p
2 CONC layed a prominent role in the development of God's plan for His future spouse.  the vigilance this good mother exercised over her youngest child's social tendencies preserved Marie Louise from steps that might otherwise prove detr
2 CONC imental to her future happiness. At one time we find Marie Louise at her married sister's home, weeping constantly in writing a letter, according to her mother's wishes, to break up relations with a young man whom her mother judge
2 CONC d unsuitable because of differences in Faith, and insufficient means.  A few years later we find her penning the following lines to her cousin while considering seriously the Vocation of the Religious life: I think the girls are h
2 CONC aving a delightful time; they have asked me to join them, but I haven't yielded to the temptation yet.-No, I couldn't call it temptation.  I haven't the slightest desire to attend their dances, and so on.  God meant for all of us 
2 CONC to be happy; it just depends on ourselves whether we are to obey Him and be happy or not.  In preparing to respond to the call of God, Marie Louise displayed a determination in her character which was to remain a stimulus in her e
2 CONC fforts to attain the spiritual perfection required of her, but which would also prove detrimental to her peace of soul in later years in trying to relinquish her own desires to the wishes of those placed over her.  The notes submi
2 CONC tted by those who knew Sister in her early years will enable us to trace a few of her characteristics which flowered into the predominant virtues of her later life. A niece recalls the following memories of her in the midst of her
2 CONC  family:- I distinctly remember how sweet and good she always was to everyone.  She loved all her family, but especial had a great love, respect and deep loyalty to her parents.  After the four boys grew up and left home, and thre
2 CONC e of the girls married, the three remaining girls divided the home and garden chores.  Marie Louise never complained of her lot, and had such a keen sense of duty and fair play, that she would never consent to leave her chores for
2 CONC  either of her sisters to do.  She had suitors besides Our Lord:-they soon realized she had one above them.  Her greatest joy came from doing for others.  The reason she did not heed the Call of Our Lord sooner, was due to her dev
2 CONC otion to her aging parents in whom she took a special interest."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the latter part of 1919 Marie Louise took the first step in accomplishing her long cherished desire of consecrating herself to God by writing the late Sister Stanislaus Malone, Sister Servant of Charity Hospital, New Orleans, L
2 CONC a.  Arrangements were made for an interview, the outcome of which is contained in the following letter received and preserved by her devoted cousin: My dear Gertrude, Today brought "my answer" which I have been waiting for to comm
2 CONC unicate to you.  At last, after three years of real effort, and five or six years of consideration, every thing is arranged for me to enter the postulatum in a very few days; so few that they seem to pass like minutes, when I thin
2 CONC k that each is the last.  Gertrude, you know that I have always spoken most frankly to you, and now I do not shrink from telling you how hard is my ordeal.  Forgetting myself, this is a sore trial to my darling Mama and Daddy and 
2 CONC Marie. Of course, the other dear members of my family will feel it too; they feel it keenly, bit I think it will be hardest for them.  Mamma and Daddy will at least, give me up with no hope of seeing me again.  Think what this mea
2 CONC ns to tow so entirely devoted to their child.  For them especially.  I claim your most fervent prayers, and for Marie that she may always be able to care for them and make them happy in the evening of their lives;--lives spent so 
2 CONC untiringly for us.  It is for this I ask you to pray-yes, even offer a Holy Communion on Sun., if you can.  I am going to ask all the family to receive with me on that day.  It seems strange that it has always been "my part" to wo
2 CONC und those whom I love most, and it has always been so.  Perhaps God is preparing me to love His suffering poor who are most wounded. I believe that in spite of the cruel wrench my poor heart shall feel in parting with those I love
2 CONC  so well, I shall be happy: decidedly so-a happiness born of doing one's duty.  And I have reason to believe that God has called me to that special field of duty.  His love and mercy for me fleeing from Him for six years, is evide
2 CONC nce that He needs me and by His Grace I will go to Him without regret.  I am not forced to this; it is my choice, my favor from Jesus Himself.  And I trust that in His generosity, God will make happy, those whom I have made unhapp
2 CONC y.  May he bless you dear, and re-unite us when we have done our life work and have gone Home to rest Louise." On Jan. 18, 1920, Marie Louise left home to begin her postulatum at Guardian Angel Settlement, St. Louis, Mo.  Sister r
2 CONC ecalls these first days of her community life a months before her death, by the following lines inher note book:-Feb. 11, 1963-Our Lady of Lourdes, Lady Immaculate, 43 years ago today you obtained for me the blessing of restored h
2 CONC ealth (hardly hoped for) that I might follow the call to my Holy Vocation." Uh, obtain for me now an ever increasing love for that sublime vocation, deeper gratitude, and above all, true repentance for my many failings." From othe
2 CONC r sources we learn that Sister had typhoid and was unconscious for sometime.  She had received the Last Sacraments, as she was not expected to live.  Apparently, her room was near the Chapel, as her first recollection of regaining
2 CONC  consciousness was the singing of the benediction hymns in the Chapel.  She soon resumed her former activities, and was ready to enter the Seminary in May. If we may judge this first year of her community life by her fidelity 43 y
2 CONC ears later to the customs then learned, we may assume that her seminary was very fervent and left a lasting impression on a soul so docile to its teachings.  This fact is verified by a Sister who was placed under Sister Smiths car
2 CONC e upon her arrival in the Seminary, and who was particularly impressed by her spirit of mortification in the discipline encountered in the everyday routine, and which she tried to implant in the heart of her new companion during t
2 CONC he heat of summer.  Sister seems up her account of these early days, by the following statement:- Even tho it is hardtop really know one in the Seminary one can really detect virtue by its simplicity and constancy.  This simplicit
2 CONC y and constancy, I understand, followed her life in the practice of virtue." The sentiments and resolutions repeated so often in Sister's private notebook, are evidence of this constancy which strengthened her virtue:- "I renew th
2 CONC e resolution taken at the Crib in the Seminary" is noted frequently, the substance of which is known only to God, to whom the secrets of hearts are revealed. After eight months in the Seminary, Sister received the Holy Habit on Ja
2 CONC n. 24, 1921.  Like the great Apostle, St. Paul, Sister could truly say, "By God's grace I am what I am, and His grace in me has not been in vain,"  as she left the seminary the following day for St. Joseph's Hospital, Alton Illino
2 CONC is, which was to be her "special field of duty" (as she had expressed her Vocation in her farewell letter to her cousin) for the remainder of her life. St. Joseph's Hospital, Alton, Ill. After completing her professional training 
2 CONC as a Registered Nurse, and later as X-ray Technician at St. Joseph's, Sister held various supervisory positions in the hospital, principally in the operating room and X-Ray departments, and in the last years of her life as Night S
2 CONC upervisor.  Nothing extraordinarily distinguished Sister in performance of her daily assignments; one had to live in close contact with her to experience the charm of her virtue.  May Sister Estelle forgive us for opening her priv
2 CONC ate notebook from time to time in compiling these notes, and disclosing some of the contents; in these we see the daily efforts her soul, apparently so serene had to make to acquire that virtue which was the radiance of a habit ac
2 CONC quired, and the authentic proof of a deep Super Natural Spirit.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of St. Catherine Laboure's contemporaries fittingly remarked of her "Love of God is the summary of life" Sister Estelle's predominant virtue of Charity is likewise depicted in the many notes on her dedicated life.  Let us revi
2 CONC ew the various ways in which this Capital virtue influenced the lives of those who daily came in contact with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through Love, she sought the Flory of God. During her Annual Retreat Aug 1921, apparently the first one she made after the reception of the Holy Habit, the following resolution was jotted down in her private notebook.  "To attain 
2 CONC closer union with our Lord, I will make a Spiritual Communion at the beginning of each exercise-to let no unkind word go unpunished-always to act for God alone."  From a Sister who postulated at St. Joseph's at this period of her 
2 CONC life, we have the following testimony of the sincerity of her efforts.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I postulated at St. Joseph's Hospital Alton, Ill.  Sister Estelle was the youngest Sister on the mission, in fact, Sister had just come from the Seminary.  I admired her goodness from the start.  I felt then that her virtue was o
2 CONC utstanding after a few days of Postulatiem, Sister Servant gave me some collars to hem.  One day when I was alone in the Community Room, Sister Estelle came in and sweetly said, "You can gain a great deal of merit just hemming col
2 CONC lars.  Let every stitch be a real prayer. You can ake them acts of love to Our Lord, acts of petition, or any kind of acts of virtue; your stitches will be real prayers, and will be pleasing to God."  So, from the start of my Comm
2 CONC unity life that is just what I did, but never without thinking about the good Sister who gave me this lesson in prayer.  I recall gracious memories of this dear Sister during my stay at St. Joseph's."
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Estelle's reflections 20 years later confirm the steadfastness of her purpose.  "Our Lord in His mercy gave me to understand that a virtue intended to please Him above all else and to glorify Him first, all other motives be
2 CONC ing secondary, to that, is sufficient to insure me against self seeking even though the actual Purity of intention does not always accompany my words and deeds. still, I should and I will strive with the help of grace to acquire t
2 CONC he holy habit of seeking God and His glory in each of my actions, and in all that I do for Him. And I make this intention now, thanking God for this grace."          
2 CONT
2 CONT  To be continued and the above needs to be proof read.  Inscription: Died 1963
