2 CONC es. Bernard became the chief of the branch of Gaudets who perpetuated in thisplace at the same time with the descendants of his uncle Pierre Gaudet dit le Jeune, his neighbor.
2 CONT Pierre l'Ainé settled at Beaubassin,≤ref> Tim Hebert; Transcriptionof the 1698 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie[http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1698cens.htm 1698 Census Transcribed]. The original census 
2 CONC can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 110-166≤blockquote>Pierre GODET 46; A
2 CONC nne BLANCHARD (wife)53; Pierre 23; Claude 21; Abraham 19; Mie, and Madeleine, twins, 16; Jeanne 14; Augustin 12; 10 cattle, 4 hogs, 10 arpents,≤/blockquote>≤/ref>≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1700 Acadian Census, at Port-R
2 CONC oyal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1700cens.htm 1700 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census micro
2 CONC film C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 167-173.≤blockquote>Pierre GODET 48; Anne BLANCHARD (wife) 55; Claude 23, Ahraham 22; Augustin 14; Marie and Madelaine (twins) 18; Jeanne 16
2 CONC ; 1 cow, 1 hog, 4 arpents, 2 guns.≤/blockquote>≤/ref> where he expanded the most vast marshland on the east coast of North America.{{Citation Needed}} He built his home on the hill beside the Tintamarre (today Tantramar) river. Al
2 CONC ong with Pierre Thibodeau, who founded the nearby Chepoudy settlement, Pierre l'aîné defied the claim of the seigneur of Chignecto, Michel Le Neuf de la Valliere et de Beaubassin, that the Chepoudy and Petitcoudiac settlements wer
2 CONC e part of his seigneury.  Gaudet and Thibodeau won their case before the colonial council at Port-Royal. Their defiance of a powerful Acadian seigneur helped weaken the hold of the local nobility on these sturdy Acadian habitants.
2 CONC
2 CONT The family returned to Port-Royal some time between 1700 and 1703 because they are listed in the census of 1703 of Port-Royal. ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1703 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.root
2 CONC sweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1703cens.htm 1703Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archiv
2 CONC es of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 212-220.≤blockquote>at Port-Royal: Pierre GODET, the eldest, his wife, 1 boy, 2 girls.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT They returned to Beaubassin after 1703.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1707 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1707cens.htm 1707 Census Transcription]. The original c
2 CONC ensus can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 221-237.≤blockquote>at Beaubassi
2 CONC n: Pierre GODET, Anne GUERIN (sic), 1 boy 14 or older; 8 arpents, 12 cattle, 5 hogs.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1714 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/g
2 CONC enealogy/1714cens.htm 1714 Census Transcription]. The original census canbe found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Re
2 CONC censements 1671 – 1752”, Images 239-.≤blockquote>at Beaubassin:  Pierre GODET and Anne BLANCHARD.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT :"Annapolis Royal, 
2 CONT :2d May 1720 O.S. 
2 CONT :At a Council held at the Honble. Lieut. Governor's house in His Majesty's garrison of Annapolis Royal upon Wednesday the 4th May 1720. 
2 CONT
2 CONT :PRESENT :His Excellency the General, the Honble. Lieut. Governor Major Lawrence Armstrong, Major Paul Mascarene, The Revd. John Harrison, Esq., Cyprian Southack, Esq., Arthur Savage, Esq., Hibbert Newton, Esq., WilliamSkene, Esq.
2 CONC , William Shirreff, Esq. 
2 CONT :The six French representatives (who appeared according to order) of the inhabitants of this river, being sent for in before the Council, were demanded to shew their power from said inhabitants who delivered intheir power in writi
2 CONC ng which was read. 
2 CONT :Their qualifications as freeholders were examined vizt. Prudent Robichaux, Alexander Robichaux, Nicolas Gautier, Barnard Gaudet, Charles Landre, and Pierre Gaudet. Nicolas Gautier not proving that he was a freeholder of this Prov
2 CONC ince only a transient person was excepted against,likewise Prudent Robichaux having but a slender propriety in the saidProvince was also excepted against. 
2 CONT
2 CONT :Advised and agreed, 
2 CONT :That another order in writing be sent to the French inhabitants of this river from His Excellency the General to choose two other persons in the room of said Prudent Robichaux and Nicolas Gautier to serve as representatives of th
2 CONC e inhabitants of this river with Alexander Robichaux, Barnard Gaudet, Charles Landre, and Pierre Gaudet. That Major PaulMascarene draw up said order for his Excellency's perusal and signing."≤ref>Nova Scotia Archives ...: Selectio
2 CONC ns from the public documents of the province of Nova Scotia. Pub. under a resolution of the House of Assembly passed March 15, 1865. Ed. by T.B. Akins. The translations from the French by B. Curren. 1869Volume 1 de Nova Scotia Arc
2 CONC hives, Nova Scotia. Commissioner of Public Records. Collaborateurs	Thomas Beamish Akins, Nova Scotia. Commissioner of Public Records Éditeur	C. Annand, 1869Original provenant de	la New York Public Library.  p. 24-25 [https://books
2 CONC .google.ca/books?id=JbgTAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA24&lpg=PA24&dq=Annapolis+Royal,+2d+May+1720+O.S.+At+a+Council+held+at+the+Honble.+Lieut.+Governor%27s+house+in+His+Majesty%27s+garrison+of+Annapolis+Royal+upon+Wednesday+the+4th+May+1720.&sour
2 CONC ce=bl&ots=jM4sNUUI88&sig=ACfU3U1MYyWjZ0mfIE_jCqxOxMHoyEpkBQ&hl=fr&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwj8m-aKmPXlAhWCslkKHYHDCNUQ6AEwAHoECAkQAQ#v=onepage&q=Annapolis%20Royal%2C%202d%20May%201720%20O.S.%20At%20a%20Council%20held%20at%20the%20Honble.%20
2 CONC Lieut.%20Governor's%20house%20in%20His%20Majesty's%20garrison%20of%20Annapolis%20Royal%20upon%20Wednesday%20the%204th%20May%201720.&f=false Google Books]≤/ref> 
2 CONT Stephen White gives the same date of death for both brothers. In the document "37 families"≤ref name=37families/> it is written that Pierre L'Aîné died on 8 December 1741 whereas in the Dictionnaire, this is Pierre Le Jeune's date
2 CONC  of death but he remains silent concerning L'Aîné.≤ref name=DGFA/> It is presumed that he died after the 1714 censusof Beaubassin since he does not appear in any record after that.≤ref>Karen Theriot Reader [https://gw.geneanet.org
2 CONC /katheriot?lang=en&pz=frederick+joseph&nz=theriot&ocz=1&p=pierre+l+aine&n=gaudet Pierre L'AinéGaudet]≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biographie==[[Gaudet-17|Pierre Gaudet l'aîné]], né vers 1652, prit pour femme, vers 1672, Anne Blanchard, fille de Jean Blanchard et de Radegonde Lambert, et veuve de François Guérin. Il demeurait à Port-Royal, où il est décédé 
2 CONC  soit le 8 décembre 1741 ou après le recensement de 1714. "≤ref name=37families>Stephen A. White, "La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des 'Retrouvailles ‘94'" in ''Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne,'' vol
2 CONC . 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994) GAUDET, p. 1, [https://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/node/55 37 Families]≤/ref>≤ref name=DGFA/> 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Sources==
2 CONT ≤references />
