1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Sources:'''
2 CONT **1671 Acadie Census - shown to be 16 years old which estimates birth c.1655.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT • Biography: Pierre settled at Saint-Joseph of Riviere-aux-Canards, at Grand Pre'. At the age of 16 in 1671, he was still living with his parents. Pierre married around 1685 [sic] to Ce'cile LANDRY, daughter of Rene' & Marie BERNA
2 CONC RD; they had no children. At the age of 23, Pierre was married and living with his wife, Cecile in Port Royal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1682 Pierre TERRIOT, of Port-Royal, founded a colony on the Saint-Antoine River not far from Pierre MELANSON's settlement at Grand-Pre', when he was age 26. Being a popular and generous man he supplied wheat without interest, a
2 CONC nd housed many while their homes were being built. He was followed by many others to the new community. In other biographies, "The chief founder of Minas was a rich inhabitant of Port Royal, Pierre Terriau, who probably settled on
2 CONC  Habitant River." 
2 CONT
2 CONT His nephew Mathieu de GOUTIN said of him: "Pierre Theriot['s] wife embraces two thirds of the colony." In a letter dated 9 Sep 1694 he also said that TERRIOT "is the most notable person at Les Mines, of which he is so to speak the
2 CONC  founder, for he has assisted almost all those who have come to establish themselves there, and his house is the refuge of all widows and orphans and people in need." Having no children of his own, four or five of his nephews live
2 CONC d with him "until such time as their own dwelling was habitable." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Next to Pierre MELANCON, age 54 with 50 arpents, this was the largest holding. Martin AUCOIN, age 35, was next with 15 arpents. There were only 10 households, with 57 people, in the community in 1686. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1703, he is listed as capable of bearing arms. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In documents from 1711-1712, Pierre TERRIOT is listed in requests for payment of sums due the inhabitants of Acadia for goods delivered to various warring parties against the British which were led by Baron de St. CASTIN and de CL
2 CONC INGANCOURT during the war of 1711-1712. He claimed 28 pounds for food and other items; he and Germain TERRIOT [not his brother, who had already died, but the nephew born in 1662?] claimed 114 pounds for a canoe and supplies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1714, he is listed as living alone with his wife. He was buried at Saint-Charles-des-Mines on 22 Mar 1725. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1678 Census
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre TERRIOT Cecile LANDRY
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 acres 4 cattle
2 CONT
2 CONT 1693 Census
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre TERRIOT 39, Cecile LANDRY 29, 2 servants; 22 cattle, 8 sheep, 8 pigs, 16 arpents, 1 gun 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Pierre settled at Saint-Joseph of Riviere-aux-Canards, at Grand Pre'. At the age of 16 in 1671, he was still living with his parents. Pierre married around 1685 [sic] to Ce'cile LANDRY, daughter of Rene' & Marie BERNARD; they had 
2 CONC no children. At the age of 23, Pierre was married and living with his wife, Cecile in Port Royal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1682 Pierre TERRIOT, of Port-Royal, founded a colony on the Saint-Antoine River not far from Pierre MELANSON's settlement at Grand-Pre', when he was age 26. Being a popular and generous man he supplied wheat without interest, a
2 CONC nd housed many while their homes were being built. He was followed by many others to the new community. In other biographies, "The chief founder of Minas was a rich inhabitant of Port Royal, Pierre Terriau, who probably settled on
2 CONC  Habitant River." 
2 CONT
2 CONT His nephew Mathieu de GOUTIN said of him: "Pierre Theriot['s] wife embraces two thirds of the colony." In a letter dated 9 Sep 1694 he also said that TERRIOT "is the most notable person at Les Mines, of which he is so to speak the
2 CONC  founder, for he has assisted almost all those who have come to establish themselves there, and his house is the refuge of all widows and orphans and people in need." Having no children of his own, four or five of his nephews live
2 CONC d with him "until such time as their own dwelling was habitable." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Next to Pierre MELANCON, age 54 with 50 arpents, this was the largest holding. Martin AUCOIN, age 35, was next with 15 arpents. There were only 10 households, with 57 people, in the community in 1686. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1703, he is listed as capable of bearing arms. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In documents from 1711-1712, Pierre TERRIOT is listed in requests for payment of sums due the inhabitants of Acadia for goods delivered to various warring parties against the British which were led by Baron de St. CASTIN and de CL
2 CONC INGANCOURT during the war of 1711-1712. He claimed 28 pounds for food and other items; he and Germain TERRIOT [not his brother, who had already died, but the nephew born in 1662?] claimed 114 pounds for a canoe and supplies. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1714, he is listed as living alone with his wife. He was buried at Saint-Charles-des-Mines on 22 Mar 1725. 
