2 CONT === Marriage ===
2 CONT
2 CONT : Husband:  [[Theriot-188|Pierre Theriot]]
2 CONT : Wife:  [[Landry-176|Cecille Landry]]
2 CONT : Marriage:  
2 CONT :: Date:  ABT 1678
2 CONT :: Place:  Port Royal, , , Acadia≤ref name="DGFA1488" />
2 CONT
2 CONT : Note:  from 1678 Port Royal Census
2 CONT :: Pierre Terriot & Cecile Landry-4 acres   4 cattle
2 CONT :: Source: [[#S68]]
2 CONT : Note:  fr 1693 Port Royal Census:: Pierre TERRIOT 39, Cecile LANDRY 29, 2 servants; 22 cattle, 8 sheep, 8 pigs, 16 arpents, 1 gun
2 CONT :: Source: [[#S70]]
2 CONT Burial/Source: Inhumé le 22 mars 1725 à Grand-Pré, Acadie.  Church publication says he died on the 24th ≤ref>Una F. Daigre and John J. Pastorek, Archivist; Editor, The Registers of St. Charles aux Mines in Acadia 1707-1748, Dioces
2 CONC e of Baton Rouge Catholic Church Records, Vol. 1a (Baton Rouge, LA: Catholic Diocese of Baton Rouge, 1999), p.187. Text: Pierre Terio, age 60, bur. 22 March 1725, d. 24 (sic) March 1725, wit. none given (SGA-2, 164)≤/ref>, while S
2 CONC tephen A. White says the 21st.≤ref name="DGFA1488" />
2 CONT ===Research Notes===::: Note:  !BIRTH-PARENTS-MARRIAGE-DEATH-BURIAL: Stephen A. White, DICTIONNAIRE GENEALOGIQUE DES FAMILLES ACADIENNES; 1636-1714 ; Moncton, New Brunswick, Centre d'Etudes Acadiennes, 1999 , 2 vols.; p. 1489; own
2 CONC  copy. #6: Pierre THÉRIOT, son of Je an & Perrine RAU, born around 1654, coloniser, judge. He di ed/was buried (Grand Pré Register) on 21/22 Mar 1725 [sic] , at age 60 years (sic).
2 CONT ::: ::: !BIRTH-PARENTS-MARRIAGE-RESIDENCES-BURIAL: Bona Arsenault , HISTOIRE ET GENEALOGIE DES ACADIENS, vols. 1-6; 1630-1775 ; Quebec, Le Conseil de la Vie Francaise en Amerique, 1965 ; pp. 805 (Port Royal) & 1293(Grand Pré); own
2 CONC  copy . Enter ed under THÉRIAULT et THE'RIOT, his name is spelled Pierr e TERRIAU, born 1655, son of Jean & Perrine BOURG[sic] o f Port Royal; he settled at Saint-Joseph of Riviere-aux-Can ards, at Grand Pré. Pierre married around
2 CONC  1685 [sic] to Céc ile LANDRY, daughter of René & Marie BERNARD; they had no c hildren. He was, with Pierre MELANSON, the co-founder of Gr and-Pré. He lived at Riviere-aux-Canards. He was buried a t Saint-Charles-des-Mines on 22 M
2 CONC ar 1725.
2 CONT ::: ::: !RESIDENCES: Bona Arsenault, HISTORY OF THE ACADIANS; 1600- 1800; Ottawa, Lemeac, 1978; p. 53; own copy. In 1682 Pierr e TERRIOT, of Port-Royal, founded a colony on the Saint-Ant oine River not far from Pierre MELANSON's s
2 CONC ettlement at Gra nd-Pré, when he was age 26. Being a popular and generous ma n he supplied wheat without interest, and housed many whil e their homes were being built. He was followed by many oth ers to the new community.
2 CONT ::: ::: !BIRTH-PARENTS-MARRIAGE-RESIDENCES-DEATH: DICTIONARY OF CAN ADIAN BIOGRAPHY; 1701-1740; vol. 2, Univ. of Toronto Press , 1969; p. 625; Sutro California State Library, F1005 D49 v .2; contains 44 Acadian biographies. Pierre
2 CONC  TERRIOT (he sig ned thus), one of the founders of LesMines (Grand Pré, Nov a Scotia), was last-born in 1654 or 1655 of the seven child ren of Jehan & Perrine RUAU (or BAU), and husband of Cécil e LANDRY, daughter of Rene' & Marie
2 CONC  BERNARD. His nephew Mat hieu de GOUTIN said of him: "Pierre Theriot['s] wife embrac es two thirds ofthe colony." In a letter dated 9 Sep 169 4 he also said that TERRIOT "is the most notable person a t Les Mines, of which he is so
2 CONC  to speak the founder, for h e has assisted almost all those who have come to establis h themselves there, and his house is the refuge of all wido ws and orphans and people in need." Having no children of h is own, four or five of
2 CONC  his nephews lived with him "until s uch time as their own dwelling was habitable." (Reference : AN, Col., B, 17, f.23v; C11D, 2, F.233.)
2 CONT ::: ::: !CHARACTER: From www.Acadian-home.org. Article by Sally Ros s & Susan Surette-Draper, "St-Charles-des-Mines Cemetery i n Grand-Pré." The Thériot family... "played an important ro le in the Minas region. Pierre Thériot and 
2 CONC his wife Cécil e Landry had the reputation of being very welcoming. Havin g no children of their own, they offered lodging and suppor t to the young men who came to the Grand-Pré area to develo p their lands and build their homes 
2 CONC before relocating thei r families. Ultimately, this practice led to a certain amou nt of gossip, which eventually involved the parish priest , the governor of the colony, and even the bishop in Quebec ! "
2 CONT ::: ::: !EDUCATION: DICTIONARY OF CANADIAN BIOGRAPHY ONLINE. "A ma n of adequate schooling--his signature appears in the paris h registers."
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1671, Port Royal, Acadia [p.5]. Listed as only unmarried child of Jehan TERRIAU & Perrinne RAU, living with t hem, age 16 years. Pierre's 6 siblings are married [one, ho wever, is not found presenton the census].
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1678, Clarence J. d'Entremont, "Recensement de Por t-Royal," in MEMOIRES DE LA SOCIETE GENEALOGIQUE CANADIENNE -FRANCAISE; vol.22, no. 4; p. 233; sent by PERSI in Jun 19 99. On Folio 20: Pierre TERRIOT & Cecille L
2 CONC ANDRY, with 4 ar pents of land, and 4 head of cattle.
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1678, Port Royal, Acadia; 1678; Clarence D'Entremo nt, "Rencensement de Port Royal, 1678 avec commentaires," i n MEMOIRES DE LASOCIETE GENEALOGIQUE CANADIENNE-FRANAISE , vol. XXII, No. 4 (Oct-Nov-Dec 1971), pp. 22
2 CONC 6-247, transla ted and corrected in "Genealogy and Archives: Census of Por t Royal, 1678" in FRENCH CANADIAN AND ACADIAN GENEALOGICA L REVIEW, vol. VII, No. 1 (Spring 1979), pp. 47-66. On p. 6 0 is the couple Pierre TERRIOT & Céci
2 CONC lle LANDRY, living o n 4 acres with 4 cattle. [He would have been about 23 year s old; she only 14 or so.]
2 CONT ::: ::: !MARRIAGE: LDS IGI gives both 1684 and 1685 as dates, wit h placesGrand Pré [Mines] or Port Royal. [Check sources.]
2 CONT ::: ::: !MARRIAGE: Janet B. Jehn, CORRECTIONS & ADDITIONS TO ARSENA ULT'S HISTOIRE ET GENEALOGIE DES ACADIENS; 1630-1830; by au thor, 863 WaymanBranch Road, Covington, KY 41015, 1988; p . 31 [for p. 1293 of Arsenault]. "Under Pier
2 CONC re TERRIAU, mar riage date should be about 1678, according to 1678 census o f Acadia."
2 CONT ::: ::: !RESIDENCES: John Frederic Herbin, THE HISTORY OF GRAND-PRE ', THEHOME OF LONGFELLOW'S "EVANGELINE"; 1504-1836; St. Jo hn, New Brunswick, Barnes, 1907, reprinted by Heritage Book s, 1991; p. 27; own copy. "The chief founde
2 CONC r of Minas wa s a rich inhabitant of Port Royal, Pierre Terriau, who prob ably settled on Habitant River about 1675." [But Pierre wou ld have been only 20 years old at the time.]
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1686, Les Mines, Acadia, name given as Philippe [s ic], age 32 years, married to Cecile LANDRY, age 22. In th e household was also a "domestique," Jacques MARTIN [who wa s he?]. They owned 1 gun, 16 arpents of wor
2 CONC kable land, 9 ho rned animals, and 8 pigs.[ Next to Pierre MELANCON, age 5 4 with 50 arpents, this was the largest holding. Martin AUC OIN, age 35, was next with 15 arpents. There were only 10 households, with 57 people, in the co
2 CONC mmunity in 1686.]
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1693, Les Mines, Acadia, age 39 years. There ar e 2 domestics; 22 cattle, 8 sheep, 8 hogs, on 16 arpents , 1 gun.
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1701, Riviere aux Canards, Acadia, 20 cattle, 6 sh eep, 12 hogs, on 9 arpents, 1 gun.
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1703, Les Mines, Acadia, without children; 1 perso n [adult male] capable of bearing arms.
2 CONT ::: ::: !CENSUS: 1707, Les Mines, Acadia, with 20 cattle, 14 sheep , 16 hogs, on 14 arpents.
2 CONT ::: ::: !MILITARY: Conrad W. Trahan, "A Closer Look at Documents o f the Period," in LE RÉVEIL ACADIEN; 1711-1712; vol. XIII , no. 4 (Nov 1997); p. 88; own copy. Pierre TERRIOT is list ed in requests for payment of sums due the in
2 CONC habitants of A cadia for goods delivered to various warring parties agains t the British which were led by Baron de St. CASTIN and d e CLINGANCOURT during the war of 1711-1712. He claimed 28 p ounds for food and other items; he an
2 CONC d Germain TERRIOT [no t his brother, who had already died, but the nephew born i n 1662?] claimed 114 pounds for a canoe and supplies.
2 CONT ::: 
2 CONT ::: !CENSUS: 1714, Les Mines, Acadia, living alone with his wif e.
2 CONT
