2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT It is presumed that Pierre, II arrived in Acadie with his father and unknown mother and sisters Catherine and Aimee. There has been no confirmation of this fact as of this time. Could Pierre be related to Martin LeJeune. Their nam
2 CONC es appear side by side in the 1686 census  of Cap de Sable. Pierre was 28 and Martin was 25.
2 CONT 1686 Pierre LeJeune dit Briars was 28 and his wife, Marie Tibaudeau, was 25. They had one arpent and 6 guns.
2 CONT 1693 census of Port Royal Pierre was 37 and his wife, Marien Tibeaudeau wqas 30. They had 3 children-Marie was 6, Pierre was 2, Jeanne was 2. They had 8 arpents, 8 cattle, 12 sheep, 6 hogs and 2 guns.
2 CONT 1698 census of Port Royal-Pierre was 48, Marie Tibaudeau, his wife, was 37. They had 6 children- Marie was 12, Pierre 9, Jeanne 8, Germain 5, Margueritte 3 and Jean 1. They had 10 1/2 arpents, 11 cattle, 14 sheep, 5 hogs, 10 fruit
2 CONC  trees and one gun.
2 CONT 1701 census - Pierre Briart age was shown as age 45, Marie Tibaudeau, his wife, was 34. They had 8 children-Pierre 13, Germain 9, Jean 6, marie 16, Jeanne 12, Marguerite 8, Anne 6 and Jacqueline (Catherine ?). They had 8 arpents, 
2 CONC 9 cattle, 15 sheep, 5 hogs and 1 gun.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to escape the hostile British, a number of LeJeune families left for Ile-Royale, present day Cape Breton, which was protected by the French fort at Louisbourg. The Acadians at this area were unaffected by the 1755 expulsi
2 CONC on by the British. After the fall of the for to the English during the summer of 1857 there was another round of deportations. There is no information as to how the children of the grandson of Martin LeJeune ended up in Port Tobac
2 CONC co Maryland but they did. These three orphans made it to Louisiana on the ship Britannia along with their aunt and her husband Honore Trahan. These three boys were creators of the South Louisiana LeJeune's.
2 CONT
2 CONT Info taken from The Advertiser, April 12, 1998.
