0 @I6000000002289938428@ INDI
1 NAME Charles /D'Amours/
2 GIVN Charles
2 SURN D'Amours
2 _MARNM D'Amours de Louvier
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 4 MAR 1662
2 ADDR
3 CITY Québec
3 STAE Colony of Canada
3 CTRY La Nouvelle-France [French Colonial North America]
1 BAPM
2 DATE 5 MAR 1662
2 ADDR
3 CITY Québec
3 STAE Colony of Canada
3 CTRY La Nouvelle-France [French Colonial North America]
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1 JAN 1716
2 PLAC At Sea
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Sieur de Louvieres
1 TITL Sieur
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.emcohanover.com/deserres2010/HODAmour.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.nosorigines.qc.ca/GenealogieQuebec.aspx?genealogie=Charles_D%27Amours&pid=7791&lng=fr
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 1698 Census
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles D'AMOURS 34; Anne-Marie TIBAUDEAU (wife) 16; 4 arpents, 1 gun, 1 servant. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-LOUVIERE-DAMOUR.htm Charles, born in March 1662, married Marie-Anne, daughter of François Genaple, at Québec in January 1688.  By the late 1690s he was living on a seigneurie on Riviè
2 CONC re St.-Jean, where his three older brothers had concessions.  This gave him the title of sieur de Louvières.  Marie-Anne gave him four children, including two sons who married into the Rouer de Villeray, Morel de La Durantaye, and
2 CONC  Renoyer families, all prominent in Canada.  His two sons also called themselves de Louvières.  In 1697, Charles remarried to Anne-Marie, daughter of Acadian Pierre Thibodeau, pioneer of the settlement at Chepoudy.  Anne-Marie gav
2 CONC e him 10 more children, including four sons who married into the Catalogne, Tonty, Couillard dit Despres, Pelletier, Boulogne, and Richaume families; two of Charles's sons by this second marriage also called themselves de Louvière
2 CONC s.  One of them, Pierre de Louvières, born at Ste.-Foy, near Québec, in February 1712, became a cadet in the troupes de la marine and was stationed at Illinois, part of upper Louisiana, in c1736, where he married the daughter of a
2 CONC  senior officer in c1743.  He settled at Prairie-du-Rocher, Illinois, died there in May 1768, age 56, and was buried in the church there.  Charles, meanwhile, drowned in 1716 at age 54, probably at Québec.  Most of Charles's child
2 CONC ren from both marriages were born at Québec and Ste.-Foy, so he probably did not reside for very long on his seigneurie at Rivière St.-Jean. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth son of Marie Marsolet, Charles Damours, sieur de Louvieres, seigneur du Lac Matapedia, baptised in Quebec March 5th 1662, married twice: First to Marie-Anne Genaple-Bellefond (daughter of Francois Grenaple)in Quebec Cit
2 CONC y in January 1683. They had two daughters and two sons:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Charles-Nicolas Damours, born in 1692, who married on May 20 1717 Angelique Rouer de Villeray and they had two daughters who entered a convent (the Hospitalieres: Mère St Stanislas and Mere St-Jean-Bte) and a son Joseph Damours
2 CONC  who married to Catherine Blondeau, but they did not have any children 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Jean Damours, married twice, first Marie-Anne Morel de la Durantaye, and they had several daughters and then in 1735 to Marie-Jeanne Renoyer, the widow of Louis Boucher- which formed the roots of the large Damours family from T
2 CONC rois-Pistoles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Marie-Anne Damours, born in 1688
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Francoise Damours born in 1689 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles D'Amours de Louvieres, Sieur de Matapedia's second marriage was done in Acadia in 1697 to Marie-Anne Thibodeau. They had 10 children. https://translate.google.com/translate?hl=en&sl=fr&u=http://www.familles-damours.org/wp-
2 CONC content/uploads/Chapitre-30.pdf&prev=search
