2 CONT **"The First Nominal Census (1769) of Colonial Settlers in Southwest Louisiana"; compiled by Donald Joseph Arceneaux; Provincial Press, Claitor's Publishing Division, Baton Rouge, Louisiana; www.ProvincialPress.us & www.Claitors.c
2 CONC om.
2 CONT **"The BROUSSARD dit Beausoleil Party to the Attakapas District, 1765" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***On list of Acadians who exchanged card money in New Orleans, Apr 1765, called Paul Thibodeau
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1766, District of the Pointe, listed as  Pablo Thibaudau
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1769, listed as Paul Tibodeau, age 36[sic]
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1771, age 40[sic]
2 CONT ***On Attakapas militia list, Jan 1773, called Paul Thibodeau
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1774, listed as Paul Tibodau
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1777, listed as Paul Thibodaut, age 48
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1781, listed as Paul Thibaudot
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1785, listed as Paul Thibaudot
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1788, Carancro, called Paul Thibaudot
2 CONT ***On Attakapas militia list, Aug 1789, called Pablo Tribodo
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana in Feb 1765, age 11
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul, son of Claude Thibodeau and Élisabeth, or Isabelle, Comeau, born at Port-Royal in July 1728, was still a bachelor when he escaped the British roundup in Nova Scotia in 1755.  He found refuge with other Acadians on the Gulf o
2 CONC f St. Lawrence shore but ended up as a prisoner of war in Nova Scotia in the early 1760s.  He married Rosalie, daughter of Joseph Guilbeau dit L'Officier of Port-Royal, probably at Halifax in c1763.  They followed her family and t
2 CONC he Broussard dit Beausoleil party to Louisiana via Cap-Français, St.-Domingue, in 1764-65.  Paul was one of the Acadians in that party who exchanged Canadian card money for Louisiana funds at New Orleans in April 1765.  He and Ros
2 CONC alie followed the Broussards to Bayou Teche that spring and remained there.  She was pregnant when she reached the colony and gave birth to their first son in August, but the baby died a week after his birth.  Paul and Rosalie set
2 CONC tled at La Pointe farther up the Bayou Teche and had many more children there, including sons.  Their daughters married into the Broussard and Trahan families.  Paul died "at his place at LaPointe" in September 1805; he was 77 yea
2 CONC rs old.  Half of his six sons created families of their own and settled in what became Lafayette and St. Martin parishes, but one of those lines died out early. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-THIBODEAUX.htm
2 CONT
