1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **"Acadian Immigrants to the Opelousas District, 1765" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1766
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1771, age 13(?)[sic, probably 23]
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1774
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1777, age 30
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1785
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1788, Bellevue
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1796, Bellevue District
2 CONT ***Successions dated Mar 1809 & 12 Mar 1810, St. Landry Parish courthouse
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana in 1765, age 18
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT A Patriot of the American Revolution for Louisiana.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A095788  DAR Ancestor # A095788]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-RICHARD.htm Acadians In Gray: Richard]
2 CONT
2 CONT Victor, younger son of of Alexandre Richard and Marie-Madeleine Thibodeau, born probably at Malpèque, Île St.-Jean, in c1747, escaped the British roundup on the island in 1758 and followed his family into exile on the Gulf of St. 
2 CONC Lawrence shore.  With them, he ended up as a prisoner in Nova Scotia in the early 1760s.  He was still in his teens when he came to Louisiana with his older brother Pierre in 1765.  He followed Pierre to the Opelousas District and
2 CONC  also settled on Bellevue prairie.  In 1774, still a bachelor, Victor owned 30 head of cattle on his vacherie at Bellevue.  He married Marie-Madeleine, daughter of fellow Acadians Cosme Brasseur dit Brasseaux and Élisabeth Thibode
2 CONC aux, probably at Opelousas later that year.  Victor's herd had increased to 70 head of cattle and nine horses by 1777, and in 1788 he owned 150 head of cattle and 29 horses, as well as three slaves.  His daughters married into the
2 CONC  Bijeaux, Cormier, Estilette, and Thibodeaux families.  Victor died in St. Landry Parish in September 1808, age 60; his succession records were filed at the Opelousas courthouse in March 1809 and March 1810.  His two older sons cr
2 CONC eated families of their own and settled in St. Landry Parish.  Some of his grandsons settled in Lafayette Parish. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
