2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT
2 CONT == Discussion =='''NOTICE''': this profile is protected by the [[Project:Acadians|Acadian Project]] because of frequent duplication, variant name spelling, attempts to add unsourced parents or is an historically important person, 
2 CONC and is in the Top 100 highly viewed Acadian profiles.  Please contact the Acadian Project before making any substantive changes. Thanks for helping make WikiTree the best site for accurate information.
2 CONT Why is Edmée called "the ancestral matriarch of the Acadian Gautrot family"? Isn't Marie Gautrot, daughter of Francois and his first wife (Unknown) also an ancestral matriarch of the Acadian Gautrot family?  Looking at Acadians bo
2 CONC rn roughly before 1763 (5 generations) Edmée hasabout 3100 people born in Acadia on Wikitree.  The first wife of Francois, Marie Unknown (who may or may not have come to Acadie) has 649 descendents in 5 generations).
2 CONT Edmée and Catherine Lejeunes parents are NOT KNOWN.  If you find solid evidence of their parents, please post on this profile in the comments.  It is often written that [[Lejeune-12|Pierre Lejeune]] was their father, but this is n
2 CONC ot known.
2 CONT '''Ethnic Origins.''' Some have speculated that Edmée had an Amerindian mother. The [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/MothersofAcadia/default.aspx Mothers of Acadia maternal DNA project] posts its ongoing Maternal DNA results 
2 CONC [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/mothersofacadia/default.aspx?section=mtresults '''here''']. To date, the haplogroup of both sisters is consistently reported as U6a7a, indicating European origins.
2 CONT Another group known as [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/AcadianAmerindian/default.aspxAMERINDIAN Ancestry Out of Acadia DNA PROJECT], posts its results [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/AcadianAmerindian/default.aspx?sect
2 CONC ion=mtresults here]. They too report that Catherine and Edmee have European haplogroups, in particular, basic testing has revealed U6a and more complete testing U6a7a.  Thus, there is a growing body of consistent and concordant re
2 CONC sults indicating European origins and nothing to the contrary.
2 CONT '''Birthplace'''. Regarding the Lejeune siblings' specific location ofbirth in France, there are no birth records.  Massignon≤ref name=mass>{{SA-GM1962|p=p32 (first French families in Acadia) p37 (Lejeune sisters' origins)}}≤/ref>
2 CONC  argues that a number of familial alliances existed among the first settlers of Acadia PRIOR to their arrival in Acadia, which points to a common French origin. She believes they lived in the Acadian Governor d'Aulnay's seigneury 
2 CONC in France near Loudon (comprising of the villages of Angliers, Aulnay, Martaizé and La Chausée). Regarding the Lejeune sisters, Massignon claims that they were allied with the Savoie through Catherine's marriage and the Gautrot th
2 CONC rough Edmee's marriage. It is not clear that the sisters married prior to their arrival in Acadia. Stephen White claims that Catherine married Savoie in Acadia.≤ref name="DGFA"/> Edméee's marriage around 1644 to Gautrot may have t
2 CONC aken place in Acadia as he was among the first settlers and was already a widower at the time of his marriage to Edmée and was definitely in Acadia prior to 1650≤ref name="DGFA"/>  (some claim 1636{{Citation Needed}}).
2 CONT D'Entremont in his "Histoire du Cap-Sable, 1763", states the first Lejeune and his French wife arrived in Acadia before or during the time of Isaac de Razilly. Isaac de Razilly was appointed Governor of Acadia in 1632 and died thr
2 CONC ee years later in 1635. D'Entremont implies that the Lejeunes arrived in Acadia sometime prior to 1636. D'Entremont alsonotes the first Lejeune couple had three children at the time of their arrival in Acadia: Aimee Lejeune, born 
2 CONC between 1622 & 1625, Pierre Lejeune, born after Aimee and before Catherine Lejeune, who was born about 1633. D'Entremont further states that Pierre Lejeune II married anAmerindienne. 
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT {{Migrating Ancestor
2 CONT | origin = France
2 CONT | origin-flag = Flags.png
2 CONT | destination = Acadia
2 CONT | destination-flag = Acadie-1.png
2 CONT }}Edmée Lejeune is the ancestral matriarch of the Acadian Gautrot family. She was born in France around 1624.≤ref name="DGFA">Dictionnaire Gnéalogique des Familles Acadiennes; Author: Stephen A. White; Publication: 2 vols., Moncto
2 CONC n, New Brunswick: Centre d'Études Acadiennes, 1999. p. 1048, 691-2≤/ref>The 1671 Port Royal Census lists Edmee's age as 47, which puts her birth at around 1624. She was the sister of [[Lejeune-11|Catherine Lejeune]] and [[Lejeune-
2 CONC 22 | Pierre II Lejeune]]. 
2 CONT Edmée and her siblings made their way to Acadia. Around 1644, Edmee married widower François Gautrot.≤ref name="DGFA" /> Between 1645 and 1668, the couple had nine children: Marie, Jean, Renée, Marguerite, François, Claude, Charle
2 CONC s, Jeanne and Germain.
2 CONT In 1654, the family would have witnessed Port-Royal's capture by [http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/sedgwick_robert_1E.html Robert Sedgwick], who led 300 British soldiers and volunteers.≤ref>[http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/sedgwick_r
2 CONC obert_1E.html William I. Roberts, 3rd, “SEDGWICK, ROBERT,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biography, vol. 1, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–, accessed November 20, 2013]≤/ref> The soldiers and employees working at the for
2 CONC t were offered transportation back to France and given enough pelts to cover their wages."≤ref name=Dunn>Dunn, Brenda. A History of Port Royal / Annapolis Royal 1605-1800. Nimbus Publishing, p 23-24(1654 Capture of Port-Royal); p2
2 CONC 5-27;29 (the English period 1654-1670).≤/ref> Most Acadians, including the Gautrot family, remained in Acadia. They were permitted to retain their land and belongings and were guaranteed religious freedom.≤ref name=Dunn/> Dunn des
2 CONC cribes life in Acadia during the 16 years of nominal British rule:
2 CONT :"During the years of British rule, most of the Port-Royal population moved upriver away from the town.  Using the agricultural practices initiated under D'Aulnay, the Acadians dyked and cultivated extensive salt marshes along the
2 CONC  river and raised livestock. Through necessity, residents had reached an accommodation with New England traders who had become their sole source for the goods that they could not produce themselves... New England traders exchanged
2 CONC  their goods for Acadian produce and furs... There were seventy to eighty families in the Port Royalarea in 1665." 
2 CONT In 1671 the family homestead had six arpents under cultivation and they had 16 cattle and 6 sheep.≤ref>[http://www.acadian-cajun.com/1671cens.htm 1671 Censes]≤blockquote>Port Royal:  Francois GAUTEROT, 58, wifeEdmee LeJEUNE 47; Ch
2 CONC ildren (married): Marie 35, Charles 34, Marie 24,Rene 19, Marguerite 16; (not married): Jean 23, Francois 19, Claude 12, Charles 10, Jeanne 7, Germain 3; cattle 16, sheep 6.≤/blockquote> ≤/ref> In 1678 the family was still in Port
2 CONC  Royal, owning 6 arpents of land.≤ref>[http://www.acadian-cajun.com/1678cens.htm 1678 Census]≤blockquote>Francois Gautreau & Aymee LeJeune, 6 acres, 8 cattle 2 boys: age 20- born 1658 Claude, 18 1660 Charles≤/blockquote>≤/ref> Nin
2 CONC e years later, all the children had fledged and Francois and Edmée lived together in Port Royal≤ref>[http://www.acadian-cajun.com/1686cens.htm 1686Census]≤blockquote>Francois GOTRO 71, Emee LEJEUNE 61; 3 guns, 4 arpents, 8 cattle,
2 CONC  5 sheep, 8 hogs.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>  
2 CONT By 1693, Edmée was now a widow (the Census used her nickname Aimée)≤ref>[http://www.acadian-cajun.com/1693cens.htm 1693 Census]≤blockquote>	
2 CONT Aimee LEJEUNE (widow of Francois GOTROT) 71≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT Edmée appears to have died between the 1693 and 1698 census.  She is thought to be buried in the Garrison Graveyard at Annapolis Royal, Annapolis County, Nova Scotia, Canada though graves at that time were marked with wooden cross
2 CONC es which have long disintegrated.≤ref>{{FindAGrave|139703075|sameas=yes}} Created by: AW  Added: 7 Dec 2014  ≤/ref>
2 CONT Francois owned a lot adjoining the side of the old Fort (which was expropriated in 1705≤ref name="DGFA"> to extend the [https://goo.gl/maps/za7bd Fort in Port-Royal]). It is not clear how long the family lived there. ≤blockquote>e
2 CONC xpropriation of a site "joining the side of the old fort" of Port-Royal. As Francois Gautrot etiat died since more than twelve years, it must be supposed that his heirs were the owners of this land in 1705.≤/blockquote>
2 CONT
