2 CONT }}'''NOTICE''': this profile is protected by the [[Project:Acadians|Acadian Project]] because he is an historically important person as patriarch of the Corporon family. Please contact the Acadian Project before making any substan
2 CONC tive changes. Thanks for helping make WikiTree the best site for accurate information.
2 CONT '''Jean Corporon''', the patriarch of the Corporon family in the New World, was born c.1646≤ref name=1671Cen /> in Poitou, Poitiers, France.{{Citation Needed}} He was a ploughman (agriculteur) who arrived in Acadie, Nouvelle-Franc
2 CONC e in 1668 at about age 21.{{Citation Needed}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ≤blockquote>The vast majority of the Acadian immigrants shared a common socioeconomic heritage. Fully three-fourths of the settlers in the 1671 census of Acadie were former peasants of the laboureur caste. Laboureurs constituted t
2 CONC he highest of the five peasant classes in France of the ancient regime. Industrious, frugal, and tied together by an intricate extended family system, they shared a deep and abiding attachment to the land they farmed. Only the rel
2 CONC igious wars of late sixteenth- and early seventeenth- century France, which destroyed the meager economic gains of their families, could have uprooted them from their home provinces and driven them across the Atlantic into the Mar
2 CONC itime Canadian wilderness. Their peasant heritage would profoundly influence the course of Acadian cultural development.≤ref>Barry Jean Ancelet, Jay Edwards, GlenPitre, ''Cajun Country,'' (Jackson, MS: University Press of Mississi
2 CONC ppi, 1991) p. 5.  
2 CONT ≤/ref>≤/blockquote>
2 CONT Not long after arriving he married [[Savoie-55|Françoise Savoie]] c.1668 or '70, age 15 or 17, at Port-Royal, Acadie. The 1670 date of their marriage is based on the date of birth of  their only child, a six-week old daughter, men
2 CONC tioned on the Port-Royal census of 1671.≤ref name=1671Cen>Tim Hebert, "Transcription of the 1671 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie," online at his website, ''Acadian-Cajun Genealogy,'' [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadianca
2 CONC jun/genealogy/1671cens.htm 1671 Census Transcribed].  The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadi
2 CONC e Recensements 1671 – 1752” Images 3-14.≤blockquote>
2 CONT :Jehan CORPORON, 25, 
2 CONT :Francoise SAVOIE 18; 
2 CONT :one daughter of 6 weeks; cattle 1, sheep 1.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>If there was a son, Jacques, born in 1668, he likely had died by 1671. (If the later naming of a child Jacques suggests anything, it can support the theory of a first 
2 CONC one's death.) If there was a child born in1668, the marriage of the parents was most likely 1668 as well, and the bride just 14 at the time of the conception. (Françoise Savoie wasborn c.1653≤ref name=1671Cen />
2 CONT at Port-Royal, Acadie.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean & Françoise had 15 or 16 children:
