2 CONT 1- Is the geni data listing Jean Corporon's (c1646-Feb. 12, 1712) Birth Place as: Poitou, Poitiers, France and a ploughman (laboureur) credible enough to adopt it on wikitree ??? Answer: No. A citation is needed for a source other
2 CONC  than a family tree. 
2 CONT 2- The succession of birth dates with later missed census data before reaching "marriageable age" (i.e. women: usually 15 min? but has been observed to be as young as 13/14; men: 18/21 min? usually required to generate a "liveliho
2 CONC od sufficient to support a marriage" or 15+ to "Venture off on their Own") but with other and/or younger siblings being reported may indicate infant mortality ... (i.e. no further census, spouse, children or death date data beyond
2 CONC  birth or "young census age").
2 CONT *What happened to Jacques id-125 (b.1668-?)? Per Louviere & Geni data,he is the son of Jean Corporon & Francoise Savoie. His Father & Mothers ages @ his birth would have been about 21 & 15 respectively. His birth year is the same 
2 CONC as their marriage year. He would have been 3 years old @ the time of the 1671 census, however, one daughter of 6 weeks is the only child listed (i.e. Marie id-14 (b.1671-?). Jacques would have been 18 years old @ the time of the 1
2 CONC 686 census. Jacques is not reported in either the 1671 or the 1686 census ... He may have ventured off on his own before age 18, 1686 census: No further census, spouse, children or death date data beyond his birth year ?? Need mor
2 CONC e data.
2 CONT *What happened to Marie id-124 (b.1678-?) between age 8 & 15 (not reported in 1693 census, should have been 15 & no further census, spouse, children or death date data) ?? ... This could be an early marriage.
2 CONT *What happened to Charles id-12 (b.1691-?) between age 2 & 7 (not reported in 1698 census, should have been 7 & no further spouse, children or death date data) ??
2 CONT 3- Jacques, id-8 b.1675 per 1693 census & Louviere data is indeed JeanJacques Corporon b.1675 per geni data. The geni data confirms the 1stname Jean & middle name Jacques. It seems logical that if the child'sfirst name is the same
2 CONC  as the father's, the "call name" of the child is often either the "middle name" or nick name to prevent address confusion in the immediate household. Their "call name" may then revert totheir given 1st name when they leave home o
2 CONC n their own & they encounter a majority of new acquaintances and/or required for legal purposes.Therefore Jean Corporon id-8 (b.1675-1716) per geni data is the same person as Jacques id Corporon-8 per 1693 census data (Similar nam
2 CONC e change situation encountered with sibling Isabelle/Elizabeth).
2 CONT 4- Isabelle, id-114 b.1680 per 1686 census & Elisabeth b.1682&3 per 1693 & 1698 census, are the same person ... Her wikitree profile data & biography does show her birth name as: Elizabeth Isabelle Corporon. Her "call-name" appare
2 CONC ntly switched  between age 6 & 11. The wikitree & geni data confirms her names, survival to marriage(s) & subsequent children.
2 CONT 5- The surname is correctly spelled "Corporon" in the 1671, 1693 & l698 census data ... The 1686 census recorder missed a bit with CORBERON ... Perhaps a quasi phonetic spelling? Were the census takers English officials "extractin
2 CONC g" census data in French communities? Francoise's name was correctly spelled "Savoie" @ the 1671 census but spelled "Savoye" @ 1686, 1693 & 1698 census.
2 CONT 6- The list of 14 children based on the 1st biography census data appears to be good solid data ... Perhaps Jacques id Corporon-125(b.1668-?) & Jean Jacques id Corporon-8 (b.1675-?) should be added to the 1st biography child list 
2 CONC reference links making it 16 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Alleged Ink Anomalies: 
2 CONT 7- Cecille's id-115 age @ 1693 census is listed @ 6 disrupting the 1686 & 1698 census data showing her about 2 years older than Marguerite &about 2 years younger than Elizabeth Isabelle. I am speculating/alleging that the intended
2 CONC  age number was 10, making that birth date 1683, commensurate with her other birth dates. However, a freshly dipped quill and/or a "fat quill" (i.e. point worn & in need of a trim) would produce heavy-wide ink flow on a flat table
2 CONC t. Any combination of minor tablet jostling and/or basic liquid surface tension between closely spaced ink lines would make the individual lines run together which when blotted would also tend to make the ink of a closely spaced 1
2 CONC  & 0 run together and look like a 6.
2 CONT 8- The 1698 census listing Ivlargueritte age 13, is obviously Marguerite by birth date. I am speculating/alleging that a capital M made with4 individual downward strokes with a sharp quill producing a thin line on a writing tablet
2 CONC  held at too steep of an angle would have very little ink remaining at the tops of the 4 individual line segments by the time it was blotted making the upper segments of the 4 individual lines composing the capital M nearly indist
2 CONC inguishable. "Smoosh" the remaining I, v & l together and you get the intended capital M.
2 CONT 9- John Baptiste Corporon id-9, one of the children noted above, is the one that I have descended from.
2 CONT 10- Curiosity compels me to ask: "What more can be told about the "1 servant" listed in 1698 census" ?? ... Remember this is the 1698 Maritime Canadian "Wilderness" ... Is "servant" 1 of the 5 peasant classes noted by Barry Jean A
2 CONC ncelet in 1st bio & the equivalent of "employee/hired-hand" or is this an indentured-servant with a finite training contract ?
2 CONT
