2 CONT }}'''NOTICE''': this profile is protected by the [[Project:Acadians|Acadian Project]] because of controversy over parents. Please contact the Acadian Project before making any substantive changes. Thanks for helping make WikiTree 
2 CONC the best site for accurate information.
2 CONT Étienne Pellerin was born around 1647, based on his age in the censuses,≤ref>Born about 1646 (rec-1686), 1647 (rec-1693), 1648 (rec-1698), 1646 (rec-1699), 1647 (rec-1701) ≤/ref> possibly in France. Genealogist Stephen White does 
2 CONC not give a location for his birth. ≤ref name=DGFA> White, Stephen A., Patrice Gallant, and Hector-J Hébert. [[Space:Dictionnaire_généalogique_des_familles_acadiennes|''Dictionnaire Génalogique Des Familles Acadiennes.'']] Moncton,
2 CONC  N.-B.: Centre D'études Acadiennes, Université De Moncton, 1999, Print, p. 1278-1280≤/ref> There is no documented proof of the names of his parents. According to Acadian genealogist Bona Arsenault,  Étienne was born in 1646 in Fra
2 CONC nce and was [[Pellerin-36|François Pellerin’s]] younger brother (Stephen A. White disagrees).≤ref name=Cormier> Steven A. Cormier, Acadians Who Found Refuge in Louisiana, February 1764-early 1800s, Acadiansin Gray, Appendices, [ht
2 CONC tp://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-PELLERIN.htm Pellerin]≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT Étienne married Jeanne Savoie, daughter of François Savoie and Catherine Lejeune about 1675,≤ref name=DGFA/> probably in Port-Royal since they appear in the 1678 census of Port-Royal with their two daughters age 2 and 6 months. ≤r
2 CONC ef>Tim Hebert; 1678 Port Royal Acadian Census noting that the correlations for this census were done by Rev. Clarence J. d'Entremont, Fairhaven, Massachusetts.[http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1678cens.htm 167
2 CONC 8 Census]≤blockquote>  E Pellerin & Jeanne Savoy. 1 acre, 4 cattle.  2 girls:   Madeleine 2  b 1676,  Marie 6/M  b 1678.≤/blockquote>≤/ref> They own 1 arpent of land, and 4 head of cattle. Between about 1676 and 1699 they had ten 
2 CONC children: Madeleine, Marie, Pierre, Anne, Jean-Baptiste, Jeanne, Charles(dit Toc), Bernard, Alexandre, Marguerite.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their growing family was counted in Port-Royal again in 1686≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1686 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1686cens.htm 1686 Census Transcrib
2 CONC ed]. The original census can be found at[http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 15-60.≤blockq
2 CONC uote>  Estienne PELLERIN 40, Jeanne SAVOYE 29; children: Magdelaine 10, Marie 8, Pierre 5, Anne 3, Jean-Baptiste 1; 3 arpents, 8 cattle, 12 sheep, 8 hogs.  ≤/blockquote>≤/ref>, 1693≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1693 Acadia
2 CONC n Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1693cens.htm 1693 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at[http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Ac
2 CONC adian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 62-108≤blockquote> 	Estienne PELERIN 46, Jeanne SAVOYE 35, Marie 13, Pierre 11, Anne 9, Jean Baptiste 8, Charles 3, Bernar
2 CONC d 2; 15 cattle, 20 sheep, 12 pigs, 16 arpents, 1 gun ≤/blockquote>≤/ref>, 1698 ≤ref> Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1698 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie[http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1698cens.htm 16
2 CONC 98 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at[http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, I
2 CONC mages 110-166≤blockquote> Estienne PELLERIN 50; Jeanne SAVOIE (wife) 33; Pierre 18 Jean 14; Anne 16; Jeanne 11; Bernard 8; Alexandre 5; Charles 5 cattle, 9 sheep, 5 hogs, 10 1/2 arpents, 7 fruit trees, ≤/blockquote>≤/ref>, 1700≤re
2 CONC f>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1700 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1700cens.htm 1700 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at[http://heritage.canadi
2 CONC ana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 167-173.≤blockquote> Estienne PELLERIN 53; Jeanne SAVOIE (wife) 42; Pierre 1
2 CONC 8; Jean-Baptiste 15;Charles 10, BERNARD 9; Alexandre 5; Anne 16; Jeanne 12; Marguerite 1;12 cattle, 24 sheep; 16 arpents, 1 gun. ≤/blockquote>≤/ref> and 1701≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1701 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal,
2 CONC  Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1701cens.htm 1701 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfil
2 CONC m C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 174-211.≤blockquote> Estienne PELLERIN 54, Jeanne SAVOYE (wife) 45; Pierre 33, Baptiste 18, Bernard 10, Alexandre 7, Charles 5, Anne 19, Jeanne
2 CONC  14, Marguerite 2; 1 gun, 6 cattle, 8 sheep, 5 hogs, 4 arpents. ≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT “  At one time Étienne owned Hog Island  (Île aux Cochons) on Rivière au Dauphin, now the Annapolis River, near Port-Royal.  In August 1714, soon after the British took over the colony, Étienne was among the Acadians who traveled 
2 CONC to Île Royale, today's Cape Breton Island, aboard the King's vessel La Marie Joseph to look at land with the possibility of removing to the French territory.  Evidently he did not like what he saw on Île Royale and returned to Ann
2 CONC apolis Royal, enduringBritish rule there.” ≤ref name=Cormier/> ≤ref name=DGFA/>
2 CONT Étienne passed away in Port Royal, Acadia in Nov 1722. The burial record mentions that he was about 80 years old and he died (date omitted)after having suffered for 18 months due to an illness. He was interred on 17 November 1722.
2 CONC ≤ref>''Nova Scotia Archives'', "An Acadian Parish Remembered - The Registers of St. Jean-Baptiste, Annapolis Royal, 1702-1755," register RG 1 volume 26 page 364; online database with images, [https://novascotia.ca/archives/acadian
2 CONC /archives.asp?ID=1572 Etienne Pellerin burial (registered in 1722)], accessed 7 Nov  2019≤/ref>≤ref>Library and Archives Canada, Fonds de la paroisse catholique Saint-Jean-Baptiste (Port-Royal, N.-É.)-1870 C-1870 (image 210) http:
2 CONC //heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1869/210?r=0&s=5≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT ≤references />
