2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category:Great Upheaval]]
2 CONT [[Category:Port-Royal, Acadie]]    
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT == Biography ==:'''NOTICE''': this profile is protected by the [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Project:Acadians Acadian Project] because of she is an historically important person as ancestral matriarch of the Acadian Surette famil
2 CONC y. Please contact the Acadian Project before making any substantive changes. Thanks for helping make WikiTree the best site for accurate information.
2 CONT Jeanne Pellerin is the ancestral matriarch of the Acadian Surette family. 
2 CONT Jeanne was born around 1688 to parents [[Pellerin-2|Étienne Pellerin]] and [[Savoie-11|Jeanne Savoie]].≤ref name=DGFA>White, Stephen A., Patrice Gallant, and Hector-J Hébert. [[Space:Dictionnaire_généalogique_des_familles_acadienn
2 CONC es|''Dictionnaire Généalogique Des Familles Acadiennes.'']] Moncton, N.-B.: Centre D'études Acadiennes, Université De Moncton, 1999, Print, p1278-1279; 1476-1477.≤/ref> She was likely born at Port Royal Acadia (Annapolis Royal, No
2 CONC va Scotia) as her parents were living there between 1678 and 1714.  Jeanne was counted with her family in the censuses at Port-Royal at the age of 11 (1698)≤ref> Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1698 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal,
2 CONC  Acadie[http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1698cens.htm 1698 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C
2 CONC -2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 110-166≤blockquote> Estienne PELLERIN 50; Jeanne SAVOIE (wife) 33; Pierre 18 Jean 14; Anne 16; Jeanne 11; Bernard 8; Alexandre 5; Charles 5 cattle
2 CONC , 9 sheep, 5 hogs, 10 1/2 arpents, 7 fruit trees, ≤/blockquote>≤/ref>,  12 (1700) ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1700 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1700cens.htm
2 CONC  1700 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752
2 CONC ”, Images 167-173.≤blockquote> Estienne PELLERIN 53; Jeanne SAVOIE (wife) 42; Pierre 18; Jean-Baptiste 15; Charles 10, BERNARD 9; Alexandre 5; Anne 16; Jeanne 12; Marguerite 1; 12 cattle, 24sheep; 16 arpents, 1 gun. ≤/blockquote>≤
2 CONC /ref> and 14 (1701).≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1701 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1701cens.htm 1701 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found 
2 CONC at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 174-211.≤blockquote> Estienne PELLERIN 54, Jeanne 
2 CONC SAVOYE (wife) 45; Pierre 33, Baptiste 18, Bernard 10,Alexandre 7, Charles 5, Anne 19, Jeanne 14, Marguerite 2; 1 gun, 6 cattle, 8 sheep, 5 hogs, 4 arpents. ≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT On February 4 1709, Jeanne married [[Surette-6|Pierre Surette]], who was a sailor by trade, and a farmer by inclination.  ≤ref>''Nova ScotiaArchives'', "An Acadian Parish Remembered - The Registers of St. Jean-Baptiste, Annapolis 
2 CONC Royal, 1702-1755," register RG 1 volume 26 page 292; online database with images, [https://novascotia.ca/archives/acadian/archives.asp?ID=1244 Pierre Suret and Jeanne Pellerin marriage 4 February 1709],  accessed 27 Nov 2019≤/ref>
2 CONC   Between 1709 and 1728, the couple had nine children, all born at Port Royal:  [[Surette-17|Pierre]],  [[Surette-2|Joseph]], [[Surette-3|Anne]] , [[Surette-121|Marie Josèphe]] ,[[Surette-122|Paul]] (twin), [[Surette-123|Madeleine
2 CONC ]] (twin),  [[Surette-120|Marguerite]], [[Surette-124|Françoise]] , et  [[Surette-61|Théotiste]].≤ref name=DGFA/>  
2 CONT The newlyweds set up housekeeping not far from the Saint Laurent parish chapel, a distance up from Port Royal.  They were enumerated in the 1710 ≤ref>The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.la
2 CONC c_reel_c9119/1315?r=0&s=4 Acadian Census microfilmC-9119 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 1315-1318. P. 26, Image 1316≤blockquote>Suret, sa femme, one son≤/blockquote>≤/ref> census at P
2 CONC ort-Royal with their son,  and again in 1714 ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1714 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1714cens.htm 1714 Census Transcription]. The orig
2 CONC inal census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 239-261.≤blockquote> SURE
2 CONC TTE and wife, 2 sons≤/blockquote>≤/ref> now with two sons.
2 CONT "On 8 December 1755, Jeanne Pellerin, widow of Pierre Surette, and 3 of their daughters, were very likely among the people who left Port-Royal aboard the Pembroke, destined for exile in North Carolina.  The Acadians on board seize
2 CONC d the ship and headed towards the St-John River. They later moved up river to Ste-Anne-du-Pays-Bas where they settled for the winter. Afterwards, Jeanne Pellerin and her daughters sought refuge in the city of Québec where Jeanne d
2 CONC ied during the smallpox epidemic that had developed between November 1757 and February 1758.≤ref>White, Stephen A. ''Dictionnaire Généalogique des Familles Acadiennes,Ajouts et corrections'' (Moncton, N.-B.: Centre D'études Acadie
2 CONC nnes,Université De Moncton, 4/11/2011) [http://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/files/umcm-ceaac/wf/wf/pdf/cor-dict.pdf Ajouts et corrections (avril 2011)]≤blockquote>* 1478 (août 2007) La même famille. Ajouter, après la Note historique
2 CONC  déjà inscrite, une nouvelle note, comme suit:«8 déc 1755: Jeanne Pellerin, veuve de Pierre Surette, et trois de ses filles sontfort probablement parmi les gens qui partent de Port-Royal à bord du senauPembroke, destinés à l'exil 
2 CONC en Caroline du Nord. Les Acadiens sur cenavire s'en sont emparés et l'ont dirigé à la rivière St-Jean. Par la suite, ils ont monté la rivière jusqu'à Ste-Anne-du-Pays-Bas, où ils ont hiverné. Jeanne Pellerin et ses filles se sont 
2 CONC ensuite rfugiées à la ville de Québec (Delaney, loc. cit.), oùJeanne est décédée durant l'épidémie de la petite vérole qui sévissait entre
2 CONT novembre 1757 et février 1758».≤/blockquote> ≤/ref>
2 CONT Jeanne died on January 27, 1758 in Quebec at around the age of 80 and was buried the next day at the Notre Dame De Quebec Basilica-Cathedral≤ref name='AncestryActes(abo1'>Actes d’état civil et registres déglise du Québec (Collecti
2 CONC on Drouin), 1621 à 1967. Online publication - Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com Operations Inc, 2008. Gabriel Drouin, comp. Drouin Collection. Montreal, Quebec, Canada: Institut Généalogique Drouin. Institut Généalogique Drouin; Montre
2 CONC al, Quebec, Canada; Drouin Collection; Author: Gabriel Drouin, comp. Search: Q > Québec > Notre-Dame > 1757-1759. Accessed on ancestry.ca, November 2019.* View {{Ancestry Image|1091|d13p_31431328|ca}} on ancestry.ca,  (requires su
2 CONC bscription).* View free [https://www.ancestry.com/sharing/18299041?h=073f72 Ancestry Sharing Image]. ≤blockquote>(above last entry on right page) “Le 28 janvier 1758 à été inhumée Jeanne Pellerin veuve Suret, Acadienne, décédée le
2 CONC  jour précédent âgée d’environ quatre-vingts ans. Étaient présents Jean Vallée, Jean Bois et autres.” ≤/blockquote> ≤/ref> ≤ref name=DGFA/>≤ref>  PRDH online. Burial Quebec Parish, no. 252915 ≤/ref> 
2 CONT '''Maternal DNA'''. Jeanne Pellerin's mother was Jeanne Savoie, whose mother was Catherine Lejeune, sister of Edmee Lajeune. Some have speculated that Catherine and Edmee were Amerindian. The [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/
2 CONC MothersofAcadia/default.aspx Mothers of Acadia maternal DNA project] posts its ongoing Maternal DNA results [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/mothersofacadia/default.aspx?section=mtresults '''here''']. To date, the haplogroup 
2 CONC of both sisters is consistently reported as U6a7a, indicating European origins. Another group known as [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/AcadianAmerindian/default.aspxAMERINDIAN Ancestry Out of Acadia DNA PROJECT], posts its r
2 CONC esults [https://www.familytreedna.com/public/AcadianAmerindian/default.aspx?section=mtresults here]. They too report that Catherine and Edmee have European haplogroups. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Timeline==
2 CONT
2 CONT :'''c1688''' birth 
2 CONT :''1697 Treaty of Ryswick restores Acadia to France; Port-Royal is itscapital''≤ref name=Dunn>Dunn, Brenda. A History of Port Royal / Annapolis Royal 1605-1800. Nimbus Publishing, , p 40,43 (1693 PR raid); p44-45 (1697 Treaty of R
2 CONC yswick); p52-53(1702 Queen Anne’s War); p61-62 (Blockade of PR); p 71-73(1707 Attack on PR); p82-85(1710 Siege of PR).≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT :'''1709''' marriage to Pierre Surette, in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''1709''' birth, son Pierre, in Port Royal
2 CONT :''1710 Siege of Port-Royal; French surrender the Fort. Port-Royal, Acadia becomes Annapolis Royal, Nova Scotia≤ref name=Dunn/>
2 CONT
2 CONT :'''1712''' birth, son Joseph, in Port Royal
2 CONT :''1713 Treaty of Utrecht. France cedes Acadia to England. Permanent British rule. ≤ref>[http://www2.umoncton.ca/cfdocs/etudacad/1755/index.cfm?id=010201000&lang=en&style=G&admin=false&linking= The Neutrality: Political Context, i
2 CONC n 1755 l'Histoire et les Histoires, University of Moncton]≤/ref>''
2 CONT :''1713-1744 Golden Age≤ref name=Griffiths92>Griffiths, Naomie E.S. The Contexts of Acadian History 1686-1784.Published for the Center for Canadian Studies Mount Allison University, Montreal: McGill-Queens University Press, 1992, 
2 CONC p61 (golden age);≤/ref> of Acadian Growth and Prosperity” 
2 CONT :''1714-15 New English King requires oaths of allegiance.≤ref name=Griffiths2005>Griffiths, Naomi E.S., From migrant to Acadian : a North-American border people, 1604-1755, Montreal (Québec), McGill-Queen's University Press, 2005,
2 CONC  p147-151 (King William’s War); p 267-268 (oaths of allegiance)≤/ref> Delegates from Port-Royal sign a [http://www2.umoncton.ca/cfdocs/etudacad/1755/index.cfm?id=010202000&overlay=doc&identifier=001461&bd=CEA&lang=en&style=P&admin
2 CONC =false&linking= '''conditional''' oath of allegiance], promising to stay true to the King of Great Britain for as long as they stayed in Nova Scotia, and to remain neutral in the event of a conflict between France and Great Britai
2 CONC n'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT :'''1715''' birth, daughter Anne, in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''1718''' birth, daughter Marie-Josephe, in Port Royal
2 CONT :''1720 and onward Acadians refuse to sign an '''unconditional''' oathof allegiance. This is tolerated by the British as they lack militarymeans to enforce the oath.≤ref name=oath>The Neutrality, 1755 Histoire et Les Histoires, Un
2 CONC iversity of Moncton≤/ref>'' 
2 CONT
2 CONT :'''1721''' birth, son Paul (twin), in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''1721''' birth, daughter Madeleine (twin), in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''b1726''' birth, daughter Marguerite, in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''1726''' birth, daughter Francoise, in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''1728''' birth, daughter Theotiste, in Port Royal
2 CONT :'''1758''' death, in Quebec 
