2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT History found in Houma Courier, Terrebonne Parish, LA, July 27, 1999
2 CONT
2 CONT History found in Houma Courier, Terrebonne Parish, LA, July 27, 1999,
2 CONT section A
2 CONT
2 CONT Bourg/Bourque
2 CONT Some say that the Acadian Bourg/Bourque family is originally from Norm
2 CONT andy and descended from William the Conqueror who invaded England in 1
2 CONT 066 and became the English king. According to this theory, the Bourg
2 CONT and Bourques of Acadia were thus related to the English royal family t
2 CONT hat had them deported from Acadia and are also related to the present
2 CONT royal family of England.
2 CONT Royalty or not, the most likely arrival date of the first Bourgs in Ac
2 CONT adia was 1632, when Antoine Bourg probably arrived with Commander Isaa
2 CONT c de Razilly who brought 300 "elite" men to the colony, including a do
2 CONT zen or so families. Antoine was the son of Simon Bourg, and he was bo
2 CONT rn in 1609 in Martaize, France, in the region of Loudon, Department d
2 CONT e la Vienne. Antoine was accompanied by his parents, and at least on
2 CONT e sister, Perrine.
2 CONT In 1636, the Bourg family moved to Port Royal, which had once again co
2 CONT me under French rule.
2 CONT Antoine married Antoinette Landry in Acadia some time around 1641-43
2 CONT . Antoinette was born in France in 1618 and came to Acadia with her s
2 CONT ister, Perrine Landry, and two of her brothers, Rene, her twin, and Je
2 CONT an Claude. Jean Claude brought his wife, Marie Salle and their child
2 CONT , Rene, as well as the three children from Marie Salle's first marriag
2 CONT e, Martin, Jeanne and Michelle Aucoin.
2 CONT Antoine and Antoinette Bourg established themselves on the west bank o
2 CONT f the R Riviere des Dauphins (Annapolis River today), and their settle
2 CONT ment eventually became known as the "hameau des Bourg" or "Bourg Villa
2 CONT ge" They had 12 children.
2 CONT By the census of 1671, Antoine had given most of his land to a son, Je
2 CONT han, who was married to Marguerite Martin and who already had two chil
2 CONT dren of his own. Jehan later added six more girls and one son to his
2 CONT family.
2 CONT Antoine's oldest son, Francois, was also married by 1671 and lived tw
2 CONT o farms down from his parents. Antoine's son, Bernard, had become fai
2 CONT rly rich by the standards to the time, with 35 parents in cultivation
2 CONT , two muskets, 10 cows, 40 sheep, 12 pigs and 45 fruit trees.
2 CONT An interesting item concerning the Bernard Bourg family is that their
2 CONT oldest daughter married Claude Dugas, while three sons and one daughte
2 CONT r from Claude Dugas' first marriage to Francoise Bourgeois married thr
2 CONT ee of the other children of Bernard Bourg. Thus Claude and three of h
2 CONT is sons and one of his daughters had not only a parent child relations
2 CONT hip, but they were also in laws.
2 CONT Martin Bourg, Antoine's fourth son, married Marie Potet and had nine c
2 CONT hildren, at least one of whom left descendants who eventually came to
2 CONT Louisiana.
2 CONT At some point in the early 1700's, a number of Bourg descendants move
2 CONT d to the Ile St. Jean (Prince Edward Island) to escape English rule.
2 CONT Two of these, who figure in the history of Bourg/Bourque families of L
2 CONT ouisiana were Francois Louis, the son of Martin Bourg and Marie Potet
2 CONT , and the grandson of Antoine's first son Francois. This grandson wa
2 CONT s Jean Baptiste, the oldest son of Francois Bourg and Marguerite Boudr
2 CONT ot.
2 CONT Francois Louis was married to Cecile Michel. They lived at Riviere de
2 CONT s Blonds on the Ile St. Jean, where they had eight children. Francoi
2 CONT s Louis and his family were deported by the English from the island i
2 CONT n 1758. Francois Louis died at sea at the age of 80.
2 CONT Jean Baptiste Bourg married Marie Pitre, who also lived at riviere de
2 CONT s Blonds until deported with their family to St. Malo. Marie Pitre Bo
2 CONT urg died at sea, and Jean Baptiste and the oldest daughter, Marguerite
2 CONT , died within a month of arriving in France.
2 CONT One of the sons of Francois Louis, Jean Charles, died in France some t
2 CONT ime between 1777 and 1785. His widow, Madeleine Blanchard, and their
2 CONT family came to Louisiana from France aboard the ship "L'Amitie" in 178
2 CONT 5. The family established itself in the vicinity of St. Martinville (
2 CONT Louisiana) and was the source of many of the Bourques in south centra
2 CONT l Louisiana.
2 CONT Charles, the youngest son of Jean Baptiste Bourg, was deported to Fran
2 CONT ce with his family and later married Anne Thibodaux. Charles died in
2 CONT France, but his widow came to Louisiana in 1785 with the surviving mem
2 CONT bers of the family. They settled first in present day Assumption Pari
2 CONT sh, near Plattenville. Some of these descendants made their way into
2 CONT Lafourche and Terrebonne Parishes and make up a portion of the Bourg f
2 CONT amily in southeast Louisiana.
2 CONT [Ancestors of Errol Sevin.GED.FTW]
2 CONT
