2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Houma Courier, July 27, 1999, section A
2 CONT History found in Houma Courier, Terrebonne Parish, LA, July 27, 1999, section A
2 CONT
2 CONT Bourg/Bourque
2 CONT Some say that the Acadian Bourg/Bourque family is originally from Normandy and descended from William the Conqueror who invaded England in 1066 and became the English king. According to this theory, the Bourg and Bourques of Acadi
2 CONC a were thus related to the English royal family that had them deported from Acadia and are also related to the present
2 CONT royal family of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Royalty or not, the most likely arrival date of the first Bourgs in Acadia was 1632, when Antoine Bourg probably arrived with Commander Isaac de Razilly who brought 300 "elite" men to the colony, including a dozen or so families. 
2 CONC Antoine was the son of Simon Bourg, and he was born in 1609 in Martaize, France, in the region of Loudon, Department de la Vienne. Antoine was accompanied by his parents,and at least one sister, Perrine. [This is inaccurate]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1636, the Bourg family moved to Port Royal, which had once again come under French rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine married Antoinette Landry in Acadia some time around 1641-43. Antoinette was born in France in 1618 and came to Acadia with her sister, Perrine Landry, and two of her brothers, Rene, her twin,and Jean Claude. Jean Claude b
2 CONC rought his wife, Marie Salle and their child, Rene, as well as the three children from Marie Salle's first marriage, Martin, Jeanne and Michelle Aucoin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine and Antoinette Bourg established themselves on the west bank of the R Riviere des Dauphins (Annapolis River today), and their settlement eventually became known as the "hameau des Bourg" or "Bourg Village" They had 12 chil
2 CONC dren.
2 CONT By the census of 1671, Antoine had given most of his land to a son, Jehan, who was married to Marguerite Martin and who already had two children of his own. Jehan later added six more girls and one son to his family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine's oldest son, Francois, was also married by 1671 and lived two farms down from his parents. Antoine's son, Bernard, had become fairly rich by the standards to the time, with 35 parents in cultivation, two muskets, 10 cows,
2 CONC  40 sheep, 12 pigs and 45 fruit trees.
2 CONT
2 CONT An interesting item concerning the Bernard Bourg family is that their oldest daughter married Claude Dugas, while three sons and one daughter from Claude Dugas' first marriage to Francoise Bourgeois married three of the other chil
2 CONC dren of Bernard Bourg. Thus Claude and three of his sons and one of his daughters had not only a parent child relationship, but they were also in laws.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin Bourg, Antoine's fourth son, married Marie Potet and had nine children, at least one of whom left descendants who eventually came to Louisiana.
2 CONT
2 CONT At some point in the early 1700's, a number of Bourg descendants moved to the Ile St. Jean (Prince Edward Island) to escape English rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of these, who figure in the history of Bourg/Bourque families of Louisiana were Francois Louis, the son of Martin Bourg and Marie Potet, and the grandson of Antoine's first son Francois. This grandson was Jean Baptiste, the ol
2 CONC dest son of Francois Bourg and Marguerite Boudrot.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francois Louis was married to Cecile Michel. They lived at Riviere des Blonds on the Ile St. Jean, where they had eight children. Francois Louis and his family were deported by the English from the island in 1758. Francois Louis d
2 CONC ied at sea at the age of 80.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Baptiste Bourg married Marie Pitre, who also lived at riviere des Blonds until deported with their family to St. Malo. Marie Pitre Bourg died at sea, and Jean Baptiste and the oldest daughter, Marguerite, died within a month 
2 CONC of arriving in France.
2 CONT One of the sons of Francois Louis, Jean Charles, died in France some time between 1777 and 1785. His widow, Madeleine Blanchard, and their family came to Louisiana from France aboard the ship "L'Amitie" in 1785. The family establi
2 CONC shed itself in the vicinity of St. Martinville (Louisiana) and was the source of many of the Bourques in south central Louisiana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles, the youngest son of Jean Baptiste Bourg, was deported to France with his family and later married Anne Thibodaux. Charles died in France, but his widow came to Louisiana in 1785 with the surviving members of the family. T
2 CONC hey settled first in present day Assumption Parish, near Plattenville. Some of these descendants made their way into Lafourche and Terrebonne Parishes and make up a portion of the Bourg family in southeast Louisiana.
2 CONT
2 CONT
