2 CONT Descendants of Jean Claude Landry
2 CONT Generation No. 3
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  FRANCOIS3 BOURQUE (ANTOINETTE (TWIN)2 LANDRY, JEAN CLAUDE1) was born Abt. 1643 in Richmond, Port Royal, Nova Scotia, and died Abt. 1684 in Richmond, Port Royal, Nova Scotia.  He married MADELEINE MARGUERITE BOUDREAU Abt. 1665 
2 CONC in Port Royal, Annapolis, Nova Scotia, daughter of MICHEL BOUDREAU and MICHELLE AUCOIN.  She was born 1648 in Port Royal, Acadia, New France, and died 09 Nov 1718 in Port Royal, Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of FRANCOIS BOURQUE and MADELEINE BOUDREAU are:
2 CONT
2 CONT 38.              i.    MICHEL aka MICHAUD4 BOURQUE, b. May 1666, St. Jean Baptiste, Port Royal, Acadia; d. 18 Nov 1721.
2 CONT ii.    MARIE ANNE BOURQUE, b. Abt. 1668.
2 CONT 39.            iii.    ALEXANDRE (aka BELLEHUMEUR) BOURQUE, b. 1671, Grand Pre, Acadia, Canada; d. 1760, Richibourctou, Westmoreland County, NB Canada.
2 CONT 40.            iv.    MARGUERITE BOURQUE, b. Abt. 1673, Port Royal, Annapolis, Nova Scotia, Canada; d. Beaubassin, Nova Scotia, Canada.
2 CONT 41.             v.    MADELEINE BOURQUE, b. Abt. 1677, St. Jean Baptiste, Port Royal, Acadia; d. Aft. 03 Dec 1730.
2 CONT vi.    PIERRE BOURQUE, b. 1683; d. 1693.
2 CONT 42.           vii.    ANNE (aka JEANNE) BOURQUE, b. Abt. 1684.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.biographi.ca/EN/ShowBio.asp?BioId=35347>:  Alexander is the son of Francois.
2 CONT
2 CONT BOURG, aka Belle-Humeur, ALEXANDRE, notary, kings attorney; b. 1671 at Port-Royal (Annapolis Royal, N.S.), son of François Bourg and Marguerite Boudrot; d. 1760 at Richibucto (N.B.).
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre Bourg settled about 1694 at Grand Pré, Nova Scotia, where he married Marguerite Melanson, the daughter of Pierre Melanson, aka La Verdure, and Marguerite Mius dEntremont; of this marriage at least 16 children were born. 
2 CONC Immediately after the conquest of Acadia by the British in 1710 he was appointed notary for Minas Basin. On several occasions he was chosen to represent the Acadians of his region in dealing with the government at Annapolis Royal.
2 CONC  Thus he was among the delegates sent by the Acadians of Minas to Annapolis in 1720 at the request of Governor Richard Philipps after the latter had tried to have them take the oath of allegiance. After the same Acadians had refus
2 CONC ed to take the oath in 1727 Bourg was called to Annapolis to explain their conduct.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1730 Governor Philipps granted Alexandre Bourg a commission as kings attorney for Minas, Pisiquid (Windsor, N.S.), Cobequid (near Truro, N.S.), and Chignecto, with authority to receive moneys due and annuities and to h
2 CONC andle all cases of seizure of property and escheat. In 1731 he was accused of negligence in his accounts, and in September 1737 the lieutenant governor, Lawrence Armstrong, dismissed him from his functions. He was replaced by Fran
2 CONC çois Mangeant, aka Saint-Germain. On 27 May 1740, Paul Mascarene, Armstrongs successor, who considered Bourg an old acquaintance, reinstated him as notary and tax-collector.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1742 Bourg and some settlers from Minas helped in finding belongings stolen by Indians from an English merchant vessel near Grand Pré. In 1744 Bourg was again accused of negligence in carrying out his duties and was even accuse
2 CONC d of having collaborated, along with his son-in-law Joseph Leblanc, aka Le Maigre, with François Du Pont* Duviviers troops during the invasion of Nova Scotia; on 17 December Bourg was suspended from office. He was taken to Annapol
2 CONC is Royal with Joseph Le Blanc and subjected to a close interrogation by Mascarene and the council. As punishment his office as notary at Minas was taken from him for good.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bourg may have left Acadia at the beginning of the 1750s when several thousand Acadians emigrated to le Royale (Cape Breton Island) and le Saint-Jean (Prince Edward Island). In any event we meet him again in 1752 at Port-Toulouse 
2 CONC (St Peters, N.S.), at the home of Joseph Le Blanc, who had been living there for three years. Bourg escaped the deportation of the Acadians in 1755 and the evacuation of le Royale after the British capture of Louisbourg in 1758 [s
2 CONC ee Wolfe]. He seems to have taken refuge at Richibucto, where he died at 89 years of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT C. J. dEntremont
2 CONT
2 CONT AN, Col., E, 277, ff.17-20; Section Outre-Mer, G1, 466, pièces 8, 13, 24, 25, 26, 27. Diocesan Archives (Baton Rouge, La.), Registres de St-Charles des Mines (Grand-Pré), 1707-1748 (baptêmes); 1709-                                
2 CONC                           1748 (mariages et sépultures) (copies at Diocesan Archives, Yarmouth, N.S., and PAC, MG 9, B8, 12). Coll. doc. inédits Canada et Amérique, II, 175. Coll. de manuscrits relatifs à la N.-F., II, 260. N.S. A
2 CONC rchives, I; II; III; IV. PAC Report, 1905, II, pt.i, 22. Murdoch, History of Nova-Scotia, I, 364, 405, 468, 478, 523; II, 4, 8, 10, 39, 71.
2 CONT
