1 NAME Christophe /Crevier de la Meslee/
2 GIVN Christophe
2 SURN Crevier de la Meslee
2 _MARNM Crevier de la Meslee
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Trois-Rivières, Mauricie Region, Quebec, Canada
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 17 FEB 1611
2 PLAC Rouen, Departement de la Seine-Maritime, Haute-Normandie, France
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 DEC 1662
2 PLAC Trois-Rivieres, Quebec, Canada
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Crevier-32
2 CONT
2 CONT https://family.crevier.org/docs/Our-French-Canadian-Forefathers.pdf
2 CONT CHRISTOPHE CREVIER
2 CONT "Christophe Crevier, Sieur de la Meslee, baptized in Saint Cande le Jeune, Rouen,
2 CONT France, on February 17, 1611, was the son of Nicolas Crevier and Anne Baziret.
2 CONT He spent his youth in Rouen and it was most likely there that he met Jeanne Evard
2 CONT (Enard), his future wife. They were married in the church in which he as baptized, on
2 CONT November 6, 1633. He was twenty-two years old and his bride was about fourteen.
2 CONT Perhaps it was the intrigue of the tales of the new colony, or perhaps it was their
2 CONT spirit of adventure that brought them to Canada in 1639. On their arrival, they settled in
2 CONT Trois-Rivieres where Crevier earned his living as a baker, and more profitably, as a fur
2 CONT trader.
2 CONT For unknown reasons, the family returned to France between 1642 and 1645.
2 CONT During this sojourn in the mother country, Crevier earned his living as a merchant. They
2 CONT resided in La Rochelle and it was there that their daughter, Marguerite, was born in 1645.
2 CONT On January 20, 1647, Crevier was a witness at the signing of a marriage contract. We
2 CONT know that he was still in France in 1651, because on July 8, of that year, in La Rochelle,
2 CONT he and his wife paid a debt of 42 “livres” 13 “sols” to Marie Capin, widow of Martin
2 CONT Poirier.
2 CONT Soon after, the family returned to Canada but, unknowingly, it was a costly
2 CONT decision, because two of their sons would succumb to the wrath of the Iroquois. The first,
2 CONT Francois, was killed on May 28, 1653; the other, Antoine, most likely in 1661. Francois
2 CONT Hertel, who had been taken prisoner that year and later released wrote of the sufferings
2 CONT and tortures endured by captives. An excerpt reads:
2 CONT “As for the little Antoine de la Maslee (the name often used by the
2 CONT family), this poor child filled me with compassion, because he had become
2 CONT the servant of these barbarians, and they killed him with knife blows as
2 CONT they hunted him down.”
2 CONT In August of 1653, Crevier was named trustee of the “habitants” of the seigneury
2 CONT of Notre Dame des Anges, near Quebec. To have received this appointment would
2 CONT indicate that the family had resided in the area for some time. But they did not remain in
2 CONT Quebec. On March 9, 1655, the Jesuits granted Crevier a parcel of land on the largest
2 CONT island situated at the mouth of the St. Maurice River. At the same time, the Jesuits sold
2 CONT parcels to Jacques Bertrand, Jacques Brisset, Jean Pecault, Pierre Dandonneau, and
2 CONT Michel Lemay. During the next few years, Christophe bought all of the shares of his
2 CONT neighbors and this land became known as the “Fief Saint Christophe.” It is still known as
2 CONT St. Christophe today.
2 CONT The first court case registered in Trois-Rivieres is dated June 1655 and involves
2 CONT Crevier’s wife, Jeanne Evard, who appears as plaintiff against Marie Sedillot, wife of
2 CONT Bertrand Fafard dit Laframboise. The case involved a calf which Jeanne Evard had cared
2 CONT for, for a few weeks at the request of the owner, Marie Sedillot. In payment, Jeanne
2 CONT Evard wanted half of the calf when it was slaughtered. The court’s verdict was that
2 CONT Jeanne Evard would receive the proportion of meat, which would be half of the weight
2 CONT gained by the calf, while it was under her care. The judge sitting o the bench was Pierre
2 CONT Boucher, son-in-law of Jeanne Evard. His judgement was considerably less than what his
2 CONT mother-in-law had sued for. It does make one wonder what happened after the trial.
2 CONT Christophe Crevier remained in Trois-Rivieres for the remainder of his life. The
2 CONT exact date of his death has never been found. From existing records, his death had to
2 CONT occur between October 10, 1661, when he was mentioned as witnessing the signing of his
2 CONT daughter’s marriage contract to Michel Gamelin, and November 20, 1663, when he was
2 CONT mentioned as deceased at the signing of his son’s marriage contract. His widow, Jeanne
2 CONT Evard, died much later, but the record of her death has also been lost.
2 CONT Christophe Crevier and Jeanne Evard had at least ten children of whom six left
2 CONT posterity."
