1 NOTE Additional references: 21-6; 45-4; L-2-28.
2 CONT Jette pg 292 shows died between 12 Apr 1693 and 29 Apr 1697.  He was  kidnapped by the Iroquois in 1693.  Ransomed by the English 31 Aug 1693.  He  died shortly thereafter.
2 CONT Index and church registers 1694-1698 are missing.
2 CONT "1667 Census of Quebec" Vol II pg 39.
2 CONT Tanguay Vol I pg 150 shows family with 5 children; Tanguay Vol III pg 199 shows family of 4 children.
2 CONT Jette pg 292-293.
2 CONT Drouin pg 325.
2 CONT Genealogy pages relative to the Crevier family acquired from the internet from "CometSystems" (copy filed "CREVIER").
2 CONT "Our French Canadian Forefathers" (copy filed 60-2).
2 CONT Crevier genealogy page (copy filed 21-6).
2 CONT Hertel genealogy page (copy filed 23-4).
2 CONT <http://www.myerchin.org/ChristopheJeanneCREVIER.html> (copy filed 21-6).
2 CONT "The Grand Families" from the internet (copy filed 21-6).
2 CONT Gordon genealogy from the internet (copy filed 45-2).
2 CONT Tanguay Vol I pg 305.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.biographi.ca/EN/ShowBio.asp?BioId=34265>:
2 CONT
2 CONT JEAN  CREVIER  DE  SAINT-FRANÇOIS,  fur trader, a prominent person in the colony; b. 3 April 1642 at Trois-Rivières, son of Christophe Crevier aka La Meslée and Jeanne Énard; d. shortly after August 1693.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 26 Nov. 1663, at Trois-Rivières, he married Marguerite, daughter of Jacques Hertel and Marie Marguerie. He fell foul of the law with respect to his fur-trading with the Indians who frequented Cap-de-la-Madeleine. He bought the 
2 CONC Saint-François-du-Lac seigneury from his brother-in-law Pierre Boucher* in 1673. He obtained ordinances from the intendants Duchesneau and de Meulles*, forbidding anyone to hunt or fish throughout the area of his seigneury. He was
2 CONC  one of the 20 principal settlers summoned to Quebec by Governor Buade de Frontenac on 26 Oct. 1678 to express their opinion on trading in spirits, and he voted in favour of it, attributing the crimes committed by the Indians to t
2 CONC heir savage disposition rather than to the use of liquor. He was carried off by the Iroquois during a raid on Saint-François in August 1693, and just as they were getting ready to burn him alive he was ransomed for 50 livres by Ma
2 CONC jor Peter Schuyler*, the commandant of the Albany garrison; but he died shortly afterwards, as a result of his wounds. His son Louis had been killed 27 March 1690, during the expedition of François Hertel* against Salmon Falls. Hi
2 CONC s widow and his son Joseph, on 23 Aug. 1700, gave part of their seigneury to the Abenakis and the Sokokis, for whom the Jesuits opened a mission which still exists today.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Charland
2 CONT
2 CONT ASQ, Polygraphic, XIII, 47. Coll. de manuscrits relatifs à la Nouv.-France, I, 589f. Découvertes établissements des Français (Margry), I, 405-20. NYCD (OCallaghan and Fernow), IV, 66; IX, 554f. T.-M. Charland, "Enlèvement et mort 
2 CONC du seigneur Crevier," BRH, XLIII (1937), 346-48; Histoire de Saint-François-du-Lac (Ottawa, 1942). J.-A. Maurault, Histoire des Abénakis (Sorel, 1866), 278-80.
2 CONT
2 CONT ? 2000 University of Toronto/Université Laval
