1 OCCU menuisier; carpenter, Menuisier a Notre-Dame de Mortagne. Immigrant, sieur de gros bois
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Notes'''
2 CONT * Location info: [https://www.geni.com/projects/Perche-France/24550 Perche, France] (birth), [https://www.geni.com/projects/Maine-province-France/24701 Maine, France] (marriage), [https://www.geni.com/projects/Trois-Rivi%C3%A8res-
2 CONC Qu%C3%A9bec-Canada/24574 Trois-Rivières] (death)
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/projects/Pioneers-of-Canada-Quebec/17839 Pionnier de la Nouvelle-France]. Arrive au Québec le 4 juin 1634 à bord du [https://www.geni.com/projects/Passagers-du-Navire-de-Nesl%C3%A9-1634/24863 « Navire de Ne
2 CONC slé »] avec sa femme et cinq enfants, Madeleine, Pierre, Nicolas, Marie et Marguerite.
2 CONT * Il était [https://www.geni.com/projects/Carpenters/17163 menuisier] par métier.
2 CONT * Selon les historiens, la maison familiale a brûlé avec Nicole et son mari Gaspard à Trois-Rivières.
2 CONT
2 CONT =From [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=buckyfontaine&id=I98953 Fontaine Family Tree]:=
2 CONT Gaspard Boucherville, QC and Nicole Lemaire were two of the premier colonists of QC having emigrated to QC with Giffard and The Company of the Hundred Associates, granted their charter in 1627. Gaspard and his wife brought with th
2 CONC em their five surviving children, Pierre, the eldest Nicolas who was 24 years old and unmarried when he died in Trois-Rivers in 1647 and their three daughters, Marie, Marguerite and Madeleine who, later on married into respectivel
2 CONC y, the LaFond, Toupin and Beaudry families. The children had all been baptized at the Church of Notre-Dame of Montagne, Perche, France with the exception of Madeleine who, to all appearances, was born during the the course of the 
2 CONC voyage on the ocean. The registers of Montagne, Perche, France, and of QC have no record of her birth but the census of QC taken in 1666, gives her an age of about 36 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Jette Page 136. He was a carpenter like his father. Gaspard and his wife were two of the first colonists of Quebec, having emigrated with Giffard and the Company of the Hundred Associates. Their charter was granted in
2 CONC  1627. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The name Boucher in French translates to Butcher. At some point in history, someone in that family had been a butcher, and that gave the family its surname. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT pp 7190-7191
2 CONT
2 CONT Account among his descentants, governors, clergymen, judges etc. A large number of more prominent families of Canada. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrived Quebec June 4, 1634. Later moved to Trois Rivieres where he had a concession of 24 acres along  the river.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: Mortague, Perche, France  
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration 1634 -- New France, Canada  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT =From [http://www.branches-n-twigs.com/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I8090&tree=allfam Branches and Twigs]:=
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher was a wood joiner in the city of Mortagne. He learned his trade from his father, Jacques. He married Nicole Lemers before 1620. They had at least 8 children, four of whom left posterity in Canada, a son and three d
2 CONC aughters. Gaspards father had received a share of a seigneury in France and this was passed on to Gaspard. He sold this share, and signed this selling document with a flourish. Gaspar had many descendants who were seigneurs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perche, France was the birthplace of Quebec. It is from this town that Robert Giffard lived an recruited settlers for the first seigneury of the colony in 1634. It is likely that Gaspard intended to go with these first settlers wh
2 CONC o accompanied Giffard to Beauport, Canada. But it took him until the following spring to finish selling his properties and arranging his affairs. He sailed in 1635 on a ship under the command of Captain de Ville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once in Quebec, Gaspard worked as a wood joiner for the Jesuits at Notre-Dame-Des-Anges. His relationship with the Jesuits was to shape the history of his family and Canada. His son, Pierre ( 1622-1717) received his education from
2 CONC  the Jesuits. When later events in the dramatic life of Pierre brought him to Trois-Rivieres, Quebec, Gaspard and his family followed him. In March 1646 Gaspard received a grant of land next to the property of his son-in-law Etien
2 CONC ne de La Fond. We do not know the history of Gaspard and his wife from that time until their deaths. His house burned, and some assume he died in that fire, but there is no evidence to support this. Both he and his wife are absent
2 CONC  from the 1666 census.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:"One Hundred French Canadian Family Histories" by Phillip J. Moore
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT =From [http://lequebecunehistoiredefamille.com/communaute/boucher Le Quebec Une Histoire de Famille.com]=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Pierre & Joseph Boucher Ancêtres des Boucher d'Amérique==
2 CONT Pierre Boucher, fils de Gaspard et de Nicole Lemaire, a été baptisé à Sainte-Jean-de-Mortagne, au Perche, le 1er août 1622. Il arrive en Nouvelle-France en 1635. Il est tout d’abord compagnon des missionnaires jésuites chez les Hu
2 CONC rons. Il entre par la suite au service de la compagnie des Cent-Associés aux Trois-Rivières.
2 CONT
2 CONT En 1649, il épouse Marie-Madeleine Ouébadinoukoué, une amérindienne chrétienne. Ils auront un fils qui décédera en bas âge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Devenu veuf assez tôt, il se remariera avec Jeanne Crevier, fille de Christophe Crevier et de Jeanne Emard, le 9 juillet 1652.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre occupera le gouvernement des Trois-Rivières à plusieurs reprises et il recevra des lettres de noblesse du roi Louis XIV en 1653.
2 CONT
2 CONT De son mariage avec Jeanne Crevier, il aura 15 enfants qui perpétuèrent ainsi son nom à travers l'Amérique.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Le Centre de généalogie francophone d'Amérique.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vers 1769, Joseph Boucher et son épouse Isabelle Martin s'installent aux Pays-Bas, sur la rivière Saint-Jean. Il semblerait que ce Joseph Boucher soit le fils d'un autre Joseph Boucher, de la seigneurie de l'Islet. Ce dernier étai
2 CONC t marié avec Geneviève Hayot et descendait de Marin Boucher de Mortagne au Perche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marin Boucher était venu s'établir en Nouvelle-France en 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lors de l'arrivée des Loyalistes au Nouveau-Brunswick, Joseph Boucher dû quitter la terre qu'il cultivait et serait déménagé dans la région de Cocagne. Plus tard, il se serait installé à Caraquet, puis à Bouctouche où il est décéd
2 CONC é le 19 septembre 1799, âgé de 72 ans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Société historique acadienne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Boucher de Boucherville portait les armoiries:
2 CONT
2 CONT     "D'azur, au chevron d'argent, sommé d'un lis de jardin au naturel accosté de deux glands d'or et en pointe d'un rocher sommé d'une croix latine, le tout aussi d'or."
2 CONT
2 CONT  Références
2 CONT
2 CONT La Société historique acadienne
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Le Centre de généalogie francophone d'Amérique URL: http://www.genealogie.org Conception et réalisation: Le Cid (Le Centre internet de développement) 1997 © Tous droits réservés.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Huit enfants sont baptisés à Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame) (Orne, 61293): Charles-1, 07-04-1620; Antoinette, 06-08-1621; Pierre (pionnier), 01-08-1622; Nicolas (pionnier), 09-09-1625; Charles-2, 04-03-1628; Marie (pionnière), 22
2 CONC -01-1629; Marguerite (pionnière), 28-07-1631 et Madeleine (pionnière), née vers 1634 (rec. 1666). Selon les historiens, la maison familiale a brûlé avec Gaspard et son épouse Nicole à Trois-Rivières, en 1663.
2 CONT
