2 CONT GASPARD  BOUCHER
2 CONT
2 CONT Related to Marin Boucher. Arrived in Canada in 1634, Quebec in 1645
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mariage": Tiré de Tanguay: Gaspard Boucher compte parmis ses descendants, des
2 CONT Gouverneurs, des évêques, des Juges, etc, et un grand nombre des plus remarquables
2 CONT familles du Canada.
2 CONT -----------------------------
2 CONT Note:
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://membres.lycos.fr/ancetre/boucherG.htm> and was translated as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT GASPARD  BOUCHER
2 CONT
2 CONT Carpenter as his father Jacques Butcher, and son of Françoise Paigné, Gaspard Butcher was born about 1598, native of the parish of Loisé in Mortagne to the Perches.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of about twenty years, Gaspard marries about 1619 at Mamers, Nicole Lemère, daughter of unknown father and of Marie de Castrie her mother.  She is native of the city of Mamers, in the diocese The Mans, at the Maine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In fourteen years, the young woman has eight children, all born in Mortagne.  The couple lives in the parish of Our Lady, at Mortagne, when he engages himself to serve in the project of Robert Giffard.  In the spring of 1635 takes
2 CONC  place the big departure of the family, towards New France, to Quebec of today.
2 CONT
2 CONT To them arrived, welcomed by Champlain at the Fort St Louis, the family establishes itself in the seigneurie Notre-Dame-des-Anges where the father is hired by the Jesuits.  Their son, Pierre Boucher, that has no link in this généa
2 CONC logie, but deserves some lines, the following year, becomes the student of the Jesuits, he accompanies in their missions, learns the Huron language and becomes later, governor of the Three Rivers then, lord of Boucherville.
2 CONT
2 CONT The one that creates the link, Marguerite Boucher, the seventh child of the couple, baptized July 28 1631, at Our Lady of Mortagne, at the Perches, current department of the decorates, becomes the spouse December 25 1645, of ances
2 CONC tor Toussaint Toupin aka Bélair, born about 1616, of unknown origin.  He was small boat master and transported uniformly men and luggage between Quebec and Montréal, then possesses an earth in the suburbs of Three Rivers.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 21 1646, the ancestor Gaspard Boucher, becomes concessionnary of an earth of 24 acres at the Three Rivers.  And, after 1662, one does not hear anymore to speak, or of Nicole Lemère, or of Gaspard Boucher, of which one does n
2 CONC ot know where, when and how they deceased.  The historians agree to say that about 1668, the house burned with the said Gaspard and his spouse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their blood flows today in the veins of the descendants that we are.
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from:  <http://branches-n-twigs.com/genealogy/getperson.php?personID=I41982&tree=allfam&PHPSESSID=edb431a6107a9688b04b061bfdd3f513>.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher[1,2]
2 CONT 1599 - 1662
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth  Abt 1599  Mortagne-au-Perche, Orne, France   [1,2]
2 CONT Sex  Male
2 CONT Personal Information  Notes by Jane Goodrich  [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher was a wood joiner in the city of Mortagne. He learned his trade from his father, Jacques. He married Nicole Lemers before 1620. They had at least 8 children, four of whom left posterity in Canada, a son and three d
2 CONC aughters. Gaspards father had received a share of a seigneury in France and this was passed on to Gaspard. He sold this share, and signed this selling document with a flourish. Gaspar had many descendants who were seigneurs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perche, France was the birthplace of Quebec. It is from this town that Robert Giffard lived an recruited settlers for the first seigneury of the colony in 1634. It is likely that Gaspard intended to go with these first settlers wh
2 CONC o accompanied Giffard to Beauport, Canada. But it took him until the following spring to finish selling his properties and arranging his affairs. He sailed in 1635 on a ship under the command of Captain de Ville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once in Quebec, Gaspard worked as a wood joiner for the Jesuits at Notre-Dame-Des-Anges. His relationship with the Jesuits was to shape the history of his family and Canada. His son, Pierre ( 1622-1717) received his education from
2 CONC  the Jesuits. When later events in the dramatic life of Pierre brought him to Trois-Rivieres, Quebec, Gaspard and his family followed him. In March 1646 Gaspard received a grant of land next to the property of his son-in-law Etien
2 CONC ne de La Fond. We do not know the history of Gaspard and his wife from that time until their deaths. His house burned, and some assume he died in that fire, but there is no evidence to support this. Both he and his wife are absent
2 CONC  from the 1666 census.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane Goodrich's source:"One Hundred French Canadian Family Histories" by Phillip J. Moore
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigration  1635  [2]
2 CONT From France to Canada
2 CONT Died  20 May 1662  [1]
2 CONT Person ID  I41982  Linda's Tree
2 CONT Last Modified  30 Apr 2006
2 CONT
2 CONT Father  Jacques Boucher, b. 1547, Mortagne, Chatres, Perche, France
2 CONT Mother  Françoise Paigné, b. 1552, Mortagne, Chatres, Perche, France
2 CONT Family ID  F15800  Group Sheet
2 CONT
2 CONT Family  Nicole Lemaire, b. 1598, La Ventrouze-Au-Perche, France
2 CONT Married  1619  France   [1]
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT >1. Françoise-Marie Boucher, b. 1615
2 CONT 2. Charles Boucher, c. 7 Apr 1620, Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame), Orne, France
2 CONT 3. Antoinette Boucher, c. 6 Aug 1621, Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame), Orne, France
2 CONT >4. Pierre Boucher dit de Grosbois, b. 1622
2 CONT 5. Nicolas Boucher, c. 9 Sep 1625, Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame), Orne, France
2 CONT 6. Charles Boucher, c. 4 Mar 1628, Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame), Orne, France
2 CONT >7. Marie Boucher, c. 22 Jan 1629, Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame), Orne, France
2 CONT >8. Marie Marguerite Boucher, c. 28 Jul 1631, Mortagne-au-Perche (Notre-Dame), Orne, France
2 CONT >9. Madeleine Boucher, b. 1635, At Sea On A Ship To Canada
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Modified  15 Mar 2006
2 CONT Family ID  F15796  Group Sheet
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT 1. [S466]   Jane Goodrich, Jane Goodrich, (The original information in this record was furnished by Jane Goodrich. Her family has researched this line for many years. Any additional sources noted are the ones I consulted to verify
2 CONC  the information which Jane provided.)
2 CONT 2. [S549]   Fichier Origine, (This list is compiled by Marcel Fournier, coordinator, then revised and broadcasted by Denis Beauregard, webmaster, in the name of the Fédération québécoise des soci??tés de généalogie (Quebec Federat
2 CONC ion of Genealogical Societies) and the Fédération française de généalogie (France's Federation of Genealogy) at fichierorigine)
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.electracat.com/Linda2/i180.htm>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:"One Hundred French Canadian Family Histories" by Phillip J. Moore
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher was a wood joiner in the city of Mortagne. He learned his trade from his father, Jacques. He married Nicole Lemers before 1620. They had at least 8 children, four of whom left posterity in Canada, a son and three d
2 CONC aughters. Gaspards father had received a share of a seigneury in France and this was passed on to Gaspard. He sold this share, and signed this selling document with a flourish. Gaspard had many descendants who were seigneurs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perche, France was the birthplace of Quebec. It is from this town that Robert Giffard lived an recruited settlers for the first seigneury of the colony in 1634. It is likely that Gaspard intended to go with these first settlers wh
2 CONC o accompanied Giffard to Beauport, Canada. But it took him until the following spring to finish selling his properties and arranging his affairs. He sailed in 1635 on a ship under the command of Captain de Ville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once in Quebec, Gaspard worked as a wood joiner for the Jesuits at Notre-Dame-Des-Anges. His relationship with the Jesuits was to shape the history of his family and Canada. His son, Pierre (1622-1717) received his education from 
2 CONC the Jesuits. When later events in the dramatic life of Pierre brought him to Trois-Rivieres, Quebec, Gaspard and his family followed him. In March 1646 Gaspard received a grant of land next to the property of his son-in-law Etienn
2 CONC e de La Fond. We do not know the history of Gaspard and his wife from that time until their deaths. His house burned, and some assume he died in that fire, but there is no evidence to support this. Both he and his wife are absent 
2 CONC from the 1666 census.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following, inre to his son Pierre, was acquired from <http://www.newadvent.org/cathen/02710b.htm>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre Boucher
2 CONT Born at Lagny, a village near Mortagne in the Perche, France, 1622, died at Boucherville, 1717. In 1634 he went to Canada with his father Gaspard Boucher, a simple joiner. At the age of eighteen he entered the service of the Jesui
2 CONC t Fathers and went to their Huron missions at Georgian Bay. On his return to Quebec in 1641 he served as a soldier in the garrison of that city. In 1645 he was interpreter of Indian languages at Three Rivers and in 1648 commissary
2 CONC -general of the trading station at that place. Elected captain of the militia in 1651, while in command of the place during an interim in 1653 he repelled an Iroquois attack. Owing to his efficient defence he was placed in charge 
2 CONC of the city, and retained the position until 1658. In 1661 he was sent to France to represent the needs of the colonies and plead the cause of the inhabitants. On his return to Canada, in 1662, he was reappointed Governor of Three
2 CONC  Rivers, an office which he resigned in 1667 when he withdrew from public affairs to found the seigniorial parish called after his name Boucherville, situated opposite Montreal. He was succeeded in the governorship of Three Rivers
2 CONC  by his son-in-law, René Gauthier de Varennes, forbear of the discoverer of Western Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1664 Pierre Boucher had printed at Paris by the press of Florentin Lambert "L'histoire veritable et naturelle des moeurs et productions du pays de la Nouvelle-France, vulgairement dite le Canada". This work was published in 184
2 CONC 9 in "L'Album Canadien", in 1882 at Montreal, and in 1896 in the "Mémoires de la Société Royale du Canada". An English translation appeared in 1883. Pierre Boucher is considered the best type of a Canadian landed proprietor, fille
2 CONC d with piety, rectitude, and honor. At his death he left a numerous posterity. The family is still in existence, and the highest stations are filled by members bearing the names Boucherville, Bruere, Niverville, Grosbois, and Mont
2 CONC izambert. Pierre Boucher was the first Canadian colonist to be ennobled by King Louis XIV. His letters of nobility, dated 1661, were renewed in 1707.
2 CONT -----------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from <http://www.grandesfamilles.org/Boucher/01.html>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher (Jacques & Françoise Paigné)
2 CONT
2 CONT from Mortagne, Perche, France; arrived 1634; cited 30-01-1645 Québec; carpenter at Notre-Dame de Mortagne
2 CONT died between 20-05-1662 and the census of '66, at Trois-Rivières
2 CONT married about 1619, at Mamers, Maine France
2 CONT Nicole Lemaire (... & Marie Castrie)
2 CONT from Mamers, Maine, France
2 CONT died between 19-06-1652 and the census of '66, at Trois-Rivières
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT Charles
2 CONT baptized 07-04-1620 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT died 17-04-1620 at Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT Antoinette
2 CONT baptized 06-08-1621 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT died in France
2 CONT Pierre    (see below for more info about Pierre Boucher)
2 CONT baptized 01-08-1622 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT Nicolas
2 CONT baptized 09-09-1625 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT buried 23-03-1649 at Trois-Rivières
2 CONT Charles
2 CONT baptized 04-03-1628 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT died in France
2 CONT Marie
2 CONT baptized 22-01-1629 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT Marguerite
2 CONT baptized 28-07-1631 at Notre-Dame de Mortagne, Perche, France
2 CONT Madeleine  (for more on Madeleine Boucher see below)
2 CONT born about 1634 (age 32 in the census of '66 and 67, age 47 in the census of '81)
2 CONT died 13 buried 14-09-1691 at Trois-Rivières
2 CONT marriage contract 18-11-1647, déposed 29-05-1650 with notary Audouart (Trois-Rivières)
2 CONT Urbain Beaudry aka Lamarche (Jean & Jeanne Bertin)
2 CONT shown in the censuses of '66, '67 and '81, at Trois-Rivières; cited 20-08-1646 at Trois-Rivières; tool maker
2 CONT baptized 06-01-1615 at Luché-Pringé, Anjou, France
2 CONT died 22 buried 23-08-1682 at Trois-Rivières
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from:  <http://www.angelfire.com/rnb/madore/bio/Gaspard_Boucher.html>
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard Boucher
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestor on the Pitt and Presse lines.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the founders of New France.
2 CONT
2 CONT They left Mortagne, France, in the spring of 1635 with their four children:  Pierre, Nicolas, Marie and Marguerite.  They were a familly of six when they borded the ship in France but when they arrived in Quebec, they were a famil
2 CONC ly of seven.  Madeleine was born on the ship.  We descend from both Marguerite & Madeleine.
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION: Carpenter
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaspard was a cabinet maker and joiner in France. Cyprien Tanguay lists his place of birth as Langy, Diocese of Mortagne, France.  Tanguay also says that he is the brother of Marin Boucher.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT The following was acquired from: http://www.geocities.com/hvidsten_m/Boucher.html>.  This information does not agree with the information that I have on the Boucher Family, but it does mention Gaspard Boucher and his son Pierre Bo
2 CONC ucher as early settlers in Canada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boucher Family History
2 CONT
2 CONT Quatrin Boucher m. Jeanne Denis in St. Etienne, Chaix, dioc. Maillezais, Poitou, France.  Their son Jean Boucher m. Marie Madeleine Pare on Nov 12, 1678 in Ste. Anne, Beaupre, Montmorency, Quebec.  My line of descent  moves with t
2 CONC heir daughter Elisabeth as shown below.  The name Boucher is a rather famous one in Quebec.  The area of Boucherville (which many of my lines descend from) was named for Pierre Boucher, who was born in Mortagne, Perche, France, wh
2 CONC ich is in the area known today as Normandy.
2 CONT
2 CONT (the following is about Pierre Boucher)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre came to Canada in 1635 with his parents, Gaspard and Nicole (Lemaire) Boucher and his sisters Marie, Marguerite, and Madeleine (born at sea).  When he was young, Pierre traveled extensively with Jesuit missionaries througho
2 CONC ut New France and learned the native languages of Huron, Algonquin, Montagnais, and Iroquois.  He was very well respected, having many responsibilities, including often acting as an interpreter.  At age 22, he settled in the city 
2 CONC of Trois-Rivieres, later becoming Governor of that same city.in 1653.  He founded Boucherville after resigning as Governor in 1667.  He died April 19, 1717 at the age of 95.  He married twice in his life: first to a Huron native n
2 CONC amed Marie Ouebadinskoue but had no children with her, second to Jeanne Crevier in 1652 and the couple had 15 children.  I do not know at this point whether or not Pierre and Quatrin Boucher were related, but the families were fro
2 CONC m the same area of Quebec, so it is possible.
2 CONT Quatrin Boucher's descendents, in the form of the LaRoche family, came to the US and settled in North Dakota just before the census of 1870.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------
2 CONT <http://www.fortlangley.ca/pepin/008_boucher/pafg03.htm> shows the following on Gaspard's daughter Madeleine:
2 CONT
2 CONT Madeleine BOUCHER 1 (Gaspard , ... ) was christened 2 in 1634. was buried 3 on 4 Sep 1691 in l'eglise des Trois Rivières, et enregistrés le 14 sept 1691 à Montréal.
2 CONT
2 CONT Madeleine married 2 I-- Urbain BAUDREAU dit Lamarche 1, son of -- Jean BAUDREAU and -- Jeanne BERTIN, in 1647. Urbain was christened in 1621. He was buried on 23 Aug 1682 in L'eglise des Trois Rivières. Urbain was employed as bour
2 CONC geois (taillandier) = edge tool maker 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT + 16 F i Marie BAUDREAU was christened on 2 Dec 1650 and was buried on 11 Dec 1734.
2 CONT 17 M ii Joseph BAUDREAU 1 was christened 2 on 13 Nov 1653 in Québec.
2 CONT + 18 M iii II-- Guillaume BAUDRY dit Des Buttes was christened on 2 Oct 1656 and was buried on 26 Feb 1732.
2 CONT + 19 F iv Jeanne BAUDREAU dit Lamarche was christened on 17 Jul 1659 and was buried on 22 Nov 1700.
2 CONT + 20 F v Madeleine BAUDREAU was christened on 19 Nov 1661 and was buried on 20 Nov 1730.
2 CONT + 21 F vi Marguerite BAUDRY dit Lamarche was christened on 22 Mar 1665 and was buried on 24 Apr 1706.
2 CONT 22 F vii Françoise BAUDREAU 1 was christened 2 on 3 Feb 1668.
2 CONT Françoise married 2 I-- Jacques RONDEAU 1, son of -- Jean-Baptiste RONDEAU and -- Marie GACHINAT, on 6 Nov 1691 in Trois-Rivières. Jacques was christened 3 in 1663.
2 CONT + 23 F viii Marie-Madeleine BAUDREAU was christened on 7 Jul 1671 and was buried on 26 May 1699.
2 CONT + 24 M ix II-- Joseph BAUDREAU dit Lamarche was christened on 19 Oct 1673.
2 CONT + 25 M x II-- Jacques BAUDREAU was christened on 13 Sep 1676.
2 CONT 26 F xi Anne BAUDREAU 1 was christened 2 on 24 Jun 1680.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Madeleine BOUCHER
