1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Notes'''
2 CONT * Location info: [https://www.geni.com/projects/Anjou-province-France/24760 Anjou, France] (birth), [https://www.geni.com/projects/Trois-Rivi%C3%A8res-Qu%C3%A9bec-Canada/24574 Trois-Rivières] (marriage,death)
2 CONT ** Place de naissance alternative : Impasse du Petit Anjou, Bécon-les-Granits, Anjou.
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/projects/Pioneers-of-Canada-Quebec/17839 Pionnier de la Nouvelle-France]. Arrive au Québec le 12 septembre 1665 à bord du ''[http://www.geni.com/projects/Passagers-du-Saint-S%C3%A9bastien-1665/25021 Saint-S
2 CONC ébastien]'' avec le [http://www.geni.com/projects/R%C3%A9giment-de-Carignan-Sali%C3%A8res/16545 Régiment de Carignan-Salières], avec lequel il était lieutenant.
2 CONT * Gouverneur de Trois-Rivière c1667-1689.
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ren%C3%A9_Gaultier_de_Varennes Wikipedia], [http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ren%C3%A9_Gaultier_de_Varennes Wikipédia]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT
2 CONT René Gaultier de Varennes (c 1635 – 4 June 1689) is best known in Canadian history as being one of the early governors of Trois-Rivières, Quebec and the father of Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La Vérendrye, a famous Canadi
2 CONC an explorer and fur trader. Part of the Ancien Régime, the Gaultier family of aristocrats came from the Anjou area of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT René married the daughter of Pierre Boucher, the governor of Trois-Rivières in 1667 and part of the marriage contract contained a request to the governor of New France, Daniel de Rémy de Courcelle, that he should retain the office
2 CONC  and the privileges of his father-in-law when Boucher retired. Boucher did retire in 1667 and René became governor.
