1 TITL Sr
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} VALCOURT, Mary Estelle (William & Estelle SMITH) b. 23 Dec. 1896 (GC Ch.: v.4, p.31)
2 CONT -------
2 CONT  Source: Southwest Louisiana Records (1750 – 1900) Book by [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=88080739  Rev. Donald J. Hébert ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sr Marie Estelle de Valcourt 
2 CONT -------
2 CONT I called the Religious of the Sacred Heart in New Orleans and I asked about their cemetery.  The lady on the telephone told me that all of the Sisters from New Orleans  were moved or buried in Grand Coteau.
2 CONT
2 CONT **************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT  Estelle de Valcourt 
2 CONT
2 CONT GRAND COTEAU- Funer- al services will be held at Sacred Heart Convent Chapel at 3 p.m. today for Mother Es- telle deValcourt, 70, who died at the Sacred Heart Convent at 4:15 p.m. Saturday, Burial will be in the convent cemeter- r
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT Survivors include four sis- ters, Mrs. Carl Bourg, Mrs. Vincent McManus and Mrs. R.R. Dailey, all of Port Ar- thur, Tex., and Mrs. O. El- mer of Groves, Tex.: eight brothers. W.T. deValcourt of  Metairie and J.A. deValcourt of New
2 CONC  Orleans; Joseph and T.I. deValcourt of Port Arthur, Tex., Tom deValcourt of Sun- set, W.W. deValcourt and H.S. deValcourt of Lafayette and Robert deValcourt of Groves, Tex.: and numerous nieces and nephews.
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of Grand Coteau, the deceased was the daughter  of the late William deValcourt and Estelle Smith.  She en- tered the convent in 1922 and  was professed in 1930.  She  served in both Grand Coteau and New Orleans.  Her ance
2 CONC s- tors gave the ground for the present site of the Sacred Heart Convent.  The body will be in the convent chapel after ?11? a. m. today and will re- main there until time for ser- vices. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Steve and Louise de Valcourt's personal collection.
2 CONT
2 CONT **********************************
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sr. Estelle de Valcourt RSCJ 
2 CONT
2 CONT Coadjutrix sister of the Sacred Heart Order (RSCJ). Began novitiate 2 June 1922 and made First Vows 15 Aug 1924, at Grand Coteau, LA. Final Profession of Vows occurred on 27 Dec 1930 in New Orleans. Served at Sacred Heart Schools 
2 CONC in Grand Coteau and New Orleans and also lived at Villa Duchesne in St. Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT  SISTER ESTELLE de VALCOURT GRAND COTEAU S.C.J.M. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sister Estelle de Valcourt lived her religious life with unassuming and simple virtue.  She had holy pride in the faith of her family and its numerous religious vocations, whereas others might be impressed by her high sounding Fre
2 CONC nch name, her lineage recorded from the early eighteenth century, and her relationship to the donor of Grand Coteau, Mrs. Charles Smith, and Mary Hardy who became [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=84244679 Rev
2 CONC erend Mother Aloysia Hardy,] Assistant General of the Society of the Sacred Heart.  The notes written after the death of dear Sister de Valcourt describe a religious who was quietly faithful, always obedient, fulfilling her duty w
2 CONC ithout noise or fanfare.  One who shared community life with her seemed to have flashed a camera upon her when she jotted down;  " I vividly remember Sister de Valcourt when I was with her at Grand Coteau.  Her beautiful bearing, 
2 CONC dignity, kindness, and efficiency impressed me.  Living with her in community reinforced and deepened these impressions.  Her great love for her family and theirs for her were paramount, too.  I remember the parlor with the huge p
2 CONC ainting of the Immaculate Conception filled with brothers and sisters and her parents - sometimes sitting almost silent.  Someone would ask for a match, and Sister would be the one to produce a pack from her pocket.  Their laughte
2 CONC r would resound.  It would be quite a ceremony for the port tress to serve them Coca-Cola.  Later on, Sister de Valcourt was as smiling and helpful in the community of the Rosary.  She returned to Grand Coteau at intervals to see 
2 CONC her parents grown too old and ill to reach New Orleans.  She would emerge from good-bye at their car, suffering herself now, yet struggling with a determined smile.  What a simple, beautiful, heroic, religious life I remember!"  "
2 CONC If there is little to say about her, "writes another who knew her well, "it is just because she was so sweet and gentle and unassuming and always ready to help."  But God exalts the humble.  He showered so much peace, that He deep
2 CONC ened our insights into her long years of goodness and fervor.  Estelle de Valcourt was born at Grand Coteau, Louisiana, on December 23, 1896.  She was baptized on February 14, 1897.  She was the oldest of the patriarchal family of
2 CONC  thirteen children:  eight boys and five girls.  twelve are her survivors, and at her Requiem Mass, July 16, 1967, their faith and generosity in sacrifice greatly edified [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=sch&
2 CONC GSiman=1&GScid=640777&GRid=129893745&  Bishop Maurice Schexnayder], several Jesuits, and a multitude of relatives and friends.  She had loved her own to the end."  Three of her sisters wrote of her: " She had loved her own to the 
2 CONC end. " Three of her sisters wrote of her:  " The most outstanding thing I could say, I am sure , recalls her devotion to duty.  Our family had become poor.  She was the oldest, and therefore she was taken out of school when she ha
2 CONC d completed the fifth grade, because our mother needed help at home.  I was allowed to continue my studies through the eighth grade.  As in her last illness, she cheerfully accepted what was asked of her, never expressing a feelin
2 CONC g of bitterness and regret because she was asked to make this sacrifice.  She was always faithful to her duties.  When she felt that the family could replace her, she answered her religious vocation.  The children were growing up,
2 CONC  our grandmother had died, and when I married, Surely God blessed Estelle. "  " It fell to her lot to take care of us, her younger brothers and sisters.  She did it wonderfully well, with deep respect and loyalty for her parents. 
2 CONC  She always did what they requested and ignored her own wishes.  No sacrifice was too costly for her as she devoted herself to the family patiently and happily. " " During the forty-five years Estelle spent in the religious life w
2 CONC e never had a doubt about her happiness. " Sister de Valcourt's very short journal of retreat notes reveals the limpid simplicity of her interior life.  She went straight to God with love and confidence.  " We are here to seek God
2 CONC , and in seeking Him we gain perfection. "  " May I accept with humble submission the little humiliations that come to me because I represent a different section. " "I want to build up my interior life by accepting with conformity
2 CONC  to God's will the hard work that comes to me through obedience. " " Our charity should make us grow in our realizing that the Eucharist is a family banquet. " " God gives us a blank sheet which we are to sign.  He puts the number
2 CONC  1. We add zeros to it.  What we add, that we will be for all eternity. " " I resolve to accept generously the mortification implied in the fulfillment of my employment, especially being tied to it at all times. "  God's poor have
2 CONC  claims on me. " " Dear Lord, you know the depths of my sinfulness, but you know also that I love you. " "We can not omit the following resolution upon which our Lord smiles: " To accept with conformity and union the difficulties 
2 CONC and disagreeable things that come up in my work.  And since I seem to be a good observer, not to observe others, especially Sister X and Sister Y. " It was in her last retreat that she wrote: " Look up, not down.  Look forward not
2 CONC  backwards.  Look outward, not inside. " It was at Grand Coteau that Sister de Valcourt entered the Society of the Sacred Heart as a coadjutrix Sister, on December 7, 1921. She received the habit on June 2, 1922.  The feast of her
2 CONC  first vows coincided with Assumption Day of 1924.  The Rosary welcomed her in 1927, and in her new home she continued to be generous in hidden devotedness.  On the feast of St. John, December 27. 1930, [http://findagrave.com/cgi-
2 CONC bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=shaw&GSbyrel=in&GSdy=1934&GSdyrel=in&GSst=20&GScntry=4&GSob=n&GRid=62690149&df=all&   Archbishop Shaw] presided at the ceremony or her profession.  It was with joy that the Society bound itself to her irrev
2 CONC ocably.  She wore her cross and ring as a true daughter of Saint Madeleine Sophie until the day of her death, July 15, 1967.  At intervals, she exchanged houses, living at Grand Coteau, Villa Duchesne, and the Rosary, but her stay
2 CONC  in St. Louis was of short duration.  Usually she was refectorian or sacristine, and in her hidden work she grew in grace and wisdom, like our Lord in His Nazareth years. Sister de Valcourt was still an active worker in the sacris
2 CONC ty when in May of 1967 the doctor informed her in a straightforward manner that she was so critically ill her death was imminent.  Her serenity remained unimpaired.  " Let not your heart be troubled," echoed back to her from the L
2 CONC ast Supper.  Our chaplain, [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GSln=coyle&GSiman=1&GScid=1966001&GRid=60438655&  Father Coyle, S.J]., gave her the sacrament of the sick in Lourdes Hospital of Lafayette. Home again, s
2 CONC he gathered her family around her for a joyful reunion under the oaks, and then she valiantly shared much of our community life.  Our prayers for our dear Sister were most bountifully heard, especially on her last day with us, Jul
2 CONC y 15.  She was conscious in the morning and received Viaticum from the chalice of the Mass.  The sacrament of the sick had been renewed.  She went to Confession as always on Saturday mornings.  The nurse remembers that she laughed
2 CONC  over an amusing blunder.  She enjoyed the letter of a little niece who wrote: " If you die, you will go to heaven because you are so kind. " The reading of the letter was hardly finished when Sister de Valcourt suddenly fell into
2 CONC  deep unconsciousness.  The community recited the liturgical prayers for the dying.  Providentially, a visiting priest entered her room at four o'clock, almost the hour of her death, and gave her a blessing and one more absolution
2 CONC .  She went into her bright eternity in peace outpoured upon her from the pierced Heart of Christ. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Letter Post Marked August 29, 2012
2 CONT  Society of the Sacred Heart Archives  United States Province 4537 West Pine Boulevard St. Louis MO 63108-2109 Province Archives Mary Lou Gaven RSCJ Assistant Archivist
2 CONT
2 CONT ************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT  [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=75209338  Mr. H. S. de Valcourt] V.P. 20 c/o Fleet Post Office San Francisco, California Post Marked APR 11 A.M. 1944 Grand Coteau, LA APR 15 A.M. Answered 4/16/44 S.C.J.M. G
2 CONC rand Coteau, La Easter Sunday  Stamped Fond Easter Greetings
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=75209338  Stephen], I received both letter and card you sent, and was especially glad of the nice long letter, let me tell you that I sincerely hope that all your guesses
2 CONC  come true.  I do not know just what you mean, by moving down under and south of here.  It would be grand if you did get off for the length of time you mentioned especially the longest.  And as to your hopes for [http://www.findag
2 CONC rave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=75209387  Louise], I have thanked God to day for her. I also had an Easter card from her, with a little word on it, she thanks me for the friendliness I showed her, and announced that she expec
2 CONC ts to become my sister soon so'..now I have had it from both.  But from what she said it sounds like it won't be here.  I would like to send her a card, but I have no address, except the postmark of Many. I am wondering if that is
2 CONC  sufficient I really meant to ask Mamma if she knew it and it slipped me.  I had a big surprise this after noon, I expected Papa and Mamma and Mildred but low and behold, when I was called to the parlor who did I see, but Phil,+ C
2 CONC arl + Carl Jr., Robert and Ivis + Bobbie Lee,+ Jimmy, and Mildred and her Carl, so you see there was a big crowd, Carl has gotten a furlough, and is moving to another place for, he thinks about three months, and then will go acros
2 CONC s, Phil is staying at home for the present.  I hope that he won't have to go across.  I suppose that you have herd that [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=111275728  Joe] had to leave [http://www.findagrave.com
2 CONC /cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=115235255  Hattie] and little [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=140084474  Starline], two weeks ago, they say it was pretty hard on the poor fellow, And little [http://www.findagra
2 CONC ve.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=140084474  Starline] is showing that she is missing him very much.  He is at a camp or Tyler Texas.  I hope that you have had a very happy Easter, our chapel was really beautiful, with a profusio
2 CONC n of roses and ferns.  I would like to know just when you will have the matrimonial knot tied, if possible, if not let me know as soon after as possible. With much love,
2 CONT  Estelle R.S.C.J.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ************************************
2 CONT
2 CONT   Sister's Habit Received. Grand Coteau, La., June 4.--Miss Estelle Devalcourt, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. William Devalcourt of Sunset and granddaughter of Judge and Mrs. J. A. Smith of Grand Coteau, received the sister's habit at 
2 CONC the Sacred Heart academy in the presence of a large number of relatives and friends.  The [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=45147279  Reverend J. B. Frankhauser. S.J]., pastor of the Sacred Heart Church, preac
2 CONC hed a sermon on the religious life. After the ceremonies breakfast was served to a large number.
2 CONT -------
