1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Allain
2 CONT
2 CONT William Alexander "Bill" Allain (February 14, 1928 – December 2, 2013) was an American politician who held office as the 59th Governor of Mississippi as a Democrat from 1984 to 1988.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Washington in Adams County near Natchez, Mississippi. He attended the University of Notre Dame in South Bend, Indiana, and received his law degree from the University of Mississippi School of Law at Oxford. Allain s
2 CONC erved in the United States Army infantry in the Korean War. A Catholic, he was a member of the American Legion and Veterans of Foreign Wars. After the war, he practiced law in Natchez, Mississippi, until his appointment as assista
2 CONC nt state attorney general in 1962.
2 CONT
2 CONT Allain was elected state attorney general in 1979, having defeated the Republican State Senator Charles W. Pickering of Laurel. Allain earned a reputation as a consumer advocate. He fought utility rate increases and stopped the st
2 CONC orage of nuclear waste in Mississippi. State labor president Claude Ramsay sought to broker an agreement between Democratic Party presidential candidate Walter F. Mondale and Allain when the latter sought a veto over the federal s
2 CONC torage of nuclear waste in Mississippi as a condition for his political support of Mondale.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also fought the powerful Mississippi Legislature, which for decades had diluted executive branch power by appointing legislators to executive department boards and commissions. The Mississippi Supreme Court, at Allain's insiste
2 CONC nce, struck the practice as a violation of the constitutional principle of separation of powers. The resulting decision, Allain v. Alexander, is sometimes referred to as "Mississippi's Marbury vs. Madison," after the landmark deci
2 CONC sion of the United States Supreme Court which delineated the powers of the three branches of the federal government. Allain's efforts strengthened the Mississippi executive and streamlined Mississippi's political processes. Allain
2 CONC  as governor instituted a legal panel to study the possibility of re-writing the 1890 Bourbon state constitution and created an administrative task force of state agency heads to reduce the use of illegal drugs. He was unsuccessfu
2 CONC l in the former and successful in the later case, particularly in the interdiction and seizure of almost a ton of cocaine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1983, while Allain was running for governor against Republican candidate Leon Bramlett of Clarksdale, private detective Rex Armistead, formerly with the Mississippi State Sovereignty Commission, helped to spread rumors that All
2 CONC ain had sexual intercourse with two African-American male transvestites. Allain denied the charges. The transvestites went on the record with a lie detector but in 1984 claimed they had never met Allain and had been paid for their
2 CONC  testimony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Allain died December 2, 2013, in Jackson, Mississippi.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William Allain was born on 14 February 1928 in the Adams County community of Washington. He was educated at the University of Notre Dame and the University of Mississippi, where he earned a law degree in 1950. Allain served in the
2 CONC  US infantry for three years during the Korean War, spending significant time in combat. He practiced law in Natchez from his discharge in 1953 until 1962, when he was appointed assistant state attorney general.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1983, while serving as Mississippi’s attorney general, Allain filed a suit asking the state supreme court to separate the functions of the executive and legislative branches of state government, especially in the budgetary proc
2 CONC ess. Members of the legislature commonly served on boards, commissions, and agencies in the executive branch, but Allain asserted that Mississippi’s 1890 constitution required a separation of powers and that legislative officials 
2 CONC could not serve in the executive branch. The Mississippi Supreme Court ruled in favor of the attorney general and ordered the two branches to remain separate. That ruling strengthened the executive branch of state government, espe
2 CONC cially the office of governor, which is considered one of the weakest chief executives in the nation. The court’s mandate was carried out in the Administrative Reorganization Act of 1984.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1962 to 1975 Allain represented the State of Mississippi in cases before state courts, the federal district court, the federal circuit court of appeals, and the US Supreme Court. In 1979 he was elected state attorney general,
2 CONC  a post in which he built a strong reputation as a consumer advocate. Two of his most notable achievements were the prevention of a utility rate increase and the exclusion of Mississippi as a nuclear waste site. In his successful 
2 CONC 1983 campaign for governor, Allain, a Democrat, carried seventy-four of the state’s eighty-two counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after his inauguration as governor on 10 January 1984, Allain appointed a 250-member commission to study the state’s 1890 constitution. After a thorough review, the commission drafted a new constitution, and Allain recomme
2 CONC nded its adoption. The state legislature, however, took no action on the proposal.
2 CONT
2 CONT A constitutional amendment restructuring the state board of education, part of the Education Reform Act of 1982, was implemented during Governor Allain’s administration. That amendment provided for an appointed superintendent of e
2 CONC ducation and a nine-member board of education.
2 CONT
2 CONT As governor, Allain continued to work to remove members of the legislature from boards that were part of the executive branch. Though unpopular with many legislators, who feared that the law gave too much power to the governor, Al
2 CONC lain’s proposal was passed by the legislature in 1984.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1985 Allain appointed Reuben Anderson to the Mississippi Supreme Court, making him the state’s first African American justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT A constitutional amendment allowing the governor to succeed himself was passed near the end of Allain’s term, with his strong endorsement. Allain considered running for reelection but eventually decided not to seek a second term.
2 CONT
2 CONT After leaving office in January 1988, Allain resumed the practice of law in Jackson. He died there on 2 December 2013.
