1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Sources:'''
2 CONT **1671 Acadie Census - shown to be 96 years old which estimates birth to be 1575.
2 CONT -https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Gaudet-21
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Arrived in Acadie in 1636, native of Martaize region of Loudun.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married twice; 4 children.
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT Jean Gaudet (abt. 1575 - bef. 1678)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean "Jehan" Gaudet aka Godet
2 CONT
2 CONT Born about 1575 in Martaize, Loudun, Vienne, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of [father unknown] and [mother unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT [sibling(s) unknown]
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Unknown (Inconnu) Gaudet — married about 1621 in France
2 CONT
2 CONT Husband of Nicole Colleson — married about 1652 in Acadie
2 CONT
2 CONT Father of Françoise Gaudet, Denis Gaudet, Marie Gaudet and Jean Gaudet
2 CONT
2 CONT Died before 1678 in Port Royal, Acadie, Nouvelle-France
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTICE: this profile is protected by the Acadian Project because of frequent duplication, variant name spelling, attempts to add unsourced parents or is an historically important person, in the Top 100 highly viewed Acadian profil
2 CONC es. Please contact the Acadian Project before making any substantive changes. Thanks for helping make WikiTree the best site for accurate information.
2 CONT
2 CONT Origins. Some family trees list Jean Gaudet as originating from Martaizé (Vienne) France. The Belle-Isle-en-Mer declarations in 1767 indicate that Acadian descendants of Jean Gaudet's daughters Marie and Françoise believed that th
2 CONC ey came from France with their husbands. Thus Massignon[1] concludes that the Acadian surnames of Gaudet, Hebert, and LeBlanc were already allied in France. Moreover, a certain Jean Gaudet was censitaire in 1634 on land at Martaiz
2 CONC é (Vienne) in the Seigneurie d’Aulnay. [2] A part of those that arrived in Acadia during the 17th century, were native of this commune. However, Massignon's research failed to find any relevant baptismal or marriage records.
2 CONT
2 CONT White and Godbout interpret the phrasing of the Belle-Isle-en-Mer declarations regarding coming from France with their spouse as not necessarily meaning that the two came together and at the same time, but simply that both the hus
2 CONC band and wife had come from France.
2 CONT
2 CONT First Spouse and Children. Some family trees identify Jean Gaudet's first spouse as Marie Daussy. This is based on a discussion in a French periodical Nova Francia (Spring 1927, Vol. II, p.188) describing a Gaudet family Jean from
2 CONC  Abbeville Picardy who was 59 years old in 1727, the son of Jean Gaudet and Marie Daussy. The birth date for this Jean Gaudet Jr. (about 1668 in France) is inconsistent with the Acadian Jean Gaudet Jr, whose parents (Jean and Nico
2 CONC le Colleson) were settled in Port Royal Acadia at that time.[3] Another hypothesis is that Jean's first spouse was Amerindian, and that Jean's daughters had two different mothers. Mitochondrial DNA from several descendants of Mari
2 CONC e and Francoise indicate that their haplogroup was European (J), and identical to one another, indicating that they were sisters of the same European mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrival in Acadia. The Gaudet families are found in the 1671 Acadian Census but when did they arrive there? Various time frames are proposed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) The ancestor Acadien arrived in America, shortly after 1634. Father Léopold Lanctot, o.m.i., claims that Jehan Godet arrived in Acadia very early, by 1610, with Charles de Biencourt. Some authors think that his first wife was M
2 CONC icmac but this is not very easy to prove. In the work " A feudal colony in America " Edme Rameau hinted in a note that a Metis branch that would have been a part of this family. On the other hand, if he arrived with Razilly by 163
2 CONC 4, his first children were born in France and his first wife would not be of MicMac origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Others think that Jean GODET arrived in Acadie by 1610, or even with Champlain in 1604, would have returned to France to look there for his family. This version is more reconcilable with the tenants of those who believe that he
2 CONC  might have left by 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) In view of his age, he could have been part of the first expedition of Poutrincourt. He is one of those rare French that remained in Acadie after the siege of Port Royal by the Kirk brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4)Stephen White[4] lists the children's marriages as follows based on the ages of their first known children: Françoise married an unknown Mercier c1644 and Daniel LeBlanc c1650. Marie married Etienne Hébert c1650. Daniel LeBlanc 
2 CONC was one of the signatories attesting to the accomplishments of d'Aulnay in Acadia. Since d'Aulnay died in May 1650[5], Daniel likely arrived in Acadia before that time.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it is not clear when Jean arrived, the timeline below sets out some of the events in his life in context with the history of Acadian settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Gaudet migrated from France to Acadia.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Father Archange Godbout described Jean Gaudet as the Abraham of Acadia, because of his numerous descendants."[6] Jean was the ancestor of 10% of the little Acadian settlement, with his two sons and two daughters, 22 grandchildren
2 CONC  and 10 great-grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Gaudet was born around 1575.[4] About 1622, he married a woman whose name is unknown. The couple had 3 children born in France: [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie-Francoise, b. 1623 Denis, b. 1625 Marie, b. 1633
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1652, Jean married a second time, to Nicole Colleson. Around 1653 they had a son Jean.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1671 Jean Gaudet, age 96, is the oldest inhabitant of Port-Royal. He was living with his second wife Nicole, age 64, and their son Jean. His three oldest children were married.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean died before 1678.[4][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Timeline
2 CONT
2 CONT c1575 birth, in France c1622 marriage to Unknown Spouse c1623 birth, daughter Françoise c1625 birth, son Denis c1633 birth, daughter Marie 1632 Treaty Saint-Germain-en-Laye cedes Acadia to France; Razilly brings ~300 elite men[9] 
2 CONC 1636 Arrival of the first French families to settle permanantly[1] c1650 Jean's daughters marry in France before Oct. 1650 Jean's son in law Daniel LeBlanc arrives in Acadia with wife Françoise Gaudet c1652 Jean marries 2nd wife N
2 CONC icole Colleson c1653 birth, son Jean 1654 British capture Port-Royal; French settlement ceases 1667-70 Treaty of Breda cedes Acadia to the French; settlement resumes[10] 1671 residence, in Port-Royal before 1678 death
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographie
2 CONT
2 CONT "Le Père Archange Godbout a décrit Jean Gaudet comme étant l'Abraham de l'Acadie, tant nombreuse est sa descendance.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Gaudet est né vers 1575.[4] Il épousa une femme inconnue vers 1622.[4] Entre 1623 et 1633, le couple a eu 3 enfants: Françoise, Denis, et Marie.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Vers 1652, Jean épousa Nicole Colleson. Vers 1653, le couple a eu un fils Jean.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean est mort avant 1678.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
