2 CONT
2 CONT «b»Discussion«/b»
2 CONT «b»Origins. «/b»
2 CONT Some family trees list Jean Gaudet as originating from Martaizé (Vienne) France. The Belle-Isle-en-Mer declarations in 1767 indicate that Acadian descendents of Jean Gaudet's daughters Marie and Françoise believed that they came f
2 CONC rom France with their husbands. Thus Massignon concludes that the Acadian surnames of Gaudet, Hebert, and LeBlanc were already allied in France. Moreover, a certain Jean Gaudet, was censistaire in 1634 on land at Martaizé (Vienne)
2 CONC  in the Seigneurie d'Aulnay. A part of those that arrived in Acadia during the 17th century, were native of this commune. However, Massignon's research failed to find any relevant baptismal or marriage records.
2 CONT
2 CONT White and Godbout interpret the phrasing of the Belle-Isle-en-Mer declarations regarding coming from France with their spouse as not necessarily meaning that the two came together and at the same time, but simply that both the hus
2 CONC band and wife had come from France.
2 CONT
2 CONT «b»First Spouse and Children. «/b»
2 CONT Some family trees identify Jean Gaudet's first spouse as Marie Daussy. This is based on a discussion in a French periodical Nova Francia (Spring 1927, Vol. II, p.188) describing a Gaudet family Jean from Abbeville Picardy who was 
2 CONC 59 years old in 1727, the son of Jean Gaudet and Marie Daussy. The birth date for this Jean Gaudet Jr. (about 1668 in France) is inconsistent with the Acadian Jean Gaudet Jr, whose parents (Jean and Nicole Colleson) were settled i
2 CONC n Port Royal Acadia at that time.[3] Another hypothesis is that Jean's first spouse was Amerindian, and that Jean's daughters had two different mothers. Mitochondrial DNA from several descendents of Marie and Francoise indicate th
2 CONC at their haplogroup was European (J), and identical to one another, indicating that they were sisters of the same European mother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrival in Acadia. The Gaudet families are found in the 1671 Acadian Census but when did they arrive there? Various time frames are proposed.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) The ancestor Acadien arrived in America, shortly after 1634. Father Léopold Lanctot, o.m.i., claims that Jehan Godet arrived in Acadia very early, by 1610 , with Charles de Biencourt. Some authors think that his first wife was 
2 CONC Micmac but this is not very easy to prove. In the work " A feudal colony in America " Edme Rameau hinted in a note th at a Metis branch that would have been a part of this family. On the other hand if he arrived with Razilly by 16
2 CONC 34, his first children were born in France and his first wife would not be of MicMac origin.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Others think that Jean GODET arrived in Acadie by 1610, or even with Champlain in 1604, would have returned to France to look there for his family. This version is more reconcilable with the tenants of those who believe that he
2 CONC  might have left by 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) In view of his age, he could have been part of the first expedition of Poutrincourt. He is one of those rare French that remained in Acadie after the siege of Port Royal by the Kirk brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4)Stephen White lists the children's marriages as follows based on the ages of their first known children: Françoise married an unknown Mercier c1644 and Daniel LeBlanc c1650. Marie married Etienne Hébert c1650. Daniel LeBlanc was
2 CONC  one of the signatories attesting to the accomplishments of d'Aulnay in Acadia. Since d'Aulnay died in May 1650, Daniel likely arrived in Acadia before that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although it is not clear when Jean arrived, the timeline below sets out some of the events in his life in context with the history of Acadian settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT «b»Biography«/b»
2 CONT "Father Archange Godbout described Jean Gaudet as the Abraham of Acadia, because of his numerous descendents." Jean was the ancestor of 10% of the little Acadian settlement, with his two sons and two daughters, 22 grandchildren an
2 CONC d 10 great grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Gaudet was born around 1575. About 1622, he married a woman whose name is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT «b»The couple had 3 children born in France:«/b»
2 CONT 1.) Marie-Francoise, b. 1623
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.) Denis, b. 1625
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.) Marie, b. 1633
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1652, Jean married a second time, to Nicole Colleson. Around 1653 they had a son Jean.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1671 Jean Gaudet, age 96, is the oldest inhabitant of Port-Royal. He was living with his second wife Nicole, age 64, and their son Jean. His three oldest children were married.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean died before 1678.
2 CONT
2 CONT «b»Timeline«/b»
2 CONT c1575 birth, in France
2 CONT c1622 marriage to Unknown Spouse
2 CONT c1623 birth, daughter Françoise
2 CONT c1625 birth, son Denis
2 CONT c1633 birth, daughter Marie
2 CONT 1632 Treaty Saint-Germain-en-Laye cedes Acadia to France; Razilly brings ~300 elite men
2 CONT 1636 Arrival of the first French families to settle permanantly
2 CONT c1650 Jean's daughters marry in France
2 CONT before Oct. 1650 Jean's son in law Daniel LeBlanc arrives in Acadia with wife Françoise Gaudet
2 CONT c1652 Jean marries 2nd wife Nicole Colleson
2 CONT c1653 birth, son Jean
2 CONT 1654 British capture Port-Royal; French settlement ceases
2 CONT 1667-70 Treaty of Breda cedes Acadia to the French; settlement resumes
2 CONT 1671 residence, in Port-Royal
2 CONT before 1678 death
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicole Colleson
2 CONT BIRTH 11 Jun 1612
2 CONT Loudun, Departement de la Vienne, Poitou-Charentes, France
2 CONT DEATH 1690 (aged 77–78)
2 CONT Port Royal, Annapolis County, Nova Scotia, Canada
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Garrison Graveyard
2 CONT Annapolis Royal, Annapolis County, Nova Scotia, Canada
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID 168710474 · View Source
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Jean Gaudet in France in 1628, after his first wife died. They are the parents of the following:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Marie (1633-1710) md (a) Etienne Hebert and (b) Dominique Gareau
2 CONT
2 CONT The following two grandchildren (Jean Gaudet and Francoise Comeau's children) were living with her on the 1678 census record:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Francois (1673-1758) md Charles Dorion
2 CONT 2) Jean (1675-) md Elisabeth Bourg
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicole was listed as 64 on the 1671 census. She was living with her son Jean, his wife Jeanne Henry, and their family, on the 1686 census record. She is not listed on the 1693 census record, so she died sometime between the two.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth info and death date provided by Nicole La Faive #17167635. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT MERGE ALERT: First wife of Jean Gaudet is not known - do not merge, adding a wife.
2 CONT Reason This Information Is Correct:
2 CONT The name of Jean GAUDET's first wife is not currently known to historical records or noted by acclaimed Acadian researcher, Stephen A. White (see image in "Sources" section above). A wife's name should not be filled in or added UN
2 CONC LESS one can provide documentary evidence (such as church, legal, or census records, etc.) and a logical argument that would override and replace current scholarship. I've started this in the "Discussions" section rather than note
2 CONC s so that entries can be made to this thread. Let's work together to keep the FamilySearch record of Jean GAUDET clean and based only on known documentary evidence. Citing someone else's compiled genealogy or things found on the i
2 CONC nternet are not evidence (they are unsupported assertions). 
2 CONT
