2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Olivier Daigre was born in France in 1643, probably in the town of Aigre in the ancient French province of Angoumois. Aigre today is a part of the Dept of LaCharente. Olivier most likely came to Acadia with Emmanuel LaBorgne, sieu
2 CONC r deCoudray, the feudal lor of Port Royal, aboard the ship LaPaix in 1664.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1671 Census - Olivier Daigre was shown as a 28 year old Farmer. His wife, Marie Gaudet, age 20 and they had 3 children Jehan 4, Jacques 2, Bernard 1. They had 2 arepents of cultivated land, 6 cattle, 6 sheep.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daigle Family Association by Florence Daigle Perkins, AGE Genealogy Exchange Vol XVII#4 :
2 CONT The Acadians Daigles descend from Olivier Daigle, who was recruited to help settle Noval Scotia in 1632. The descendants of Olivier Daigle lived a good life in the Port Royal and Grand Pre area of Nova Scotia for three generations
2 CONC . In 1755, because they would not sign an unconditional oath of allegiance to England, they were deported with nothing but what they had on their backs. Their ships were sent to Virginia, who refused to accept any Acadians. They w
2 CONC ere then sent to England, stayed there as "prisoners of war" for seven years, and then sent to France, where they had been promised land equal to what they had lost in Nova Scotia. The land they were given was not fit to grow crop
2 CONC s, so they spent 29 years in France trying to find a home. In 1768, they found themselves again on the French government dole and living in Nantes, France. The Government of Spaid in 1785 acquired seven ships and began sending the
2 CONC  French Acadians to Louisiana.
