2 CONT IMPORTANT NOTE: This family had two sons named Jean-one born about 1667 and the other born about 1676.
2 CONT Jean was thought to have been born in 1676≤ref name=White>Stephen A. White, DICTIONNAIRE GENEALOGIQUE DES FAMILLES ACADIENNES; 1636-1714; Moncton, New Brunswick, Centre d'Etudes Acadiennes, 1999, 2 vols.; p. 447≤/ref>The younger J
2 CONC ean must have been born before the 1678 census≤ref>Clarence J. d'Entremont, "Recensement de Port-Royal," in MEMOIRES DE LA SOCIETE GENEALOGIQUE CANADIENNE-FRANCAISE; vol. 22, no. 4; p. 229; ::On Folio 18: One of six boys age 12 an
2 CONC d under, with two girls, in household of Olivier DAIGRE & Marie GODET.≤/ref>, though he isn't listed.  He is found at age 10 in 1686 living with his mother, her new husband Jean Fardel and his  siblings.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcript
2 CONC ion of the1686 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1686cens.htm 1686 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_r
2 CONC eel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 15-60.≤blockquote>Jean FARDEL 46, Marie GODET 36; children: Bernard 16, Louis 13, Olivier 12, Jean 1
2 CONC 0, Marie 9, Anne 7, Pierre 5; 1 gun, 5 arpents, 13 cattle. 13 sheep, 8 hogs.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT Both he and his brother Louis disappear after the 1700 census togetherwith their mother and stepfather, and the rest of the family≤ref name=White /> even though she died in Port Royal in 1734, a widow.
