2 CONT
2 CONT Olivier's fifth son Olivier, fils, born at Port-Royal in c1674, married Jeanne, daughter of Guillaume Blanchard and Huguette Gougeon, at Port-Royal in c1699.  They remained in the Port-Royal area, where they had six children, incl
2 CONC uding three sons who married into the Arseneau, Cormier, Granger, and Thériot families.  Their three daughters married into the Thériot, Trahan, and Richard families.  Olivier, fils's sons moved to Chignecto and Minas. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT [[Category:Port-Royal, Acadie]]
2 CONT == Biography ==Olivier was born around 1674 likely at Port Royal (Annapolis, Nova Scotia, Canada).≤ref name=DGFA/> By the time he was 12, his father had died and he was living with his mother and stepfather Jean Fardel.In 1699, 26
2 CONC  year old Olivier married Jeanne Blanchard, daughter of Guillaume Blanchard and Hugette Gougeon, around 1699.≤ref name=DGFA/>Olivier and Jeanne, like many of the Acadian pioneers, lived off the land, farming to feed their family. 
2 CONC  Their homestead was located about 23 km east of the Fort at Port Royal. The farm was on the [https://maps.google.ca/maps?q=44%C2%B0+50.030',+-65%C2%B0+16.562'&ie=UTF8&t=m&z=15 south bank of the Dauphin (Annapolis) River] across t
2 CONC he river from present day Bridgetown.≤ref name=homestead>1707 homestead location of Olivier Daigle. In Au Coeur de l'Acadie Acadian Settlement on the Annapolis River 1707 Map Parks Canada≤/ref>≤ref name=Dauphin>[http://en.wikipedi
2 CONC a.org/wiki/File:Port_Royal,_Nova_Scotia_-_circa_1609_-_Project_Gutenberg_etext_20110.jpg Circa 1609 Map of Port Royal showing rivière du Dauphin]. Map originally published in [http://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/20110 ''Canada: the Em
2 CONC pire of the North'' by Agnes C. Laut]≤/ref> Within 11 years of their marriage, the couple had six children: Paul, Marie, Olivier, Jeanne, Marguerite, and Jean-Baptiste (posthumous).≤ref name=DGFA/>Sadly, Olivier did not see his la
2 CONC st son Jean-Baptiste as he died  in 1709 at Port-Royale.≤ref name=DGFA/>
2 CONT Olivier D' Aigre passed away in September 1709, aged about 40 years and was buried 22 September 1709 at Port Royal, Acadia.≤ref>An Acadian Parish Remembered The Registers of St. Jean-Baptiste, Annapolis Royal, 1702-1755 Olivier D'
2 CONC  Aigre burial RG 1 volume 26 page 350 https://novascotia.ca/archives/acadian/archives.asp?ID=1459≤/ref>≤ref>Library and Archives Canada, Fonds de la paroisse catholique Saint-Jean-Baptiste (Port-Royal, N.-É.)-1870 C-1870 (image 19
2 CONC 7) http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1869/197?r=0&s=5≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Timeline==
2 CONT :'''c1674''' birth, probably in Port-Royal
2 CONT :'''1686''' residence Port-Royal, with stepfather as his father died:''1690 British capture and sack Port-Royal; nominal rule''≤ref name=biophips>[http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/phips_william_1E.html C.P.Stacey, “PHIPS, SIR WILLIA
2 CONC M,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biography, vol. 1, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003, accessed November 20, 2013]≤/ref>:''1690-93 Port-Royal subjected to raids with looting and burning.'≤ref name=Dunn />: ''1697 Treaty of
2 CONC  Ryswick cedes Acadia to France; Port-Royal the capital'.≤ref name=Dunn />
2 CONT :'''c1699''' marriage to Jeanne Blanchard
2 CONT :'''c1701''' birth, son Paul
2 CONT :'''c1702''' birth, daughter Marie
2 CONT :'''1703''' birth, son Olivier in Port-Royal:''1702 Queen Anne’s War between English and French''≤ref name=Dunn>Dunn, Brenda. A History of Port Royal / Annapolis Royal 1605-1800. Nimbus Publishing, p 40-73≤/ref>
2 CONT :''1704 Blockade of Port Royal''≤ref name=Dunn />
2 CONT :'''1706''' birth, daughter Jeanne in Port-Royal:'''1707''' homestead, southern bank of Dauphin river, about 23 km east of the Fort.
2 CONT :''1707 Burning and pillaging of Port-Royal'≤ref name=Dunn />
2 CONT :'''1708''' birth, daughter Marguerite in Port-Royal 
2 CONT :'''1709''' death, in Port-Royal
2 CONT :'''1710''' posthumous birth, son Jean-Baptiste in Port-Royal
2 CONT ==Biographie==Olivier est né environ 1674≤ref name=DGFA>White, Stephen A., Patrice Gallant, and Hector-J Hébert. ''Dictionnaire Généalogique Des Familles Acadiennes.'' Moncton, N.-B.: Centre D'études Acadiennes, Université De Monc
2 CONC ton, 1999, Print, p450.≤/ref> probablement á Port-Royal (Annapolis, Nouvelle-Écosse, Canada) Il épousa Jeanne Blanchard, fille de Guillaume Blanchard et Hugette Gougeon, vers 1699.≤ref name=DGFA/> Olivier et Jeanne, comme plusieur
2 CONC s pionniers acadiens, étaient fermiers que vivaient de la terre pour nourrir leur famille. Leur ferme était située vers 23 km a l'est du fort à Port-Royal, sur la [https://maps.google.ca/maps?q=44%C2%B0+50.030',+-65%C2%B0+16.562'&
2 CONC ie=UTF8&t=m&z=15 rive sud de la rivière du Dauphin (Annapolis)] en face de Bridgetown.≤ref name=homestead/>
2 CONT Entre environ 1701 et 1710, le couple a eu six enfants: Paul, Marie, Olivier, Jeanne, Marguerite, Jean-Baptiste (posthume).≤ref name=DGFA/>Malheureusement, Olivier est mort en 1709 à Port-Royale et il n'ai jamais vu son fils Jean-
2 CONC Baptiste.≤ref name=DGFA/>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
