2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Bernard Gaudet Bernard is the son of Pierre Gaudet and Anne Blanchard. He married Jeanne in 1693, in Acadie. They are the parents of the following: 1) Pierre Gaudet (1698-) md Marie Beliveau 2) Agnes Gaudet (1705-) md Charles Beli
2 CONC veau 3) Jeanne Gaudet (1708-) md Pierre Belliveau 4) Madeleine Gaudet (1710-) md Jean Belliveau 5) Claude Gaudet (1713-) md Chaterine-Josephe Forest 6) Isabelle Gaudet (1720-) unmarried
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category:Port-Royal, Acadie]]
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT "Bernard Gaudet was the eldest of Pierre Gaudet and Anne Blanchard's nine children. He was born around 1673." ≤ref name=White/>  In the 1678census of Port-Royal, six  year old Bernard was enumerated with his family who owned 3 acr
2 CONC es of land and 4 head of cattle.≤ref>Tim Hebert; 1678 Port Royal Acadian Census noting that the correlations for this census were done by Rev. Clarence J. d'Entremont, Fairhaven, Massachusetts.[http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadi
2 CONC ancajun/genealogy/1678cens.htm 1678 Census]≤blockquote>Pierre Godet Lesne & Anne Blanchard, 3 acres,   4 cattle, 3 boys :  Bernard  6, Pierre 4, Claude 2.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>  The growing family was counted again in 1686. His fathe
2 CONC r Pierre Godet l'Aîné was 35 years old, his mother Anne Blanchard was 41 years old,their children were: Gérard (stepson), aged 20 years; Bernard, aged 13; Pierre, aged 11; Claude, aged 9; Abraham, aged 7; Marie, aged 4; Madeleine,
2 CONC  aged 4; and Jeanne, aged 2. Pierre l'Ainé had 4 arpents of cultivated land.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1686 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1686cens.htm 1686 
2 CONC Census Transcribed]. The original censuscan be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archivesof Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Image
2 CONC s 15-60.≤blockquote>Port Royal, Acadia, age 35 years, living with wife age 41 and 8 children. They have 4 arpens worked land and 10 pigs. Living next door is his sister Marie 1, one year older, with family.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>   Be
2 CONC rnard  was listedin 1693 at 19 years of age.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1693Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1693cens.htm 1693 Census Transcribed]. The original
2 CONC  census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 62-108≤/ref> 
2 CONT At the age of 20, he married Jeanne Thériault, daughter of Claude Thriault and Marie Gautreau. ≤ref>White, Stephen A., Patrice Gallant, and Hector-J Hébert [[Space:Dictionnaire_généalogique_des_familles_acadiennes|''Dictionnaire G
2 CONC énéalogique Des Familles Acadiennes.'']] Moncton, N.-B.: Centre D'études Acadiennes, Université De Moncton, 1999, Print, p. 671, 674-675.  ≤/ref>
2 CONT Between about 1695 and 1720, they had eight children, all born in Port-Royal. There were two boys named Claude born in this family. The first one,  born around 1698,  probably died young because he disappeared from the records aft
2 CONC er the census of 1701 at age 3.  The second Claudewas born in 1713.  Their daughter Marie passed away at the age of about 18.
2 CONT Pierre l'Ainé left the Port Royal river valley in 1696, leaving his farm to his  Bernard. In 1698, the farm had an orchard of 50 trees.≤ref> Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1698 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal,Acadie[http://freepag
2 CONC es.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1698cens.htm 1698 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National 
2 CONC Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 110-150≤blockquote>Bernard GODET 25; Jeanne TERRIOT (wife) 23; 12 cattle, 18 sheep, 8 hogs, 16 arpents, 50 fruit trees, 3 gun,≤/blockquote>≤/ref>  Bernard became the ch
2 CONC ief of the branch of Gaudets who perpetuated in this place at the same time with the descendants of his uncle Pierre Gaudet dit le Jeune, his neighbor.
2 CONT Bernard and Jeanne remained in Port-Royal as shown in the censuses of 1700≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1700 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1700cens.htm 1700 Ce
2 CONC nsus Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Image
2 CONC s 151-173.≤blockquote>Bernard GODET 26; Jeanne TERRIAU (wife) 25; Pierre 2, 5 cattle, 3 sheep.8 arpents.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>, 1701 ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription ofthe 1701 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.ro
2 CONC otsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1701cens.htm 1701 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Arc
2 CONC hives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 174-211.≤blockquote>Bernard GODET 28, Jeanne TERRIOT 23 (wife); Jean 13, Pierre 6, Claude 3; 1 gun, 7 cattle, 10 sheep, 5 hogs.4 arpents.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>, 1703 ≤ref>Tim
2 CONC  Hebert; Transcription ofthe 1703 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1703cens.htm 1703 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana
2 CONC .ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 212-220.≤blockquote>Bernard GODET, 1 boy, 1 girl, 1 arms hearer. ≤/blockquote>≤
2 CONC /ref> and 1714 ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription ofthe 1714 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1714cens.htm 1714 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [ht
2 CONC tp://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 239-261.≤blockquote>Bertrand GODET and wife, 2 sons, 4 d
2 CONC aughters.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT Bernard passed away in Port-Royal on 17 March 1751. ≤ref>''Nova ScotiaArchives'', "An Acadian Parish Remembered - The Registers of St. Jean-Baptiste, Annapolis Royal, 1702-1755," register RG 1 volume 26a page 429; online database 
2 CONC with images, [https://novascotia.ca/archives/acadian/archives.asp?ID=3252 Bernard Godet burial 19 March 1751], accessed16 December 2019≤/ref>≤ref name=White/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biographie=="Bernard Gaudet était l'aîné des neuf enfants de Pierre Gaudet et d'Anne Blanchard. Il est né vers 1673. À l'âge de vingt ans il épousa Jeanne Thériault, fille de Claude Thériault et de Marie Gautreau.Bernard s'est é
2 CONC teint à PortRoyal, le 17 mars 1751. Bernard Gaudet et Jeanne Thériault ont eu huit enfants, dont Pierre, surnommé Pitre,né vers 1695."≤ref name=White>Stephen A. White, "La généalogie destrente-sept familles hôtesses des 'Retrouvai
2 CONC lles ‘94'" in ''Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne,'' vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994) GAUDET, p. 1, [https://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/node/55 37 Families]≤/ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
