2 CONT Acadiens-m a Beaubassin 22-02-1713 a Agnes Chiasson fille de Sebastien & Marie Belou;
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT After marrying in 1713, Augustin Gaudet and Agnès Chiasson settled at Tintamarre, where they raised their 13 children. That locality is presently known as Upper Sackville, New Brunswick. West from Amherst, along the Trans Canada h
2 CONC ighway, near the Sackville "Waterfowl Park," on the right at a distance is a church steeple and a few houses and barns, is where Augustin's property was located.
2 CONT
2 CONT The deportation of the Acadians began on 11 August 1755 at Fort Beauséjour. Small numbers of Acadians from almost every settlement managed to escape. They formed settlements along the St-John River and upon the Petitcoudiac River.
2 CONC  Most of them settled in small numbers at Shédiac, Cocagne, Bouctouche, Richibucto, and elsewhere in the small harbours along the coast. The refugee settlement on the Miramichi formed in 1750 by the Acadians from the Chignecto pen
2 CONC insula had greatly increased in 1755 by refugees escaping the expulsion. Having some 3500 inhabitants, they greatly suffered from famine in 1757. Many removed to Restigouche and found the town of Petit Rochelle on the north side o
2 CONC f the Restigouche River above present day Campbellton. The English destroyed the settlement on the Miramichi in 1758, the remaining settlers left and rejoined the Acadians at Petit Rochelle.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is from a compilation of research by Glenn Laffy and of others, submitted to Glenn Laffy. May not be published without permission.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category:Beaubassin, Acadie]]
2 CONT [[Category:Great Upheaval]]
2 CONT [[Category:Port-Royal, Acadie]]
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT == Biography ==:'''Augustin Gaudet''' was born about 1686 in Port-Royal, Acadie, the son of [[Gaudet-17|Pierre Gaudet]] and [[Blanchard-186|Anne Blanchard]].≤ref name="DGFA">"Dictionnaire Généalogique des Familles Acadiennes"; Ste
2 CONC phen A. White; 2 vols., Moncton, New Brunswick: Centre d'Études Acadiennes, 1999; p. 671 #14
2 CONT : Augustin died after the 1754/1755 census.:BIRTH-PARENTS-CENSUS-MARRIAGE≤/ref> He appears on the censuses of Port-Royal in 1693, age 7 years; Beaubassin in 1698, age 12 years and Port-Royal, 1700, age 14 years. Augustin married A
2 CONC gnes Chiasson, daughterof Sebastien Chiasson and Marie Blou 22 February 1713 at Beaubassin.≤ref>Library and Archives Canada Fonds des Archives départementales dela Charente-Maritime [La Rochelle, France] : C-1207 Registres de Beau
2 CONC bassin - reel_c1207 MG 6 A 2 (Image 17, folio 35) http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1207/17?r=0&s=6≤/ref>
2 CONT :Together they had 14 children:≤ref>"La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des ‘Retrouvailles ‘94"; WHITE, Stephen A.; Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne, vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994) [http://www.umoncton.ca/um
2 CONC cm-ceaac/node/55 Genealogy of 37 Families]≤/ref>
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-202|Marie Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-299|Anne Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-344|Pierre Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-1376|Agnes Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-297|Marguerite Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-816|Jeanne Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-296|Michel Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-35|Louis Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-306|Jean Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-298|Paul Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-817|Anne Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-267|Françoise Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-302|Joseph Gaudet]]
2 CONT # [[Gaudet-818|Rosalie Gaudet]]
2 CONT :Augustin died after the 1754/1755 census estimated at 1760, ≤ref name="DGFA"/>probably in Restigouche, Nouveau-Brunswick.
2 CONT :In the 1693 census of the Port Royal river valley, Pierre Godet l'Aîné gives his age as 41 years old, his wife Anne Blanchard was 48 years old; their children were: Bernard, aged 19 years; Pierre, aged 17 years; Claude, aged 15; 
2 CONC Abraham, 13; Magdeleine and Marie (twins), 11; Jeanne, 9; '''Augustin, 7'''; and Jean, aged 3 years. Pierre l'Aîné had 26 arpents of cultivated land, 10 cattle, 12 sheep, 10 pigs, and 3 shot guns. ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of
2 CONC  the 1693 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1693cens.htm 1693 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c
2 CONC 2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 62-108≤/ref> Pierre l'Ainé left the Port Royal river valley in 1696, leaving his farm to his eldest son, 
2 CONC Bernard "le Vieux" (the old), who married Jeanne Terriot. In 1698, the farm had an orchard of 50 trees. Bernard became the chief of the branch of Gaudets who perpetuated in this place at the same time with the descendants of his u
2 CONC ncle Pierre Gaudet dit le Jeune, his neighbor.
2 CONT :Pierre l'Ainé settled at Beaubassin,≤ref> Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1698 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie[http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1698cens.htm 1698 Census Transcribed]. The original censu
2 CONC s can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 ofthe National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 110-166≤blockquote>Pierre GODET 46; 
2 CONC Anne BLANCHARD (wife) 53; Pierre 23; Claude 21; Abraham 19; Mie, and Madeleine, twins, 16; Jeanne 14; '''Augustin 12'''; 10 cattle, 4 hogs, 10 arpents,≤/blockquote>≤/ref>≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1700 Acadian Census, a
2 CONC t Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1700cens.htm 1700 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Cens
2 CONC us microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 167-173.≤blockquote>Pierre GODET 48; Anne BLANCHARD (wife) 55; Claude 23, Ahraham 22; '''Augustin 14'''; Marie and Madelaine (twins)
2 CONC  18; Jeanne 16;1 cow, 1 hog, 4 arpents, 2 guns.≤/blockquote>≤/ref> where he expandedthe most vast marshland on the east coast of North America. He built his home on the hill beside the Tintamarre (today Tantramar) river. Along wit
2 CONC h Pierre Thibodeau, who founded the nearby Chepoudy settlement,Pierre l'aîné defied the claim of the seigneur of Chignecto, MichelLe Neuf de la Valliere et de Beaubassin, that the Chepoudy and Petitcoudiac settlements were part of
2 CONC  his seigneury.  Gaudet and Thibodeau won their case before the colonial council at Port-Royal.  Their defiance of a powerful Acadian seigneur helped weaken the hold of the local nobility on these sturdy Acadian habitants. {{Citat
2 CONC ion Needed}}
2 CONT :The family returned to Port-Royal some time between 1700 and 1703 because they are listed in the census of 1703 of Port-Royal. ≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1703 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.roo
2 CONC tsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1703cens.htm 1703 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Arch
2 CONC ives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 212-220.≤blockquote>at Port-Royal: Pierre GODET, the eldest, his wife, 1 boy, 2 girls.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT :They returned to Beaubassin after 1703.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcriptionof the 1707 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1707cens.htm 1707 Census Transcription]. The original c
2 CONC ensus can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 221-237.≤blockquote>at Beaubass
2 CONC in: Pierre GODET, Anne GUERIN (sic), '''1 boy 14 or older'''; 8 arpents, 12 cattle, 5 hogs.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1714 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadian
2 CONC cajun/genealogy/1714cens.htm 1714 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “A
2 CONC cadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 239-.≤blockquote>at Beaubassin:  Pierre GODET and Anne BLANCHARD.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT == Biographie ==:Il y a aussi au sud-est du Nouveau-Brunswick des descendants d'Augustin Gaudet, fils de Pierre Gaudet l'aîné et d'Anne Blanchard. Né vers 1686, Augustin se maria, à Beaubassin, le 22 février 1713, à Agns Chiasson,
2 CONC  fille de Sébastien Chiasson et de Marie Blou.≤ref>[http://www.archinoe.fr/console/ir_ead_visu_lien.php?m=&ir=23107&id=365149634#Mariage Registre paroissiale de Beaubassin - 1712-1748 Vue 42/360]
2 CONT ≤/ref>
2 CONT :Leur famille comptait quatorze enfants.≤ref>Stephen A. White, "La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des 'Retrouvailles 94,' in ''Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne'', vol. 25, nos. 2 et 3 (1994): Gaudet.
2 CONT ≤/ref>
2 CONT
