2 CONT == Biography ==Guillaume Gaudet, son of Pierre le jeune Gaudet and Marie Blanchard, was born about 1683 in Port-Royal, Acadie. He was a navigator. Guillaume and Marie Boudrot, daughter of the late Abraham Boudrot and Anne Melancon
2 CONC  married at Port-Royal 21 Oct 1709. Signed by the groom and DenisPetitot. The bride and her father made their marks.≤ref>Library and Archives Canada, Fonds de la paroisse catholique Saint-Jean-Baptiste (Port-Royal, N.-É.)-1870 C-1
2 CONC 870 (image 139) http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1869/139?r=0&s=5≤/ref>≤ref>An Acadian Parish Remembered The Registers of St. Jean-Baptiste, Annapolis Royal, 1702-1755 Guillaume Godet and Marie Boudrot marriage  
2 CONC RG 1 volume 26 page 293https://novascotia.ca/archives/acadian/archives.asp?ID=1248≤/ref>
2 CONT [http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=katheriot&id=I1638 Children]
2 CONT # Charles GAUDET b: 1 Aug 1712 in Port Royal
2 CONT # Jeanne GAUDET b: Abt 1714 in Port Royal
2 CONT # Anne GAUDET b: 15 Mar 1718 in Port Royal
2 CONT # Marie GAUDET b: Abt 1720 in Acadia
2 CONT # Jean GAUDET b: Abt 1722 in Acadia
2 CONT # GAUDET b: Abt 1724 in  Acadia
2 CONT # Marguerite GAUDET b: Abt 1726 in  Acadia
2 CONT [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1686.html 1686 Acadian Census], at Port-Royal, Acadia, age 3 years.
2 CONT [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1693.html 1693 Acadian Census], at Port-Royal, Acadia, age 9 years.
2 CONT [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1698.html 1698 Acadian Census], at Port-Royal, Acadia, age 16 years.
2 CONT [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1700.html 1700 Acadian Census], at Port-Royal, Acadia.
2 CONT [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1701.html 1701 Acadian Census], at Port-Royal, Acadia, as Augustin age 18 years (sic).
2 CONT Extract of a letter of Governor Armstrong to the Lords of Trade in Londondated 5th September 1725:≤ref>Selections from the Public documents of Nova Scotia F100 N85 volume 1 pages 62 - 63≤/ref> 
2 CONT "I hope you will take into your consideration, and to lay the same before His Majesty as may forward the security of its settlement by a royal fortification, for the subjects are as yet discouraged, having no shelter from the dail
2 CONC y insults and cruel Massacres of the Indians, who are supported and clandestinely encouraged by the French; who envying our growth and increase, supply them with powder and Ball, &c, in order to disturb our settlements; And last w
2 CONC inter they were even piloted to this place, by one William (Guillaume) Gaudet and one Barthélémy Petipas (through no doubt the instigation and contrivances of most on the Island of Cape Breton.) 
2 CONT Being informed of these, and a great many more of their underhand dealings, I judged it my duty to write to Governor St. Ovide for redress. 
2 CONT And having herewith transmitted you my letter to him, his answer, my instructions to Hibbert Newton Esq. and Ensign John Broadstreet, and their Journal of Proceedings with him at Louisburg you will see that allthe satisfaction I c
2 CONC ould get, is only pretended ignorance of notorious matters of fact and ambiguous fair promises of friendship without any punctual performances. And for his excuse for supplying of about twohundred of them this year, says that it i
2 CONC s his most Christian Majesty's orders to him to distribute to the Indians their annual presents which chiefly consists of Arms, Powder and Ball &c, by which we Brittishsubjects do greatly suffer, the War being thereby fomented and
2 CONC  prolonged."
2 CONT
2 CONT He died between 1735 - 1741 in Acadia.
2 CONT
