1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **"Acadian Immigrants to Lafourche des Chitimachas/Valenzuéla, 1785-86" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***At Anse-à-Pinnet, Île St.-Jean, Aug 1752, age 27
2 CONT ***Deported from Île St.-Jean to St.-Malo, France, aboard one of the Five Ships 25 Nov 1758, arrived St.-Malo 23 Jan 1759, age 31[sic]
2 CONT ***At Ploubalay, France, 1759
2 CONT ***At Trigavou, France, 1759-63
2 CONT ***At Pleslin, France, 1763-69
2 CONT ***At Plouër-sur-Rance, France, 1766
2 CONT ***At St.-Malo 1772
2 CONT ***On list of Acadians at Nantes, France, Sep 1784, listed as Marguerite Hébert, widow Alexis Guatreau
2 CONT ***Sailed to la Luisiana on ''Le St.-Rémi'', age 59
2 CONT ***Valenzuela, la Luisiana census, 1788, listed as Margrithe Hébert widow Gautreaut, age 63
2 CONT ***Valenzuela, la Luisiana census, 1791, left bank, listed as Margrithe, age 66
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana Nov 1785
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The other couple named Jean-Baptiste LeBlanc and Marguerite Hébert has long
2 CONT
2 CONT been a source of confusion because their marriage record no longer exists,
2 CONT
2 CONT probably because it was recorded in the parish of St-Joseph de la
2 CONT
2 CONT Rivière-aux-Canards. They were married about 1745. This Jean-Baptiste was
2 CONT
2 CONT the son of Pierre LeBlanc and Marie Landry, and his Marguerite Hébert was
2 CONT
2 CONT the daughter of Jean Hébert and Élisabeth Granger. Winslow's List shows
2 CONT
2 CONT that before the Expulsion they lived in the Village des Pinou at Grand-Pré,
2 CONT
2 CONT alongside Marguerite's sister Françoise Hébert and her husband Augustin
2 CONT
2 CONT LeBlanc, who was a son of Pierre dit Pinou LeBlanc and Françoise Landry (who
2 CONT
2 CONT were not the same people as the parents of Marguerite's husband
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste). In 1755 most of the inhabitants of their village were
2 CONT
2 CONT deported to Massachusetts. In 1763 this Jean-Baptiste LeBlanc and his wife
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite were listed in Massachusetts among those Acadians who wished to
2 CONT
2 CONT go to French territory. They appear on the list immediately before the
2 CONT
2 CONT Widow Pierre LeBlanc, who was Jean-Baptiste's mother. They subsequently all
2 CONT
2 CONT went to Yamachiche, where this Jean-Baptiste and Marguerite had the six
2 CONT
2 CONT children who had been born to them during their exile all baptized together
2 CONT
2 CONT in September 1767.
2 CONT
2 CONT F. L.-Desaulniers acknowledged in Les vieilles familles d'Yamachiche that
2 CONT
2 CONT there was some problem with this family, but he failed to solve it. He of
2 CONT
2 CONT course went wrong by mistakenly identifying this Jean-Baptiste and
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite as the couple who were married at Grand-Pré in 1741. But he had
2 CONT
2 CONT also the integrity to lay out what he saw as a conflict in the available
2 CONT
2 CONT data by noting on page 123 of the fourth volume of his work that the writer
2 CONT
2 CONT Raphaël Bellemare (whose paternal grandmother was one of this Jean-Baptiste
2 CONT
2 CONT and Marguerite's eleven children) had told him that his father had often
2 CONT
2 CONT gone to visit his uncle Honoré Hébert at St-Grégoire de Nicolet. The
2 CONT
2 CONT problem was that this Honoré Hébert was one of four well-known brothers, who
2 CONT
2 CONT were sons of Jean Hébert and Élisabeth Granger. Unfortunately, M.
2 CONT
2 CONT Desaulniers did not have enough information to alert him to the fact that
2 CONT
2 CONT there were two couples named Jean-Baptiste LeBlanc and Marguerite Hébert,
2 CONT
2 CONT and he could not figure out how Raphaël Bellemare could have been Honoré
2 CONT
2 CONT Hébert's great-grandnephew, to his "great regret."
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding the dispensation received by Jean-Baptiste and Marguerite's
2 CONT
2 CONT daughter Anne when she married Étienne Melanson in 1778, this was
2 CONT
2 CONT necessitated by the fact that Anne's maternal grandfather, Jean Hébert, was
2 CONT
2 CONT a brother of Étienne's maternal grandmother, Jeanne Hébert (who married
2 CONT
2 CONT Claude Benoit).
2 CONT
2 CONT Stephen A. White
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.nosorigines.qc.ca/GenealogieQuebec.aspx?genealogie=Leblanc_Jean-Baptiste&pid=84049 ID No:	84048 Prénom:	Marguerite Nom:	Hebert Sexe:	F Occupation:	 Naissance:	1726 vers Paroisse/ville:	 Pays:	Canada Décès:	07 décembre 1
2 CONC 798 - âge: 72 Paroisse/ville:	Yamachiche Pays:	Canada Inh./Source:	8 décembre 1798, Marguerite Ebert, veuve Joseph Leblanc, âgée de 72 ans Information, autres enfants, notes, etc.  Message S.A. White 27 août 2012 
2 CONT
2 CONT À propos de la nommée Marguerite Hébert qui a été enterrée à Yamachiche le 8 décembre 1798, elle n’était pas la fille de René Hébert et de Marie Boudrot qui est mentionnée à la page 831 de la première partie du Dictionnaire généal
2 CONC ogique des familles acadiennes, mais plutôt la fille de Jean Hébert et d’Élisabeth Granger dont la famille figure dans la deuxième partie de mon ouvrage. Toutes les deux Marguerite ont épousé des Jean-Baptiste LeBlanc. Comme il di
2 CONC t à la page 831 de la publication précitée, la fille de René s’est mariée au fils de René LeBlanc et de Jeanne Landry. La fille de Jean Hébert a cependant épousé vers 1745 le fils de Pierre LeBlanc et de Marie Landry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
