1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **"Acadian Immigrants to the Opelousas District, 1765" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1766, listed as Pedro Richard
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1771, age 40
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1774
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1777, age 48
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1785, called Pre
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1788, Bellevue, called Pre.
2 CONT ***Opelousas, La Luisiana census, 1796, Bellevue District, called Pierre Richard
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana in 1765, age 36
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-RICHARD.htm Acadians in Gray: Richard]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre, elder son of Alexandre Richard and Marie-Madeleine Thibodeau, born probably at Chignecto in c1729, followed his family to Malpèque, Île St.-Jean, in c1741 and married Marguerite, daughter of Alexis Dugas and Marie Bourg of
2 CONC  Cobeguit, on the island in c1752.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They escaped the British roundup there in 1758, and Pierre joined his cousins, the Beausoleil Broussards, as part of the Acadian resistance movement in present-day New Brunswick and Nova Scotia.  During the early 1760s, Pierre and
2 CONC  his family, with the Broussards, ended up as prisoners in Nova Scotia.  They followed the Broussards to Louisiana via Cap-Français, St.-Domingue, in 1764-65 but did not remain with them on lower Bayou Teche.  Nor did they settle 
2 CONC near Pierre's paternal uncle Jean-Baptiste Richard, who had come to Louisiana the year before and settled at Cabanocé/St.-Jacques on the river.  Pierre and younger brother Victor settled, instead, in the Opelousas District, on Bel
2 CONC levue prairie, south of the present city of Opelousas.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre and Marguerite had more children in Louisiana.  Their daughter married a Bourg cousin.  By 1774, Pierre owned 50 head of cattle on his vacherie at Bellevue.  His herd had increased to 100 head by 1777, and to 140 head of ca
2 CONC ttle and 10 horses by 1788; he also owned three slaves in 1788.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In his late 60s, Pierre remarried to fellow Acadian Élisabeth, or Isabelle, Aucoin, widow of Jean-Baptiste LeBlanc, at Opelousas in August 1797.  Élisabeth was a native of Minas and had come to Louisiana from France aboard L'Amiti
2 CONC é, the fifth of the Seven Ships, in 1785; she and her first husband may have lived at San Bernardo, on the river below New Orleans, before moving on to the Opelousas area.  She gave Pierre no more children.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre, père died at Opelousas in May 1806; the priest who recorded his burial said that Pierre died "at age about 78 yrs."  All six of his sons created families of their own.  Most of them settled in St. Landry Parish and in what
2 CONC  became Acadia Parish, but some drifted down into the old Attakapas District and settled in St. Martin, Lafayette, and St. Mary parishes.  Despite their living in a predominantly Creole community, Pierre's descendants in St. Landr
2 CONC y Parish married a surprising number of fellow Acadians.  Most of the Richards of southwest Louisiana descend from Pierre.  Singer/song writer/poet/musician/environmental and Cajun activist Zachary Richard is one of them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT !RICHARD, Pierre m. Marguerite DUGAS (OpelCh)  SWLR-v.1,p.480. 
2 CONT RICHARD, Pierre d. 3 May 1806 at age 78 yrs (Opel ch.v.1,p.85)  SWLR-v.1,p.480.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
