2 CONT Andrée was baptized on August 21, 1646 in Notre-Dame Parish, La Chaussée,  province of Poitou (today département de la Vienne), France.≤ref>[http://archivesnumerisees.cg86.fr/v2/ark:/28387/mpzd6tk3201sk09q Baptism Record] Archives
2 CONC  départementales de la Vienne, Les registres paroissiaux et d’état civil de la Vienne en ligne des origines à 1912, Localité de La Chaussée, Paroisse Notre-Dame,  baptêmes 1635-1649, p. 56/82. (Last entry on left page).≤/ref>≤ref>
2 CONC [[Massignon-1|Geneviève Massignon]], [[Massignon-1#Les_parlers_fran.C3.A7ais_d.27Acadie.2C_enqu.C3.AAte_linguistique| ''"Les parlers français d'Acadie, enquête linguistique"'']], Librairie Klincksieck, Paris, 1962, p. 36. [http://
2 CONC books.google.com/books?id=csAqAAAAMAAJ&focus=searchwithinvolume&q=baptis%C3%A9e+Andrée (snippet from Google Books)]≤/ref>
2 CONT She married Germain Thériot about 1668.  No place is given for the marriage by Stephen White. ≤ref name=DGFA>White, Stephen A., Patrice Gallant, and Hector-J Hébert. Dictionnaire Généalogique Des Familles Acadiennes. Moncton, N.-B
2 CONC .: Centre D'études Acadiennes, Université DeMoncton, 1999, Print. Copy in possession of  [[Girouard-4019| Jacqueline Girouard]]; pp. 289 & 1499≤/ref>  They had 3 children: Germain, Pierre and Catherine.  They appear in the first c
2 CONC ensus of Port-Royal in 1671.   Andrée was 25 years and Germain was the same age. They had one child, Germain, age 2. They possessed 2 "arpents" of land, 5 cattle and 2 sheep. Andrée was also listed in her parents'  entry as marrie
2 CONC d, age 24.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1671 Acadian Census, atPort-Royal, Acadie. [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1671cens.htm 1671 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://h
2 CONC eritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752” Images 3-14.≤blockquote>Germain TERRIAU 25, wife, Andree BRUN 25; Child
2 CONC : Germain 2; cattle 5, sheep 2, 2 arpents≤/blockquote>≤/ref>
2 CONT The widowed Andrée Brun and Emmanuel Hébert were married around 1680. Between about 1681 and 1687 they had 6 children: 5 boys and 1 girl. 
2 CONT In 1686, the Hébert family seemed to be doing well in Port-Royal. They owned 14 cattle, 21 sheep, 8 hogs, 4 arpents of land and 1 gun.  They were counted with their 5 children ages 1 month to 6 years, plus the3 children of Andrée'
2 CONC s former marriage with Germain Thériot, now age 12 to 18.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1686 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1686cens.htm 1686 Census Transcribed]
2 CONC . The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 15-60.≤blockquo
2 CONC te> Emanuel HEBERT 30, Andree BRUN 40; children: Guillaume 6, Marguerite 5, Jean 3, Jacques 2, and a 1 month old baby not baptized; childrenfrom her first marriage to Germain TERRIOT: Germain 18, Pierre 15. Catherine 12; 1 gun, 4 
2 CONC arpents, 14 cattle, 21 sheep, 8 hogs.≤/blockquote>≤/ref>  By 1693, they  had a prosperous farm with 30 arpents of cultivable land, 20 cattle, 40 sheep, 15 hogs and 1 gun.≤ref>Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1693 Acadian Census, a
2 CONC t Port-Royal, Acadie [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1693cens.htm 1693 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Cens
2 CONC us microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 62-108≤blockquote> at Port-Royal, Acadie Emanuel HEBERT 40, Andree BRUN 46, Guillaume 12, Jean 9, Jacques 8, Alexandre 7, Martin 6, 
2 CONC Catherine TERIOT (Andree's daughter) 22; 20 cattle 40sheep, 15 pigs, 30 arpents, 1 gun.≤/blockquote>≤/ref> 
2 CONT Emmanuel and Andrée continued to farm and to raise their children in Port-Royal as shown in the censuses between 1698 and 1707.≤ref> Tim Hebert; Transcription of the 1698 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie[http://freepages.root
2 CONC sweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1698cens.htm 1698 Census Transcribed]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Censusmicrofilm C-2572 of the National Archives
