2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre and his family probably were among those who were deported from Grand Pre in 1755. (Journal of Colonel John Winslow of the Provincial Troops, While Engaged in Removing the Acadian French Inhabitants from Grand Pre and Neigh
2 CONC boring Settlements. Online transcription at the Public Archives of Nova Scotia.) In a list of neutral French in Pennsylvanis in 1756, Peter is shown with his wife and 7 children. (www.acadian-home.org) Pierre resided in Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia in 1763. (Stephen A. White, The Dictionnaire Genealogique Des Families Acadiennes, Moncton: University of Moncton, vol II, 2000) Eventually some members of the family resided in Falmouth, England and some in Philadelphia, PA, U
2 CONC SA. (www.bluepete.com/Genealogy/LandryOA.htm) Pierre and his family were exiled to Philadelphia, PA in 1763. (Stephen A. White, The Dictionnaire Genealogique Des Families Acadiennes, Moncton: University of Moncton, English Supplem
2 CONC ent, 2000) Babineau provided a list of acadiens in Pennsylvania on June 20, 1763 that included a Pierre Landry with three infants along with the widow of Pierre Aucoin with seven children. (Babineau, Rene, The Acadian Exiles in Pe
2 CONC nnsylvania. Philadelphia: Lippincott, 1858 for The Historical Society of Pennsylvania. Copied and appended by Rene Babineau in 1984) 
2 CONT
