1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **Drouin Institute (Archived marriage record - see attached in Media tab)
2 CONT **Passenger List of the first Acadians to arrive in present day Louisiana - February 1764 - From the Province of Georgia via Mobile, British West Florida to New Orleans, La Luisiana, New Spain (age: 36)
2 CONT **On a list of Acadians in the Province of South Carolina dated Aug 1763
2 CONT **On a list of Acadians in the Province of Georgia in 1763
2 CONT **Cabanocé, La Luisiana census, 1766 (listed as Ollivie Landry)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'
2 CONT **Exiled to Province of Georgia in 1755 from Acadie (age 27)
2 CONT **Moved to Province of New York and held in New York from 1756 to 1763
2 CONT **Among the very first 21 "Acadians" to reach present day Louisiana
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From Rootsweb:
2 CONT
2 CONT LANDRY, OLIVIER
2 CONT
2 CONT  Olivier Landry, born 1725, son of Joseph Landry[1] and Marguerite 
2 CONT  Forest[2] married 24 September 1748[3] Cécile Poirier[4], born 1725, 
2 CONT  daughter of Jean-Baptiste Poirier and Marie Cormier.
2 CONT
2 CONT  This family was deported to South Carolina and arrived there 17 
2 CONT  November 1755. Aboard the Sloop Dolphin, William Hancock, Master was 
2 CONT  listed[5]:
2 CONT  Jolour Lundrie. wife, 3 children
2 CONT  [Olivier Landry age 30
2 CONT  Cécile Poirier, wife age 30
2 CONT  Joseph, child age 4
2 CONT  Marie, child age 4
2 CONT  Child, name unkown][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT  In Georgia on a prisoner list dated 23 August1763[7] is found:
2 CONT  Olivier Landry, wife, three children
2 CONT  [Olivier Landry age 38
2 CONT  Cécile Poirier age 38
2 CONT  Joseph age 13 born 1750
2 CONT  Marie age 12 born 1751
2 CONT  Jean-Antoine age 2 born 13 November 1760[8]]
2 CONT
2 CONT  This family along with 3 other close knit families, boarded a ship in 
2 CONT  December 1763 to leave Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT  In the [Savannah] GEORGIA GAZETTE dated December 22, 1763[9] is found:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Yesterday more of the Acadians, in number about 21, went in a vessel 
2 CONT  for Mobile, from which place they are to go to New Orleans.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sailed for Mobile
2 CONT  Dec. 21, Sloop Savannah Packet, John Somerville [Master]
2 CONT
2 CONT  These Acadians arrived in Mobile on or just before 22 January 1764 
2 CONT  where the marriage of Jean-Baptiste Poirier and Madeleine Richard was 
2 CONT  blessed by the first priest they had seen in years. A copy of this 
2 CONT  ceremony is in Vidrine’s “Love’s Legacy[10].
2 CONT
2 CONT  Note this indicates a journey of about one month to Mobile. In the 
2 CONT  GEORGIA GAZETTE dated 26 February 1764, the return of the Savannah 
2 CONT  Packet to Savannah is noted[11], a return voyage of one month. This 
2 CONT  indicates the Acadians in Mobile boarded a different ship for New 
2 CONT  Orleans which could have been bound from New York to New Orleans. 
2 CONT  From Mobile, they went on to New Orleans arriving on or just before 
2 CONT  26 February 1764 when Jean-Antoine was baptized.
2 CONT
2 CONT  LANDRY, Jean Antoine (Olivier and Cecile Poiriez [sic for Poirier]), 
2 CONT  bap. Feb. 26, 1764, born Nov. 13, 1760, sponsors Antoine OLIVIER and 
2 CONT  Magdeleine BRAZIER (SLC, B5, 40)[12]
2 CONT
2 CONT  Sometime after 6 April 1764, this family settled on the west bank of 
2 CONT  the Mississippi just down river from Verret’s Plantation.
2 CONT
2 CONT
