1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **"Acadian Immigrants to Cabanocé/Cabahannocer, 1765" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***On list of Acadian prisoners at Halifax, Aug 1763, listed as Entoine
2 CONT ***Cabanocé, La Luisiana census, 1766, listed as Antonio Labaube, age 40
2 CONT ***Cabanocé, La Luisiana census, 1769, age 4[sic, probably meant 44]
2 CONT ***St.-Jacques, La Luisiana census, 1777, age 50
2 CONT ***St.-Jacques, La Luisiana census, 1779
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana in 1765, age 39
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine, fils, son of Antoine Labauve and Catherine Lejeune, was born probably at Grand-Pré in c1726. He escaped the British roundup of the Acadians in the fall of 1755 and went into exile on the Gulf of St. Lawrence shore, where 
2 CONC he married Anne Vincent in c1756. In the late 1750s or early 1760s, he and his family fell into the hands of the British, who held them as prisoners at Halifax. British officials counted them there in August 1763. They came to Lou
2 CONC isiana via St.-Domingue in 1765 and settled at Cabanocé/St.-Jacques, where Spanish officials counted them on the left, or east, bank of the river in 1766 and 1769. Antoine and Anne had many more children in Louisiana, including so
2 CONC ns. Their daughters married Legendre and Vincent cousins. Antoine died at St.-Jacques in March 1779, in his early 50s. Three of his five sons created families of their own. Two of them settled at Baton Rouge, another at New Orlean
2 CONC s. Four of his grandsons moved to the western prairies in the early 1800s. His other grandsons remained on the river, at Baton Rouge and in St. James Parish. His fourth son's line was especially vigorous.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine Labauve of Grand-Pré, age 39, came with wife Anne Vincent, age 27, two young sons, perhaps twins--Jean and Marin, age 6--and nephew Jean-Baptiste Labauve, age 15, son of his brother Charles. Antoine and Anne had more child
2 CONC ren in Louisiana. Jean-Baptiste created a family of his own, not on the river but on the western prairies.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Antoine, fils, son of Antoine Labauve and Catherine Lejeune, was born probably at Grand-Pré in c1726.  He escaped the British roundup of the Acadians in the fall of 1755 and went into exile on the Gulf of St. Lawrence shore, where
2 CONC  he married Anne Vincent in c1756.  In the late 1750s or early 1760s, he and his family fell into the hands of the British, who held them as prisoners at Halifax.  British officials counted them there in August 1763.  They came to
2 CONC  Louisiana via St.-Domingue in 1765 and settled at Cabanocé/St.-Jacques, where Spanish officials counted them on the left, or east, bank of the river in 1766 and 1769.  Antoine and Anne had many more children in Louisiana, includi
2 CONC ng sons.  Their daughters married Legendre and Vincent cousins.  Antoine died at St.-Jacques in March 1779, in his early 50s.  Three of his five sons created families of their own.  Two of them settled at Baton Rouge, another at N
2 CONC ew Orleans.  Four of his grandsons moved to the western prairies in the early 1800s.  His other grandsons remained on the river, at Baton Rouge and in St. James Parish.  His fourth son's line was especially vigorous. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine Labauve of Grand-Pré, age 39, came with wife Anne Vincent, age 27, two young sons, perhaps twins--Jean and Marin, age 6--and nephew Jean-Baptiste Labauve, age 15, son of his brother Charles.  Antoine and Anne had more chil
2 CONC dren in Louisiana.  Jean-Baptiste created a family of his own, not on the river but on the western prairies.
