1 NOTE Old Acadia might easily be outlined on a map <../Hist/NovaScotiaBk1/Part1/Maps/MapNS1.gif> of modern day Nova Scotia, an eastern province of Canada. The original territory of Acadia was fully contained within peninsular Nova Scoti
2 CONC a towards its north-western end; its shores being washed by the eastern waters of the Bay of Fundy.3 These shores would include those of the Annapolis Basin; Minas Basin; and Chignecto Bay with its further extensions, Shepody Bay 
2 CONC and Cumberland Basin.
2 CONT Old Acadia was one of the first European settlements in North America. The area was likely explored by European fishermen, many, many years ago; but the first settlers, who overwintered, came in 1605, viz., when French merchants s
2 CONC ent Monsieur de Monts <../Hist/BiosNS/1600-00/Monts.htm> out with a small crew of Frenchmen. The first French family immigration took place around the year of 1636; such immigration was not extensive and pretty much came to an end
2 CONC  by 1671. From this small population base arose the French Acadian population; which, by 1671, consisted of three hundred and sixty one; and which, by 1750, had grown to some twelve thousand4 -- unassisted and mostly ungoverned. B
2 CONC ecause of international conflict, the Acadians, both by the actions of the French and English authorities, were, in the end (1748-1763), driven from their ancestral lands to all corners of the western hemisphere.
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Landry m. Anastasie Leblanc. Paul, d. 1785 L'Assomption, est le fils de René & Madeleine Melanson. Source: S.W. p948. Origine: Grand-Pré, Boxford Mass.Enfants mariés dans la région Lanaudière, source Ferland:Anastasie m. Pier
2 CONC re Brassard, 1790 St-Jacques.Marguerite m. Charles Charbonneau, 1778 L'Assomption.Madeleine m. Alexis Girouard, 1796 St-Jacques.Marie m. Noël Ratel, 1782 L'Assomption
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul (Placite?) Landry (b.c.1728):
2 CONT This Paul married, c. 1750, Marguerite Bourg. While one source says this family established themselves at Nicolet, Quebec; yet, at p. 2533, Arsenault makes an entry about a "Paul Landry, originaire de Pisiguit, Acadie, marié à Mar
2 CONC guerite Bourque. Arrivé en Louisiane avant 1767 it est décédé à Donaldsonville, le novembre 1829." (p. 2533)
