2 CONT [[Category:Grand-Pré, Acadie]] [[Category:Beaubassin, Acadie]] [[Category:Nominated Profiles]] 
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography =='''NOTICE''': this profile is protected by the [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Project:Acadians Acadian Project] because of frequent duplication, and potential relative confusion (see Research Notes). Please contact 
2 CONC the Acadian Project before making any substantive changes. Thanksfor helping make WikiTree the best site for accurate information. '''Pierre Cormier and Cécile Thibodeau are the ancestors of all the Cormiers  in southeastern New B
2 CONC runswick'''<ref name=SW94/> '''Pierre Cormier''' was born some time after the 1703 census <ref name=census1703>Tim Hebert, "Transcription of the 1703 Acadian Census, atPort-Royal, Acadie," online on his website, ''Acadian-Cajun Ge
2 CONC nealogy,'' [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1703cens.htm 1703 Census Transcription]. The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 AcadianCensus microf
2 CONC ilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “AcadieRecensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 212-220.<blockquote>  :Pierre CORMIER and his wife, 1 arms bearer. </blockquote></ref>and before the one in 1707<ref name=census1707>Tim Heber
2 CONC t; Transcription of the 1707 Acadian Census, at Port-Royal, Acadie," online on his website, ''Acadian-Cajun Genealogy,'' [http://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1707cens.htm 1707 Census Transcription]. The original 
2 CONC census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 of the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 221-237.<blockquote>  :Pierre 
2 CONC CORMIER,  :Catherine LEBLANC,  :'''2 boys less than 14'''; 6 arpents, 9 cattle, 10 sheep, 8 hogs. </blockquote></ref> near Beaubassin (now Amherst, Nova Scotia, Canada) on his parents' homestead in [http://goo.gl/maps/AUGXn Ouesco
2 CONC que] (Amherst Point).<ref name=surette />  He was Pierre Cormier and Catherine LeBlanc's eldest child in a family of 11 children: 4 boys and 7 girls.  His nickname was Palette, like his father's.<ref name=surette /> Pierre was men
2 CONC tioned for the first time in records in the 1707 census in which he waslisted with his parents and a sibling in Beaubassin. No age was givenfor the members of the family in the census.  His uncles François , Germain and Alexis Cor
2 CONC mier were living nearby. His grandparents, Thomas Cormier and Madeleine Girouard, were among the first pioneers at Beaubassin and had a large farm at Ouescoque. « The Cormiers made Beaubassin their legacy.  For at least three quar
2 CONC ters of a century, many settled there and at nearby Rivière-des-Hébert ». <ref>« Un historique de la famille Cormier 1644-1994 ». Shédiac: Tours Placide Gaudet Inc, 1994, p. 14.</ref> When Pierre was around twenty-five years of ag
2 CONC e, he married seventeen-year-old Cécile Thibodeau, daughter of Jean Thibodeau and Marguerite Hébert. They were married on 17 July 1730 in Grand-Pré (now Nova Scotia, Canada).<ref>Paroisse de St. Charles des Mines, Grand Pré, Acadi
2 CONC e, Mariages, vol. 12-3, p. 646 [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1870/646?r=0&s=5 entry for Pierre Cormier & Catherine Tibaudot], 17 July 1730; digital images, Héritage, Genealogy collection,Library and Archives 
2 CONC Canada, reel C-1869, "Parish Registers: Nova Scotia : C-1869," roll 1, accessed 3 Nov 2019. ''This register is a transcript written around 1895 of the original registers currently held at the Baton Rouge Diocese Archives in Louisi
2 CONC ana, USA.''</ref><ref name=DGFA />   They settled on a farm at Ouescoque, as his father was able to provide land for all of his children.<ref name=surette /> The couple's 11 children , 8 boys and 3 girls, were born between about17
2 CONC 33 and 1751, mostly in the Beaubassin area: Jean Baptiste; Pierre called Pierrot à Nanette; Marie; François; Joseph; Michel; Marie-Joseph; Charles; Marie-Cecile; Jacques; and Amand.<ref name=SW94> White, Stephen A. ''La généalogie
2 CONC  des trente-sept familles hôtesses des Retrouvailles 94,'' online articles, Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne, vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994), [https://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/files/umcm-ceaac/wf/wf/pdf/37fam-cormier.p
2 CONC df CORMIER], page 1-2 </ref>  This generation was the most affected by Father Le Loutre's War and The Great Upheaval.  While the children were born in Beaubassin, they would die elsewhere.<ref name=SW94 />  The family was counted 
2 CONC in the 1750-1751 census at Ouescoque (Vechkok).<ref name=census1750>Acadian & French Canadian Ancestral Home"; 2005 – Present, hosted by Lucie LeBlanc Consentino; [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1751.html 1750/1751 Census] [htt
2 CONC p://freepages.rootsweb.com/~acadiancajun/genealogy/1732cens.htm 1752 Census Transcribed].<blockquote>Pierre CORMIER, his wife and 10 children.</blockquote></ref> In 1750, Beaubassin was the center of a boundary dispute between the
2 CONC  French and English.<ref name=surette /> French soldiers erected small forts on the north side of the Mesagoueche River (the border between Nova Scotia and New Brunswick) including one at [https://goo.gl/maps/bzC5T Beauséjour ridg
2 CONC e]. When the British Commander Lawrence tried to gain a foothold on the river, the priests LeLoutre and Germain burned the Beaubassin church and forced the villagers to burn their houses. TheBritish retreated but eventually built 
2 CONC fortifications on the south side ([http://goo.gl/maps/ZpAKO Fort Lawrence]).  The French soldiers ordered the Acadian families in the southern villages to move to the north side for their protection. Virtually no one complied with
2 CONC  the order, including Pierre's family, whose Ouescoque homestead was located south.  To force them to move, the soldiers and Mi'kmaq warriors (of the fanatical priest [http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/le_loutre_jean_louis_4E.html Je
2 CONC an-Louis Le Loutre] burned the southern villages, including Ouescoque, where the Cormiers had lived for three generations.  The attitudes of the southern villagers are described below:
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>The missionary Le Loutre thought that they were ready to abandon theirland, and even to take up arms against the British... They were, however, perhaps not as determined to emigrate as Le Loutre maintained. Since 1713 
2 CONC the Acadians had always accommodated themselves to the British régime, and it was difficult for them to leave fertile lands that they had cleared and settle in French territory without being assured that sooner or later it would n
2 CONC ot become British. On behalf of the French government Le Loutre promised to establish and feed them for three years, and even to compensate them for their losses. They were not easily convinced, and the missionary apparently used 
2 CONC questionable means to force them to emigrate – threatening them, among other things, with reprisals from the Indians. The Acadians who moved, whether of theirown free will or not, found themselves in an unenviable situation. Both 
2 CONC on Île Saint-Jean and in the Fort Beauséjour region it was difficult to produce sufficient food to meet the needs of the new arrivals.<ref name=Leloutre>[http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/le_loutre_jean_louis_4E.html Gérard Finn, “LE
2 CONC  LOUTRE, JEAN-LOUIS,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biography, vol. 4, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–, accessed November 30, 2013.] </ref> </blockquote> After the destruction of their home, Pierre's family sought refuge
2 CONC  with relatives who lived on the French side at Le Lac (Aulac) across fromJolicoeur (Jolicure NB).<ref>[http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/cormier_pierre_5E.html Stephen A. White, “CORMIER, PIERRE,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biography
2 CONC , vol. 5, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–, accessed November 25, 2013]</ref><ref name=surette />  At Le Lac, several refugees died "from the shock of their upheaval or from the ill treatment they endured."<ref name=s
2 CONC urette /> This included 46-year-old Pierre Cormier, spouse of Cecile Thibodeau who was listed as a widow in the 1752 census at Le Lac, with 5 children.<ref name=census1752>Acadian & French Canadian Ancestral Home"; 2005 – Present,
2 CONC hosted by Lucie LeBlanc Consentino; [http://www.acadian-home.org/census1752.html 1752 Census] The original census can be found at [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c2572/2?r=0&s=1 Acadian Census microfilm C-2572 o
2 CONC f the National Archives of Canada] “Acadie Recensements 1671 – 1752”, Images 277-308.<blockquote> The widow CORMIER, 2 boys, 3 girls.</blockquote></ref> Cécile was only 40 years of age. Although he suffered an early death, Pierre 
2 CONC Cormier left a profound legacy. His widow Cécile and many of his children would stick together in their perilous exile to the French territories (New Brunswick and Quebec). Many of the children would eventually settle in Cormier's
2 CONC  Cove, New Brunswick.  What happened to Pierre's beloved Ouescoque? He would likely approve that most of Amherst Point now is a [http://goo.gl/maps/0CwSb bird sanctuary], a place of peace, owned by the people. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Timeline===
2 CONT :'''c1703-1707''' Birth, in Ouescoque near Beaubassin:'''1704''' Benjamin Church raids Beaubassin again: “The Acadians were in arms and an indecisive skirmish ensued. After the Acadians retreated into the woods, Church and his men
2 CONC  found that the inhabitants hadremoved as much of their household and farm goods as possible. Churchset the buildings on fire and killed about 100 cattle before leaving to return to Boston”<ref name=Griffiths2005>Naomi E.S. Griffi
2 CONC ths, ''From migrant to Acadian : a North-American border people,'' 1604-1755 (Montreal, Québec: McGill-Queen's University Press, 2005) p. 208 (1704 Church’s Raid on Beaubassin) </ref>:'''1710''' Siege of Port-Royal; French surrend
2 CONC er the Fort. Port-Royal, Acadia becomes Annapolis Royal, Nova Scotia<ref name=Dunn>Brenda Dunn, ''A History of Port Royal / Annapolis Royal'' 1605-1800 (Halifaz, N.S.:Nimbus Publishing) pp. 82-85 </ref>:'''1713''' Treaty of Utrech
2 CONC t. France cedes Acadia to England. Permanent British rule<ref name=utrecht>[http://cfml.ci.umoncton.ca/1755-html/index8c0a.html?id=010200000&lang=en&style=G&admin=false&linking= "TheNeutrality: Political Context", in ''1755 l'Hist
2 CONC oire et les Histoires''] (Moncton, N.B.: University of Moncton) </ref>:'''1715''' Delegates from Beaubassin sign a [http://cfml.ci.umoncton.ca/1755-html/indexd093.html?id=010202000&lang=en&style=G&admin=false&linking= conditional 
2 CONC oath of allegiance], promising to stay true to theKing of Great Britain for as long as they stayed in Nova Scotia, and to remain neutral in the event of a conflict between France and Great Britain :'''1720''' and onward: Acadians 
2 CONC refuse to sign an unconditional oath of allegiance. This is initially tolerated by the British as they lackmilitary means to enforce the oath.<ref name=oath>[http://cfml.ci.umoncton.ca/1755-html/indexd093.html?id=010202000&lang=en
2 CONC &style=G&admin=false&linking= "The Neutrality: The First Acadian Delegates", in ''1755Histoire et Les Histoires''], University of Moncton </ref> :'''1713-1744'''  Golden Age<ref name=Griffiths92>Naomie E.S. Griffiths, ''The Contex
2 CONC ts of Acadian History'' 1686-1784 (Montreal, Quebec: McGill-Queens University Press, 1992) p. 61</ref> of Acadian growth and prosperity. Beaubassin is productive in raising cattle, growing grain,and trading fur with the Mi’kmaq an
2 CONC d Maliseet.  Surpluses are traded with both the English (Bay of Fundy) and the French (Baie Vert). <ref name=Brun>Régis Brun with contributions by AJB Johnston and E Clarke, "Fort Beauséjour/Fort Cumberland: Une Histoire/A History
2 CONC ", Société du Monument Lefebvre in Collaboration with Parks Canada, PDF, accessed online in February 2020 at http://parkscanadahistory.com/brochures/beausejour/booklet-history-c1991.pdf</ref> :'''1730''' Marriage to Cécile Thibode
2 CONC au, in Grand Pré :'''1733''' Birth of son, Jean-Baptiste, in Beaubassin :'''1734''' Birth of son, Pierre (Pierrot), in Beaubassin :'''c1736''' Birth of daughter, Marie :'''c1740''' Birth of son, François :'''c1740''' Birth of son,
2 CONC  Joseph :'''1741''' Birth of son Michel, in Beaubassin :'''1742''' Birth of daughter, Marie-Josèphe,in Beaubassin :'''1744''' Birth of son, Charles, in Beaubassin :'''c1746''' Birth of daughter, Marie-Cécile :'''c1748''' Birth of 
2 CONC son, Jacques :'''c1750''' Birth of son, Amand  :'''1750'''  French soldiers erect small forts along their frontier atthe north bank of the Mesagoueche river (now the New Brunswick- Nova Scotia border) including one at Beauséjour r
2 CONC idge. When British Commander Lawrence tries to gain a foothold on the river, the priests LeLoutre and Germain burned the Beaubassin church and force the villagers toburn their houses. The British retreated but eventually establish
2 CONC ed fortifications on the south side.  French soldiers order the Acadian families in the southern villages to move to the north side for their protection. When no one complies, the soldiers and Mi'kmaq warriors (of the priest LeLou
2 CONC tre) burn the southern villages. Most burnt-out families seek refuge north of the French-English boundary in territory surrounding Le Lac (Aulac NB).:'''1750'''  Ouescoque, where the Cormiers lived for three generations, is also b
2 CONC urned. The Cormiers move north, close to their relatives atLe Lac (Aulac, N.B.) who live across from Jolicoeur (Jolicure N.B.). <ref name=census1750/> :'''1750-51''' Death, Pierre Cormier.<ref name=census1752/>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Research Notes ==All these men were cousins, lived in the Beaubassin area and were bornaround the same time. They all have nicknames. Two of them married women with surname Cyr. There continues to be confusion with their childr
2 CONC en, parents and spouses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT *Pierre [[Cormier-2]] dit Rossignol Cormier; married Marie Anne Cyr
2 CONT *Pierre [[Cormier-735]] dit de la Cote; married Marguerite Cyr
2 CONT *Pierre dit Palette Cormier; married Cecile Thibodeau*Pierre [[Cormier-1666]] dit le Grand; married Anne Marie Pitre & Jeanne Thibodeau There are also many discrepancies concerning the places where Pierre lived after he married, a
2 CONC nd his date of death. The website [http://ourmaritimeties.com/tng/getperson.php?personID=I1058&tree=Annette Our Maritime Ties], hosted by Annette and Arthur Owen, lists these differencesand their sources.<ref>Annette and Arthur Ow
2 CONC en, [http://ourmaritimeties.com/tng/getperson.php?personID=I1058&tree=Annette Pierre Cormier] at Our Maritime Ties.</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biographie =='''Pierre Cormier, dit Palette''', est né après le recensement de 1703<ref name=census1703/> et avant celui de 1707<ref name=census1707/> à [https://goo.gl/maps/hzkVhe9zfG12 Ouescoque] (aujourd'hui Amherst Point, N
2 CONC ouvelle Écosse, Canada) qui est près de Beaubassin (Amherst).<ref name=surette>Surette, Paul, ''Atlas of the Acadian Settlement of the Beaubassin'' 1660 to 1755 (Tintamarre and Le Lac: Tantramar Heritage Trust, 2005) p. 199 (Suret
2 CONC te calls Thomas Cormier's son 'Pierre Palette', and Pierre's son 'Pierre à Pallette'); pp. 44-45 (Ouesqoque homestead at Amherst Point); p. 177 (inheritance of the land by all Pierre & Catherine Hebert's children); pp. 30-31 burni
2 CONC ng of Beaubassin andsouthern villages including Ouescoque); p. 185 (burned out Ouescoque Cormier refugees at Le Lac. </ref> Il épousa Cécile Thibodeau, fille de Jean Thibodeau et Marguerite Hebert, en 1730 à Grand Pré.<ref name=DG
2 CONC FA>Stephen A. White, Patrice Gallant, Hector-J. Hébert, [http://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/node/38 ''Dictionnaire Généalogique des Familles Acadiennes''] (Moncton, N.-B.: Centre D'études Acadiennes, Université De Moncton, 1999) p.
2 CONC  406 </ref> Ils se sont installés à Ouescoque parce que le père de Pierre avaitassez de terres pour tous ses enfants. Entre 1733 et 1751, le couple a eu 11 enfants nés pour la plupart à Beaubassin: Jean Baptiste, Pierre (Pierrot à
2 CONC  Nanette), Marie, Francois, Joseph, Michel, Marie-Joseph, Charles, Marie-Cécile, Jacques, et Amand.<ref name=SW94 /> Cette génération a été la plus touchée par la guerre de [http://www.biographi.ca/fr/bio/le_loutre_jean_louis_4E.h
2 CONC tml Père LeLoutre] etle Grand Dérangement. Bien que les enfants soient nés à Beaubassin, ils vont mourir ailleurs.<ref name=SW94/> Pierre est mort entre  1750 et 1752 après l'incendie de Beaubassin par Père LeLoutre et ses guerrie
2 CONC rs Indiens.<ref name=DGFA />Malgré sa mort prématurée dans la quarantaine, l'héritage de Pierre est profonde. II est l'ancêtre de tous les Cormiers du sud-est de Nouveau-Brunswick.<ref name=SW94/> Qu'est-il arrivé à Ouescoque? Pie
2 CONC rre aurait probablement approuvé que Amherst Point soit devenu un [http://goo.gl/maps/0CwSb sanctuaire d'oiseaux], un havre de paix.
2 CONT
