2 CONT Biography of Jean-Baptiste Cormier JEAN-BAPTISTE CORMIER - AN ACADIEN PIONEER Jean-Baptiste Cormier, 'Jean' for short, was born to Pièrre and Cécile Cormier in a small farming village called 'Ouescoque' (al- ternately, Vechkok or 
2 CONC Westcock) in an old Acadien-French set- tlement dubbed 'Beaubassin', which was close to the present- day border of New Brunswick and Nova Scotia. In 1644, Jean's great-grandfather, Thomas Cormier, had emigrated from France as a bo
2 CONC y and later became one of the original pioneer settlers at Beaubassin, from whence he propagated the Cormier name in America. Believed to have been the eldest of eleven child- ren, Jean-Baptiste was a pioneer in his own right - bu
2 CONC t not by choice. Though he lived a long life and carried the legacy of his Acadien ancestors to new frontiers,his path was ladened with hardship and sorrow and many unsettling events. Jean's childhood and teenage years were undoub
2 CONC tedly typical and uneventful. As the eldest son of a farmer, he learned to tillthe soil, tend to the animals, and teach his younger brothers the same; but by age eighteen his father had passed away, leaving Jean responsible for hi
2 CONC s family's well-being. Beaubassin, at the time, had already become the target of British military forces who were determined to dislodge the Acadiens from the region; and at the insistence of a missionary priest who organ- ized th
2 CONC e French resistance, it's believed that the Cormiers left their farm in Ouescoque in 1751, moving further inland to a small village named 'Le Lac', near present-day Jolicure, New Brunswick. Over the next four years, the Brits sche
2 CONC med and strategized and continued to rankle the French inhabitants; and in June of 1755, they executed a plan that would change the face of Acadia forever. It was the start of a horrendousperiod in Acadien-French history which wou
2 CONC ld later come to be called 'Le Grand Dérangement' (The Great Deportation). Jean was twenty-two-years-old when British forces stormed the region to begin the round-up and expulsion of Acadia's French people. During that period, fro
2 CONC m 1755 to 1763, thousands of families were torn apart and forever banished from their home- land. Their farms were confiscated;their fields were trampled; their houses were torched; and a countless number of Acadi- ens were impris
2 CONC oned and subsequently dispersed along the At- lantic seaboard and abroad. Jean-Baptiste Cormier,however, was among the fortunate few who managed to escape their would-be captors. The first wave of the Acadien upheaval began at Bea
2 CONC ubassin. Many, including Jean-Baptiste and most of his immediate fami- ly, fled northward into the woods; but two of Jean's brothers, Joseph and Michel, were later captured, imprisoned, and de- ported to South Carolina. Although n
2 CONC either was ever seen or heard from again, history reveals that the two brothers (along with a Cormier cousin) eventually made their way to the Lou- isiana Territory, which was then controlled by the Spanish - and predominantly Cat
2 CONC holic. Settling near Opelousas, which is now a city in the southwest corner of our modern-day state, they ultimately established 'Cajun' lines of the family name. Another of Jean's brothers, Pièrre III, was also captured and put a
2 CONC board a southbound vessel; but according to a well-told story from that era, he managed to slip overboard under the cover of night as the ship awaited a morning departure. Years later, he and his brothers François, Jacques, and Am
2 CONC and (who had also eluded the British soldiers) would be credited with having established the Cormier name throughout southeastern New Brunswick, especially in and around Moncton and Bouc- touche. The fate of yet another three sibl
2 CONC ings has since been lost to history. Following that fearful summer, Jean-Baptiste and his family ini- tially soughtrefuge along the lower Rivière Saint-Jean and es- tablished themselves near Sainte-Anne-des-Pays-Bas - which came t
2 CONC o be known as St. Anne's Point at present-day Freder- icton, New Brunswick, and where for three years they lived as nomads. But by the autumn of 1758, word had arrived that the British were planning an attack along the Saint-Jean,
2 CONC  forcing the exiled Acadiens to flee yet again. Abandoning their beloved 'Acadie', the Cormiers set out on a long westward trek to Qué- bec, where for nearly a decade they lived in exile along the south shore of the Saint Lawrence
2 CONC  River. The Cormiers first settled in L'Islet, a village about forty miles northeast of Québec City; and although the region proved to be a safe haven for hundreds of Acadien outcasts, there was trouble brewing on the Saint Lawren
2 CONC ce River. The conflict be- tween Britain and France, later dubbed 'The Seven Years War', continued to escalate; and according to oral family tradition, three of Jean's brothers were serving in the French militia in 1759 when the B
2 CONC ritish captured Québec City. At some point in the early 1760s, Jean-Baptiste moved further east to Kamou- ouraska, where in 1762 at L'Église de St-Louis-de-Kamouraska, he exchanged wedding vows with an Acadien gal named Made- lein
2 CONC e Landry. Soon after marrying, however, the couple relo- cated to Montmagny, about sixty miles upstream, where Jean established a sawmill business with one of his cousins (Jean-Baptiste Thibodeau) and Madeleine gave birth to the c
2 CONC ouple's four eldest children. Sadly, two of the four died as infants. The war officially ended in 1763 with the signing of the Treaty of Paris; and though France had been defeated, ceding most of its North American territory to Br
2 CONC itain, French inhabitants no longer had to live in fear of deportation. Québec had served as a welcomed respite for many displaced Acadiens, but most longed to return to their roots. Thus, in January of 1768, Jean and his cousin s
2 CONC old their sawmill business; and within months, Jean and his wife, with two young daughters, had made their way back to Acadien soil. Along with his widowed mother, his siblings, and about a dozen other families, Jean resettled alo
2 CONC ng the south shore of the Riv- ière Saint-Jean - just west of Sainte-Anne-des-Pays-Bas, in the same place from which they had fled ten years earlier. For the most part, the region had remained as they left it, still ru- ral and un
2 CONC developed, and their only neighbors were the native Malecite Indians. At long last, the Acadiens had come home; but their lives had been changed forever and there was more trouble yet to come. Jean-Baptiste's family would grow to 
2 CONC include nine more children (eight of whom would be born along the Rivière St-Jean); and by the end of the decade, another fifteen families had arrived from southern Québec. With a renewed spirit, they cleared the land, built house
2 CONC s, planted crops, fished the river, and hunted the forests; but none of them held an official title to the land on which they had settled. In fact three years earlier in 1765, in a weak bid to promote growth in the region, large t
2 CONC racts of land had been doled out as townships to English entrepreneurs whose efforts to attract suitable tenants had met with little success. Thus, the returning Acadiens had no problem staking out lots for their families. As devo
2 CONC ut Catholics, they erected a crude chapel, baptized their children, celebrated the marri- ages of young couples, and prayed at the graves of those who died; and from time to time, a missionary priest would pass by to conduct the f
2 CONC ormal ceremonies of the church andofficially document the events. Then along came the American Revolu- tion of 1776. After losing the war against America's thirteen colonies, thou- sands of British loyalists withdrew to Nova Scoti
2 CONC a, establishing themselves in former Acadien villages. By 1780, however, many had been enticed to settle north of the peninsula - near Ste- Anne-des-Pays-Bas; and once again, the Acadiens were faced withtoubling issues and tough d
2 CONC ecisions. In 1783, according to a military survey dubbed 'The Studholme Report', there were about sixty French families squatting along the river from Ste- Anne to Kingsclear; and on that list appeared the name 'Jean Baptiste Corm
2 CONC ier' (with a wife and ten children), who stated that he had lived there for sixteen years and is said to have cleared twelve acres of land. The following year, Britain divided Nova Scotia into three colonies, one of which was name
2 CONC d 'New Brunswick'; and on the appointment of a new governor, many of the Acadiens petitioned for titles to theirfarms. Some, in- cluding the Cormiers, were successful in their efforts; but the loyalists continued to harass the Aca
2 CONC diens - and even tried to have two of Jean's sons evicted from their land. A now famous quote attributed to Acadien elder Jean-Baptiste Cyr sums up the feelings of that era: 'My God, can it be true that there is no place left on e
2 CONC arth for a 'cadien?'. Realizing that they had few alternatives and that a peaceful coexistence with their Protestant counterparts would be difficult at best, Acadiens once again petitioned the governor with a request: that they be
2 CONC  granted some land along an uninhabited stretch of the up- per Saint-Jean - above the Grand Falls. It was a prudent de- cision. Their plea was heard and in 1785 their request was ap- proved. Each family was promised a 200-acre par
2 CONC cel of land along the river, in a remote part of northwestern New Bruns- wick which later came to called 'The Madawaska Settlement'. By the fall of 1785, a small contingent of Acadien men hadar- rived in Madawaska to scout out the
2 CONC  area and assess their op- tions; and the following spring, Acadien families quit Sainte- Anne for good. After a two-week journey along the river, Jean and his family safely arrived in Madawaska. His aging mother, however, and mos
2 CONC t of his siblings would stay behind and later set foot in the opposite direction - to establish themselves in and around present-day Bouctouche, Moncton, and Memram- cook. Tragically, the family matriarch, who had been born in 171
2 CONC 2 and endured more heartache than any mother should, died en route to Moncton in her mid seventies. In Madawaska, the Cormiers settled along the west bank of the Rivière Saint-Jean about ten miles downstream from Saint- Basile, (o
2 CONC pposite La Grand Island, in present-day Lille, Maine); and in 1787, atabout age forty-four, Jean's wife gave birth to her thirteenth child (Marie Françoise). Being that the newly or- ganized settlement was then far removed from an
2 CONC y other, sev- eral years would pass before an official parish emerged - and it was doubtful that any missionary priest would come to call. Thus, the Cormiers returned to the Québecois village where they had married - and where Mad
2 CONC eleine's father (and most of her siblings) had established permanent roots; and on Sep- tember 16, 1787, at L'Église de St-Louis-de-Kamouraska, their lastborn child was christened - with Madeleine's eldest broth- er Charles and hi
2 CONC s wife Josèphte serving as the godparents. Although the Madawaskan pioneers were by then accustomed to starting their lives anew in unfamiliar places, it would take them a few years to settle in - and for the governor to fulfill h
2 CONC is promise. At long last, in 1790, forty-nine Acadiens at the north end of the settlement were granted titles to their farms; and in 1794, in what came to be called the 'Soucy Conces- sion', the Cormiers were among two dozen more 
2 CONC at the south end of the settlement to be granted ownership of their lots. As shown on the old map attached to this memorial, 'Baptiste' and his sons Pièrre, François, and Alexis had planted them- selves on adjacent tracts of land 
2 CONC fronting on the river, each almost a thousand feet wide and about a-mile-and-a-three-quarters deep; and it was there on a 210-acre parcel that Jean established his farm and lived out the remainder of his life. After nearly four de
2 CONC cades of unrest and uncertainty, Jean and Madeleine quietlyresumed a normal life. By 1792, their three eldest daughters had married; and by the end of the century, they had danced at the weddings of three more children and were al
2 CONC ready surrounded by grandchildren. Unfortunately, how- ever, their time together was also nearing an end; and in early 1803, Madeleine (Landry) Cormier succumbed at age sixty and was laid to rest in the parish cemetery at Saint-Ba
2 CONC sile. Though Jean would survive his beloved wife by almost twenty years, he never remarried. Given that a man's life expectancy in that era is estimated to have been forty years, Jean-Baptiste couldbe counted among the blessed. Pe
2 CONC rhaps owing, in part, to his own personal stam- ina, this old 'cadien lived to the venerable age of eighty-eight. Like his wife, Jean passed away in the cold of winter- on St. Valentine's Day in 1822, half a month shy of his eight
2 CONC y-ninth birthday. At the time of his death, he was survived by seven of his thirteen children; though none of the thirteen had inher- ited their father's genes and all but one would succumb before age sixty. His legacy, however, i
2 CONC s that he was the progenitor of almost all Cormiers in the Madawaska region and in northern Maine. His children had showered him with at least seventy- eight grandchildren - of which sixty-five survived infancy and grew to adultho
2 CONC od; and one might presume that he had passed on to them the stories of his Acadien ancestors and instilled in them a spirit of determination. Two days after his death, Jean was interred in the parish cemetery in Saint-Basile; and 
2 CONC to this day, according to one of his many descendants, his grave is marked by a centuries-old monument. May he rest in peace. The above biography was researched and written by The Aca- dien (findagrave contributor #47535334), who 
2 CONC added this me- morial and is a great-great-great-grandson of Jean-Baptiste. ------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
