2 CONT '''Pierre Cormier''', also known as Pierrot, was born in Beaubassin, Acadie (present-day Amherst, Cumberland County, Nova Scotia, Canada) onAugust 3, 1734<ref>Library and Archives Canada Fonds des Archives départementales de la Ch
2 CONC arente-Maritime [La Rochelle, France] : C-1207 Registres de Beaubassin - reel_c1207 MG 6 A 2 (Image 52) http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1207/52?r=0&s=6</ref><ref name="SW">Stephen A. White, ''La généalogie des t
2 CONC rente-sept familles hôtesses des "Retrouvailles 94", Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne,'' vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994); p. 1-2 [https://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/files/umcm-ceaac/wf/wf/pdf/37fam-cormier.pdf CORMIER] <b
2 CONC lockquote>2. ii. Pierre dit Pierrot à Nannette, n Beaubassin 3 août 1734; m v 1755 Anne dite Nannette GAUDET
2 CONT (Augustin & Agnès Chiasson); d Memramcook 24 mars 1818.</blockquote> </ref>to parents [[Cormier-77|Pierre Cormier]] and [[Thibodeau-22|Cécile Thibodeau]]. Stephen White also mentions Rivière-des-Héberts (near River Hebert), N.S. a
2 CONC s place of birth.<ref  name="SWonline"/>  Pierrot was raised on a nearby farm in Ouescoque Heights (Amherst Point, Nova Scotia).<ref name="Surette2005">Paul Surette,  ''Atlas of the Acadian Settlement of the Beaubassin: 1660 to 17
2 CONC 55'' (Tintamarre and Le Lac: Tantramar Heritage Trust, 2005) pp. 45, 177 </ref> His family were French neutrals who were living in the formerly French, then-British-held territory of Nova Scotia.   In 1750 the French erected [http
2 CONC s://www.pc.gc.ca/en/lhn-nhs/nb/beausejour/culture/histoire-history/construction Fort Beauséjour] on the north side of the Missaguash River, and the British erected [https://www.pc.gc.ca/en/lhn-nhs/nb/beausejour/culture/histoire-hi
2 CONC story/construction Fort Lawrence] on the south side of the river. The French soldiers ordered the Acadian families in British territory to move to the Frenchside for their protection; however, fearing the loss of good farmlandthei
2 CONC r families had worked for generations, they refused. The British burned the southern Acadian villages<ref name="Surette2005" /> in events that would lead up to the expulsion of the Acadians, historically known as "le grand dérange
2 CONC ment" or "the Great Upheaval."  Pierrot's family was forced to abandon their homestead and seek refugewith relatives who lived on the French side across from Jolicoeur (now Jolicure, New Brunswick). That same year, Pierrot's fathe
2 CONC r died.<ref>"1750 Acadian Census" online at  [http://www.acadian-home.org/frames.html Acadian and French-Canadian Ancestral Home] </ref><ref>"1752 Acadian Census" online at [http://www.acadian-home.org/frames.html Acadian and Fren
2 CONC ch-Canadian Ancestral Home] </ref> 1755 was another momentous year for Pierrot. First he married his beloved [[Gaudet-299|Anne "Nanette" Gaudet]]  (daughter of [[Gaudet-146 |Augustin Gaudet]] and [[Chiasson-49|Agnès Chiasson]]).<r
2 CONC ef name="SW" /><ref  name="SWonline">Stephen White,  “Pierre Cormier,” ''Dictionary of Canadian Biography,'' vol. 5 (Toronto, ON: University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003, accessed July 2011) [http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/co
2 CONC rmier_pierre_5E.html Stephen White,  “Pierre Cormier,” in ''Dictionary of Canadian Biography] </ref> Pierrot would come to be known as "Pierrot à Nannette."
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:<ref name="SW" />
2 CONT #Pierre, b  Pays-Bas, Rivière St-Jean 1756
2 CONT #François dit Dougre, c 1758
2 CONT #Joseph-Michel dit José, b L'Islet 1761
2 CONT #Marie-Anne, b L'Islet 1762
2 CONT #Marie-Cécile, b L'Islet 1764
2 CONT #Claude, b 1765
2 CONT #Ignace, b Rivière St-Jean 1770 Later in the year Pierrot was taken prisoner at Jolicoeur and imprisoned at [https://www.pc.gc.ca/en/lhn-nhs/nb/beausejour/culture/histoire-history Fort Beauséjour] for several weeks. There are seve
2 CONC ral accounts of his escape from the British on the eve of the expulsion of the Acadians in 1755, including one that involves dressing as a woman. The one with the most credibility, according to Stephen White, comes from genealogis
2 CONC t Placide Gaudet in 1877:
2 CONT
2 CONT <blockquote>"Pierrot, taken prisoner with his brothers at Jolicœur, was put aboard a Carolina-bound deportation vessel but slipped overboard the night before its departure. By creeping through the tall hay on shore he attained an 
2 CONC aboiteau [dike] guarded by British soldiers and, when their backs were turned, clambered onto the butt of a timber over the water. Swinging from one butt end to another, he succeeded in crossing the aboiteau unobserved. On the oth
2 CONC er bank he again crept through the fieldsuntil he was able to break for the woods. After narrowly evading a band of soldiers tracking him with a dog, he arrived at an extent of water separating him from an Acadian encampment. Once
2 CONC  recognized he was soon crossed over. Learning from these families that his own had fled the night before toward Quebec, Pierrot immediately left in search of them. The Cormiers were reunited at Sainte-Anne (near Fredericton, N.B.
2 CONC ), where they remained until Robert Monckton’s raids persuaded them to move to Kamouraska (Que.), likely in 1758."<ref name="SWonline"/></blockquote> Pierrot and Nanette had two sons during this time: Pierre (b. 1756)  and Françoi
2 CONC s, dit Dougre (b. 1758).<ref name="SW"/>   The move to French-controlled Québec was not without its perils for Pierrot and his brothers Jacques and François. They served in the militia during the fall of Québec in 1759 and then jo
2 CONC ined a French frigate at Pointe-Lévy (Lauzon/Lévis, Québec) in hopes of earning passageto France. After engagement with two British war vessels, likely the encounter off Cap-Rouge between Jean Vauquelin and Robert Swanton in May 1
2 CONC 760 near the Jacques-Cartier River, the frigate ran aground. The three Cormier brothers were among the 60 of 160 crew members who managed to survive, swimming ashore through the April icy waters.<ref name="SWonline"/> During exile
2 CONC  in Québec, Pierrot and Nanette lived at L'Islet in the Kamouraska region between 1761 and 1764.<ref name="SWonline"/>  They had three more children there: Joseph-Michel, dit José (b. 1761), Marie-Anne (b. 1762), and Marie-Cécile 
2 CONC (b. 1764).<ref name="SW"/> About 1765 Pierrot and Nanette returned to Sainte-Anne with their children, Pierrot's mother, and his four brothers.<ref name="SWonline"/> There, Pierrot and Nanette had two more children: Claude (b. 176
2 CONC 5) and Ignace (b. 1770).<ref name="SW"/> By July 1783 Pierrot had cleared 20 acres, which he occupied for 13 years. Unfortunately, the Acadians of Sainte-Anne had not secured titlesto all of the land they farmed, and grants to dis
2 CONC banded soldiers and loyalists were soon encroaching on what they considered to be their land. Since their small acreage was insufficient to support their families, they moved between 1786 and 1787 to vacant land on the west side o
2 CONC f the Memramcook River.<ref name="SWonline"/>  However the so-called “vacant” land where the Cormiers settled hadalready been granted to Joseph Goreham and then sold to Joseph Frederick Wallet DesBarres in 1775. On June 5, 1792 th
2 CONC e Cormiers and others presented a petition to the New Brunswick government complaining of the "extravagant" demands of DesBarres and arguing that his land should be granted to them in consideration of the substantial improvements 
2 CONC made during their occupation. Their efforts were thwarted by DesBarres and his agents, but the Cormiers remained on the land through at least 1809.<ref name="SWonline"/>The dispute, however, would drag on among the descendants for
2 CONC  50 years.<ref name="Surette2003">Paul Surette, ''Memramcook: La lutte pour laterre'', Memramcook: Société historique de la vallée de Memramcook, 2003 Cormier Genealogy in Appendix; also 1792 map. </ref>The Cormiers’ farm was at L
2 CONC ’Anse des Cormiers (Cormier Cove, New Brunswick, Canada).<ref name="Surette2003"/> All the dangers, numerous relocations, and disputes faced by Pierrot since he was 16 did not prevent him from living a long life. On March 24, 1818
2 CONC  Pierrot died at the age of 84 and was buried the following dayin the St. Thomas Parish Cemetery in Memramcook, Westmorland County, New Brunswick.<ref>Ancestry.com. Acadia, Canada, Vital and Church Records (Drouin Collection), 175
2 CONC 7-1946 [database on-line]. Provo, UT, USA:Ancestry.com Operations, Inc., 2007. Original data: Gabriel Drouin, comp. Drouin Collection. Montreal, Quebec, Canada: Institut Généalogique Drouin.  Search: M > Memramcook > 1816-1818 > i
2 CONC mage 28 of 44. Accessed on ancestry.ca, April 2020.* View {{Ancestry Image|1110|d13p_34030619|ca}} on ancestry.ca,  (requires subscription).* View free [https://www.ancestry.ca/sharing/19597172?h=38878a Ancestry Sharing Image]. <b
2 CONC lockquote>Pierre died March 24, 1818 at the age of 88 and was buriedMarch 25, 1818 at the catholic cemetery of the church of Saint Thomas, Memramcook New Brunswick Canada. In attendance were Simon Leblanc and Joseph Doirion. The f
2 CONC uneral service was conducted by the Rev. Brodeur, Ptre.</blockquote> </ref><ref>Memramcook, Nouveau-Brunswick, Registre de la paroisse Saint-Thomas, catholique, Baptêmes et Mariages 1806-1870, Sépultures 1807-1870 ( F.M. 9, A 11, 
2 CONC vol 8), Image # 1004, [http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c3018/1004?r=0&s=2 entry for Pierre Cormier] Burial 25 March 1818, digital images, Héritage, Genealogy collection, Library and Archives Canada, reel C-3018, 
2 CONC Parish Registers: New Brunswick : C-3018 accessed March 2020. </ref>
