2 CONT [[Category:Great Upheaval]]
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT :Born 1738, Etienne was deported to South Carolina with his brothers Michel and Joseph, and headed to Louisiana, where he without doubt diedsoon after their arrival.<ref>BIRTH-NAME-MARRIAGE-CHILDREN-RESIDENCES: Bona Arsenault, HIS
2 CONC TOIRE ET GENEALOGIE DES ACADIENS, vols. 1-6; 1630-1775; Quebec, Le Conseil de la Vie Francaise en Amerique, 1965; p. 920-921; Univ. of Calif., Berkeley Library, CS31 A77 MAIN. Note: source published by Karen Theriot Reader, Rootsw
2 CONC eb.com.</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Research Notes=="Bona Arsenault lists Etienne as one of his sons and says that Etiennewas deported along with his brothers, Michel and Joseph, to South Carolina. Michel and Joseph later settled in Louisianna but there is no furt
2 CONC her mention of Etienne. None of the other sources ([https://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/files/umcm-ceaac/wf/wf/pdf/37fam-cormier.pdf Stephen A. White] and Pierre Surette in Atlas of the Acadian Settlements ofthe Beaubassin) mention
2 CONC  Etienne as a son of Pierre and Cecile."<ref>Annette and Arthur Owen [http://ourmaritimeties.com/tng/getperson.php?personID=I1058&tree=Annette Pierre Cormier] in Our Maritime Ties.</ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT <references /> See also:*Birth - Leger, Beloved Acadia of my Ancestors (Translated by Antoine Bugeauld; Montreal: Fleuve, 1992), Family 152, Pg 316.
