2 CONT
2 CONT === Biography ===Around 1740,<ref name=SW> White, Stephen A. ''La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des Retrouvailles 94,'' online articles, Les Cahiers de la Société historique acadienne, vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994), [h
2 CONC ttps://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/files/umcm-ceaac/wf/wf/pdf/37fam-cormier.pdf CORMIER], page 1-2 </ref> François, nicknamed "Le Rebelle” was born on a farm southeast of the village of Beaubassin (Amherst, Nova Scotia, Canada). He
2 CONC  lived with his parents Pierre Cormier and Cecile Thibodeau at [https://goo.gl/maps/hzkVhe9zfG12 Ouescoque] (Amherst Point)<ref name=Surette2005>Surette, Paul. Atlas of the Acadian Settlement of the Beaubassin 1660 to 1755. Tintam
2 CONC arre and Le Lac.  Tantramar Heritage Trust. 2005, p 45, 177.</ref> He was the fourth generation to live there since Thomas Cormier settled it 1679. Ouescoque and Beaubassin village were in the British-held territory of Nova Scotia
2 CONC  south of the Mesagoueche river (now the New Brunswick- Nova Scotia border).  The Cormiers were considered French “neutrals”. 
2 CONT By the time François was 10, the world he knew was shattered by a boundry dispute between the French and English. In 1750<ref name=Surette2005/>,  French soldiers erected small forts along their frontier at the north bank of the M
2 CONC esagoueche river (now the New Brunswick- Nova Scotia border) including one at [https://goo.gl/maps/bzC5T Beauséjour ridge]. When British Commander Lawrence tried to gain a foothold on the river, the French priests LeLoutre and Ger
2 CONC main burned the church and forced the Beaubassin villagers to burn their houses. The British retreated but eventually established fortifications on the south side.  Thechurch in which François was baptized was gone, save for the C
2 CONC hurch bell. Then French soldiers ordered the Acadian families in the southern villages including Ouescoque to move to the north side of the river for their protection. When no one complied, the soldiers and Mi'kmaq warriors (of th
2 CONC e priest LeLoutre) burned the southern villages. Most burnt-out families sought refuge north of the French-English boundary interritory surrounding Le Lac (Aulac NB).  François’s family moved near relatives living there across fro
2 CONC m Jolicoeur (Jolicure NB). Tragically, his father died within the year. <ref>1750 Acadian Census. Accessed at Acadian and French Canadian Ancestral Home http://www.acadian-home.org/frames.html; 1752 Acadian Census. Accessed at Aca
2 CONC dian and French Canadian Ancestral Home http://www.acadian-home.org/frames.html</ref> In 1755<ref name=SWonline>[http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/cormier_pierre_5E.html Stephen A. White, “CORMIER, PIERRE,” in Dictionary of Canadian 
2 CONC Biography, vol. 5, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–, accessed December 19, 2013.]</ref>, the Acadians of Beaubassin were the first to be imprisoned. Fortunately, many had hidden in the forests as their priest, Father 
2 CONC Le Guerne was warned in advance of the English attack. The Cormier brothers were taken prisoner at Jolicoeur (Jolicure NB near Sackville). Pierrot Cormier’s dramatic escape froma deportation ship is described in his Cormier-143|bi
2 CONC ography. Theescape of other Cormiers is described by the [http://139.103.17.56/1755-html/index3428.html?id=010502000&lang=en&style=G&admin=false&linking= University of Moncton]::‘’’Amongst the Acadians who resisted the Deportation
2 CONC , some thwarted the British plans by escaping from the prisons and the forts where they were held. The example that is the most often cited is the escape from the Fort Cumberland (the former Fort Beauséjour), made possible by the 
2 CONC direct involvement and cleverness of the Acadian women who had not been imprisoned. In fact, after the capture of Fort Beauséjouron June 16, 1755, the British troops imprisoned many Acadian men, butfor financial reasons authorized
2 CONC  the Acadian women to take care of their needs. Hence, by concealing arms in the food and by sneaking in extra ladies' clothing, some of them succeeded in facilitating the escape of their husbands or other relatives. Members of th
2 CONC e Cormier family escaped from Fort Cumberland disguised as women.1755 Histoire et Histoires’’’   The Cormier family made their way to French territory at Sainte-Anne des-pays-d’en-bas (now Fredericton, N.B.). The story of the refu
2 CONC gees along the St John river is described in detail [http://www.acadian-home.org/saw-acadians-st-john-river.html here]. The Cormiers remained in the area until about 1758 when Monckton's raids persuaded them to moveto Kamouraska (
2 CONC Quebec).<ref name=SWonline/> The move to French-controlled Quebec was not without its perils for François and his brothers!<ref name=SWonline/> According to another oral tradition, they served in the militia during the fall of Que
2 CONC bec in 1759 and then joined a French frigate at Pointe-Lévy (Lauzon and Lévis). After engagement with two British war vessels, the frigate ran aground. The brothers were among the 60 of 160 crew members who managed to swim ashore 
2 CONC through the icy waters. Stephen White speculates that this story likely refers to the encounter off Cap-Rouge between Jean Vauquelin and Robert Swanton in May 1760. In 1761<ref name=SW/> 21 year old François chose a 22 year old wi
2 CONC dow Anastasie Melanson to be his bride. She was the daughter of Paul Melanson and Marie Theriault and the widow of Claude Brun. They were married in St Pierre du Sud, Montmagny, Quebec.<ref> "Québec, registres paroissiaux catholiq
2 CONC ues, 1621-1979," database with images, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3QS7-8993-FG9F?cc=1321742&wc=HCQJ-2NL%3A22343501%2C22343502%2C23427603 : 16 July 2014), Saint-Pierre-Montmagny > Saint-Pierre-du-Sud > In
2 CONC dex 1727-1876 Baptêmes, mariages, sépultures 1727, 1728, 1748-1771 > image 363 of 457; Archives Nationales du Quebec (National Archives of Quebec), Montreal. </ref> Two oftheir children were baptised in Quebec: Marie Cécile at St-
2 CONC Thomas-de-Montmagny and Marguerite at Ste-Anne-de-la-Pocatiere in Kamouraska.  Around 1765 the families of Francois and his brothers, including theirmother Cécile Thibodeau, returned to St Anne (Parish of Rivière St Jean, now Fred
2 CONC ericton).<ref name=SWonline/> Four more of Francois' andAnastasie’s children were born there: Marie Joseph, Francois, Germain, and Sylvain.<ref name=SW/>  By 1783<ref name=SWonline/>, the Cormier brothers had cleared 20 acres, whi
2 CONC ch they occupied for 13 years (See [http://www.acadian-home.org/StudholmReport.html Studholm Report]). However, the Acadians of Sainte-Anne had not secured title to their farms. Grants to disbanded soldiers and loyalists were soon
2 CONC  encroaching on what the Acadians considered to be their land. The property rights issue is described by the University of Moncton <ref>[http://139.103.17.56/1755-html/index4383.html?id=010803000&lang=en&style=G&admin=false&linkin
2 CONC g= University of Moncton,1755 Histoire et Les Histoires]</ref>: :’’’Acadians living in Acadie after the Deportation did not havea quiet life. When they arrived in what is today's Maritimes, Acadians had no property rights, apart f
2 CONC rom a few exceptions. Many occupied lands’ to which the colonial administration considered they had no rights and were driven off when these lands were purchased. The War of American Independence, which brought a massive arrival o
2 CONC f Loyalists in the Maritimes, also forced many Acadians to move away. This was the case with those from the region formerly known as Saint-Anne-des-Pays-Bas (today's Fredericton)’’’ Around 1786, the Cormier brothers and some of th
2 CONC eir married children moved to vacant land on the west side of the Memramcook River (known asLa Pointe).  Their aged mother Cecile Thibodeau died during the trip.<ref name=SWonline/> Around 1788 Abbé Thomas Francois LeRoux  of Memr
2 CONC amcook described these 20 families: <ref>Gaudet, Placide. Arbre Genealogique de Monsieur L’Abbe Joseph Francois Xavier Cormier, Cure du Barachois, N.B. Centre d’études acadiennes, Fond Placide-Gaudet. Callnumber 1.95-2 page 8 of 1
2 CONC 0.</ref> :’’’The 20 families from the St John River are very poor (which I want to correct).  I have succeeded with some but others remain rebels.  This is now my disgrace.  I have supported almost all of them as much as I can, bu
2 CONC t I am obliged to buy grain for myself in order to live.  If it were not for the glorious people of the St-John River, my flock would be wise and calm.  I do not know which of these household heads are rebellious and arrogant. The
2 CONC se families have not had spiritual support for 20 years with the exception of priests’ visits in 1768and 1774.’’’  Around 1792<ref name=SWonline/>, the land where the Cormiers settled had been granted to Joseph Goreham and then so
2 CONC ld to Joseph Frederick Wallet DesBarres in 1775. The Acadians petitioned the New Brunswick government complaining of the "extravagant" demands of DesBarres's agent, Mary Cannon, and argued that his land should be granted to them i
2 CONC n consideration of the substantial improvements made during their occupation. Their efforts were thwarted by DesBarres and his agents. The dispute dragged on among the descendants for 50 years.<ref name=surette2003/> The Cormiers,
2 CONC  whose farm was located at l’Anse des Cormier (Cormier Cove), resolved their dispute earlier than some. In 1822 they purchased their land from DesBarres.  Although Surette calls it a “capitulation”, the Cormiers are finally landow
2 CONC ners!  For others, they waited until 1841 to purchase their land for $1 per acre.<ref name=surette2003>Paul Surette, Memramcook: La lutte pour la terre, Memramcook: Société historique de la vallée de Memramcook, 2003, pages 118, 1
2 CONC 68-169.</ref> In 1816, François’ beloved Anastasie died at the age of 77.<ref>Ancestry.com. Acadia, Canada, Vital and Church Records (Drouin Collection), 1670-1946 [database on-line]. Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com Operations Inc, 2
2 CONC 007.Original data: Gabriel Drouin, comp. Drouin Collection. Montreal, Quebec, Canada: Institut Généalogique Drouin. St-Thomas Parish at Memramcook 1816-1818 image page 11 of 44.</ref> In 1825, Francois died at the age of 85. His e
2 CONC xploits and hardships did not stop him from living a long life! His legacy was as the generational bridge between the first Cormier homestead in Ouescoque to a new homestead at Cormier Cove where subsequent generations prospered.<
2 CONC ref name=SW/>
2 CONT
