2 CONT A Patriot of the American Revolution for LOUISIANA.  DAR  Ancestor #: A026105  --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category: Great Upheaval]]
2 CONT [[Category: Louisiana First Families]] [[Category: Acadian Immigrants to Louisiana]] [[Category: The Wall of Names at the Acadian Memorial]] [[Category: Louisiana Acadian Coast Settlers]]
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT {{Louisiana Family}}
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT
2 CONT {{Migrating Ancestor
2 CONT | origin = Acadie
2 CONT | origin-flag = Acadie-1.png | destination = Louisiana | destination-flag = Louisiana_Families_Project_Images-1.png }}Joseph Cormier is on the Wall of Names at the Acadian Memorial in St. Martinville, Louisiana, Plaque  2 Right. W
2 CONC ith him are his first spouse, Marguerite Saulnier, and their children, Susanne, Marie Louise, Felicite; and his brother, '''Michel Cormier'''.<ref>''The Wall of Names at the Acadian Memorial,'' compiled by Jane G. Bulliard and the
2 CONC  Wall ofNames Committee (Opelousas, LA: Bodemuller, 2015)  p. 15 </ref>
2 CONT ----
2 CONT <!-- '''5th Generation- From Ouescoque Heights (Amherst Point, NS) near Beaubassin, to prison, and to Louisiana, USA''' -->'''Michel Cormier''' was born on the 19th of January, 1741, the sixth child of Pierre Cormier and Cecile Th
2 CONC ibodeau. He was baptized at Beaubassin, Acadie (now Amherst, Nova Scotia, Canada).<ref>Library and Archives Canada Fonds des Archives départementales de la Charente-Maritime [La Rochelle, France] : C-1207 Registres de Beaubassin -
2 CONC  reel_c1207 MG 6 A 2 (Image 78) http://heritage.canadiana.ca/view/oocihm.lac_reel_c1207/78?r=0&s=6</ref><ref name=SW94>Stephen A. White,  ''La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des "Retrouvailles 94", Les Cahiers de la 
2 CONC Société historique acadienne,'' vol. 25, nos 2 et 3 (1994). (Cormier) </ref>His family had a  farm nearby on the Ouescoque Heights (now Amherst Point, Nova Scotia). By the time he was 9 years old, the world he knew would be shatte
2 CONC red and he would lose or become separated from all but one of his family members.     '''1750-55:''' 9-year-old Michel and his family were refugees from their burnt-out home and moved across the French-English boundary to Le Lac (
2 CONC now Aulac, NB)<ref name=AtlasASB> Paul Surette, ''Atlas of the Acadian Settlement of the Beaubassin 1660 to 1755, Tintamarre and Le Lac''  (Tantramar Heritage Trust, 2005) </ref> His father died within the year.<ref>Acadian Census
2 CONC  1750, 1752, 1755 Accessed at Acadian and French Canadian Ancestral Home http://www.acadian-home.org/frames.html </ref><ref name=AtlasASB /> '''1755:''' Some of the Cormier brothers were taken prisoner at Jolicoeur (now Jolicure, 
2 CONC New Brunswick) and were able to escape and rejoin their mother and siblings.<ref>Stephen White, “Pierre Cormier” in ''Dictionary of Canadian Biography Online.''  Accessed July 2011 at: http://www.biographi.ca/009004-119.01-.php?Bi
2 CONC oId=36465&PHPSESSID=73cd94f0sto8ap6bp2uml5u6l6 </ref> Michel and his brother Joseph and a younger first cousin, Jean-Baptiste, became separated from the rest of the family. "They either fell into British hands during the family's 
2 CONC wanderings from Chignecto to Québec and back, or they became separated early and joined the Acadian resistance in the Petitcoudiac area led by Joseph Broussard, dit Beausoleil.  Joseph and Michel's mother was a Thibodeau, the fami
2 CONC ly into whichthe Broussard, dit Beausoleil brothers had married.  Jean-Baptiste Cormier, fils's mother was a Richard, another family close to the Broussard clan.  So the Cormier boys may have been part of the Broussards' large, ex
2 CONC tended family.  Joseph, Michel, and Jean-Baptiste, fils surrendered to the British probably in the early 1760s when the Broussards surrendered.  And, like hundreds of other captured Acadians, they spentthe next few years in a Hali
2 CONC fax prison waiting for the war to end."<ref name=AIG>Steven Cormier, "Acadians in Grey," website accessed July 2011 at http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-CORMIER.htm . </ref>  The war finally ended in 1763. After the w
2 CONC ar, Joseph and Michel and the Beausoleil group worked for ayear to earn passage to sail ultimately to New Orleans. They settled in Opelousas, Louisiana, and prospered as cattlemen.<ref name=AIG /> Around 1769 Michel married Anne S
2 CONC aulnier, daughter of Jacques Saulnierand Anne Hebert, and widow of Basile Babin.<ref name=SW94/> Around 1775 Michel married Catherine Stelly, daughter of Johann Georges Stelly and Christine Edelmeyer.<ref name=SW94/>  In 1789 Mich
2 CONC el married a third time on February 10 at St. Martinville,Louisiana. His bride was Madeleine Breau, daughter of Charles Breau and Claire Trahan.<ref name=SW94/>  In 1790, only a year after his marriage, Michel died at the age of 4
2 CONC 9 on December 30th in Opelousas, Louisiana.<ref name=SW94/>
2 CONT
