2 CONT **Drouin Institute (Archived marriage record - see attached in Media tab)
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Married September 5, 1774, St. John River, NB, Canada, with Marie-Josephe Roy † (Parents: Joseph Roy † & Mary Agnes D'Amours Chauffours †) which;
2 CONT
2 CONT Benoni Cormier †
2 CONT
2 CONT "François" said Francis Cormier †
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Cormier ca 1780-1866
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes Cormier †
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste Cormier †
2 CONT
2 CONT Denis Cormier †
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Historier et Genealogie des Acadien  Bona Arsenault, Compiler: Bona Arsenault  CHAU-TV, Baie des Chaleurs, 1988 3rd Printing 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Vol. III P. 914 Beaubassin 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Historier et Genealogie des Acadien  Bona Arsenault, Compiler: Bona Arsenault  CHAU-TV, Baie des Chaleurs, 1988 3rd Printing 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT Vol. II P. 922 Beaubassin 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT [[Category:Great Upheaval]] [[Category:Rivière-Saint-Jean, Acadie]] [[Category: Beaubassin, Acadie]]
2 CONT {{Acadian}}
2 CONT == Biography == 
2 CONT Amand was born about 1750 in Acadia as the youngest son to parents Pierre Cormier and Cécile Thibodeau.<ref name="Arsenault">Bona Arsenault, ''Histoire et Généalogie des Acadiens'', vols. 1-6; 1630-1775; Quebec, Le Conseil de la V
2 CONC ie Française en Amérique, 1965; p. 921 (Beaubassin); :Born 1750, Amand married at Sainte-Anne-du-Pays-Bas on 5 Sep 1774 to Marie-Josephe ROY, daughter of Joseph & Marie-Annette D'Amours de Chauffour. Amand was at Madawaska in 1777
2 CONC , and settled at Memramcook around 1787.</ref><ref name="Cormier">White, Stephen A. ''La généalogie des trente-sept familles hôtesses des "Retrouvailles 94"'' (Cormier), Les Cahiers, La Société Historique Acadienne, Vol. 25, #2-3,
2 CONC  1994; Université de Moncton [https://www.umoncton.ca/umcm-ceaac/node/55 37 Families].<blockquote>14. xi. Amand, m Rivière St-Jean 5 sept 1774 Marie-Josèphe ROY (Joseph & Marie-Agnès D'Amours de Chauffours); d v 1786.</blockquote>
2 CONC </ref> Amand's parents had a farm at Ouescoque (Amherst Point, Nova Scotia) near Beaubassin, Acadia - an area whose ownership, at the time, was in dispute between the British and the French. Soon after Amand was born, the British 
2 CONC seized control and burned the area, including the family homestead, and the family and the rest of the Acadian population were forced to abandon it. The family moved to Le Lac (Aulac, New Brunswick) to stay with relatives and shor
2 CONC tly thereafter, Amand's father died. 
2 CONT In 1754, the French and Indian War or the Seven Years' War broke out between the colonies of British America and those of New France. In 1755, the Expulsion of the Acadians, which was the forced removal by the British of the Acadi
2 CONC an people from present day Nova Scotia, New Brunswick, and Prince Edward Island (collectively known as Acadia) began andAmand's brothers were imprisoned. The family and many other displacedAcadians fled to the Rivière Saint-Jean (
2 CONC St. John River) Valley in New Brunswick. In 1758, the British began a campaign of attacking and burning settlements along the banks of the St. John River Valley<ref>White, Stephen A., ''Acadians on the St. John River 1755-1760,'' 
2 CONC  accessed at http://www.acadian-home.org/saw-acadians-st-john-river.html</ref>. The Treaty of Paris was finally signed in 1763, which formally ended the war. Amand was married to Marie-Josephe Roy by Missionary Joseph M. Bourg<ref
2 CONC >Osborne, Rev. W. J., M.A., M.Ph. ''Joseph Mathurin Bourg: First Acadian Priest.'' University of Manitoba [http://www.umanitoba.ca/colleges/st_pauls/ccha/Back%20Issues/CCHA1950/Osborne.pdf].</ref> on the 5th of September 1774<ref>
2 CONC Ancestry.com. Acadia, Canada, Vital and Church Records (Drouin Collection), 1757-1946 [database on-line]. Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com Operations, Inc., 2007. Original data: Gabriel Drouin, comp. Drouin Collection. Montreal, Quebe
2 CONC c, Canada: Institut Généalogique Drouin.  Search: R > Restigouche > Registres des Missionnaires > 1759-1795 > image 30 of 86. Accessed on ancestry.ca, December 2019.* View {{Ancestry Image|1091|d13p_1657B1542/1488097|ca}} on ances
2 CONC try.ca,  (requires subscription).* View free [https://www.ancestry.com/sharing/18429624?h=2b0de4 Ancestry Sharing Image].  </ref><ref name="Arsenault" /><ref name="Cormier" /> along the St. John River and together they had 7 known
2 CONC  children<refname="Arsenault" /><ref name="Cormier" />:
2 CONT
2 CONT #Jean-Baptiste, born about 1775
2 CONT #François/Francis, born about 1777
2 CONT #Bénoni, born about 1778
2 CONT #Marie, born about 1780
2 CONT #Agnès, born about 1781
2 CONT #Pierre, born about 1783
2 CONT #Denis, born about 1787
2 CONT
2 CONT He was at Madawaska in 1777.<ref name=Theriot/> Amand, Marie-Josèphe and their children later followed his brothers and settled in Sainte-Anne-des-Pays-Bas (near Fredericton).  They were counted in the 1783 Studholm Report which s
2 CONC howed that there were 5 children in their household , they had cleared 15 acres of land that they had occupied for 16 (sic) years.<ref>"Nbgenweb: Census 1783  Studholm Report". 2019. Sites.Rootsweb.Com. https://sites.rootsweb.com/
2 CONC ~cannb/NB_Census_1783Studholm_Report.htm.</ref> "The Acadians of Sainte-Anne had not secured title to their farms, however, and grants to disbanded soldiers and loyalists were soon encroaching on what they considered to be their l
2 CONC and. They deemed the small acreage reserved to them insufficient to support their families. Learning of vacant land on the west side of the Memramcook River, about 20 families moved there between autumn 1786 and summer 1787, inclu
2 CONC ding those of Pierre Cormier and four of his married children. "<ref>Stephen A. White, “CORMIER, PIERRE,” in Dictionary of Canadian Biography, vol. 5, University of Toronto/Université Laval, 2003–, accessed December 3, 2019, http:
2 CONC //www.biographi.ca/en/bio/cormier_pierre_5E.html.</ref> According to Bona Arsenault, Amand settled in Memramcook around 1787 .<ref name=Theriot>Karen Theriot Reader [https://gw.geneanet.org/katheriot?lang=en&pz=frederick+joseph&nz
2 CONC =theriot&ocz=1&p=charles+amand&n=cormier Charles Amand Cormier] citing  Bona Arsenault, HISTOIRE ET GENEALOGIE DES ACADIENS, vols. 1-6; 1630-1775; Quebec, Le Conseil de la VieFrancaise en Amerique, 1965; p. 921 (Beaubassin); Univ.
2 CONC  of Calif., Berkeley Library, CS31 A77 MAIN. <blockquote>Born 1750, Amand married atSainte-Anne-du-Pays-Bas on 5 Sep 1774 to Marie-Josephe ROY, daughter of Joseph & Marie-Annette D'AMOURS de CHAUFFOUR. Amand was at Madawaska in 17
2 CONC 77, and settled at Memramcook around 1787.</blockquote></ref> However, according to other sources, Amand died about 1786 or 1787 <ref name="Cormier" /> in Sainte-Anne (near Fredericton, N.B.) at the ageof about 37, therefore maybe
2 CONC  he did not have time to move to Memramcook.<ref> “390ième anniversaire de l’Acadie, un historique de la famille Cormier 1644-1994.” Tours Placide-Gaudet Inc. Shédiac, 1994,p. 16, 29-30. </ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT == Sources ==
2 CONT <references /> See also:*Léger, Yvon. ''Beloved Acadia of My Ancestors: History and Genealogy'' (Translated by Antoine Bugeauld); Montreal: Fleuve, 1992, Family 152, Pg 316.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----:TO DO: Check Paul Surette's book (Trois Rivieres) to see if he fled with his brothers to the Kamouraska area in Quebec (c1758), back to theSt John River (c1763), and then to Memramcook at L'Anse des Cormiers.
2 CONT * Marriage Record Missionary records of Restigouche/Port Royal/Riviere St Jean/Perce
2 CONT
2 CONT
