1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **Drouin Institute (Archived marriage record - see attached in Media tab)
2 CONT **Burial:  Fr. Jean-François's original "parish" records - F, v.1#21 
2 CONT **"The BROUSSARD dit Beausoleil Party to the Attakapas District, 1765" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***On Acadian census, 1700, Port-Royal, called Allexandre liqlarie, age 18[mos.]
2 CONT ***On Acadian census, 1701, Port-Royal, age 2
2 CONT ***On Acadian census, 1703, Port-Royal, unnamed
2 CONT ***Deported to Province of South Carolina, 13 Oct 1755 aboard HMS Syren, arrived Charleston 27 Nov 1755, age 56, in chains, with nephew Victor Broussard
2 CONT ***Escaped from workhouse in Charleston, Feb 1756
2 CONT ***Surrendered to British authorities, Fort Cumberland (formerly Beauséjour), 16 Nov 1759, age 60, & held hostage until surrender of his brother Joseph & other Acadian resistance fighters
2 CONT ***Held at Georges Island, Halifax, 1760-63
2 CONT ***On list of Acadian prisoners at Halifax, Aug 1763, called Alexandre Brousare
2 CONT ***Signed DAUTERIVE agreement in New Orleans, 4 Apr 1765
2 CONT ***On list of Acadians who exchanged card money in New Orleans, Apr 1765
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana in Feb 1765, age 66
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Signed the Dauterive agreement of April 1765
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre dit Beausoleil, whose nickname came from the haute rivière village where the Broussards lived, born in c1699, married Marguerite, daughter of Michel Thibodeau and Agnès Dugas, at Annapolis Royal in February 1724.  In c17
2 CONC 30, they moved to Chepoudy, where her kinsmen lived, and then to LeCran, today's Stoney Creek, on Rivière Petitcoudiac, probably to put more distance between themselves and British authority in Nova Scotia.  In c1740, they moved f
2 CONC arther upriver to Village-des-Beausoleils, present-day Boundary Creek, New Brunswick.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexandre Broussard dit Beausoleil, age 66, came with wife Marguerite Thibodeau, age 60, and four unmarried children--Sylvain, age 24, Simon, age 21, Anne-Henriette, age 18, and Pierre, age 14.  The tough old resistance fighter an
2 CONC d his wife perished in the epidemic that killed dozens of their fellow Teche valley Acadians that summer and fall.  Five of their sons and a grandson settled at Attakapas.  Daughter Anne married into the Berard family and died in 
2 CONC St. Martin Parish in November 1820, in her early 70s.  
2 CONT
2 CONT www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-BROUSSARD.htm
2 CONT --------------------
