1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Sources:'''
2 CONT **"The First Nominal Census (1769) of Colonial Settlers in Southwest Louisiana"; compiled by Donald Joseph Arceneaux; Provincial Press, Claitor's Publishing Division, Baton Rouge, Louisiana; www.ProvincialPress.us & www.Claitors.c
2 CONC om.
2 CONT **"The BROUSSARD dit Beausoleil Party to the Attakapas District, 1765" - http://www.acadiansingray.com
2 CONT ***Signed Dauterive agreement in New Orleans, 4 Apr 1765
2 CONT ***On list of Acadians who exchanged card money in New Orleans, Apr 1765, called Jean-Bpt.
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1766, District of the Pointe, listed as Juan Baptista Broussard
2 CONT ***Elected co-commandant of Attakapas District & served with René Trahan, 1767-70
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1769, listed as Jean B (Baptiste), age 37
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1771, age 40
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1774, listed as Jean Bte.
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1777, age 44[sic
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1781
2 CONT ***Attakapas, La Luisiana census, 1785, listed as J B
2 CONT ***Succession dated 7 Aug 1834, Lafayette Parish courthouse
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT *'''Notes:'''
2 CONT **Arrived in current day Louisiana in Feb 1765, age 33
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Baptiste le jeune, eldest surviving son of Alexandre Broussard dit Beausoleil and Marguerite Thibodeau, born at Chepoudy or Petitcoudiac in c1731, married Anne Brun probably at Petitcoudiac in c1750.  After his father was dep
2 CONC orted to South Carolina, Jean-Baptiste and his family followed his mother into exile in present-day southeastern New Brunswick, where he served in the Acadian resistance with his father and uncle.  Jean-Baptiste and his family fol
2 CONC lowed his parents into imprisonment in Nova Scotia in 1760 and to Louisiana in 1764-65.  Jean-Baptiste was one of the eight signers of the Dauterive agreement in April 1765.  He and Anne followed his kinsmen to Bayou Teche, where 
2 CONC they had more children, and where, in his later years, Jean-Baptiste served as church warden.  Jean-Baptiste was elected co-commandant of the Attakapas District in 1768 and served with René Trahan until 1770.  Jean-Baptiste and An
2 CONC ne's only daughter Perpétué married into the Comeaux family.  Jean-Baptiste remarried to Élisabeth-Augustine, daughter of fellow Acadian Jean-Baptiste Landry and widow of Joseph Dugas and Amand Landry, at Attakapas in September 17
2 CONC 99; Jean-Baptiste was in his late 60s at the time of the wedding.  Élisabeth was a native of Plouër, France, near St.-Malo, had come to Louisiana aboard La Bergère, the second of the Seven Ships, in 1785, and was in her late 30s a
2 CONC t the time of the wedding.  Jean Baptiste died at the home of son Jean in Lafayette Parish in October 1825.  The priest who recorded his burial said that Jean Baptiste died "at 7:00 p.m. at age 98 years," but he was closer to 94. 
2 CONC  His succession record was filed at the Vermilionville courthouse in August 1834.  Two of his three sons married, but this family line was perpetuated only by son Jean. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.acadiansingray.com/Appendices-ATLAL-BROUSSARD.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Broussard-227 WikiTree Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Aug 27 2018, 22:52:40 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/65254445/jean-baptiste-broussard
2 CONT ----
