2 CONT !St.Martin Parish History - F147, p.154. "Around 1740, Joseph Brossard and his brother, Alexandre, founded Beausoleil (Boundary Creek) in southeastern New Brunswick. During the British invasion of Acadia, many villages were burned
2 CONC , the Acadian refugees were discovered and deported. In the fall of 1758, the British burned Beausoleil and the Brossards fled into the woods. 
2 CONT Joseph became the head of the Acadian resistance in the region. He was 
2 CONT captain of a militia of sharpshooters who took a heavy toll of English soldiers sent into the area to capture the refugees. 
2 CONT Following the defeat of Montcalm and the fall of Quebec to the British in 
2 CONT September of 1759, Joseph, in despair, went to Fort Cumberland (Beausejour). On November 16, 1759, he, his brother Alexandre, Jean Basque, and Simon Martin went as representatives of several hundred Acadian refugees who were threa
2 CONC tened with famine. They delivered a petition to the commanding officer, Major Frye, and surrendered. They, along with others, were made prisoners and sent to Halifax, where 193 of them were held until after the Treaty of Paris was
2 CONC  signed in 1763. 
2 CONT In 1764, Joseph Brossard Dit Beausoleil was placed on a schooner with a 
2 CONT number of other Acadians bound for Santo Domingo. From there they left for 
2 CONT Louisiana, arriving in New Orleans in February of 1765. Joseph Brossard dit 
2 CONT Beausoleil was appointed "capitaine de milice et commandant des Acadiens," and the Acadians settled in the Attakapas District in April of that year. 
2 CONT Shortly after the Acadians arrived in St. Martin Parish, an apparent smallpox or yellow fever epidemic broke out. Joseph died on September 5th and his brother Alexander on September 18th." 
2 CONT Don Boudreaux has marriage 1st - Cecile Babin @@ 1729; 2nd - Ann Thidodeaux @@ 
2 CONT 1748. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Essay 
2 CONT http://www.acadianmemorial.org/ensemble_encore2/record_detail.php?id=121
2 CONT
2 CONT
