2 CONT Amand was a cattleman and planter. He is listed on the roster of "La Compagnie de Milice des Attakapas" dated May 1, 1777. In 1792 Amand is again listed in the Second Company of the Attakapas Militia. - St. Martin Parish History; 
2 CONC F146,p153. 
2 CONT VERMILION VILLE brochure: Fausse Pointe, a living history farm, was once the 
2 CONT home of the Amand Broussard family. Amand arrived in Louisiana from Canada at 
2 CONT the age of eleven. He lived with a brother until he married at the age of 
2 CONT sixteen, and registered his first cattle brand that same year. The following 
2 CONT year his wife died in childbirth leaving him with a son. At eighteen he was 
2 CONT given a land grant. Four years later Amand married again. Thirteen children 
2 CONT were born to him and Anne Benoit. He was a Patriot of the American Revolution 
2 CONT and at his death at the age of 64, his properties were worth over $65,000. The inventory included besides this house nine outbuildings: a kitchen, a potato shed, a stone-mill, a smithy, a school-house, a schoolmaster's house, a co
2 CONC tton mill and press and two barns as well as other tracts of land. 
2 CONT It is easy to imagine yourself a member of this family. The house is the 
2 CONT oldest (1790) and largest of the houses at Vermilionville. Of traditional 
2 CONT "colombage" and "bousillage" construction, it is a French Creole house which 
2 CONT borrows from Anglo-American architecture. The gable end roof is Anglo-American, adapted by the Acadians for the large attic spaces. The roof is made of hand-cut cypress shingles. Notice the topmost row of shingles which extends ov
2 CONC er the top. These generally faced the windward side as protection against rot and leakage from rainy weather. The porch, with three doors, serves as a passage way from the large center room and two side rooms. Look at the hand-wro
2 CONC ught rams-horn hinges. In the living room is a French wrap-around mantel. Dark grey and black were popular colors for mantels. Furniture in this house may have come from the Atlantic coast by way of New Orleans. The chandeliers ar
2 CONC e French. The Louisiana "armoires" are made of "cipre" or baldcypress, which is not a true cypress, but a "taxodium". Cypress was always painted to conceal what Acadians considered to be an inferior wood. "Gros rouge" and Paris gr
2 CONC een were favorite Acadian colors. 
2 CONT The kicthen of this house would have been detached. 
2 CONT From Clarence T. Breaux - Metairie, La.: Amand (Armand) Broussard - Born about 1749 at Village des Beausoleils (now Boundary Creek near Moncton, New 
2 CONT Brunswick), baptized in the Parish of St. Jean; died 8 January 1818 at Fausse 
2 CONT Pointe (now Loreauville), Louisiana. On 24 May 1775, at St. Martinville, 
2 CONT Louisiana, married: Anne Benoit - born about 1758 at Newton, Massachusetts; 
2 CONT died 18 September 1830 at Fausse Pointe. 
2 CONT Amand probably arrived in Louisiana in early 1765 with his parents. The 25 
2 CONT April 1766 census shows him living at Bayou Queue de Tortue, in the Attakapas 
2 CONT District. On 15 July 1771, at Pointe Coupee (now New Roads), Louisiana, he 
2 CONT married Helene Landry but she died within a few years. In February 1772 the 
2 CONT Spanish Governor, Luis de Unzaga, gave him a 451 arpent land grant on both 
2 CONT sides of Bayou Teche at the upper end of the place known as Fausse Pointe. 
2 CONT Amand is on the Roll of the Attakapas Militia on 20 June 1774 and again on that of 1 May 1777. By 1792 he had become an affluent cattleman and planter, having acquired the nearby land grants of his brothers Claude and Joseph, also
2 CONC  1600 arpents of land near Lake Fausse Pointe and lands at Petit Anse, Lake Peigneur and Grosse Isle. At about this time he built his home on the land acquired from Joseph at Fausse Pointe. The inventory taken at his death showed 
2 CONC that the plantation at Fausse Pointe included the main house, a separate kitchen, a potato cabin, a small barn, a schoolhouse, a schoolmaster's house, and old barn, a cotton gin and press, a stone mill and a blacksmith shop. The i
2 CONC nventory also included 21 slaves with their 6 children, farm implements, household items, animals and six tracts of land. The main Acadian house has been restored, placed on the National Register of Historic Places, and relocated 
2 CONC first to New Iberia then to Lafayette (Vermilionville). Amand and Anne had twelve children, all born in St. Martin Parish. 
