1 CONC ont signe  Notes for JOSEPH BROUSSARD, DIT BEAUSOLEIL:  Joseph was called Beausoleil because he was born at a place called Beausoleil near Port Royal. He first settled at Chipuody, then at Petitcoudiac River which hecalled Village
1 CONC  des Beausoleils (now Boundary Creek, New Brunswick). At the beginning of 1747 he and several other Acadians were with a detachment of FrenchCanadian troops under the command of Coulon de Villiers which, during a snowstorm at nigh
1 CONC t, attacked British troops at Grand Pre, commanded by Arthur Noble,inflicting heavy casualties. Later that same year, the Governor of Massachusetts declared Joseph and eleven other Acadians outlaws for having provisioned Frenchtro
1 CONC ops. During the years 1749 to 1755 Joseph was fur trader for his own account and reported to the French authorities on the movement of the British. He was a friend and ally of the Indians, having learned to speak their language. H
1 CONC e took part in the defense of Fort Beausejour in June 1755, but following its fall he presented himself on June 18 before Lt. Col. Robert Monckton to act as mediator in the peace negotiations between the British and Indians on con
1 CONC dition he be granted amnesty. Monckton accorded the pardon conditional upon its confirmation by Lieutenant Governor Charles Lawrence. Apparently such confirmation was never given and a  few weeks later Joseph and a number of other
1 CONC  Acadians were taken prisoner by Monckton. These were deported to South Carolina, except for Joseph, who managed to escape from Fort Lawrence, fled into the woods, lived with the Indians and became leader of the French guerilla sh
1 CONC arpshooters who harrassed the British forces at Fort Beausejour and Port Royal. Governor Vaudreuil authorized Joseph to arm a vessel on the Baie Francaise ( the Bay of Fundy) for incursions against the British. On July 1, 1758 a d
1 CONC etachment of British forces attacked the Acadian guerillas along the Petitcoudian River, inflicting casualties. Joseph was wounded in the ankle and went to Miramichi for treatment there. Following the capture of Louisbourg shortly
1 CONC  thereafter, the British redoubled their campaign against the Acadian guerillas, burning their settlements at the mouth of the Miramichi River and along the Petitcoudiac River, including the Village des Beausoleils. Joseph's vesse
1 CONC l was also seized. Joseph and him men nevertheless continued the struggle, but when they learned of Montcalm's defeat at QC in September 1759, most gave up hope. Futhermore, the Acadian refugees, lacking food and other necessities
1 CONC , faced the coming of another winter. Therefore, Joseph, along with his brother Alexandre and two other men, went to Fort Cumberland on November 16, 1759 as delegates for several hundred starving Acadian refugees and made their su
1 CONC bmission to the commandant of the fort, Colonel J. Frye.  Alexandre remained at Fort Cumberland as hostage while the other delegates returned to the Acadian refugees. Despite having promised to surrender everyone at Fort Cumberlan
1 CONC d in the spring of 1760, Joseph and several other Acadians preferred to remain in the woods. They chose the mouth of Burnt Church River, in the Miramichi region, as their refuge. In August 1760 they were harassing the English alon
1 CONC g the Riviere St. Jean. In the winter of 1761 the French troops at Miramichi, commanded by Grandpre de Niverville, aid down their arms in accordance with the QC surrender terms. Joseph  then became the commander of the Acadians wh
1 CONC o wished to continue the struggle. Captain Roderick Mackenzie succeeded in capturing several of the Acadians in the area of Nipisguit (now Bathurst) and Miramichi, but Joseph escaped into the interior of the woods. However, he was
1 CONC  apparently captured some time later that year or in the spring of 1762, since in the summer of 1762 he is listed with his family at Fort Edward in Pisiguit (now Windsor, Nova Scotia). After the Treaty of Peace of 1763 French auth
1 CONC orities began effors to repatriate to France the Acadian exiles in the British colonies and Acadians remaining in Acadia. In August 1763 Joseph was caught with a copy of a circular letter detailing these efforts. He was brought be
1 CONC fore the Governor's Council in Halifax and shortly  thereafter jailed. In 1764 a number of Acadians, including Joseph and his brother Alexandre and their families, desiring to live in French territory, refused to swear allegiance 
1 CONC to the British government. At the end of November they chartered a vessel to take them from Halifax to Santo Domingo, where they remained a short while, going then to New Orleans on April 4, 1765 with Captain Bernare D'Autrive to 
1 CONC raise cattle on shares in the Attakapas District, the initial herd to be supplied by D'Autrive. Other partners were Alexandre Broussard (Joseph's brother), and his son Jean Baptiste, Jean Dugas, Joseph Guilbeau, Pierre Arceneau an
1 CONC d Oliver Thibodeau. Upon Governor Vaudreuil's recommendation to the Spanish Governor, on April 8, 1765 before leaving New Orleans for the Attakatpas District, Joseph  was appointed "Captain of the Militia and Commandant of the Aca
1 CONC dians." Joseph, his brother Alexandre and wife, Jean Dugas and Joseph Guilbeau all died within a few months after their arrival in the Attakapas District, probably during a smallpox epidemic. From the marriage of Joseph and Agnes 
1 CONC there were eleven children.[]   He was commandant des Acadiens aux Attakapas.   Generation No. 3  3.  CHARLES4 THIBODEAU (MICHEL3, PIERRE2, MATHURIN RHIBAUDA1 THIBODEAUX) was born May 27, 1713 in Port Royal, Acadie, and died Augus
