0 @N6000000000987225940@ NOTE Pierre arrived at Port Royal, Acadia in 1654, under contract for three years at a wage of 100 livres per year (this information is contained in the "Catalogue des Immigrants" By Marcel Trudel). 
1 CONT
1 CONT Listed as a farmer (the 1671 census states "a plowman having seven arpents of cleared land"), he was 
1 CONT a miller (un meunier), owned a grist mill at Pree' Ronde, on upper Port Royal River, 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1698 with four of his sons and several of his compatriots he founded the town of Chipoudy, now Hopewell Hill, New Brunswick. 
1 CONT
1 CONT There is good documentation about Pierre as one of his daughters married the secretary of the governor of Acadia. He was so popular with the governor and his co-settlers that he was given the role of "Sieur", a semi-noble title. P
1 CONC ierre and Jeanne had a total of 16 known children. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1981 a monument to him was erected at Round Hill, Annapolis County, Nova Scotia as the first white settler to live there. 
1 CONT
1 CONT (This information provided by Shirley LeBlanc and her research of the Thibodeau Family).
0 @N6000000003704584582@ NOTE [2414274. ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT From France[v02t3249.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT !LIFE: From "Terrebonne Life Lines" Vol 11, No 1, p. 72. Son of Mathurin
1 CONT Rhibouda Thibodeau RIN 1091 and Marie Delbeau RIN 1092. Born in 1631 in
1 CONT Poitou, France. Married Jeanne Theriault RIN 558 in 1660 in Port Royal.
1 CONT [2713952.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Pierre settled approximately 17 kilometres from the mouth of the Port Royal River in a lovel y spot called Pre Ronde. There he built his house, his farm and his flour and grist mill. Pi erre was undoubtedly prosperous but he had l
1 CONC arger ambitions.
1 CONT On 20 JUNE 1695, Governor De Fr ontenac granted him on the Kennebec River (Maine), a "Seigniory" two leagues deep and a leag ue on each bank of the Kennebec River along with islands. At age 67, he decided to foun d a community on 
1 CONC the northern part
1 CONT of Baie Francaise (Bay of Fundy) called Chipoudie, now Ho pewell Cape. He associated his sons and a few neighbors for this new foundation and had th e necessary machinery for a flour and grist mill brought over from Boston. In add
1 CONC ition t o his
1 CONT occupations as a "farmer" and a "miller", Pierre THIBODEAU was also a "merchant". He t raded furs with the Indians.[2703197.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT REFN: 4005
