0 @N6000000003087543417@ NOTE Sources:Acadian-Cajun Mailing List:
1 CONT Lucie LeBlanc Consentino, (LucieMC@aol.com, http://www.geocities.com/Heartland/Pointe/6106.
1 CONT "CHÉTICAMP - IT'S PIONEERS", by Jean-Doris LeBlanc, Published by:
1 CONT La Société St. Pierre, January 1983.
1 CONT
1 CONT from "CHÉTICAMP - IT'S PIONEERS", by Jean-Doris LeBlanc:
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1763, at Plouer, in France, were also Pierre Aucoin and his brother, Joseph. Both were born at Cobéquid (Truro), Pierre about 1737 and Joseph in 1742. They were the sons of Jean Aucoin and Marguerite Pitre.
1 CONT They were deported to Virginia, which refused to accept them, and were sent on to England, where they were imprisoned with about 1,500 others, in barracks on the docks until 1763. Because of the cold, sickness and hunger, and in s
1 CONC pite of the births during this time of imprisonment, there were only about 300 still alive when released in 1763. 
1 CONT  During his captivity in England, Pierre married Félicité LeBlanc, daughter of Pierre LeBlanc and Françoise Terriot (which makes him brother-in-law to Anne, 3rd wife of Grégoire Maillet). 
1 CONT In 1773, Pierre and Joseph Aucoin left France to return to Acadia with Joseph Deveau, Grégoire Maillet, and the Boudreau's, in the boats of the Robins, who were sending over many Acadians from France that year.
1 CONT Pierre Aucoin wandered with his wife and three children, Anselme, Pierrot and Marie-Félicité. He was for a while in P.E.I., for his son Joseph was born there in 1778. It was also at P.E.I. that his son, Anselme, was married to Ros
1 CONC e Chiasson, daughter of Paul, on February 3, 1787.
1 CONT Again Pierre was at Tracadie where his son, Pierrot, married Luce Babin, daughter of Claude Babin and Marie-Madeleine LeBlanc. At Tracadie in Nova Scotia, Geneviève was born. She later married Isaac LeBlanc.
1 CONT Pierre married twice, the second time in 1813 to Marie Doucet, widow of Martin Larade.
0 @N6000000003087577465@ NOTE Source:
1 CONT Acadian-Cajun mailing list:
1 CONT Lucie LeBlanc Consentino, (LucieMC@aol.com):
1 CONT Information from Stephen White's genealogy of 37 families - prepared for the 1994 Acadian Reunion in Moncton.
1 CONT Maureen Theriot, (Pinestgr@aol.com).
1 CONT
1 CONT From: Bona Arsenault, Tome 2, p.804:
1 CONT
1 CONT At the time, it was common practice to modify ones family name. And so records
1 CONT indicate today's "Theriault" name came from "Terriot", "Theriot", "Theriau", "Therriault", and finally to "Theriault". 
1 CONT
1 CONT It would appear that JEHAN TERRIOT, the grand daddy of all "Theriault's" in
1 CONT North America, was recruited as a laborer in New France, by Charles de Menou, 
1 CONT Sieur D'Aulnay and Charnisay and Gouvernor of Acadia from 1604-1650. 
1 CONT
1 CONT While JEHAN TERRIOT'S birth certificate is not available, the first Acadia Census of 1671 indicates that he lived in Port Royal and was, at the time, 70 years old.
1 CONT It is therefore safe to assume, that JEHAN was born "ABT 1601". The 1671 Port Royal Acadian Census, indicates that JEHAN and his family owned six cattle and one sheep, at the time the census was taken. 
1 CONT Historians have not been able to locate records of JEHAN, Perrine and Claude (their oldest son)'s "boarding pass" registering their sea voyage from France to Acadia, New France however it is estimated they arrived in the late 1630
1 CONC 's or early 1640's. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Historical documents confirm that JEHAN was in fact a recruit of Charles de Menou in 1635. We also know that Charles de Menou of D'Aulnay, conducted two significant "recruitment voyages", one in 1642 and another in 1644. On both o
1 CONC ccasions, he transported a number of families from his home  parish (La Chaussee) in France, to Acadia, New France. During this period of time, JEHAN worked as a laborer, residing in the village of Martaize, located just a few kil
1 CONC ometers from La Chaussee. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Given these two facts (working for Charles de Menou and residing in his close proximity), is it possible that JEHAN accepted a 36 month work contract and traveled from Port de la Rochelle (France) to Port Royal (Acadia), and that 
1 CONC he and his family (wife Perrine and oldest son, Claude) were one of the twenty French families, on the 1644 voyage.
1 CONT
1 CONT While historians are unable to confirm JEHAN'S actual date of death, the Acadia
1 CONT Census of 1681 records "Perrine Brault is the WIDOW of JEHAN TERRIOT". By
1 CONT 1681, many of the "Therriot Family" had moved to Beaubassin, Grand-Pre,
1 CONT Riviere-aux Canards and Cobequid. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Historical records indicate, that by the early 1730's, some of the "Terriot families" had traveled up the St. John River and re-located in areas known today as the Province of New Brunswick. In early 1730, some of the Theriault fa
1 CONC milies traveled up the St. John River and located in what is today known as the Province of New Brunswick. 
0 @N6000000003704592012@ NOTE Arrived in Canada about 1637.
1 CONT [v02t3249.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT !LIFE: From "Terrebonne Life Lines" Vol 11, No 1, p 71. Left Martaize, France
1 CONT for Acadia after his marriage to Perrine Bourque RIN 563 in 1635. Appears on the
1 CONT 1671 census ordered by de Grandfontaine. Their first child Claude RIN 735 was
1 CONT born in Port Royal in 1637. From p. 75, born 1601 in Martaize.
1 CONT [2713952.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT THE NAME(S) THERIAULT...At the time, it was common practice to modify ones family name. An d so,records indicate today's "Theriault" name came from "Theriault"-"Theriault"-"Theriau"-"The rriault" and finally to "Theriault".
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT It would appear that JEHAN Theriault, the grand daddy of all "Theriault's"in North America, wa s recruited as a labourer in New France, by Charles deMenou, Sieur D'Aulnay and Charnisay an d Gouvernor of Acadia from 1604-1650.While
1 CONC  JEHAN Theriault'S
1 CONT birth certificate is not availabl e, the first AcadiaCensus of 1671 indicates that he lived in Port Royal and was, at the time , 70 yearsold.It is therefore safe to assume, that JEHAN was born "ABT 1601".  The 1671 Por t Royal Aca
1 CONC dian Census,
1 CONT indicates that JEHAN and his family owned six cattles and one sheep , at the time the census was taken. Historians have not been able to locate records of JEHAN , Perrine and Claude (their oldest son)'s "boarding pass" registering
1 CONC  their Sea voyage
1 CONT from F rance to Acadia, New France however it is estimated they arrived in the late 1630's or earl y 1640's. Historical documents confirm that JEHAN was in fact a recruit of Charles de Menou i n 1635. We also know that Charles de 
1 CONC Menou of
1 CONT D'Aulnay, conducted two significant "recruitmen t voyages", one in 1642 and another in 1644. On both occasions, he transported a number of fa milies from his home parish (La Chaussee) in France, to Acadia, New France. During this 
1 CONC perio d of time,
1 CONT JEHAN worked as a labourer, residing in the village of Martaize, located just a fe w kilometers from La Chaussee.  Given these two facts (working for Charles de Menou and resid ing in his close proximity), is it possible that JEHA
1 CONC N accepted a 36
1 CONT month work contract an d travelled from Port de la Rochelle (France) to Port Royal (Acadia), and that he and his fam ily (wife Perrine and oldest son, Claude) were one of the twenty french families, on the 164 4 voyage?  While his
1 CONC torians are
1 CONT unable to confirm JEHAN'S actual date of death, theAcadia Ce nsus of 1681 records "Perrine Breau is the WIDOW of JEHANTheriault" By 1681, many of the "The rriot Family" had moved to Beaubassin,Grand-Pre, Riviere-aux Canards and Co
1 CONC bequid.  Historic
1 CONT al records indicate, that by the early 1730's, some of the "Theriault Families" had travelled u p the St.. John River and re-located in areas known today as the Province of New LeBrunswick. I n 1730, some of the Theriault families
1 CONC  travelled up the
1 CONT St.. John River and located in what i s today known as the Province of New LeBrunswick.[2414274. ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Downloaded from Colin Michaud's Rootsweb WorldConnect genealogy pages. [2414274. ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT From FranceArrived in Canada about 1637.
