0 @N6000000003087575788@ NOTE Sources:
1 CONT Peter Landry  (peteblu@blupete.com), 
1 CONT P.O. Box 1200, Dartmouth, Nova Scotia. CANADA.   B2Y
1 CONT Acadian-Cajun Mailing List:
1 CONT H. George Friedman, (friedman@cs.uiuc.edu).
1 CONT
1 CONT From James Hannay's 'Eight Paper', from his "First Families Series", published in the NB Magazine circa 1899-90:
1 CONT
1 CONT "When the census of 1686 was taken, some of the Landry's had taken up their residence at Mines. Claude Landry, a son of René, was one of the first settlers of Mines, and he had associated with him Antoine Landry, who may have been
1 CONC  a brother of his, but who's name does not appear in the census of 1671."
1 CONT
1 CONT "Antoine may not have been born by the Census of 1671, but it was more likely his name was omitted from it by accident."
1 CONT
1 CONT "In 1721 Antoine Landry was sent by the inhabitants of Mines to the council at Annapolis to make explainations in regard to the pillage of the vessel of one Alden by the Indians."
1 CONT
1 CONT Antoine Landry was one of the inhabitants of Mines who in 1744 refused to give supplies to DuVivier who was then about to besige Annapolis.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Notes from H. George Friedman, (friedman@cs.uiuc.edu):
1 CONT
1 CONT An ongoing problem is a certain Antoine Landry, b. 1660 in Port Royal,
1 CONT d. 1711 in Grand Pre, m. 1681 at Grand Pre Anne Marie Thibodeau.  It
1 CONT seems that he was the son of one of the Rene Landrys, but it is uncertain
1 CONT which one.
0 @N6000000002627610411@ NOTE This branch of the Landry family , through your compiler traces his roots , was located at the head of the St. Croix River in Present day Hants County. 
