0 @N6000000001354562576@ NOTE DICTIONARY OF CANADIAN BIOGRAPHY In answer to his fellow countrymanRobert Giffard,to settle in Canada, Gaspard Boucher sold his farm atMortagne on Feburary 1, 1634. We have no written undertaking on Gaspard'spart towards Robert Gi
1 CONC ffard,but it is possible that Gaspard was one of agroup which sailed for New France that same year. Some historians haveexpressed the opinion that on this first voyage he brought his wifeNicole Lemer,and his children: Pierre the e
1 CONC ldest, Nicolas , Marie andMarguerite, allfour had been baptized at Mortagne, we do not know wherehis youngest daughter , Madeleine was born, perhaps on the way over.Alfard Cambray, whosupports the idea that the family arrived in 1
1 CONC 634,thinks they were on a ship commanded by Captain de Ville,which may havealso carried Abbe Jean Le Sueur and Jean Bourdon. On the other handMadame Pierre Montagne states that these last two passengers accompiedRobert Giffard and
1 CONC  his family on a ship commanded by Captain de Nesel.Madame Montagne also hints vaguely that the Boucher family was part ofthisgroup in 1634. In a report drafted in 1695, by Pierre Boucher statedthat he had been brought to Canada i
1 CONC n 1635 at the age of 13. If gaspardBoucher came over in 1634, he may have returned the following year tofetch his family and belongings; it was during a voyage in this year thathe is thought to have had an altercation with his com
1 CONC patroit ThomasGiroux. The act by Guillaume Trenquet, dated September 12,1638 ,thatrecords this incident, specifies that Boucher and his family made thecrossing on a ship commanded by de Ville. Noww the four ships that sailedin 163
1 CONC 4 were comannanded by Du Plessis-Bochart , Nesle , Bontemps , andLormel. Captain de Ville , however may have commanded on of the eightlarge ships, mentioned in in the 1635 RELATIONS,under the date of July4th, six of which anchored
1 CONC  at Tadoussac before proceeding to Quebec.Gaspard was a carpenter by profession,was hired by the Jeusits to work ontheir farm at Notre-Dame-de -Anges. It had been affirmed that thereligious took over the education of the children,
1 CONC  and particular that ofPierre. However in acts that concerned them, the latter sisters statedthat they could neither read or write, nor sign their name. On June9,1652 Nicole Lemer is refered to as being a godmother at Trois-Rivier
1 CONC es, subsequently there is no further mention of her. As for Gaspard, it hasbeen asserted that he died in a fire that distroyed his house; this isbased on a somewhat ambigous statement made by his daughter,Madeleine,who on June 27,
1 CONC 1668 affirmed that that the title to the landgrant of 1646 ' Has been burnrd with the said Gaspard Boucher's house, inwhich it ( he ? )was....." Weather she is talking about the title orhimself or both we do not know, although if 
1 CONC it had been the later theRELATIONS( Jesusit records ) or the parish register would have mentionedit. The last reference made to Gaspard was May 20, 1662 which broughtactionaganist Jacques Besnard. OUR FRENCH CANADIAN ANCESTORS Gas
1 CONC pardBouchermay count among his descendents a great number of the mostremarkable famillies in Canada. Such is in substance , the finding ofMsgr. Cyprien Tanguay contained in a short three line notation, whichmust be readwith a magn
1 CONC ifying glass because it appears as a footnote toa page in his monumental work " DICTIONNAIRE GENEALOGIQUE DES FAMILLESCANADIENNES ". Indeed, this remarkable desendancy includes a Governor, aPrime Minister, Ministers, Legislators,B
1 CONC ishops, illustrious warriors aswell as renowned male and female educators. We have here an entiredynasty about which one could write a saga more captivating even than the'CRONIQUE DES PASQUIER, JEAN CHISTOPHE, or the COMEDIE HUMAI
1 CONC NE . thereare also various intrigues from which an expert writer could create acompellingly passionate tale. In the meantime, while waiting for thelegend ofthe Boucher's to flow from the pen of an historian who, with alittle poeti
1 CONC c flavor, would reveal the exploits and feat-of-arms of thesethree centuries and until all of the scattered documents have beenassembeled, let us portray the ancestor Gaspard as a humble settler whocame from Perche with his entire
1 CONC  family in 1635. He had no intention ofaccomplishing feats of arms and entering his name in the history books.He would leave this task to his son Pierre and to the latter's offspring. Morever , if Gaspard and Pierer had not taken 
1 CONC the initiative ofsettling at Trois-Rivieres, the destiny of this family would have beendifferent. Providence, in its unfathomable wisdon,often knows how toplace good people in the right place at the right time. From 1629 to1632, w
1 CONC hile New France suffered under the yoke of the Kirke brothers,very few Frenchmen lived in this part of the world. For this part, RobertGiffard,Seigneur of Beauport, had returned to France. In 1634 he decidedto come back to new Fra
1 CONC nce, but this time with his wife , his childrenand as many colonists as he could recruit. Jean Guyon, Zacharie Cloutier,Noel Langlois, and Jean Juchereau came with this first group. Meanwhile,gaspard Boucher was making his final d
1 CONC ecision to leave the land of hisfathers. Born at the end of the XVI century, Gaspard, probably the son ofJacques and Francoise Paigne, was married to Nicole Lemarie when he wasabout 20 years old. At least 8 children were born of t
1 CONC his union,5 ofwhom crossed the seas with their parents. However on 25 May 1633,it isnot certain that our ancestor had any intention of leaving his homeland.On that date our master carpenter acquired from his mother-in-law,MarieGas
1 CONC trie ( Castrie ), a plot of land and an orchard at Perruchet, in theParish of St. Hillire. But he sold the land on 1 Feburary 1634. Had healready made the decision to go to New France ? However he was not on thefirst voyage , the 
1 CONC one in 1634 , as some historians stated. Besides onthe following 20 April, we learn that Marie Gastrie, " abandoned wife ofPierre delorme, her second husband " ensured her son-in-law and daughtera legacy surperior to that of her o
1 CONC ther heirs becauseGaspard and Nicolehad provided for the forsaken lady without recompence for 18 months.Pierre Boucher himself stated in his memoires that hewas one of theearliest inhabitants of New France. Having been broughtover
1 CONC  to thiscountry by his late father in 1635. In addition , an act by noteryDecourtemanche discovered by Madame pierre Montagne in the archives ofAlencon confirms this. On19 March 1635 , the Bouchers were still inFrance, but on that
1 CONC  day they sold , left ceded and transfered forever toJean Desjouis, two parsels of plowed but unsown land atCharbonniers,ajoining the monastery of Val-Dieu. This was probablyGaspards last transaction in his native Perche.A few day
1 CONC s later thefamily bade adieu to their relitives and friends,recieved the blessing ofthe Cure Alexandre Chevalier, travled 40 leagues ( 160 KM ) by wagon.From Mortagne to Dieppe and embarked on one of the ships commanded byDuplessi
1 CONC s-Bouchart. Comming out of the English Channel, the convoy evadeda fleet of Turkish ships and made the transit, beset by the usualAtlantic storms. the voyage was longer than the one the previous year,but they finally reached the p
1 CONC romise land 4 July. A long boat sent fromTadoussac announced their imminent arrival at Quebec. The good shipSaint-Jacques, commanded by Roche-Jacquelin,arrived there 8 days later.Samuel de Champlain, who was still living ( died Ch
1 CONC ristmas day 1635 ),writes Montarville de la Bruere , welcomed the new arrivals at FortSaint Louis. Without a doubt he could not have suspected that among them,a boy of 13 ,( Pierre Boucher ) would later be one of the principalinst
1 CONC ruments of the salvation of his dear colony. However, it was greatconsolation during his last days, to see added to his nation such anindustrious and honest family. Gaspard himself asked Champlain forprotection on his arrival at Q
1 CONC uebec. He than took advantage of this toinform the Governor of an incident in which he was a victim. ThomasGiroust, a traveling companion to whom he had intrusted some personaleffects,refused to return them, Champlain ordered Giro
1 CONC ust to return theproperty that he had kept illegally, Giroust pretended to obey, but laterswore he would not.This dragged out for three years,and finally justicewas done. On 12 September 1638, Gaspard recounted that that he had as
1 CONC kedGiroust to takehim from Mortagne to Dieppe, with baggage whichcontained, among otherthings " two large salt cellers, two large flowervases, two large plates, six porridge bowls, all in a tin case, eight toten plates, three smal
1 CONC l bowls, two of pewter one of tin, a ceramic bottlecontaining rosewater, two bushles of cooked pears, and one bushel ofcooked prunes. " all of these items were put on a ship and brought toQuebec. This theftof utensils did not beni
1 CONC fit Giroust, because itappears that he was obigated to sell his property and return to France.During the first ten years of his stay in New France, it seems thatGaspard was a farmer forthe Jesuits at Beauport. In 1644 he recieved 
1 CONC asection of land at Trois-Rivieres, probably granted by Governor CharlesHault de Montmagny. The Boucher family was well established by 1646,because in that year two of the ancestor's daughters ,12 year oldMadeleine and 15 year old
1 CONC  Marguerite, were listed in the civil records ofthe town as godmothers, paired with Michel Leneuf and Jean Godefroy. " ATTHE POST OF TROIS- RIVIERES, writes Sister Estelle Mitchell, the Boucherfamilyhad great influence, being made
1 CONC up of 18 people when the totalpopulation did not exceed 100.--------- Gaspard as well as his sons-inlaw, Baudry and Lafond, signed the official report of the election ,astrustee , Michel Leneuf du Herisson in September 1648 .-----
1 CONC ---------- OnSunday 17 January 1649, at the home of Gaspard Boucher, a marriagecontract between his son Pierre and Marie Chrestien, a Huron woman livingin Trois-Rivieresand who was related to the Pachirine family. MarieMadeleine C
1 CONC hretienne, as historians call her , was a boarder at theUrsuline convent, there Mother Marie I'Incarnation said about her: Thereare some among the Hurons who presevered in their studies and who weraised as French girls. We provide
1 CONC  for them and they do very well inreading, writing and speaking French. We have introduced one of thesegirls to Monsieur Boucher who has been for some time the Governor ofTrois-Rivieres. " the whole Boucher family approved of this
1 CONC  marriage. On16 June 1649, Gaspard ,sold his property to Jean Sauvaget, but Governord'Ailleboust granted him another on 1 June 1650, as, a place to build ahouse at the bottom of the hillside where the fort was situated. Thisproper
1 CONC ty had an area of 300 toises : 10 in frontage and 30 in debth,lacated near the road which ran from the town to the common . Gaspard'simmediate neighbor washis son Pierre, who also recieved a grant of landon the same day. He was lo
1 CONC cated on the north-west , on land they calledthe" MARQUISAT du SABLE " a boundry today which corresponds with the RueBadeaux. From 1650 until his unexpected death, few documents detailGaspard's life in Trois-Rivieres. Undoubtely h
1 CONC e cullivated his land,worked at his trade of carpenter, and above all, he influcened his son,Pierre, who often needed wise council. On 22 November 1655 , the archivesof Provost of Trois-Rivieres mentioned a lawsuit brought by Gasp
1 CONC ardaganist two competiors, the master carpenter Elie Bourbaut and hisassociate Michel Lemay: than we here no more . On 27 June 1668,Madeleine Boucher , Gaspard's daughter and wife of Urbain Baudry ditLamarche , declared that the d
1 CONC eed to the 24 arpents of land that herfather had recieved from Governor Huaalt de Montmagny on 21 March 1646 ,situated on the Saint MauriceRiver " was burned with the house of thesaid Gaspard Boucher, in which it was kept ". Can w
1 CONC e infer from thisevidence that our ancestor himself perished in this house ? Mosthistorians think so . It is certain that Gaspard parished between 1662and 1668. As for his wife Nicole Lemarie , was she also a victim of thisfire ? 
1 CONC We just don't know . It wassaid she was still alive 19 June 1652when records mention her as a godmother. After that date there wasnothing more. The burial records of Gaspard and Nicole havr fallen intoone of the numerous voids in 
1 CONC the registry of Trois-Rivieres.
0 @N6000000002801462359@ NOTE Jean-Pierre Levasseur's Internet site, Our French-Canadian Ancestors, Vol. 6, by Thomas J. Laforest, and the Internet Site, Genealogy Home Page of Jacques L'Heureux, have Nicole's last name spelled "Lemaire." The Dictionary of Can
1 CONC adian Biography gives "Lemer" as an alternate spelling.
