0 @N6000000002627610517@ NOTE While one source of this family says it established themselves in Nicolet, Quebec , Arsenault makes an entry about a Paul Landry originally of Pisiquit , Acadie, married to a Marguerite Bourg. Arrived in Louisiana in 1767 and died
1 CONC  at Donaldsonville, Louisiana in November of 1829.
0 @N6000000004121749944@ NOTE [2703197.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT REFN: 10529
0 @N6000000004121767697@ NOTE [2713952.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT They had seven children, three were male. Pierre had his farm on the banks of the Riviere de s Habitants (Canning area).  After the deportation, members of this family could be found a t Falmouth, England  and Philadelphia.  There
1 CONC  was a Pierre
1 CONT Landry who was among the 483 men wh om Col. Winslow confined on 10th September 1755 at Grande Pre. he is listed to be from the v illage of "de Landry" and was the owner of 6 bullocks, 8 cows, 7 Young cattle, 20 sheep, 16 h ogs and
1 CONC  1 horse; four
1 CONT sons and four ?s.
1 CONT [2703197.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT REFN: 10530
0 @N6000000004121767850@ NOTE [2703197.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT REFN: 19519
0 @N6000000002627610506@ NOTE Pierre and his family were deported to Philadelphia and eventually established themselves at Nicolet, Quebec.
